window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"django-dinah":["One of the first recordings by the Quintette du Hot Club de France: an American pop tune taken at a bright, bouncing tempo, played entirely by strings. Two rhythm guitars chug in the steady, percussive 'pompe' style, the bass thumps underneath, and Stéphane Grappelli's violin and Django Reinhardt's guitar take turns at the melody and at dazzling improvised solos. The sound, with no drums and no horns, is light, springy and instantly recognisable as a new kind of jazz.","Dinah was written by Harry Akst in 1925 and was a standard by the 1930s. Reinhardt, a Manouche guitarist who had taught himself to play again after a caravan fire badly burned his left hand, met Grappelli in Paris, and with the backing of the Hot Club de France, a jazz appreciation society, they formed a string band. They recorded it for the Ultraphone label in late 1934, one of the first sides to present a European jazz group to the world.","Listen to Django's left hand: because two fingers were crippled by the fire, he played most solos with just two fingers on the fretboard, which pushed him toward fast runs along a single string and toward chord shapes no one else used. The breathless octave lines and sudden chromatic slides that sound like pure showmanship are partly solutions to a physical limit. The rhythm section includes his brother Joseph on guitar.","The Quintette's records were quickly admired in France, Britain and the United States, and American musicians were surprised to hear such original jazz from Europe. Critics now regard these 1934 sessions as the birth of what came to be called gypsy jazz, and Reinhardt as the first major jazz innovator from outside America. Some older writers treated the string band as a novelty; that view has vanished.","Listen first to the rhythm guitars alone: they are the engine. Then follow the solos and hear how different violin and guitar sound over the same chords. Compare Louis Armstrong's Hot Five, the American model, then play Minor Swing, three years later, to hear the group fully mature."],"django-bach-double-concerto":["A jazz treatment of the first movement of Bach's Concerto for Two Violins. The two violinists, the American Eddie South and Stéphane Grappelli, play Bach's interweaving lines more or less as written, while Django Reinhardt's guitar and a bass replace the Baroque string orchestra with a swinging rhythm. Then, gradually, the violins break loose into improvisation over Bach's harmonies. The pleasure is hearing a strict Baroque counterpoint start to swing.","Recorded in Paris in 1937 for Swing, the new record label founded by Charles Delaunay and the Hot Club de France. Eddie South, a classically trained Black American violinist barred by racism from an orchestral career, had studied in Paris and Budapest and was performing in Europe. Jazz musicians of the 1930s often 'swung the classics', usually as a novelty, but these players knew the music and treated it with real respect.","The title is the joke and the manifesto in one: a solemn, fully formal French label describing a three-minute jazz record, as if claiming the same standing as the concert hall. Listen for the moment the players stop reading Bach and start inventing: it is subtle, because they keep Bach's own chord sequence, which fits jazz improvisation surprisingly well.","Jazz critics prized it as an early example of the dialogue between jazz and classical music, long before third-stream experiments. Classical listeners sometimes found it disrespectful; today it is more often cited as proof of how naturally Bach's counterpoint fits swing, anticipating the Modern Jazz Quartet and Jacques Loussier's Play Bach. Eddie South's playing, long overlooked, has also been rediscovered through it.","Listen first to a period-instrument recording of Bach's Concerto for Two Violins, then play this and notice what stays and what changes: the melody lines are the same, the rhythm is transformed. Listen for the point where the violins begin to improvise. Then hear the Modern Jazz Quartet's Django for another meeting of Bach and jazz."],"django-djangology":["Recordings from Rome in 1949 that reunited Django Reinhardt and Stéphane Grappelli, backed by an Italian rhythm section of piano, bass and drums rather than the old rhythm guitars. They revisit their classics, including Minor Swing and Nuages, and other favourites, but the playing has changed: Reinhardt's lines are longer, harmonically bolder and closer to bebop, while Grappelli is as elegant as ever. The feel is relaxed, like two old friends catching up, with brilliant flashes.","The Second World War had split the partners: Grappelli stayed in London and Reinhardt in occupied Paris, where he became a star. After the war they reunited only occasionally. Bebop had arrived from New York, and Reinhardt listened hard to Charlie Parker and Dizzy Gillespie. These Rome sessions were recorded in early 1949, and the tapes were issued much later, in 1961, after Reinhardt's death in 1953, under the title Djangology.","Listen to the chords behind Reinhardt's solos and the notes he chooses: he is using the altered harmonies and quick, angular phrases of bebop inside tunes he had played for fifteen years. Without the old pompe rhythm guitars, the drummer and pianist give a more modern pulse, and the two soloists have more space. It is the sound of the founder of a style updating himself in public.","When it appeared, some admirers of the 1930s Quintette felt the old magic was missing, and it has sometimes been treated as a coda. Others hear it as some of Reinhardt's most mature playing, a guitarist absorbing bebop without losing his voice, and regard the late recordings as underrated. It remains the best-known document of the pair's reunion and a favourite among guitarists.","Play the Rome version of Minor Swing next to the 1937 original and listen for what has changed in Reinhardt's lines. Notice the drums and piano replacing the rhythm guitars. Then hear Charlie Parker's Ko-Ko to hear the new music that was pulling him forward, and the Modern Jazz Quartet's Django for the tribute that followed his death."],"amalia-rodrigues-uma-casa-portuguesa":["A bright, swaying song rather than a lament: the singer describes a humble Portuguese home where bread, wine and a warm welcome matter more than money, and where a stranger at the door is invited to the table. The Portuguese guitar skips along in a lilting major-key rhythm, and Amália sings with a smile in the voice, holding back the sobbing ornaments she reserved for tragic fados. It lasts barely three minutes.","The music is by Artur Fonseca and the words by Reinaldo Ferreira and Vasco de Matos Sequeira; Amália took it up in the early 1950s, when she was already Lisbon's most famous singer and beginning to tour abroad. Portugal was then governed by Salazar's Estado Novo, whose official picture of the nation was pious, rural, modest and content, a picture this song fitted almost too neatly for later comfort.","Strictly speaking this is not fado at all but a marcha-like popular song dressed in fado instruments; notice that there is none of the free, speech-like stretching of the line that defines a true fado. Listen too for how the guitar answers each vocal phrase with a little cadence of its own, the conversational habit that the Portuguese guitar brings to everything it accompanies.","It became one of the most recognised Portuguese songs in the world and a staple for later singers. After the 1974 Carnation Revolution, however, its cosy image of contented poverty was heard by many as dictatorship propaganda, and Amália herself was unfairly branded a creature of the regime. Today it is usually enjoyed as period charm, with the political argument remembered rather than resolved.","Hear it first simply as a dance song and let the lilt carry you. Then play it next to Barco Negro to hear the difference between Amália's light popular manner and her tragic voice, and afterwards try a later cover by a contemporary fadista to hear how a new generation reclaims it with irony or tenderness."],"amalia-rodrigues-estranha-forma-de-vida":["A Strange Way of Life: the singer speaks to her own heart as if it were a separate, wilful creature, asking why it insists on living for a love that does not come, and wishing it would either stop or follow her will. The accompaniment is the classic fado trio texture reduced to two guitars, the Portuguese guitar weaving a restless filigree while the classical guitar marks a steady walking bass beneath the voice.","Amália wrote the words herself and set them to an existing tune by Alfredo Marceneiro, the revered old master of Lisbon fado, known as his Fado Bailado. By the early 1960s she was an international star who was increasingly writing and choosing serious poetry, and this song shows her as a poet in her own right, speaking in the plain, direct language of the Lisbon neighbourhoods where fado was born.","Fado singers traditionally set new poems to a stock of established melodies, the way hymn texts are fitted to hymn tunes, so the same Marceneiro tune exists in other songs with other words. Once you know that, listen to how Amália bends the given melody to her own verses, delaying a syllable here and swelling a vowel there, which is where the real authorship of a fado performance lies.","It has become one of the most sung fados of all, recorded by almost every major fadista of the following generations, and is often cited as proof that Amália was a writer as well as a voice. Critics generally rank her own recording above later versions for its restraint: the emotion comes from what she holds back rather than from the volume she could easily have used.","Listen to the first verse only for the guitar, which never stops moving, then again for the voice, noticing how still it sounds against that motion. Afterwards hear a later version by a younger fadista to compare phrasing, and follow with the album Com Que Voz to hear where her taste for poetry led."],"amalia-rodrigues-com-que-voz":["An album of fados composed by Alain Oulman on poems by major Portuguese-language writers, from the Renaissance poet Luís de Camões, whose sonnet gives the title track, to modern poets. The instruments are the usual pair of guitars, but the songs are through-composed and harmonically adventurous, closer to art song than to the tavern. Amália's voice, now darker and heavier than in the 1950s, carries long, grave lines over them.","Oulman, a composer from a Franco-Portuguese family, had been writing for Amália since the early 1960s, beginning with the Busto album. In the late 1960s he was arrested by the Salazar regime's political police and forced to live abroad, so this record was made in the shadow of his exile. It came out in 1970, the year of Salazar's death, when the dictatorship was weakening but censorship was still very much in force.","The title track sets a sixteenth-century sonnet by Camões, the national poet, yet sounds wholly of its own time: listen for the unexpected turns in the guitar harmony at the end of each line, which is Oulman's classically trained hand at work. Poems by others on the album carry veiled melancholy about loss and absence that listeners under the dictatorship could hear as political without anything being said outright.","Many fado lovers and Portuguese critics consider it Amália's finest album, and it is often named the greatest fado record ever made. At the time some traditionalists felt Oulman's music had taken fado too far from the popular melodies of the old quarters. That debate is now largely settled in the album's favour, and it became the model for the literary fado of later singers.","Start with the title track and give it complete attention; it is a slow, unfolding song that rewards stillness. Then listen to the album straight through at night. Compare it with Busto to hear how the partnership deepened, and with Schubert's songs to notice how close this is to the European art-song tradition."],"paco-de-lucia-almoraima":["An album of solo-guitar flamenco pieces, each built on a traditional form: bulerías, alegrías, rumba, soleá and others. Paco's guitar is at the centre, playing thundering rasgueado strums, machine-gun picado runs and delicate thumb melodies, while bass, percussion and handclaps fill in beneath some tracks. The title track, named after a place near his home region in Cádiz, opens the record with a flash of the new, harmonically richer flamenco he was inventing.","In the mid-1970s Paco de Lucía was the most famous flamenco guitarist in the world, riding the success of Entre dos aguas and touring widely. Spain was emerging from Franco's dictatorship, which ended with his death in November 1975, and a younger generation wanted flamenco to sound modern rather than folkloric. Almoraima answered that wish while staying rooted in the forms Paco had learned from his father in Algeciras.","Listen to the chords rather than the speed. Paco was bringing in close, jazz-tinged harmonies, with added notes that make a chord shimmer, while keeping the rhythmic cycles exactly as a traditional dancer would expect them. On the rumba track, notice how bass and percussion lock into a groove that looks forward to his sextet of the following years, where the Peruvian cajón would become a permanent flamenco instrument.","Guitarists regard it as one of the essential Paco albums of the 1970s, often grouped with Fuente y caudal as the pair that redefined what a flamenco guitar record could be. Purists at the time worried that his virtuosity and new harmonies diluted the tradition; the verdict of later players, nearly all of whom learned from records like this, has been overwhelmingly on his side.","Put it on loud and start with the opening title track, following the handclaps to feel the twelve-beat cycle of the bulerías. Then pick a slower piece and listen for the thumb melody in the bass strings. Afterwards compare Friday Night in San Francisco to hear the same fingers in a jazz setting."],"paco-de-lucia-siroco":["A return to the solo flamenco guitar after years of group work, named after the hot wind that blows from Africa across southern Spain. Each track is a traditional form played with new depth: a soleá dedicated to the guitarist Niño Ricardo, a rondeña written for his son, and quicker pieces full of rhythmic fire. The sound is intimate and close-miked, one guitar filling the room, with little or nothing added around it.","Through the late 1970s and 1980s Paco had led a sextet with bass, flute, cajón and his brothers, and had toured with jazz guitarists John McLaughlin and Al Di Meola. Siroco was a deliberate homecoming to the essential flamenco guitar, made by a man of forty who had absorbed jazz and Latin harmony and now wanted to fold it back into the old forms. Spain, by then a democracy in the European Community, was rediscovering flamenco as national art.","In the soleá for Niño Ricardo, listen for the pauses: Paco lets phrases hang in silence, which is a tribute to the older master's grave, measured style rather than to his own speed. In the rondeña, notice the unusual tuning, with a low string dropped so the guitar rings with open, drone-like resonance, a device associated with the pioneer Ramón Montoya.","Many flamenco guitarists and critics regard Siroco as Paco's finest solo album, the one that best balances innovation with depth. It is widely studied by players and transcribed note for note. Some listeners prefer the raw brilliance of his 1970s records, but the prevailing view is that Siroco is where his mature voice is most completely heard.","Begin with the rondeña for its slow, ringing, almost harp-like sound, then go to the soleá and follow the long, stern phrases. Save the fast tracks for last. Afterwards try Albéniz's piano pieces Asturias or Sevilla to hear how classical composers imagined these same Andalusian sounds from outside."],"camaron-al-verte-las-flores-lloran":["Camarón's first album, recorded with Paco de Lucía as guitarist, and usually known by the title of its opening bulerías. The sound is spare and traditional: a young, cracked, piercing voice over one guitar and handclaps, working through the standard forms of flamenco song. What immediately marks it out is the raw urgency of the voice, which pushes hard at the top of its range and seems to catch fire on the long held notes.","José Monje Cruz, nicknamed Camarón, the shrimp, was a young gypsy singer from San Fernando near Cádiz who had come to Madrid to sing at the tablao Torres Bermejas, where he met Paco de Lucía. This record began a partnership of some nine albums over the next decade, largely produced within Paco's family, with his brother Pepe writing many of the songs. Flamenco was then heard mostly in tablaos for tourists and in private gatherings.","Listen to the way Paco's guitar never simply accompanies: between vocal lines he answers with short falsetas, compact melodic phrases, that anticipate where the voice will go next. The two were barely out of their teens, and the tightness of their exchanges, heard in the smallest delays and accents, already shows the telepathy that later records would make famous.","It was well received among aficionados and established Camarón quickly as the most exciting new voice in flamenco. Later critics have valued these early Philips albums as the purest record of his singing before his experiments of 1979. Some prefer them to everything after; others hear them mainly as the necessary ground from which La leyenda del tiempo could take off.","Start with the title bulerías and listen only to the voice for a minute, noticing its roughness and the way it bends notes. Then listen again and follow the guitar replies. Afterwards compare the Antología del cante flamenco to hear the older singers Camarón learned from, and then leap to La leyenda del tiempo."],"camaron-como-el-agua":["A record that returned Camarón to a warmer, more traditional sound after the shock of La leyenda del tiempo, with Paco de Lucía and the young Tomatito sharing the guitar parts. The title song, gentle and flowing, compares love to water, and the album moves between songs of this kind and harder, older forms. The voice is at its most expressive: husky, supple and able to switch between tenderness and ferocity within one phrase.","La leyenda del tiempo in 1979 had sold poorly and baffled traditional fans, and Camarón needed to reconnect with his audience. Pepe de Lucía, Paco's brother, wrote much of the material, and Tomatito, a gypsy guitarist from Almería still in his early twenties, was becoming Camarón's regular accompanist on stage. Spain was in its first years of democracy and flamenco was starting to be heard by a younger, urban audience.","With two guitarists present, listen for how the roles are shared: one guitar tends to keep the harmonic floor while the other adds decorative runs and answers the voice, and they swap. Tomatito's attack is slightly rounder than Paco's, so on careful listening you can often tell which hands are playing, a first sign of the partnership that would carry Camarón through the rest of his life.","It was received as a welcome return and has remained one of the best-loved Camarón records, with the title song among his most popular. Critics sometimes treat it as a consolidation rather than an adventure, but many aficionados consider it the finest balance between his traditional and modern selves, and Tomatito's arrival is now seen as a turning point in its own right.","Start with the title song, which is the easiest way into Camarón's voice for a newcomer. Then find a slower, deeper track and notice how his tone darkens. Compare with La leyenda del tiempo to hear what he had stepped back from, and with Potro de rabia y miel to hear the same trio a decade later."],"camaron-soy-gitano":["The most commercially successful flamenco album ever made, pairing Camarón and Tomatito with orchestral arrangements on several tracks. The title song, a proud declaration of gypsy identity sung as bulerías, sets the tone; around it are love songs, a tango and slower pieces where strings swell behind the voice. The sound is polished and big, a studio production far from the tablao, yet the voice at its centre has lost none of its grit.","The album was produced by Ricardo Pachón, who had also been behind La leyenda del tiempo, and the orchestral parts were reportedly recorded with a London orchestra. Camarón was by now a national figure, adored by young people who knew little flamenco, and Spain was preparing for 1992, the year of the Seville Expo and the Barcelona Olympics, keen to present its culture as modern and international. The album sold in quantities no flamenco record had approached.","Listen for how the orchestra is kept away from the rhythm: the strings mostly sustain long chords and countermelodies, while the compás, the rhythmic cycle, stays entirely with guitar, palmas and voice. That separation is why the record still sounds like flamenco rather than pop with a Spanish tinge; the orchestra decorates the room but never walks onto the dance floor.","It was a huge success and is often called the best-selling flamenco album of all time. Critics were divided: some heard the orchestra as sentimental gloss, others as a natural widening of the frame. Today it tends to be valued above all for Camarón's singing and for Tomatito's playing, with the orchestral dressing seen as a product of its moment.","Start with the title track and follow the handclaps; the cycle of the bulerías is the thread through everything. Then listen for one of the orchestral songs and ask whether the strings add or distract. Compare Falla's El amor brujo, a classical composer's orchestral flamenco from the opposite direction."],"camaron-potro-de-rabia-y-miel":["Camarón's final album, a colt of rage and honey in its title, reuniting him with Paco de Lucía and with Tomatito on guitars. The arrangements are lean and traditional after the orchestral scale of Soy gitano: voice, guitars, handclaps and a little percussion. The voice has grown hoarser and more worn, but that roughness gives the slow songs a devastating weight, and the fast bulerías still crackle with nervous energy.","The album was released in 1992, only months before Camarón died in July of that year, at forty-one, of lung cancer. His life had been marked by exhausting tours and addiction, and he was in poor health during the sessions. Reuniting with Paco, after years in which Tomatito had been his main guitarist, gave the record the feel of a closing circle; his funeral in San Fernando drew enormous crowds.","Listen for the moments when two guitars play together behind the voice: Paco's sharper, more harmonically daring lines against Tomatito's warmer, more rhythmic style. Knowing the circumstances, listeners often hear farewell everywhere, but the most revealing thing is purely musical: how carefully the voice now paces itself, placing strength where it counts rather than spending it freely as in 1969.","The album was greeted with reverence, and after his death it took on the status of a testament. Critics praise it as a return to essentials and as one of his most moving records. Some note that the voice is diminished; most answer that the loss of power is exactly what makes the singing so affecting, as with late recordings of Billie Holiday.","Listen to it straight through, then return to his debut from 1969 to hear the same partnership twenty-three years earlier: bright youth against worn experience. Pay attention to the slowest song on the album and how long he lets each phrase breathe. Afterwards try Enrique Morente's Omega for another view of where flamenco singing went next."],"carlos-paredes-verdes-anos":["A short instrumental theme for Portuguese guitar with a second guitar beneath, written for a film and now the best-known piece of Portuguese instrumental music. A gently rocking accompaniment supports a melody that is at once wistful and quietly determined, played with the bright, bell-like tremolo of the twelve-string Portuguese guitar. It unfolds in repeated waves, each slightly more intense, and ends as simply as it began.","Paredes wrote it for Os Verdes Anos, The Green Years, a 1963 film by Paulo Rocha about a young man from the country adrift in Lisbon, a key work of the new Portuguese cinema. Paredes, heir to a family of Coimbra guitarists, earned his living as a hospital clerk; a few years earlier he had been imprisoned by the Salazar regime's political police for his communist sympathies, and he had lost his job as a result.","Listen to the right hand rather than the tune: Paredes uses a rapid, nervous alternation of strokes that makes long notes shiver, a technique rooted in the Coimbra guitar style of his father Artur Paredes rather than in Lisbon fado. The melody never becomes a song for a singer; its phrases are shaped by what the fingers do, which is exactly what makes the piece sound instrumental through and through.","The theme became famous far beyond the film and is now something like an unofficial anthem of Portuguese melancholy, played at ceremonies and covered by many musicians. Critics have seen in it the birth of an independent concert music for the Portuguese guitar, freed from its role as accompanist to fado singers. Its fame has sometimes overshadowed Paredes's larger, more complex compositions.","Listen first for the melody, then a second time only for the lower guitar and its rocking pattern. Then watch the film, if you can find it, to hear the theme in context. Follow it with his album Guitarra Portuguesa and then Movimento Perpétuo to hear the same voice expanded to larger forms."],"carlos-paredes-movimento-perpetuo":["An album of original instrumental pieces for Portuguese guitar with a second guitar, named after its restless title track. The music draws on fado, Coimbra student songs and rural Portuguese dances, but it is composed concert music, with long developing sections, sudden changes of mood and passages of great speed. The recognisable sound is the Portuguese guitar's tremolo running almost without pause while the second guitar provides harmony and drive.","Released in 1971, in the last years of the Estado Novo under Salazar's successor Marcelo Caetano, the record appeared when Paredes was still working a day job and performing relatively rarely. His music, wordless yet widely understood as a voice of the people, carried a resistance meaning that did not need lyrics to get past the censor. After the 1974 revolution he became a symbol of the new, free Portugal.","In the title track, listen for how a single figure keeps circling, like a machine or a heartbeat, while the melody grows above it: the perpetual motion of the title is both a Baroque idea and an image of relentless labour. Paredes played with an unusually aggressive attack, and you can often hear the percussive sound of nail on string, a physical intensity closer to a flamenco player than to a salon guitarist.","It is often ranked alongside Guitarra Portuguesa as his most important record and a founding document of modern Portuguese instrumental music. Critics have compared his compositional ambition to that of classical composers, while noting that he was largely self-taught in composition. Later guitarists, and rock and jazz musicians in Portugal, have cited it as a key influence.","Start with the title track and let the momentum carry you, then go back and listen to the slower pieces, where the tremolo becomes lyrical rather than driving. Compare the Prelude of Bach's First Cello Suite for another perpetual-motion idea, and Paco de Lucía's Almoraima for a neighbouring Iberian guitar tradition from the same decade."],"enrique-morente-homenaje-flamenco-a-miguel-hernandez":["An album of poems by Miguel Hernández set to flamenco song forms, sung by the young Enrique Morente with guitar. Instead of the traditional short verses of flamenco, the voice carries literary poetry about love, poverty, war and imprisonment, fitted to forms such as the soleá, the seguiriya and the fandango. The texture is severe, one voice and one guitar, and the focus is on words as much as melody.","Miguel Hernández, a poet of peasant origin who fought for the Republic in the Civil War, died in a Francoist prison in 1942, and his name remained politically charged in Franco's Spain. For a flamenco singer to devote an album to him in 1971 was a bold gesture, aligning Morente with the anti-Franco left and with the young people who were rediscovering banned or neglected poets.","Listen to how Morente cuts and stretches Hernández's lines to fit flamenco's fixed forms: he repeats words, breaks them across a phrase, or adds the traditional wordless lament on a single vowel so that the poem becomes a cante without losing its sense. This was a technique he would take much further later, in his settings of Lorca and on Omega.","The album marked Morente as an intellectual among flamenco singers and won him a following beyond the flamenco world, while some traditionalists regarded literary settings as foreign to the art. Today it is recognised as a pioneering record in the long line of flamenco albums devoted to poets, and as a document of cultural resistance in the late Franco years.","Read a translation of a Hernández poem before listening to its setting, and then follow how the words are bent to the melody. Start with a slower track where the voice is almost alone. Compare Camarón's La leyenda del tiempo, which set Lorca eight years later, and Victor Jara's songs from Chile for protest sung in a different Hispanic tradition."],"enrique-morente-homenaje-a-don-antonio-chacon":["A tribute to Antonio Chacón, the great cantaor of the early twentieth century, in which Morente revives the songs and styles associated with him, with Pepe Habichuela on guitar. The repertoire leans towards the ornate, melodically elaborate forms Chacón favoured, such as malagueñas, cartageneras and other mining songs of the east. The voice is controlled and highly ornamented, and the guitar is restrained, leaving space for long, winding vocal lines.","Chacón, from Jerez, had been the most celebrated singer of flamenco's golden age, praised for bringing refinement and range to the art, but his style had fallen out of fashion. Morente, from Granada's Albaicín quarter, was known as a modernist, and this deeply researched record proved he was also a master of tradition. It appeared in 1977, as Spain held its first free elections since the 1930s.","Chacón's recordings survive only in scratchy early discs, so Morente was reconstructing a style partly from memory, other singers and those old records. Listen for the long, florid melismas, many notes on one syllable, and for how he keeps an even, almost sweet tone rather than the rasping cry of gypsy singers such as Camarón; that contrast is the real subject of the album.","The album was acclaimed by aficionados and critics, and it is widely regarded as one of the essential flamenco recordings of its decade, proving that the singer who later made Omega had earned his experiments. It is often cited as a model of how to honour a historical style without museum stiffness. Some listeners find its refinement austere next to more fiery gypsy singing.","Listen to one malagueña slowly and follow the voice through each ornament. Then play Camarón on the same kind of song, if you can find one, to hear the difference between refinement and raw force. Afterwards go straight to Omega and listen for how the same technique survives in a noisy rock setting."],"markos-vamvakaris-piraeus-quartet":["The body of songs made by the Tetras i Xakousti tou Peiraia, the Famous Quartet of Piraeus, which brought together Markos Vamvakaris, Giorgos Batis, Anestis Delias and Stratos Pagioumtzis, heard today in compilations. Rough male voices, the bright jangle of bouzouki and the small, high baglamas, and a guitar keeping the dance rhythm: this is rebetiko in its first, hardest form, sung in the zeibekiko, hasapiko and other dance metres of the port.","In the early 1930s the port of Piraeus was full of refugees from Asia Minor, dockworkers and men living on the margins, who met in cafés and hashish dens to play. Vamvakaris, a former docker from the island of Syros, had taken up the bouzouki, then considered a disreputable instrument. From 1936 the Metaxas dictatorship imposed censorship that pushed this underworld subject matter out of the recording studios.","Listen for the rhythmic character of the zeibekiko, a slow dance in nine beats, grouped irregularly so that it seems to limp and lurch; it was danced by a single man, improvising, and the music gives him that unsteady ground. Notice also how the singers' pitch is modal rather than major or minor, using scales, called dromoi, that link rebetiko to Ottoman music.","These records were long despised by respectable Greek society; only from the 1950s and 1960s, after composers such as Hadjidakis praised rebetiko, did the old Piraeus style gain prestige. Today the Quartet's recordings are regarded as founding documents of modern Greek popular music, often compared to early blues for their mix of rough sound and deep feeling.","Start with any compilation of Vamvakaris from the 1930s and focus on the bouzouki's introductions, the taximi, where the player improvises over a drone before the song begins. Then listen for the limping nine-beat dance. Compare early Delta blues by Robert Johnson or Son House, then continue to Tsitsanis for the gentler postwar style."],"markos-vamvakaris-ta-matoklada-sou-lampoun":["Your eyelashes shine: a love song in which the singer compares the gleam of a woman's eyes to the flash of blades, a mixture of adoration and danger typical of rebetiko. It opens with a bouzouki introduction and settles into a slow, swaying dance, with Vamvakaris's hoarse, untrained voice carrying a simple melody. It is one of his signature songs and a standard of the rebetiko repertoire.","Vamvakaris recorded it in the 1930s, the decade when he was establishing the bouzouki as rebetiko's lead instrument and himself as the genre's patriarch. He came from the Catholic community of Syros, had worked on the docks of Piraeus from his teens, and taught himself the instrument after hearing older players in the port. His recordings sold to working-class audiences across Greece and the diaspora.","Listen to Vamvakaris's voice itself: it is famously rough, almost cracked, and he makes no attempt to smooth it. Singers and critics later called this voice the sound of the rebetiko world, precisely because it was not trained. Also notice how simply he plays the bouzouki, with few notes and strong rhythm, in contrast to the flashier players who came after him.","The song has been recorded by many later singers, and is regarded as a classic of the early Piraeus style. Critics prize it for the directness that later, more commercial rebetiko often lost. Some modern listeners find the old recordings hard to hear through the surface noise, but most accounts of rebetiko place songs like this at the genre's heart.","Listen to the bouzouki introduction and then to how the rhythm settles. Imagine a single dancer improvising slowly to it. Then compare a later cover by a polished singer to hear what has been gained and lost. Go on to Tsitsanis, whose songs show how the next generation refined this sound."],"tsitsanis-omorfi-thessaloniki":["Beautiful Thessaloniki: an affectionate song praising the northern Greek city, its beauty and its people, and confessing that the singer's heart belongs there. The bouzouki plays a lilting introduction and a clear, singable melody that is easy to remember after one hearing. Compared with the harsh Piraeus style of the 1930s, the sound is smoother and warmer, and the melody more like a popular song than a drinking-den improvisation.","Vassilis Tsitsanis, from Trikala in Thessaly, lived in Thessaloniki during the German occupation, when he ran a small music café there and wrote many songs that could only be recorded after the war. This song is his tribute to the city that sheltered him. After 1946 the record industry resumed, and Tsitsanis became the leading figure of a gentler, more melodic rebetiko that appealed to a much wider audience.","Listen to how the melody is built in balanced, repeating phrases, almost like a Western popular song; this was a deliberate move by Tsitsanis away from the modal improvisations of older players. At the same time, the bouzouki's ornaments still use the old Ottoman scales, so the song sits between two worlds, which is exactly the balance that made him so popular.","It became one of the best-loved Greek songs, sung by many performers and adopted as an informal anthem of Thessaloniki. Some rebetiko purists have seen Tsitsanis's melodic style as a softening of the genre, but most critics credit him with widening its expressive range and making it a national music rather than a subculture.","Listen to the opening bouzouki phrase, then try humming the melody back after one hearing. Compare it with Vamvakaris's Frangosyriani to hear how the style moved from Piraeus to a broader popular music. Then listen to Synnefiasmeni Kyriaki, which shows Tsitsanis's darker side from the same years."],"tsitsanis-ninou-live-1955":["A rare live recording of Tsitsanis and the singer Marika Ninou performing in an Athens music club, reportedly taped on a portable machine and released only decades later. The sound is rough and full of room noise, but it captures the real atmosphere of a rebetiko night: Ninou's strong, dark voice, Tsitsanis's bouzouki flashing in introductions and solos, and audience reactions around them. It includes many of their best-known songs.","In the early 1950s Tsitsanis and Ninou, a singer of Armenian origin, formed one of rebetiko's great partnerships, and their club performances drew devoted crowds. Studio records of the time were limited to about three minutes, so this tape preserves something no commercial disc could: the extended playing and interaction of a live evening. Ninou died of cancer only a couple of years later, still young.","Listen to the bouzouki solos, which are longer and freer than on studio records, because in the club the musicians played for dancers and could stretch a song as long as they wished. Notice also how Ninou varies her phrasing from verse to verse, something frozen out of studio takes, and how the audience's voices occasionally join in.","When it finally appeared, it was greeted as a precious document, one of very few live recordings of classic rebetiko, and it has become a treasured record among Greek listeners. Critics admit its sound quality is poor, but most feel the atmosphere and the performances outweigh the noise, and it gives unique evidence of how the music actually lived.","Treat it as a night out rather than a hi-fi record: put it on, accept the crackle and listen for the room. Follow Ninou's voice first, then the bouzouki's breaks. Compare Synnefiasmeni Kyriaki in its studio form, and then Umm Kulthum's live concert recordings for another great singer heard in front of her audience."],"tyagaraja-jagadanandakaraka":["The first of Tyagaraja's five Pancharatna kritis, the Five Gems, set in raga Nata and Adi tala. After a stately opening refrain and a second section, the song unrolls through a long chain of verses, each first sung to bare note-names and then repeated with words. The text, unusually for Tyagaraja, is in Sanskrit rather than Telugu, and it strings together epithets of Rama. Nata's bright, emphatic leaps give the whole piece the feel of a procession.","Tyagaraja spent almost his whole life in Tiruvaiyaru, a temple village on the Kaveri near Thanjavur, singing to Rama and teaching a circle of disciples who carried his songs out into South India. The five Pancharatna kritis, in the ragas Nata, Gaula, Arabhi, Varali and Sri, were probably not conceived as a set; the grouping belongs to later tradition. The Maratha court at Thanjavur was the region's great patron, but this music was made for the shrine, not the palace.","Listen to how the verses are built: each one is first sung entirely in solfa syllables, the Carnatic equivalent of do-re-mi, and only then does the same melody return carrying text. You hear the tune naked, then clothed. Scholars have counted the Sanskrit epithets and argue they approach the traditional hundred and eight names of Rama, which would turn the song into a sung rosary as much as a composition.","Jagadanandakaraka opens the annual Tyagaraja Aradhana in Tiruvaiyaru each January, when hundreds of musicians sing the five Gems together at his shrine. That massed unison is a twentieth-century practice, and some musicians feel it flattens the solo subtleties of the style. Because no manuscript in Tyagaraja's hand survives, the versions sung by different teaching lineages differ in small melodic turns, and each school defends its own.","Start with the massed Aradhana recording to feel the scale of the thing, then hear a single great voice sing it, where the slides between notes become audible. Count the verses and notice that each begins with syllables before words arrive. Nata is bright and forceful; compare it with the gentler, sadder Varali Pancharatna to hear how much a raga changes a song's temperature."],"tyagaraja-samaja-vara-gamana":["A kriti in raga Hindolam and Adi tala, addressed to Krishna, whose gait the opening line compares to that of a noble elephant. Hindolam uses only five notes, so the melody moves in wide, dark steps with no small half-step comforts. The song runs through its three sections, refrain, second theme and verse, and in concert usually grows a long improvised preface, a wandering exploration of the raga, and passages of improvised note-name singing before the final return.","Tyagaraja's output was vast, perhaps several hundred surviving songs, mostly to Rama. This one turns instead to Krishna as musician: the verse speaks of the seven notes born from the Vedas and of the flute-player who delights in them. It belongs to Tyagaraja's lifelong theme that music itself, rightly sung, is a road to liberation, an idea he inherited from earlier devotional singers and set down in songs his disciples memorised and later wrote out.","Hindolam is the same five-note scale that North Indian musicians call Malkauns, the midnight raga. Hear a Hindustani Malkauns next to this kriti and you will recognise the pitches at once, yet the two sound unlike each other: Carnatic singing shakes and bends almost every note, while the northern style lets notes sit and glow. It is the clearest single demonstration of how two sister traditions treat one scale.","Samaja Vara Gamana is one of the most performed kritis in the repertoire and a favourite for showing off improvised note-singing, which some purists think now overwhelms the composed song. It also crossed into Telugu cinema and light music, which spread it well beyond the concert hall. Lineages disagree over a few phrases, and it is sung at slightly different speeds by different schools, a normal condition for an orally transmitted repertoire.","Listen first for the opening words and the stately upward climb of the tune, then for the moment the singer drops the text and sings note-names at speed, trading phrases with the violinist. Afterwards, play a Hindustani Malkauns and hear the same five notes behave in a completely different way. Recordings by the great mid-century vocalists are the place to begin."],"tyagaraja-nidhi-chala-sukhama":["A kriti in raga Kalyani and Misra Chapu, a lilting seven-beat cycle grouped three plus two plus two. The text is a question: does wealth bring happiness, or does the presence of Rama? The melody poses it in a rising refrain and answers in long, arching phrases. Kalyani, with its raised fourth note, gives the song a bright, open, almost festive colour that makes the renunciation it describes sound like a choice made gladly rather than grimly.","Tradition ties the song to an invitation from the Thanjavur court, often said to be from the Maratha king Serfoji II, offering Tyagaraja gifts and a place among the palace musicians. He declined, and this song is remembered as his reply. The story cannot be verified, but it fits what is known: he lived modestly on alms gathered while singing in the streets, and he refused professional court service all his life.","Listen to the tala rather than the tune. Misra Chapu's uneven seven-beat cycle makes the refrain lean forward and catch itself, which suits a song built on a hesitation between two choices. The drummer's pattern repeats the lopsided grouping throughout; once you can hear the short-long shape, the question in the words seems to be asked by the rhythm too.","Nidhi Chala Sukhama has become a statement of the artist's independence from patronage, which is how it is often introduced in concerts and textbooks. Historians note that the anecdote attached to it was written down only long after Tyagaraja's death, by disciples with a hagiographer's instincts. The song is loved regardless, and it remains one of the standard Kalyani pieces every serious student learns.","Tap the seven-beat cycle as three then two then two, and the song opens up. Then notice how the singer lingers on the raised fourth note, the one that gives Kalyani its brightness. Pair it with a Hindustani Yaman, the northern cousin of this raga, and with the Kalyani entry elsewhere in this catalogue to hear the scale in other hands."],"dikshitar-kamalamba-navavarana":["A cycle of eleven Sanskrit songs to the goddess Kamalamba of the Tiruvarur temple: an opening meditation in raga Todi, nine songs for the nine enclosures of the Sri Chakra, and a closing blessing in Sri raga. Each enclosure song takes a different raga, among them Anandabhairavi, Kalyani, Sankarabharanam, Kambhoji, Bhairavi, Punnagavarali, Sahana, Ghanta and Ahiri. The pace is slow and weighty, the melodies dense with ornament, the texts packed with Tantric names and diagrams.","Muthuswami Dikshitar was born in Tiruvarur and returned to its temples throughout his life. He was trained in Sanskrit learning and in Srividya, the Tantric worship of the goddess through the Sri Chakra, a geometric diagram of nested triangles and petals. The Navavarana songs are a worship ritual turned into music, meant to be sung in sequence, often on specific festival days, by a composer who was also a practising initiate.","Dikshitar sets each of the nine enclosure songs in a different grammatical case of the goddess's name: the first names her as subject, the next as object, then 'by her', 'for her', 'from her', 'of her', 'in her', and finally calls to her directly. The cycle is a declension as well as a pilgrimage, and a Sanskrit speaker hears the name bend at every step inward toward the centre of the diagram.","Singers and scholars treat the cycle as the most intellectually ambitious work in Carnatic music, and it is performed complete more rarely than it is praised. Some find the density forbidding compared with Tyagaraja's warmth; others hear in Dikshitar's slow, ornament-heavy style the deepest statement of what a raga can hold. Text and melody variants between the lineages that preserve it are still being compared in modern editions.","Do not take it in one sitting at first. Begin with the Kambhoji or Sankarabharanam song, the grandest melodies, then the opening Todi meditation. Notice how slowly the lines unfold and how each syllable carries several notes. Once familiar, hear the cycle complete, ideally from a singer trained in the Dikshitar lineage, and follow the journey inward from the outer square to the centre."],"dikshitar-navagraha-kritis":["A set of Sanskrit songs to the nine heavenly bodies of Hindu astrology: the Sun, Moon, Mars, Mercury, Jupiter, Venus and Saturn, with Rahu and Ketu, the two shadow planets of the eclipses. Each song has its own raga, from Saurashtram for the Sun and Asaveri for the Moon to Yadukula Kambhoji for Saturn, and the seven planetary songs are set in the seven principal talas of the Carnatic system, one rhythmic cycle for each.","Dikshitar spent much of his life travelling between the temples of the Tamil country, and many South Indian temples have a shrine to the nine planets. Dikshitar was steeped in Sanskrit astrology and ritual. The planetary songs describe each deity's colour, vehicle, gemstone, day of the week and powers in the compressed, catalogue-like Sanskrit that marks his texts.","The seven planet songs map neatly onto the seven suladi talas, but the songs for Rahu and Ketu do not fit the scheme. Many scholars think those two were added later, perhaps by a disciple or a later musician completing the set, because early sources name only seven. So the famous nine may be seven plus two, and hearing the set as a strict rhythmic system shows where the seam lies.","The Navagraha songs are widely sung, individually more often than as a set, and the Saturn song in Yadukula Kambhoji is especially popular because of Saturn's feared influence in astrology. Their attribution debate concerns only the last two. Some critics find the texts more list than poetry; defenders answer that the lists are the point, a worship formula and a musical demonstration of raga and tala at once.","Start with the Sun song in Saurashtram and the Jupiter song in Athana, which are vigorous and bright, then the Saturn song for contrast in its darker colour. Try to feel how the tala changes from song to song; a teacher's recording where the beat is clapped aloud helps. Hear it beside Holst's Planets to compare two very different ways of turning astrology into music."],"dikshitar-sri-subrahmanyaya-namaste":["A Sanskrit kriti in raga Kambhoji and Rupaka tala, a brisk three-beat cycle, saluting Subrahmanya, the god Murugan, son of Shiva. Kambhoji is one of the broad, majestic ragas of the tradition, full of wide swings and long held notes, and Dikshitar lets it expand at a slow, dignified pace. The song is commonly treated as a main piece in a concert, preceded by a long improvised exploration of the raga and followed by passages of improvised note-singing.","Murugan had a special place in Dikshitar's life. Tradition says the god appeared to him at the hill temple of Tiruttani and set him on his path as a composer, and Dikshitar signed his songs with the name Guruguha, a title of Murugan, ever after. This kriti belongs to the body of songs he addressed to his chosen deity, written in the dense, learned Sanskrit that separates his style from Tyagaraja's Telugu.","Listen for the name Guruguha tucked into the last section of the text. Every Dikshitar kriti carries it as a signature, but here it doubles as the name of the god being praised, so the composer's signature and the song's subject become the same word. Listen also for the rhyming syllables Dikshitar plants at the same beat positions in successive lines, an ornament of the text that the melody underlines.","Sri Subrahmanyaya Namaste is counted among the finest Kambhoji compositions and is a standard concert centrepiece. Critics admire how Dikshitar lets the raga's characteristic phrases emerge without hurry, and singers of the Dikshitar lineage insist on a slower tempo than is sometimes heard. The kriti remains a test of whether a singer can sustain weight and gravity rather than dazzle.","Listen first to the improvised exploration of Kambhoji that usually precedes it, so the raga's shape is in your ear, then follow how the composed song fixes those shapes into a melody. Notice the three-beat cycle beneath the long vocal lines. Compare it with Dikshitar's Vatapi Ganapatim, which is quick and bright, to hear the two ends of his style."],"dikshitar-meenakshi-me-mudam":["A kriti in raga Gamakakriya, close kin to the raga now called Purvikalyani, in Adi tala, addressed to Meenakshi, the fish-eyed goddess of the great temple at Madurai. The text asks her simply for joy. The melody is grave and tender, full of long bends between notes, and it moves in slow, curving phrases that circle back to the words of the opening request. It is among the best-loved of Dikshitar's songs to the goddess.","Madurai's Meenakshi temple is one of the largest in South India, and Dikshitar composed several songs for it during his years of temple pilgrimage. Tradition ties this one to his death, at Ettayapuram on Deepavali, 1835: he is said to have asked his disciples to sing it and to have died as they reached the line calling the goddess the one who frees from bondage. The story is devotional legend, but it fixed the song's aura.","Gamakakriya and Purvikalyani are nearly the same scale, and which name to use is a live argument. Dikshitar's school preserved older raga forms described in a treatise associated with Venkatamakhin, so his Gamakakriya is not quite the Purvikalyani sung in most concerts today. Listen for the way the upper notes are approached obliquely; it is one of the details that separates the old raga from the modern one.","The song is widely performed and often chosen by singers as a slow, reflective centre to a recital. The deathbed story is repeated in almost every programme note, and scholars treat it with caution. Musicologists value the kriti as evidence of how Dikshitar's lineage kept alive earlier raga shapes, and modern editions based on the Dikshitar family tradition have reopened questions about how it should be sung.","Hear it sung slowly by a mature voice and listen for the repeated return of the plea for joy, each time slightly differently ornamented. Then play a modern Purvikalyani piece and ask what has changed. Pair it with Nagumomu for a contrast: Tyagaraja complains to his god, Dikshitar asks quietly and waits."],"purandara-dasa-pillari-gitas":["A small group of simple songs in raga Malahari, beginning with Sri Gananatha in Rupaka tala, a salute to Ganesha. They move almost one syllable to one note, with little ornament and a steady beat, and each lasts only a minute or two. Their texts are a devotional patchwork in Sanskrit and Kannada. After scales and rhythmic exercises, these are the first real songs a Carnatic student learns, and nearly every South Indian musician has sung them as a child.","Purandara Dasa, born around 1484 and active in the Vijayanagara empire, is called the grandfather of Carnatic music less for any single song than for the teaching sequence credited to him: graded scale exercises in raga Mayamalavagowla, rhythmic patterns in the seven talas, then these first songs. Vijayanagara's capital, Hampi, was one of the great cities of the sixteenth century, and its devotional singers took music out of the temple and into the street.","Malahari is chosen deliberately. It is a simple, rather sombre raga whose notes sit comfortably in a child's voice and whose shape overlaps with the scale exercises the student has just finished. The gitas are thus built to carry a learner from drill to song without a jump. Listen to how the tune keeps outlining the scale in plain steps: it is a piece of teaching design disguised as a hymn.","Nobody thinks these little songs are major art, and some teachers now question whether a sixteenth-century curriculum is the right start for modern students. Yet the sequence has held for centuries across a whole region, and scholars regard it as the backbone of Carnatic pedagogy. Attribution to Purandara Dasa rests on tradition rather than documents, like most of the huge body of songs carrying his name.","Look for a recording by children or a teacher's demonstration rather than a concert artist, because the plainness is the point. Listen for the steady beat and the one-note-per-syllable setting. Then play Dikshitar's Vatapi Ganapatim, another Ganesha song, and hear how far a Carnatic composition can travel from this starting line in two and a half centuries."],"purandara-dasa-bhagyada-lakshmi-baramma":["A Kannada devaranama inviting Lakshmi, goddess of fortune, to come into the house, usually sung today in raga Madhyamavati and Adi tala. The refrain is a warm, open call, and the verses picture her footsteps, her anklets ringing, as she enters the home. The melody is simple enough for anyone to join and Madhyamavati's bright pentatonic shape makes it feel celebratory and auspicious, which is why it so often closes a concert or blesses a household event.","Purandara Dasa's songs were written for singing by ordinary devotees, in Kannada rather than Sanskrit, in the Haridasa movement that spread Vaishnava devotion through the Vijayanagara empire. This song belongs to domestic ritual: in Karnataka it is sung at weddings, housewarmings and the festival of Varamahalakshmi. What reached us is the text and the tradition of singing it; the tune now used was fixed by later musicians.","The song crossed the North-South divide. Bhimsen Joshi, the Hindustani vocalist from Karnataka, recorded it in his own northern style, and many listeners in India know it best that way. Compare his version with a Carnatic one and you hear the same Kannada words carried by two different ideas of what a note is: held and glowing in the north, constantly bent and shaken in the south.","Among Kannada speakers it is perhaps the most familiar song attributed to Purandara Dasa, sung by people who would never attend a concert. Scholars note the usual caveat: thousands of compositions carry his signature, Purandara Vittala, and some are certainly by followers. This one is securely in the core tradition, and its popularity has never been in doubt, even among critics who find it slight beside the great kritis.","Hear one Carnatic recording and Bhimsen Joshi's back to back; the comparison is the best way in. Listen for the refrain returning after each verse, and for the image of the goddess's steps in the text. Then turn to Kumar Gandharva's devotional songs in the northern bhajan tradition to hear how widely this kind of singing reaches across India."],"purandara-dasa-venkatachala-nilayam":["A devotional song to Venkateshwara, the god of the Tirumala hill temple above Tirupati, usually sung in raga Sindhubhairavi and Adi tala. Sindhubhairavi allows notes from outside its basic scale, so the tune has a wistful, folk-like flexibility, sliding between bright and shadowed versions of the same note. The refrain names the god as the one who dwells on the Venkata hill, and each short verse returns to it like a pilgrim's chant.","Tirumala was one of the great pilgrimage centres of the Vijayanagara world, and the rulers of the empire patronised it heavily. Purandara Dasa, a Madhva Vaishnava devotee, wandered as a singing mendicant after renouncing his merchant fortune, and songs like this one were made to be sung on the road and at the shrine. It is a devaranama rather than a concert kriti, with simple structure and memorable refrain.","Sindhubhairavi is a raga of the lighter end of the concert, where borrowed notes are allowed, and the tune currently sung almost certainly dates from long after Purandara Dasa. Singers choose it because the raga's mixed colours suit a song about longing for a distant hill. Listen for the moment the melody touches a note that does not belong to the raga's main scale; it gives the song its ache.","M. S. Subbulakshmi's recording made the song famous far beyond Karnataka, and her voice is now inseparable from it for many listeners, as it is from the Venkatesa Suprabhatam sung at the same shrine. Some critics regret that the tuneful concert version has eclipsed older, plainer ways of singing the Dasa songs, but it has kept this text alive in every corner of the Carnatic world.","Start with Subbulakshmi's recording, then her Venkatesa Suprabhatam, which addresses the same god at the same temple. Listen for the returning refrain and the slight darkening whenever the melody bends into a borrowed note. It is usually sung near the end of a concert as a lighter piece; hear it as a song of the road rather than of the recital hall."],"purandara-dasa-kallu-sakkare-kolliro":["A Kannada devaranama built as a street cry: the singer is a hawker calling out that he has rock sugar for sale, and the sugar turns out to be the name of Krishna, sweeter than anything in the market and never spoiling. The song is short, strophic and bouncing, with a refrain meant for everyone to shout back. It is sung to more than one tune in different traditions, always brisk and cheerful rather than solemn.","Vijayanagara's bazaars were famous; foreign visitors described streets of traders selling jewels, spices and cloth. Purandara Dasa had been a wealthy merchant, a pawnbroker by tradition, before he gave everything away, and many of his songs borrow the language of trade, debt and weighing. Here the former merchant turns his old trade into a sermon, selling the one commodity he now valued.","Listen for the list of the sugar's virtues in the verses: it does not rot, it needs no carrying cart, no tax is paid on it, it costs nothing. Each is a joke against the market and a claim about grace. Knowing that the singer had been a rich trader turns the comedy into something sharper, a man mocking the life he left behind in its own vocabulary.","The song is a favourite in Karnataka and in the Hindustani repertoire of singers from the region, and it shows the humour and plain speech that made the Dasas popular. Critics sometimes pass over such pieces as light, but historians of the Haridasa movement cite them as evidence of how devotional song reached people through everyday images. As with all his songs, the tune is later than the text.","Listen for the call-and-response energy of the refrain and imagine it shouted in a crowded bazaar. Compare a Carnatic performance with a Hindustani devotional rendering from a Karnataka singer. Then turn to Kumar Gandharva's nirguni bhajans for another tradition of saint-poets who used plain, earthy images to talk about God."],"yatsuhashi-kengyo-fuki":["Fuki is the first of the kumiuta, the song suites for koto that Yatsuhashi Kengyō established as the core of the blind musicians' repertoire. The player sings while accompanying on the koto, and the piece moves through a series of short verses, each fitted to the same fixed musical length. The voice is slow and restrained, the koto answers each phrase with plucked patterns, and the whole has the grave, ceremonial pace of old court song.","Yatsuhashi, a blind musician working in Kyoto and Edo in the mid-seventeenth century, took the koto music of the Tsukushi school, a Buddhist-connected tradition from Kyushu, and reshaped it for the blind guild that controlled professional music. He is credited with the hirajōshi tuning that gives koto music its familiar melancholy scale. Kumiuta were his teaching and performing core, learned in strict order, and Fuki came first.","The verses of a kumiuta usually have nothing to do with each other: they are borrowed poems from different sources, strung together by musical form rather than meaning. So do not listen for a story. Listen instead for how each verse lands at the same length and closes with similar koto figures, as though the music is a frame into which any poem could be placed.","Kumiuta are now performed far less than instrumental danmono like Rokudan, and many listeners find the slow, unaccompanied-feeling vocal line austere. Scholars nevertheless regard Yatsuhashi's kumiuta as the foundation of sōkyoku, the art music of the koto, and the Ikuta and Yamada schools still teach them. How much of the music is Yatsuhashi's own, and how much inherited from the Tsukushi repertoire, is still debated.","Listen once without trying to follow the words, just to the relation between voice and koto: the voice sustains, the koto punctuates. Then hear Rokudan, the instrumental piece credited to the same musician, and notice the shared tuning and plucked patterns. A recording by a senior player of the Ikuta school is the best starting point."],"yatsuhashi-kengyo-midare":["A danmono, an instrumental piece in sections, for solo koto, traditionally credited to Yatsuhashi Kengyō. Where Rokudan's six sections are all the same length, Midare's are deliberately irregular: its name means disorder, and it is also called Jūnidan, twelve sections. The music begins slowly and gathers speed across the piece, with plucked phrases, sliding pitches made by pressing the string behind the bridge, and sudden flourishes that break the expected pattern.","Danmono were the instrumental showpieces of the blind guild's koto repertoire, learned after the kumiuta songs. They were played in the homes of merchants and samurai in Kyoto, Osaka and Edo, where koto study became a mark of refinement, especially for daughters of prosperous families. Midare stands with Rokudan and Hachidan as one of the three danmono attributed to Yatsuhashi, though the attribution rests on guild tradition.","In standard danmono each section has the same fixed number of beats, 104, so a listener raised on Rokudan expects a section to end at a certain moment. Midare plays against that expectation: sections run short or long, and cadences arrive early or late. The disorder is calculated. Knowing the regular pattern first is what lets you hear the irregular one as wit.","Midare is considered one of the most demanding and interesting of the classical danmono, and it remains a standard piece for advanced koto students. Some historians think it may be the work of Yatsuhashi's followers rather than his own, and editions differ in details. It has attracted modern composers and scholars interested in its flexible structure, which seems freer than much Edo-period instrumental music.","Hear Rokudan first so the regular section length is in your body, then play Midare and notice where the phrases stop matching. Listen for the pressed notes that bend upward and the acceleration toward the end, following the jo-ha-kyū principle of slow beginning, development and rush. Afterward, try Takemitsu's November Steps for a modern view of the same instruments' world."],"itri-rast-nat":["A na't, a hymn in praise of the Prophet Muhammad, in makam Rast, setting a Persian poem by Jalāl al-Dīn Rūmī. It is sung by a single voice, unaccompanied and without a fixed beat, in long melismatic phrases that rise and fall around the central notes of Rast. This is the piece that opens the Mevlevi sema ceremony: the singer's line hangs in silence, and only afterwards do the drum and the ney flute enter.","Buhurizade Mustafa Itri worked in Istanbul in the second half of the seventeenth century, under the sultans Mehmed IV, Süleyman II and Ahmed II, and was connected with the Mevlevi order, the followers of Rūmī. The Mevlevi lodges were among the chief schools of Ottoman music, where ney players and singers trained. His setting of this na't became fixed in the ritual and has been sung at the beginning of every sema since.","Rast's third and seventh notes sit between the Western major and minor positions, slightly lower than a piano's. Listen for the way the singer approaches those pitches, and for the note held before the final descent to the home note. Because it is unmetered, each singer shapes the timing differently, so no two ceremonies sound identical, yet the melodic path is fixed and recognisable.","Among Ottoman musicians the Rast Na't is regarded as one of Itri's greatest works and one of the highest achievements of Turkish religious music. After the Mevlevi lodges were closed in 1925 it survived in recordings and in the government-sanctioned performances at Konya, which some traditionalists felt turned a rite into a spectacle. It is still the standard test of a Mevlevi naathan, the ceremony's solo singer.","Listen before you know anything, simply to the lone voice and the silence around it. Then watch a full sema and hear how this solo opens the ceremony before the drum strikes. Compare it with Itri's Segâh Tekbir, sung by many voices, and with Dede Efendi's Ferahfeza Âyin, the kind of work this na't introduces in practice."],"itri-neva-kar":["A kâr in makam Nevâ, the largest and most elaborate vocal form of Ottoman secular art music. The kâr sets a short poem in long, ornamented lines, stretching syllables over many notes and filling gaps with terennüm, vocables like ya-lel-li sung purely for sound. It unfolds over a long rhythmic cycle played on small kettledrums, and it moves through several sections, touching neighbouring makams before settling back in Nevâ.","The kâr stood at the summit of the fasıl, the suite in a single makam that was the standard form of Ottoman courtly music-making. Itri composed for the palace and for elite gatherings in late seventeenth-century Istanbul, and his secular works were passed down orally, teacher to student, for two centuries before being written in Western notation in the late Ottoman and early Republican periods. This kâr is one of the works that established his standing.","Listen for the terennüm passages, where words give way to syllables with no meaning. They are not filler; they let the melody expand without the constraint of a line of poetry, and in a kâr they can outweigh the text. Hear how the drums keep a long cycle of many beats going underneath, so a single phrase of melody can stretch over what feels like several bars.","Ottoman writers and modern Turkish musicologists place Itri's kârs among the classics of the repertoire, though, as with most music of his time, the version sung today reflects two centuries of oral transmission, and the notated editions differ. The early Republic's hostility to Ottoman art music pushed pieces like this out of radio for a period, and their revival since has been a matter of cultural politics as much as taste.","Try following only the drums at first; once the long cycle is clear, the vocal line's great arcs make sense against it. Then listen for the moments words dissolve into syllables. A recording by a Turkish state classical ensemble or a specialist early-music group is the best way in. Compare Umm Kulthum's Al-Atlal for a later Arabic art of long, ornamented song."],"tanburi-cemil-bey-muhayyer-saz-semaisi":["A saz semâîsi, the instrumental piece that traditionally closes an Ottoman fasıl, in makam Muhayyer. It has four sections, called hanes, each followed by a recurring refrain, the teslim. The first three hanes run in the limping ten-beat semâî rhythm; the fourth breaks into a faster, lighter meter, a burst of virtuosity before the refrain returns one last time. Cemil Bey's lines run fast and angular, full of leaps that suit the plucked tanbur.","Tanburi Cemil Bey was the outstanding Ottoman instrumentalist of the empire's last decades, a player of the long-necked tanbur and the small bowed kemençe who also wrote a method book on Ottoman music. Istanbul around 1900 was changing fast: gramophone companies were recording local musicians, Western music was fashionable at court, and the old fasıl was moving into cafés and private concerts. He composed within the classical forms while playing them with new brilliance.","Listen for the fourth hane: after the steady ten-beat limp, the meter suddenly shifts and the tune starts to run. Cemil Bey makes the change sound like an improvised break, but the notes are composed. Then listen to the teslim coming back afterward and notice how the slower refrain now feels like a return home. The contrast is the form's oldest trick; he plays it harder than his predecessors.","Cemil Bey's saz semâîs are among the most frequently performed modern additions to the Ottoman instrumental repertoire, and this one is a standard piece for advanced tanbur and ensemble players. Some purists have found his writing too showy, too close to the café. Most modern players and scholars take the opposite view, hearing in it a late flowering that kept the classical forms alive into the recording age.","Count the four sections and wait for the refrain after each. The fourth section's change of pace is the moment to listen for. Then hear his own 78-rpm recordings of improvised preludes, so you can compare the composer's written music with his improvising. Anouar Brahem's oud records offer a modern continuation of the same sound world."],"tanburi-cemil-bey-sedaraban-saz-semaisi":["A saz semâîsi in makam Şedaraban, one of the Hicaz family of makams, whose scale contains two of the wide, exotic-sounding steps that Western listeners associate with the Middle East. The four sections and the refrain follow the classical plan, but the melody is dark and chromatic, twisting around those wide steps. On the tanbur the long sustain of each plucked note gives the lines a bell-like, brooding quality.","Cemil Bey wrote relatively few composed pieces; his fame rested on his playing and recordings, and on his treatise on Ottoman music, the Rehber-i Mûsikî. His peşrevs and saz semâîs were written for the ensembles he played in and were spread by students and recordings. Şedaraban was a makam rarely used for major works, and his piece helped keep it in circulation in the twentieth-century repertoire.","Listen to the interval between certain adjacent notes, wider than any step in a Western major scale. Şedaraban has two such gaps, and Cemil Bey arranges his lines so that the ear keeps falling through them. On a good recording you can also hear the tanbur's fretted pitches, which divide the octave far more finely than a piano, placing notes between the keys.","The piece is regarded as one of the classics of the late Ottoman instrumental repertoire and is played by Turkish classical ensembles and by musicians from Greece and Armenia who share the tradition. Opinions differ, as with all his music, over tempo: some players take it very fast to emphasise virtuosity, others slower, closer to the older practice, letting the unusual intervals sound fully.","Play it after the Muhayyer semâî: same form, completely different colour. Listen for the gapped steps and how the refrain keeps returning to them. A performance on solo tanbur lets you hear the instrument's resonance; an ensemble version shows how the tune sounds doubled by ney and kemençe. Follow with Cemil Bey's own improvised recordings for his sound as a player."],"miyagi-michio-mizu-no-hentai":["A song for voice and koto setting a sequence of short classical poems, each describing water in a different form, among them mist, rain, snow and dew. The structure follows the traditional tegotomono pattern, sung passages alternating with instrumental interludes in which the koto develops the material alone. The writing is traditional in scale and gesture, but the instrumental interludes already show a composer eager to make the koto sing more expansively.","Miyagi was born in Kobe, lost his sight as a child and trained as a professional koto player in the guild system. His family moved to Korea, then under Japanese control, and he wrote this piece there in 1909, at fourteen, as his first composition. The poems came from a school textbook. Its success in Japan helped launch his career and his later move to Tokyo.","The sequence of poems is a small lesson in natural science: water changing state through the seasons. A teenage composer took schoolbook verses and set them in the old guild form, so the piece looks both backward, to Edo-period tegotomono, and forward, to the modern Japanese interest in educational and seasonal music. Listen for how each change of water gets a change of texture in the koto part.","The piece is still widely taught and performed, and it shows that Miyagi mastered the classical style before he began to blend it with Western harmony. Critics have treated it as an unusually mature first work. It is less famous abroad than Haru no Umi, but in Japan it is often cited as the moment a new composer of the koto announced himself.","Follow the images: listen for the koto trying to imitate falling rain, then the stillness of frost. Pay attention to the interludes, which carry the most interesting instrumental writing. Afterwards hear Haru no Umi, written twenty years later, to measure how far he travelled, and Rokudan, to hear the tradition he started from."],"miyagi-michio-seoto":["A duet pairing the ordinary thirteen-string koto with the seventeen-string bass koto, the jūshichigen, which Miyagi himself developed. The bass instrument lays down a steady, rushing pattern like water over stones, while the treble koto spins bright figures above it. The piece moves in sections from gentle flow to fast rapids and back, with the two instruments sometimes answering each other and sometimes locking together in the same rhythm.","In the early 1920s Miyagi was working in Tokyo to build a modern Japanese music, the movement known as Shin Nihon Ongaku, drawing on Western harmony and ensemble writing. He wanted a bass voice for koto ensembles, which traditionally lacked one, and designed the seventeen-string koto in 1921. Seoto was one of the pieces written to put the new instrument to work, and it became a staple of the modern koto repertoire.","Listen to the bottom line. The bass koto often plays repeated figures and supporting tones that behave rather like a Western bass part, holding the harmony underneath the melody. Traditional koto music has no such layer. Once you notice it, you can hear Miyagi importing the logic of Western chamber music into a Japanese ensemble while keeping Japanese scales above.","The piece remains one of the most performed koto duets and a standard vehicle for the seventeen-string instrument. As with much of Miyagi's work, traditionalists once objected to the Western touches, while later composers took them as a starting point. Details of its composition date vary slightly in sources, but its role in establishing the bass koto is not in dispute.","Listen first only to the bass koto's rushing pattern and imagine the stream. Then shift attention to the treble koto and its play of light across the water. Compare with Debussy's La mer, another picture of water in sound, and Haru no Umi, Miyagi's more famous duet, to hear how he developed his ideas."],"dede-efendi-saba-ayin":["A complete âyin, the music for the Mevlevi sema ceremony, in makam Sabâ. It is laid out in four selâms, sections in which the dervishes turn, each in its own rhythmic cycle, from slow and weighty to quick and light, before the concluding peace. Male voices sing Persian poems by Rūmī, accompanied by ney flutes and kudüm drums. Sabâ, with its unstable, sinking fourth note, gives the whole ceremony an atmosphere of longing and grief.","Hamamizade İsmail Dede Efendi was a Mevlevi dervish of the Yenikapı lodge in Istanbul and the most admired composer at the courts of Selim III and Mahmud II. Mahmud II's reforms, including the destruction of the Janissaries in 1826, reshaped Ottoman life and introduced Western military music under Giuseppe Donizetti. Dede Efendi continued to compose for the Mevlevi rite, and this âyin is among his most loved.","Sabâ's fourth note is lower than expected and the scale seems to sag under its own weight; Ottoman listeners associated it with sorrow and devotion. Listen for the way the melody keeps approaching that note and turning back, as if unwilling to go through. In the fast third selâm the rhythmic cycle shifts markedly; when the drums change pattern, the dervishes' turning is supposed to change too.","Dede Efendi's Sabâ âyin is considered one of the peaks of the Mevlevi repertoire and is performed in Konya and Istanbul. After the lodges were closed in 1925 these works survived through musicians who kept them in notation and recordings. Debate continues over the tempo and style: modern concert performances tend to be faster and more polished than the older lodge practice described in the sources.","Start with the ney solo and the first selâm; the long, slow cycle takes time to feel, but after a few minutes the voices and drums lock into a trance-like pace. Then compare the Ferahfeza âyin by the same composer to hear how a change of makam alters the whole ceremony. A filmed performance with the turning dervishes makes the structure clear."],"dede-efendi-yine-bir-gulnihal":["A yürük semâî, a lively song in a quick six-beat rhythm, in makam Hicaz. The poem is a lover's complaint: once again a slender rose sapling has captured the singer's heart. The melody is light and graceful, moving easily across the Hicaz scale with its wide middle step, and it rolls along with the dancing lilt of the yürük semâî rhythm. It is among the best-known Ottoman art songs and often closes a Hicaz suite.","Dede Efendi wrote in every form of his day, from the Mevlevi ceremony to the courtly suite, and his secular songs were admired at the palace of Mahmud II, himself an amateur musician. The yürük semâî was the fast closing song of the fasıl, the Ottoman suite, and composers used it for their most tuneful ideas. This one has been sung continuously since his time and remains one of the most recognisable songs of the repertoire.","Listen to the rhythm under the voice: the yürük semâî can be heard as two groups of three beats, but the melody often pulls against that, stretching a word across the divide. That gentle cross-rhythm is what gives the song its rocking, dance-like quality. Listen too for the singer's ornaments on the long syllables, which vary from performer to performer.","The song is one of Dede Efendi's most popular works and a standard in Turkish classical and light classical concerts. It is also sung by pop and folk singers, sometimes in simplified form, which traditionalists regret. Historians use it as an example of how an Ottoman composer who wrote grave religious music could also write a song of great melodic charm.","Hear it as a dance: feel the six-beat lilt first, then let the melody sit over it. Compare it with his Sabâ âyin to hear how the same composer moves between ritual and pleasure. For a sense of the wider tradition, follow with a Greek rebetiko song or an Umm Kulthum performance, both of which share the modal world of Hicaz."],"elizabeth-cotten-shake-sugaree":["The second Folkways album of Elizabeth Cotten's music is a quiet domestic record: a woman in her seventies at home, playing guitar and five-string banjo, sometimes singing in a small, unforced voice, and sometimes letting her children's children sing for her. The guitar pieces are rags, dance tunes, hymns and blues, picked with a steady alternating bass under a bright, ringing melody. The title song, a gentle, circling tune, is the one people carry away with them.","Cotten had worked for years as a housekeeper for the Seeger family in Washington, D.C., before the young Mike Seeger realised she was a remarkable guitarist and began recording her at home. Her first album of 1958 made her a figure of the folk revival; by the mid-1960s she was playing festivals and college concerts. This follow-up, taped informally in her own house, captures her repertoire at greater length and in a more relaxed setting than the first.","The lead voice on the title song is not Cotten's but that of her young great-granddaughter, Brenda Evans, and the song itself is Cotten's own, built from a childhood phrase about pawning everything one owns. Listen also for the banjo tracks: she plays the banjo the same way she plays guitar, as a left-hander on an instrument strung for a right-handed player, so the melody sits under her thumb.","Folk-revival listeners welcomed the album as a fuller portrait of a musician they had come to love through a handful of tunes. Over time 'Shake Sugaree' became one of her most covered songs, recorded by folk, rock and jazz musicians alike, and critics have come to see the record less as a curiosity from a 'discovered' elder than as the work of a composer with a distinct melodic voice and a whole private tradition of Piedmont guitar.","Start with the title song and notice how little it needs: a child's voice, a guitar, a tune that goes round and round without hurry. Then play one of the instrumental rags and listen for the bass notes walking steadily while the melody skips over them. Put it beside Mississippi John Hurt's late recordings, which share the same gentleness and the same thumb."],"elizabeth-cotten-live":["A concert album by a guitarist then close to ninety. Cotten plays her best-loved pieces, among them 'Freight Train' and 'Oh Babe, It Ain't No Lie', and between them she talks: about learning to play as a girl on her brother's instrument, about putting music aside for decades, about how songs came to her. The playing is slower and more deliberate than on her early records, but the alternating bass and bell-like melody are unmistakably hers.","By the early 1980s Cotten had become a revered elder of American folk music, touring widely long after most performers retire and receiving a National Heritage Fellowship from the National Endowment for the Arts in 1984. Chris Strachwitz's Arhoolie label, which specialised in regional and vernacular American music, issued this live set, and it won the Grammy for best traditional folk recording in 1985, a rare mainstream honour for a musician of her kind.","The spoken passages are as much a part of the record as the tunes. Her stories explain things the studio albums never could: why she plays the guitar upside down, how church elders once persuaded her that secular music was sinful, and how she came back to the instrument in middle age. Listen, too, for the audience singing along on 'Freight Train', a song written by a girl of about twelve and now older than most of the listeners.","The Grammy made headlines partly because of her age, and some critics treated the award as a tribute to a long life rather than to the performance. Later listeners have been kinder: the record is valued as an oral history as well as a concert, and as proof that a late-life style can be a style in its own right, less nimble but more spacious and deliberate than the playing of her youth.","Listen once straight through, stories included, as though you were in the room. Then compare her concert 'Freight Train' with the 1958 studio take on her first Folkways album and notice what age has changed in the tempo and touch, and what it has not. Seek the full-length reissue so that none of her talk is trimmed away."],"loewe-brigadoon":["A two-act musical fantasy, with book and lyrics by Alan Jay Lerner, about two New Yorkers on a hunting trip in the Scottish Highlands who stumble on a village that wakes for a single day every hundred years. Frederick Loewe's score leans on Scottish colour, with lilting tunes, drones and a real bagpipe, while keeping Broadway's romantic ballads. 'Almost Like Being in Love' and 'The Heather on the Hill' are its best-known songs; the long dance sequences are just as central.","Loewe, born in Berlin in 1901 and raised in the world of operetta, had struggled for years on Broadway before this, his first real success with Lerner. It opened at the Ziegfeld Theatre in March 1947, in the season after Oklahoma! and Carousel had made dance and serious story central to the musical. Agnes de Mille, who had choreographed both of those shows, created its dances, and it ran for well over a year.","The emotional climax is a dance, not a song. When the jealous Harry Beaton is killed during a chase through the woods, the villagers mourn him with a funeral dance to the sound of the bagpipe, and the show goes quiet in a way few musicals of its time dared. Listen also for how the orchestra imitates the drone of pipes elsewhere in the score, so that the real instrument sounds like the music's source when it appears.","Critics praised it warmly in 1947, and de Mille's dances won a Tony Award for choreography in the awards' first year. Some later writers pointed out a resemblance to a nineteenth-century German tale about a village that appears once a century, which Lerner denied knowing. The 1954 MGM film with Gene Kelly flattened much of its magic, and revivals have struggled with its whimsy, but the score remains among the most tuneful of the Golden Age.","Start with 'The Heather on the Hill' for Loewe's gift for a long, unhurried melody, then find a recording that keeps the dance music, above all the chase and the funeral. Compare the Scottish colouring with the Viennese sweep Loewe brought to My Fair Lady nine years later; the original 1947 cast album is short but vivid."],"loewe-camelot":["A musical in two acts on the story of King Arthur, Guinevere and Lancelot, adapted by Alan Jay Lerner from T. H. White's novel The Once and Future King. It begins as comedy, with a nervous young king and a bride who wants romance, and darkens into betrayal, trial and war. Loewe's score mixes courtly pastiche, bright ensemble numbers such as 'The Lusty Month of May', and big romantic ballads, the best known being Lancelot's 'If Ever I Would Leave You'.","Lerner and Loewe came to it straight after the triumphs of My Fair Lady and the film Gigi, and expectations were enormous. The production was troubled: the director Moss Hart suffered a heart attack during the tryouts and Lerner was ill, while the show ran far too long. It opened on Broadway in December 1960 with Richard Burton, Julie Andrews and Robert Goulet, to mixed reviews, and was saved when the cast performed excerpts on television.","Its fame outgrew the stage after the assassination of John F. Kennedy in November 1963, when Jacqueline Kennedy told an interviewer that her husband had loved the closing lines of the title song, and the word 'Camelot' became shorthand for his presidency. Listen, too, to Arthur's closing scene, where he knights a young boy and asks him to carry the story on: the whole show is quietly about how ideals survive their failure.","First-night critics found it overlong and unsure whether it was a comedy or a tragedy, and those complaints have followed it through every revival. Yet the original cast album sold hugely, and the score is generally judged among Loewe's richest. It was, in effect, his farewell to Broadway: he largely retired after it, returning only for a few later projects, so it is often read as the end of the partnership's great run.","Begin with the original cast album and Richard Burton's half-spoken, half-sung 'How to Handle a Woman', a lesson in acting through a song. Then hear Goulet's 'If Ever I Would Leave You' for Loewe at his most operatic. Compare the 1967 film with Richard Harris, which is lusher and slower, and notice how the story's darkening changes the music's colour."],"loesser-the-most-happy-fella":["Frank Loesser wrote the music, lyrics and book for this three-act show about Tony, an ageing Italian winegrower in California's Napa Valley, who woos a San Francisco waitress by letter and, ashamed of his looks, sends her a photograph of his handsome foreman. Most of the story is sung: arias, duets and ensembles flow into one another, with some forty numbers in all. Broadway hits such as 'Standing on the Corner' and 'Big D' sit beside long, near-operatic scenes.","Loesser followed the huge success of Guys and Dolls by adapting Sidney Howard's Pulitzer-winning play They Knew What They Wanted, a very different kind of story, and spent years on it. It opened on Broadway in May 1956 with the opera baritone Robert Weede as Tony and Jo Sullivan as the waitress, whom Loesser later married. Columbia recorded almost the whole score on three LPs, an unprecedented release for a Broadway cast.","Loesser refused to call it an opera, describing it as a musical with a great deal of music, yet the score is built like one, with recurring themes that bind the characters together. Listen to how Tony's music draws on Italian song, while the young people around him sing in American swing and Broadway idioms: the clash of musical languages is the clash of generations and cultures that drives the plot.","It ran successfully but was overshadowed that spring by My Fair Lady, and critics disagreed about whether its mix of opera and musical comedy held together. Its standing has grown steadily: opera companies, including New York City Opera, have staged it, and a 1992 Broadway revival used a scaled-down two-piano version. It is now often cited as a bridge between the Golden Age musical and the through-sung shows of later decades.","Start with 'Joey, Joey, Joey', the foreman's restless song about the wandering life, and then the first-act finale for Loesser writing on an operatic scale. The complete original-cast recording is the one to hear, because the connective music between numbers is where much of the drama lies. Listen next to Puccini's La bohème for the tradition Tony's music comes from."],"loesser-how-to-succeed-in-business":["A brisk satirical musical in two acts about J. Pierrepont Finch, a window washer who climbs to the top of the World Wide Wicket Company by following a self-help book, flattering his superiors and outwitting the boss's nephew. Loesser's songs are sharp and economical: 'The Company Way' mocks corporate conformity, 'Coffee Break' turns a missing coffee supply into office panic, and 'Brotherhood of Man' ends the show as a mock revival meeting. Its best-known song, 'I Believe in You', is sung by Finch to himself.","The book, by Abe Burrows with Jack Weinstock and Willie Gilbert, was based on Shepherd Mead's 1952 parody of success manuals. It opened in October 1961, at the height of the organisation-man era of grey flannel suits and new corporate towers, with Robert Morse as Finch and the former crooner Rudy Vallée as the company president. It ran for over three years and won both the Tony for best musical and the Pulitzer Prize for Drama.","'I Believe in You' is staged as a love song, but Finch sings it into a washroom mirror while other executives shave around him, and the accompaniment includes a buzzing that imitates their electric razors. Loesser takes a sincere ballad form and aims it at pure vanity. Listen, too, to 'Brotherhood of Man', where a secretary's gospel-style solo briefly turns office satire into church music.","The reviews were ecstatic, and it became only the fourth musical to win the Pulitzer Prize for Drama. Some later critics found its satire gentle, even affectionate towards the corporate world it mocks, and its treatment of secretaries dated. Major Broadway revivals in 1995, with Matthew Broderick, and 2011, with Daniel Radcliffe, confirmed that the score's wit survives, though directors now tend to sharpen its view of office sexism.","Begin with 'I Believe in You' and 'The Company Way' on the original cast album, and listen for how much comedy Loesser gets from rhythm and rhyme. Then watch the 1967 film, which preserves Morse's performance. Compare it with Guys and Dolls to hear the same composer trading New York gamblers for the executive suite."],"big-joe-turner-roll-em-pete":["Just two men: Big Joe Turner shouting the blues and Pete Johnson playing boogie-woogie piano. Johnson's left hand pounds a rolling eight-to-the-bar bass figure while his right hand throws out trills, repeated chords and riffs; Turner rides over the top with huge, relaxed power, stringing together verses about a woman he admires. The tempo is fast and never lets up, and the whole thing lasts about three minutes. It sounds like a party already in progress.","Turner and Johnson had worked together for years in the clubs of Kansas City, where Turner tended bar and sang, often calling out verses from behind the counter. The producer John Hammond brought them to New York for his From Spirituals to Swing concert at Carnegie Hall in December 1938, which set off a national boogie-woogie craze. They recorded this number for Vocalion days after the concert, capturing the Kansas City sound at its height.","Turner's verses are mostly floating lines shared across the blues tradition rather than a fixed lyric; he assembles the song on the spot, as he would have in a club. Notice how he sings slightly behind Johnson's beat, so the voice seems to lean back while the piano drives forward. That tension between a propulsive rhythm section and an unhurried shouter is exactly what rhythm and blues and early rock and roll would inherit.","It was a modest record at the time, overshadowed by the boogie-woogie piano trios that followed the Carnegie Hall concert. Historians now routinely name it among the candidates for the first rock and roll record, alongside later jump-blues sides. The argument is partly a game, but the claim is serious: the beat, the shouting vocal and the dance-floor purpose are all here sixteen years before Turner's own 'Shake, Rattle and Roll'.","Listen first only to Johnson's left hand and feel how it never stops, then follow Turner's voice as it sits on top. Next play Turner's 1954 'Shake, Rattle and Roll' and Bill Haley's 'Rock Around the Clock' to hear what this grew into. His 1956 remake on The Boss of the Blues makes a good comparison with a full band."],"big-joe-turner-the-boss-of-the-blues":["Subtitled Joe Turner Sings Kansas City Jazz, this album returns the singer to the swinging small-band sound of his youth. A compact group of horns plays riff-based arrangements behind him, with his old partner Pete Johnson at the piano. The programme mixes blues he had long sung, such as 'Cherry Red', 'Wee Baby Blues' and a new 'Roll 'Em Pete', with standards including 'St. Louis Blues' and 'You're Driving Me Crazy'. The voice is enormous, warm and utterly unhurried.","In 1956 Turner was a rhythm-and-blues star, selling records to teenagers on the strength of 'Shake, Rattle and Roll', while white cover versions took his songs further up the charts. Atlantic, the same label that issued his rock and roll hits, recorded this LP in New York for the growing adult jazz album market, with Nesuhi Ertegun producing. It placed him back among jazz musicians just as the singles market was turning towards younger performers.","Listen to how the horns answer Turner phrase by phrase, exactly as Kansas City bands answered blues shouters in the 1930s: the arrangements are simple riffs, meant to push rather than decorate. The remake of 'Roll 'Em Pete' is worth setting beside the 1938 original, since the same partnership, nearly twenty years on, trades raw speed for a looser, heavier swing. 'Piney Brown Blues' remembers a real Kansas City nightlife figure from their early years.","Jazz critics received it warmly as proof that Turner was a great jazz singer as well as a hit-maker, and it has often been named his finest album. Some rock historians barely mention it, since it complicates the neat story of the blues shouter who became a rock pioneer. Today it is usually heard as the best single document of Kansas City blues singing in high-fidelity sound.","Start with 'Cherry Red' for the voice at its richest, then 'Roll 'Em Pete' for the old partnership. Listen for the moment each horn riff enters behind him and how it lifts the beat. Compare Jimmy Rushing's records with Count Basie to hear the other great Kansas City blues voice, and choose a remastered edition for the full weight of the band."],"gil-evans-the-individualism-of-gil-evans":["Gil Evans's own orchestra in its most personal album of the 1960s. The pieces unfold slowly over long spans, built on vamps, drones and shifting clouds of sound from flutes, French horns, tuba and muted brass, with soloists drifting in and out. 'The Barbara Song', from Kurt Weill's Threepenny Opera, becomes a slow, brooding meditation; 'Las Vegas Tango' is a dark, heavy-footed dance. The record sounds less like a big band than a small orchestra dreaming aloud.","By the early 1960s Evans was famous for his arrangements for Miles Davis, but his own band worked rarely and he recorded slowly, notoriously reluctant to call a piece finished. Creed Taylor, then building Verve's catalogue, produced these sessions across 1963 and 1964 in New York. Jazz was moving towards modal and free forms, and Evans used that openness to write long pieces that sit on one harmony and let colour carry the change.","Listen to how little happens harmonically and how much happens in timbre: a chord may hold for a long time while its instrumentation changes underneath it, the way a painter changes a single colour's texture. Weill's song, originally sharp and ironic, is slowed until its melody barely surfaces. This habit of treating an existing tune as raw material for colour is the same method Evans used for Rodrigo and Falla on Sketches of Spain.","It sold modestly and received less attention than his collaborations with Davis, but arrangers and composers treasured it, and it is often cited as his most personal statement. Some listeners find its slow, open forms unfocused; admirers answer that the drift is the point. Later reissues added previously unissued tracks, which has sharpened the view of this period as his creative peak as a leader.","Begin with 'The Barbara Song' and let it take its time; listen for the French horns and flutes that colour the low chords. Then play the Weill original from The Threepenny Opera to hear what Evans kept and what he dissolved. Out of the Cool and Sketches of Spain are the natural neighbours; choose an expanded CD edition."],"gil-evans-plays-the-music-of-jimi-hendrix":["A large jazz orchestra, amplified and electrified, plays songs by Jimi Hendrix. Evans's band of the 1970s blends tubas, French horns and saxophones with electric guitars, electric bass and early synthesizers, so that Hendrix's riffs are passed around a wall of brass and the ballads, such as 'Angel' and 'Little Wing', are voiced as slow, glowing chorales. Some tracks are tight and funky, others open out into long collective improvisation. Hendrix's sound becomes orchestral colour.","Evans admired Hendrix as a sound-painter, and the two had arranged to meet and discuss a possible recording in 1970; Hendrix died in September that year before it could happen. Four years later Evans recorded this tribute in New York for RCA with his working band, sharing out the arranging among several of its members. It came at a time when Miles Davis and others were bringing rock rhythm and electric instruments into jazz, and Evans took the same path.","Much of the album's weirdness comes from the low end: the tubas and synthesizers double or replace Hendrix's guitar feedback with sustained, buzzing tones, an orchestral equivalent of distortion. Listen for how the melodies of the ballads are handed to horns rather than to the guitar, which in Hendrix's originals carried both tune and texture. It is the same colourist thinking that shaped Sketches of Spain, now applied to rock.","Reviews were mixed: some jazz critics found it loose and uneven, and some rock listeners missed Hendrix's guitar. Over time it has come to be seen as a pioneering cross-over, one of the first serious treatments of rock songs as material for a jazz orchestra, and a model for the many jazz tributes to rock musicians since. Evans continued playing Hendrix's music with his bands for the rest of his life.","Play Hendrix's own 'Little Wing' or 'Angel' first, then Evans's version, and notice what has happened to the guitar's role. Listen next for the tubas in the up-tempo pieces, which give the band a floor-shaking weight. Pair it with Miles Davis's On the Corner, and with Jaco Pastorius's big-band work, which learned from Evans."],"cl-franklin-dry-bones-in-the-valley":["A recorded sermon on Ezekiel's vision of a valley of dry bones that come together, grow flesh and breathe again at God's command. Rev. C. L. Franklin opens in a clear, measured speaking voice, reading the text and explaining it almost like a lecture. Then, as the congregation answers him, his delivery gathers rhythm and pitch until the final third becomes a chanted, sung climax, driven by the organ and the church's cries of response. The shape is a long, steady ascent.","Franklin preached at New Bethel Baptist Church in Detroit, where the record-shop owner Joe Von Battle taped his Sunday sermons and issued them on his JVB label, with Chess of Chicago later distributing them nationally. Franklin's weekly radio broadcasts and records made him one of the most famous Black preachers in America. For listeners who had come north in the Great Migration, a sermon about scattered bones rising again spoke to their own experience.","Franklin treats the vision as a message about the present, not just prophecy: the dry bones are a people who have been told they are dead, and the sermon insists they can be made whole. Listen for how he returns again and again to the question God asks the prophet, using it as a refrain that grows more musical each time. The spiritual 'Dem Bones', which many in his congregation knew, lurks behind the imagery.","It is among the most admired of Franklin's recorded sermons. Folklorists and historians, notably Jeff Todd Titon, who transcribed and published a selection of them, have treated these records as literature in performance, and Nick Salvatore's biography of Franklin placed them at the heart of Detroit's Black public life. Some listeners find the early expository stretch slow, but that patience is the preparation for the climax.","Resist skipping ahead: the chanted ending lands only if you have heard the calm reading that starts it. Listen for the first shouts from the congregation and track how they grow more frequent. Then hear The Eagle Stirreth Her Nest for comparison, and Aretha Franklin's Amazing Grace to hear his cadences passed down to his daughter."],"cl-franklin-the-prodigal-son":["Franklin's sermon on the parable from Luke's Gospel of the younger son who takes his inheritance, wastes it in a far country, and returns home to a father who runs out to meet him. He tells the story as a drama, voicing the son's pride, his hunger among the pigs and the father's joy, with humour and vivid everyday detail. As the story reaches the homecoming, his speech rises into the rhythmic, sung chant of the whoop, and organ and congregation surge with him.","Like his other sermons, this was recorded live at New Bethel in Detroit and sold nationally on records, reaching listeners who could never visit the church. Franklin's audience included many who had left Southern homes for Northern cities, and the parable of a child who goes far away and is welcomed back had a particular weight for families divided by migration. He was also building a reputation as a preacher who spoke plainly about city life and its temptations.","Listen to how Franklin keeps the father offstage for most of the sermon and then makes the father's running to meet his son the emotional turning point, where speech tips into song. The drama is carefully paced like a piece of music, with quiet storytelling as verses and the chanted climax as a final chorus. Knowing that Franklin's own family life was troubled, some listeners hear a personal note in his tenderness towards the returning child.","Collectors and scholars count it among his most characteristic records, showing his narrative gift as clearly as his vocal power. His ministry also drew criticism in his lifetime, for his wealth, his fame and his private life, and those debates have coloured how his sermons are received. Recent scholarship tends to separate the man from the art, treating the recordings as central documents of African American oral performance.","Follow it as you would a radio play: the characters, the far country, the long road home. Notice where Franklin's voice first begins to sing rather than speak, and how the organ joins him. Then listen to Sam Cooke's Night Beat or Aretha Franklin's gospel records to hear how the same rise from calm to fervour passed into soul singing."],"violeta-parra-la-carta":["A short, driving song for voice and a hard-strummed guitar, built on the verse forms of Chilean country song but sung with a bite that has nothing rustic about it. The singer tells how a letter reached her abroad with bad news from home: her brother has been thrown in jail for standing with striking workers. Verse by verse the anger widens from one family to a whole country, and the refrain lands each time like a fist on a table.","Parra was living in Europe in the early 1960s, singing in Paris cafés and showing her paintings and tapestries, when news came that a brother had been arrested during labour unrest in Chile. She answered with this song, probably in 1962. It was one of the first times she turned the folk forms she had spent a decade collecting into direct political comment, a step that the younger Chilean musicians of the Nueva canción would soon make the heart of their movement.","Listen to how traditional the machinery is: the guitar pattern and the verse shape come from the rural songs Parra had recorded from old singers in the Chilean countryside. The protest works because it wears peasant clothes. Notice too how little she decorates the melody; she almost speaks some lines, and the plain voice makes the accusation feel like testimony rather than performance.","The song circulated widely in Chile and then across Latin America, and after the 1973 coup it became one of many Parra songs that could get a singer into trouble. Some critics have preferred her gentler songs and her collecting work, seeing the political pieces as occasional; others hear in them the real hinge of her career, the point where the archivist became the voice of the movement that Víctor Jara and Inti-Illimani would carry on.","Hear it first straight after Gracias a la vida to feel the range of one songwriter: thanks and fury from the same voice and guitar. Then play Víctor Jara's Te recuerdo Amanda to hear what the next generation did with her example. Seek Parra's own recording rather than the many later covers; the roughness of her voice is part of the message."],"violeta-parra-casamiento-de-negros":["A quick, bouncing folk song with a guitar that strums and slaps like a small dance band. The text is a comic tale in the old Chilean popular manner: a wedding at which everything, guests, clothes and family, is black, which then tips abruptly into a funeral. Parra sings it with a bright, nasal, almost shouted country voice, clipping the words and leaning into the jokes, and it is over almost before it has begun.","This was one of Parra's first solo recordings for Odeon in Santiago, in the early 1950s, when she had just begun travelling the Chilean countryside to note down songs from elderly rural singers. Before that she had sung in bars and circuses with her sister in a duo playing Spanish and Mexican popular music. The record was a real hit, and its success let her move from the entertainment circuit toward the collecting and composing that made her name.","The text is not her own invention: she adapted verses from the rural popular tradition, and the word black is doing double work, pointing at mourning dress as much as at the couple, so the wedding carries its own funeral from the start. Listen to the guitar's percussive strokes on the body of the instrument, a country technique she would keep through her whole career.","In its day it was simply a lively novelty that made Parra famous. Later listeners have been more uneasy, since the humour turns on race in ways a modern audience notices, and some Parra anthologies now pass over it. Scholars tend to treat it as a document of where she started: a popular entertainer drawing on the countryside before she began to write the songs that made her a national figure.","Listen for the guitar before the words: the scrape, slap and rush of it is the sound of Chilean country dance music that Parra would spend her life documenting. Then jump thirteen years to Las últimas composiciones to hear how the same voice, older and slower, carried much heavier material. A compilation of her 1950s Odeon sides gives the best context."],"elmore-james-shake-your-moneymaker":["Two and a half minutes of pounding dance blues. Elmore James's electric slide guitar opens with a hard, fuzzy triplet figure, the band locks into a stomping shuffle, and James shouts a party chant that is really just one instruction repeated with rising urgency. There is barely a verse structure: the song is a riff, a groove and a voice that sounds as if it is straining against the edge of the microphone, with the guitar screaming back between lines.","James recorded it for Bobby Robinson's Fire label in New York in 1961, late in a career that had begun with his 1951 recording of Dust My Broom. By then he was moving between Chicago and the East Coast, cutting sides for small independent labels aimed at Black dance audiences. Two years later he was dead of a heart attack at forty-five, just before a new generation of white listeners in Britain turned his records into textbooks.","Notice how little of the song is a song: the lyric is essentially a chant, and the real content is the interlocking of guitar riff and drums. James's sound came partly from his amplifiers, which he is said to have modified himself, pushing them into a distortion that few studios would have allowed. That grainy tone, not only the slide figure, is what the rock guitarists of the late sixties were chasing.","It was a minor record on release and became a standard only afterwards, when Fleetwood Mac, under Peter Green and Jeremy Spencer, made it a centrepiece of their 1968 debut, and countless bar bands followed. Blues purists sometimes find it thin next to James's slow numbers; others argue that its crude, relentless energy is exactly what made him the bridge between Delta slide and rock guitar.","Turn it up and listen to the first five seconds: that slide figure is the whole Elmore James style. Then compare it with Dust My Broom, recorded a decade earlier, to hear how the same idea got louder and rougher. Follow it with Fleetwood Mac's early version and with Hendrix to hear where the sound travelled."],"pran-nath-midnight-raga-malkauns":["A single raga sung across more than an hour: Malkauns, the five-note midnight raga without a second or a fifth degree. Pran Nath begins with a long unmetred exploration over the tanpura drones, touching one note at a time and holding it until it settles, then moves into slow and faster compositions with tabla. The voice is thin and old, but every pitch is placed with such care that the whole performance feels like one long act of listening.","The recording comes from the years after 1970, when Pran Nath had moved to New York at the invitation of La Monte Young and Marian Zazeela and was teaching a small circle of American musicians, among them Terry Riley. Much of what he sang in those years was recorded privately by his students and only issued long after his death in 1996; this performance appeared on CD in 2006, through the circle around Young and Zazeela.","The drone is part of the composition. Pran Nath's students tuned the tanpuras with an exactness unusual even in India, and his placement of each note against them is the real subject of the performance. Listen for the moment a held note stops wavering and locks against the drone; that small settling is what he taught Young and Riley to hear, and it runs directly into their tuned-piano and drone works.","Indian listeners who knew him valued him as a keeper of the Kirana style's slow, meditative approach rather than as a concert star, and his recorded legacy is small and scattered. In the West these posthumous releases have been received mainly through the minimalist composers he taught, which some find distorting; the best of them, this among them, let him be heard on his own terms as a great singer of a demanding raga.","Do not start in the middle. Give the opening unmetred section, the alap, at least fifteen minutes, ideally late at night and in quiet, and let the drone become the room. Then hear Amir Khan's slow Darbari Kanada for comparison, and afterwards La Monte Young's Well-Tuned Piano to hear what an American composer took from this voice."],"nat-king-cole-the-christmas-song":["Three minutes of winter warmth. Over soft strings and a gentle rhythm section, Nat King Cole sings a slow, winding melody that lists the small sensory pleasures of the season, food, cold air, carols and children waiting for presents, and ends with a plain greeting to every listener. The tune moves through unexpected harmonies for a holiday song, and Cole's unhurried baritone, each consonant placed exactly, makes it sound less like a party than a fireside conversation.","Mel Tormé and Bob Wells wrote the song in 1945, reportedly on a hot California summer day while trying to think cool thoughts. Cole's trio recorded it for Capitol in 1946, first without strings and then, at his insistence, with a small string section, an unusual expense for the label. He re-recorded it in 1953 and again in stereo in 1961, and it is that later version that most radio stations now play every December.","Listen to the harmony under the opening line: it slips through chords that belong more to a jazz ballad than to a carol, a sign that the writers were songwriters of the Broadway and nightclub world. Cole's piano, the thing that made him famous before his voice did, is almost invisible on the later versions; in the 1946 record you can still hear the trio texture underneath.","It was a hit at once and has never gone away; it is among the most performed holiday songs in America. Critics sometimes treat it as the moment Cole's jazz trio career gave way to a softer pop career, a trade that jazz writers long lamented. Others hear in it exactly the qualities of his jazz playing, clarity, restraint and timing, simply put to a different use.","Play the 1946 and 1961 recordings back to back: the song is the same, but the first sounds like a small club and the second like a lavish living room. Listen for how Cole holds the last greeting without any extra emphasis. Then hear Irving Berlin's White Christmas and Phil Spector's Christmas album for two other American answers to the season."],"nat-king-cole-after-midnight":["Nat King Cole back at the piano with a small group, singing and playing standards the way he had in the trio days. Guitar, bass and drums swing lightly behind him, and on different tracks a guest soloist joins in: Harry Edison's muted trumpet, Willie Smith's alto saxophone, Juan Tizol's valve trombone, Stuff Smith's jazz violin. Songs like Sweet Lorraine, Route 66 and It's Only a Paper Moon get relaxed, conversational treatments, with Cole's piano as bright as his voice.","By the mid-1950s Cole was one of the biggest pop singers in America, recording with large string orchestras and, in 1956, hosting his own national television show, a first for a Black American entertainer. These sessions for Capitol in 1956 were a deliberate return to his jazz roots, a chance to play with the kind of musicians he had started among in the Los Angeles clubs of the late 1930s and early 1940s.","Listen to the piano. Cole was one of the great jazz pianists of the swing era, a light, crisp player with a right hand that influenced Oscar Peterson and many others, and on this album his solos are as long and considered as his vocals. Notice how he voices chords sparsely behind the guest soloists, leaving space, and how the singing and playing share the same easy timing.","Jazz critics who had written Cole off as a pop crooner welcomed the album as proof of what he could still do, and it has remained the record most often recommended to people who know only his ballads. Its reissues added extra tracks from the sessions. Some find it too comfortable; most hear the ease as the point, a master working among peers without pressure.","Start with Sweet Lorraine, which he had first recorded with the trio years earlier, and compare the two versions. Then follow a guest through a track: Stuff Smith's violin brings a rougher, bluesier edge. For contrast hear Nature Boy to measure the gap between the pop and jazz Coles, then Lester Young with the Oscar Peterson Trio for kindred small-group ease."],"jean-ritchie-at-folk-city":["A live evening in a Greenwich Village club with two Appalachian musicians taking turns and joining forces. Jean Ritchie's clear, straight voice and plucked dulcimer carry old ballads and hymns from Kentucky; Doc Watson's warm baritone and nimble flat-picked guitar carry North Carolina songs and fiddle tunes. When they sing together, unaccompanied or over guitar, the two mountain traditions meet, and the easy talk between numbers makes the record feel like a family gathering.","Gerde's Folk City in New York was the centre of the early-1960s folk revival, the club where Bob Dylan had played his first important sets. Ritchie had been a fixture of the New York folk world since the early 1950s; Watson was a recent discovery, brought north after 1960 by Ralph Rinzler. Moses Asch's Folkways label released this concert, capturing the revival's interest in real tradition-bearers rather than city interpreters.","Listen to the harmonies: neither singer uses the smooth close harmony of commercial country music. They sing with the hard, lonesome, slightly unaligned sound of church and family singing in the mountains, where each voice keeps its own line. Notice too how the audience laughs and settles; the recording documents a city crowd learning how to listen to this music.","It was a modest release, but it has come to be valued as one of the best documents of the folk revival at its most serious, when a club audience came to hear source singers. Reviewers often single out the duets. It has been reissued on CD through Smithsonian Folkways, and it is one of the few places to hear both musicians in one room.","Pick the unaccompanied duets first and listen to the two voices rub together without an instrument to smooth them. Then compare Ritchie's solo Elektra album of 1952 and Watson's first Vanguard album to hear each apart. Clarence Ashley's recordings, where Watson was first heard, make a natural next step."],"jean-ritchie-the-l-and-n-dont-stop-here-anymore":["A new song in an old mould. Over a simple accompaniment Jean Ritchie sings a slow tune that sounds as though it could be a century old, in the voice of a Kentucky miner's family watching their coal town die. The mine has closed, the railway no longer halts, and the singer counts what has been lost: work, neighbours, health, a future for the children. There is no chorus to shout, only verses that build a quiet, bitter picture.","Ritchie grew up in Perry County, Kentucky, in the heart of the eastern coal field, and by the 1960s the region she came from was being hollowed out by mechanisation, strip mining and closures. She began writing her own songs about it, alongside her work as a collector and performer of traditional ballads. The Louisville and Nashville Railroad, the L&N of the title, had carried coal out of those mountains for generations.","The craft lies in its disguise: Ritchie borrowed the modal feel, plain phrasing and verse shape of the family ballads she had sung all her life, so a protest song about modern industry sounds like inherited tradition. Listen for the way the train, once a sign of connection to the wider world, becomes a symbol of abandonment. The melody never rises to a climax, which is its own kind of statement.","The song spread through the folk and country worlds and was taken up by other singers, including Kathy Mattea on her album of coal songs, Coal, in 2008. It is often placed beside Merle Travis's Sixteen Tons and Hazel Dickens's mining songs as part of a small canon of Appalachian protest. Some admirers of Ritchie the tradition-bearer paid less attention to her songwriting; that view has faded.","Listen to it straight after one of the old ballads on her 1952 Elektra album and see how hard it is to tell the new song from the old. Then hear Loretta Lynn's Coal Miner's Daughter for a very different, prouder view of the same world, and Woody Guthrie's Dust Bowl Ballads for another songwriter using folk forms to report a disaster."],"lord-kitchener-sugar-bum-bum":["A big, bright carnival record: a brass section punching out short, catchy phrases, electric bass and drums driving a fast, bouncing groove, and Lord Kitchener's voice, now older and rounder, riding on top with a playful flirtatious story about admiring a woman's dancing. The chorus is short enough for a whole street to shout back, and the arrangement is built for movement, repeating and building rather than developing like an old narrative calypso.","Kitchener had returned to Trinidad in 1962 after fourteen years in Britain and became the dominant calypsonian of Carnival, winning the Road March, the title for the song most played by bands on the streets, many times. By the late 1970s Trinidadian music was shifting toward soca, a faster, dance-driven style influenced by soul and funk. Sugar Bum Bum showed that the veteran could master the new sound, and it was the Carnival hit of 1978.","Compare the rhythm section with his 1951 London recordings: the old loping two-step has become a propulsive, electric dance beat, and the band plays the role that the singer's wit once played. Listen to the horn lines, which are designed to be easy for steelbands to arrange; Kitchener always wrote with the pan players in mind, and his hits were quickly taken up across the Carnival.","It was a huge success in Trinidad and across the Caribbean diaspora, and remains one of his best-known songs. Some calypso traditionalists saw the soca turn as a loss of the lyrical and political bite that defined the form; others credit Kitchener, along with Lord Shorty, with carrying calypso into a new era rather than letting it harden into nostalgia.","Hear it as dance music first: the groove is the point. Then play London Is the Place for Me to hear how far one artist travelled in twenty-seven years, and Lord Shorty's Endless Vibrations to hear the soca experiment that set the scene. Mighty Sparrow's records make the natural comparison with Kitchener's great rival."],"lord-kitchener-pan-in-a-minor":["A calypso about the steel pan, written to be played by steel pans. Kitchener sings with obvious delight about the instrument and the players who hammer music out of oil drums, and about a tune in a minor key that the steelbands take up, while the band builds a driving groove with a strong, memorable melody. The key named in the title is itself a kind of salute, since pan players think in keys and chords as orchestral musicians do, and the melodic line is built to be arranged.","Since 1963 Trinidad's Carnival had included Panorama, a competition in which large steel orchestras, sometimes a hundred players strong, perform elaborate arrangements of a current calypso. Kitchener wrote more songs chosen for Panorama than anyone, and in the 1970s and 1980s he wrote a string of calypsos about pan itself. Pan in A Minor belongs to that series, a tribute from Trinidad's best-loved songwriter to its national instrument.","The song lives two lives. On record it is a sung calypso; at Panorama it becomes raw material for an arranger, who stretches it into a ten-minute orchestral variation form with key changes, breakdowns and a returning theme, played entirely on steel drums. Listen to how the melody is written with clear, repeatable phrases and a strong minor-mode colour that pan arrangers could transform.","Kitchener's pan songs are treated in Trinidad as a body of work in their own right, and many pan players regard him as the instrument's great composer even though he did not play it. Outside the Caribbean they are less known than his London calypsos. Scholars of the steelband see in them a rare case of a songwriter shaping a whole instrumental tradition through the tunes he gave it.","Listen to Kitchener's own recording first, then find a Panorama steelband performance of one of his pan tunes: the leap from a six-minute song to a massed orchestra of drums is the whole experience. Notice the minor-key colour. Compare the scale of a Panorama band with a symphony orchestra; the question of what an orchestra is gets interesting."],"albert-king-ill-play-the-blues-for-you":["Albert King's guitar, thick-toned and bent far out of pitch, sings over a warm Memphis soul band with horns and organ. The title track opens the album at a slow, patient tempo and runs several minutes, with King talking to the listener as much as singing, offering the blues as comfort. Other tracks move into funky, mid-tempo soul-blues, and the whole record balances his stinging, economical lead lines against grooves built for Black radio.","King had been a Stax artist since 1966, when Born Under a Bad Sign fused his blues with the label's house band. By 1972 Stax was at its commercial peak after Isaac Hayes's success, and King was recorded with younger Memphis musicians including the Bar-Kays and the Memphis Horns. The album was made for the Black audience that still bought blues and soul singles, even as white rock fans were discovering him at the Fillmore.","King played left-handed on a guitar strung for a right-hander, upside down, and used unusual tunings, so his bends pull the strings downward rather than pushing them up. That is why he could bend a note so far, often a step and a half, with such a vocal, crying sound. Listen for how few notes he plays in each fill; the space between them is as expressive as the notes.","It was one of his most successful albums and the title track became a standard of the modern blues club repertoire. Critics sometimes place it just below Born Under a Bad Sign, which had the stronger songs, but many consider it the best showcase of his mature voice and playing. Stevie Ray Vaughan and many other guitarists took his phrasing directly from records like this.","Start with the title track and pay attention to a single bent note held and released; that is the Albert King sound. Then compare him with BB King's The Thrill Is Gone to hear two contrasting ideas of modern blues guitar. Otis Redding's Otis Blue gives the Stax background, and Freddie King's Hide Away completes the three Kings."],"dexter-gordon-our-man-in-paris":["A quartet session of bebop standards with a warm, generous tenor at its centre. Gordon plays Scrapple from the Apple, Willow Weep for Me, Broadway, Stairway to the Stars and A Night in Tunisia with Bud Powell on piano, Pierre Michelot on bass and Kenny Clarke on drums. The tempos are relaxed, the solos long and conversational, and Gordon's huge, slightly behind-the-beat sound fills the room while Powell comps with sparse, splashy chords.","Gordon had moved to Europe in 1962, as had several of his bebop contemporaries. Powell had lived in Paris since 1959, and Clarke, one of the founders of bebop drumming, had settled there in 1956. Blue Note recorded the session in Paris in 1963, so the album joined three of the music's original architects, two of them expatriates, in a city that offered them respect and steady work that America often did not.","Listen to Powell. He was in fragile health, and his playing here is simpler than on his famous 1940s and early 1950s records, but the few chords he places behind Gordon are exactly timed. On A Night in Tunisia, listen to Clarke: the tune's Latin-tinged rhythm was part of the language he helped build twenty years before, and he plays it like a founder rather than a follower.","It was well reviewed and has become one of Gordon's most admired albums, second in fame perhaps only to Go. Some listeners hear a slightly tired Powell; others argue that his restraint suits Gordon's spacious style. It is frequently held up as a model of the old bebop repertoire played with the maturity of musicians who had long since stopped needing to prove anything.","Begin with Willow Weep for Me and hear how slowly Gordon lets a ballad unfold, then jump to Scrapple from the Apple for his swing at speed. Compare Powell here with his own Amazing Bud Powell albums from the early 1950s. Then play Go, recorded the year before in New York, to hear Gordon with a younger rhythm section."],"dexter-gordon-homecoming":["A double album recorded in a small New York basement club, with Dexter Gordon leading a quintet featuring the trumpeter Woody Shaw. The tunes are long, several stretching well past ten minutes, and the band plays hard bop with a fierce modern edge: Shaw's angular trumpet lines push against Gordon's big, relaxed tenor. Between numbers Gordon speaks to the audience in his deep, amused voice, and the sense of a jubilant reunion is audible throughout.","Gordon had lived in Copenhagen since the early 1960s and returned to New York in December 1976 for a run at the Village Vanguard. The crowds were enormous, the press called it a triumph, and Columbia, under Bruce Lundvall, signed him. In a decade when jazz audiences were drifting toward fusion, the homecoming showed a public still hungry for acoustic bebop-based playing, and it helped spark a broader revival of that style.","Listen to how the two horn players differ: Shaw was one of the most harmonically adventurous trumpeters of the 1970s, drawing on McCoy Tyner and modal jazz, while Gordon remained a bebop storyteller. The contrast is the album's engine. Gordon also still quotes songs in his solos; a stray phrase from a pop tune dropped into a long improvisation is his trademark wink to the listener.","It was received as an event, and it revived Gordon's career in America, leading to further Columbia albums and eventually his starring role in the film 'Round Midnight in 1986, for which he was nominated for an Oscar. Some critics find the long tracks sprawling; many regard it as the best live record of a veteran's return in jazz, a companion to Rollins's comeback years.","Choose one long track and follow it from start to finish, listening to how the solos build over many choruses. Then compare Gordon's sound here with Go and Our Man in Paris: the tone has grown even larger and slower. Monk's Round Midnight, the tune that gave his film its title, makes a good companion."],"doc-watson-doc-watson-and-son":["Doc Watson plays and sings with his teenage son Merle on second guitar. The record mixes old-time songs, blues, ballads and fiddle tunes in a relaxed, homely style: Doc's warm baritone and fast, clean flatpicking over Merle's steady rhythm and occasional leads. The sound is two acoustic guitars working as one instrument, and it has the unhurried feel of music made in a family living room.","After his first Vanguard solo album in 1964, Watson became a star of the folk revival. He was blind and needed a travelling companion, and his son Merle, named after the country guitarist Merle Travis, learned guitar quickly to join him on the road. This album, released by Vanguard in 1965, was Merle's recording debut and the start of a musical partnership that lasted until Merle's death in a tractor accident in 1985.","Listen for how Merle supports his father: rather than doubling the melody, he plays a firm bass line with his thumb and chords with his fingers, freeing Doc to take quick, fiddle-like runs. That division of labour, learned at home, became a model for bluegrass and folk guitar duos. Notice also how many tunes come from old country and blues records rather than oral tradition alone.","It was well received as a gentle, charming record and a showcase for a promising young guitarist. Critics have always placed it slightly below Watson's solo debut in historical importance, but it laid the groundwork for decades of Doc and Merle collaborations, including their later work with the Nitty Gritty Dirt Band. Many flatpickers name it as an early source.","Put it on after Watson's 1964 solo album and listen for what a second guitar adds: fuller rhythm, more room for Doc to run. Try following Merle's bass line alone on one track. Then go back to Clarence Ashley's recordings to hear where Doc was first heard, and to Flatt and Scruggs for the bluegrass counterpart."],"doc-watson-southbound":["Doc Watson, with Merle again at his side, moves beyond pure tradition into a broader mix of country, blues, folk and newer songs. The flatpicking is even more assured, the singing warm and conversational, and the two guitars mesh more tightly than on their first record together. The instrumental Windy and Warm, with its gently syncopated, rolling guitar pattern, became one of his signature pieces and a staple for fingerstyle guitarists everywhere.","By 1966 the urban folk revival was losing ground to folk rock, and Watson, who had grown up playing everything from Jimmie Rodgers to rockabilly, was free to show that range. Vanguard continued to record him regularly, and he toured widely with Merle. This album signalled that he would not be confined to the museum label of traditional folk; he was a working musician who played whatever suited him.","Windy and Warm was written by John D. Loudermilk, a Nashville songwriter, not a mountain tradition-bearer; Watson's recording made it sound like an old family tune. Listen to how he combines alternating thumb bass with syncopated melody, a technique associated with Merle Travis and Chet Atkins. It is a small lesson in how folk tradition absorbs commercial music and gives it back sounding ancient.","Reviewers welcomed it as proof of Watson's range, and guitarists have treated its instrumentals as essential study pieces ever since. Some revival purists regretted the move away from strictly traditional repertoire, but that view now seems narrow, since Watson's music had always drawn freely on radio and records. It remains one of his most loved Vanguard albums.","Start with Windy and Warm and try to follow the thumb and fingers separately; it sounds like two guitars but is one. Then hear the vocal tracks for his easy phrasing. Compare with his 1964 debut to measure how he widened the repertoire, and follow with The Byrds' Sweetheart of the Rodeo for a rock band heading in the opposite direction."],"george-russell-jazz-workshop":["Twelve short, tightly written pieces for a sextet Russell called his Smalltet: Art Farmer on trumpet, Hal McKusick on alto saxophone and flute, Barry Galbraith on guitar, Bill Evans on piano, with bass and drums. Nothing lasts much over four minutes, yet every track is packed with shifting metres, sudden unison lines and harmonies that hover rather than resolve. The openers Ye Hypocrite, Ye Beelzebub and Ezz-thetic sound like bebop run through a prism, bright, angular and faintly unsettling.","Russell, a drummer turned composer from Cincinnati, had spent years laid up with tuberculosis working out a theory of harmony, published in 1953 as the Lydian Chromatic Concept of Tonal Organization. It argued that jazz could organise itself around scales rather than rapid chord changes. RCA gave him the chance to record the ideas in New York in 1956, a moment when the major labels were briefly curious about serious, experimental small-group jazz alongside the commercial West Coast cool sound.","Listen for how rarely the band simply blows over a chord sequence. Soloists are handed scales and colours instead, so Evans and Farmer sound as if they are floating across the harmony rather than chasing it; this is the idea that, via Miles Davis, would become modal jazz two years later. Concerto for Billy the Kid is a showcase for the young Evans, who plays with a density and harmonic daring few people had yet heard from him.","The record sold poorly and was treated for years as a musician’s album, admired by players and composers but little known to the public. Its reputation has grown steadily as historians traced the path from Russell’s concept to Kind of Blue, and it is now regarded as one of the key documents of 1950s jazz composition. Some listeners still find it cerebral; others hear exceptional wit and swing in its miniatures.","Take it a track at a time rather than as background, because each piece is a compact puzzle. Start with Ezz-thetic, then Concerto for Billy the Kid for Bill Evans at full stretch. Notice how the guitar and piano thicken the harmony without ever settling it. Then play Kind of Blue straight afterwards and listen for the family resemblance in the way the solos breathe."],"george-russell-ezz-thetics":["Six tracks by the George Russell Sextet, with Russell himself at the piano, Don Ellis on trumpet, David Baker on trombone, Steve Swallow on bass and Eric Dolphy guesting on alto saxophone and bass clarinet. The record mixes Russell originals such as the title track, a remake of his 1950s tune, with Miles Davis’s Nardis and a long, broken-open reading of Thelonious Monk’s ’Round Midnight. The sound is lean, spiky and restless, the ensemble writing always pulling against the soloists.","By 1961 Russell had moved from big theoretical statements to a working small band that could play his ideas night after night, and he had begun teaching the Lydian concept to younger musicians. Free jazz had just arrived with Ornette Coleman, and Russell’s group sat in an interesting position between it and composed modernism. Riverside, a New York label better known for Monk and Bill Evans, recorded the sextet in the spring of that year.","The ’Round Midnight here is effectively a feature for Dolphy, and it shows how far the tune could be pushed: the familiar melody is stated, then dissolved into a solo of wide leaps, vocal cries and sudden runs that barely touch the original harmony, while the band’s written backgrounds keep reminding you where the song is. Listen also to Swallow, still playing upright bass here, holding the whole structure together.","Critics at the time were divided between those who heard arid theory and those who heard a composer finding his most personal voice. Posterity has sided firmly with the second camp: the album is now widely considered Russell’s finest small-group record and one of the best showcases of Dolphy as a sideman. Debate continues mainly over whether Russell’s own piano playing adds or distracts.","Begin with ’Round Midnight, since you probably know the tune and can hear exactly what is being done to it. Then go back to the opening title track to hear how Russell’s writing makes a small band sound large. Compare Dolphy here with his own Out to Lunch three years later, and listen for the trombone and trumpet trading jagged unison lines."],"tito-puente-oye-como-va":["A relaxed cha-cha-chá built on a two-chord piano and bass figure that repeats for almost the whole track, a hypnotic vamp that is the song’s real hook. Over it a flute and strings weave lines in the charanga style, the voices chant a short refrain inviting you to hear how the rhythm goes, and Puente’s timbales crackle at the edges. It is a groove rather than a melody, cool and unhurried.","Puente, a New York-born Puerto Rican who had studied at Juilliard, was the timbales-playing king of the Palladium Ballroom’s mambo era. By the early 1960s the mambo craze had cooled and the gentler cha-cha-chá, and the flute-and-violin charanga band, were in fashion. He recorded this for the Tico label, adapting his big-band sound to the new style. It was a modest dance-floor number in its first life.","The famous vamp was not conjured from nothing: it closely resembles the montuno of Chanchullo, a 1957 danzón-mambo by the Cuban bassist Israel López, known as Cachao, and Puente’s tune is generally understood as a reworking of it. Listen, too, for how little actually changes across four minutes; the excitement comes from the percussion and the flute improvising around a fixed floor, the essential logic of Cuban son.","Its second life began in 1970, when Santana recorded it for the album Abraxas and turned the piano riff into an organ and electric guitar anthem that reached the American top twenty. Puente at first bristled at a rock band profiting from his tune, then warmly embraced the royalties and the new audience. Today the song is a standard of Latin music worldwide, played by everyone from school bands to salsa orchestras.","Play Puente’s original first and listen to the bass and piano alone for a minute; everything else is decoration on that loop. Then hear how the flute breathes and bends over it. Follow it with the Santana version and notice what survives the move from dance hall to rock stage: the vamp, and very little else. Finally try Cachao’s Chanchullo to hear the seed."],"tito-puente-top-percussion":["An album almost entirely of drums and voices. Puente strips away his big band and leads a battery of Afro-Cuban percussionists through chants and rhythms rooted in the religious music of Santería, alongside several extended drum features of his own. There are no horns to speak of and few chords: the music is carried by interlocking conga, bongo and timbales patterns, call-and-response singing, and the dry, cracking sound of Puente’s sticks on metal and wood.","Recorded in New York for RCA in 1957, the same year as the best-selling Dance Mania, this was a deliberate step away from the ballroom. Puente’s circle included Cuban drummers such as Mongo Santamaría and Willie Bobo, and the city’s Latin musicians were increasingly interested in the sacred and folkloric sources behind the dance music. That a major American label released such an uncompromising drum record says much about Puente’s commercial clout at the time.","Several tracks invoke specific orishas, the deities of Afro-Cuban religion, through chants that would have been heard at ceremonies rather than in clubs, so the record quietly carried sacred practice into the pop marketplace. Listen for the way the rhythms are layered in fixed parts, each drummer holding a pattern that only makes sense against the others, while one lead voice converses with the singers above.","Top Percussion sold far less than Puente’s dance records and puzzled some of his fans, but among percussionists it became a touchstone, passed hand to hand for decades. Later scholars of Latin music often cite it as an early and serious attempt to document Afro-Cuban ritual drumming for a mass audience. It is now regarded as one of the most important records in Puente’s long catalogue.","Do not wait for a tune; listen instead for the conversation between the drums. Pick one instrument, the high bongo or the lowest conga, and follow it through a whole track, then switch. Notice when the voices answer the lead singer. Hear it beside Dance Mania to understand what Puente’s big band was built on, and beside Gillespie’s Manteca for the jazz side of the same story."],"clara-ward-how-i-got-over":["Clara Ward’s own song of thanksgiving, sung by the Ward Singers with Clara at the piano. It starts almost conversationally, a testimony that the singer has come through hardship and wants to look back and marvel at how she survived, then builds through repetition until the group is shouting over hard, rolling piano chords. The harmonies are close and bright, and the leads trade phrases in the ecstatic, slightly ragged manner of the Holiness church.","The Ward Singers, led by Clara and managed by her formidable mother Gertrude Ward from Philadelphia, were the most glamorous female gospel group of the early 1950s, touring with gowns, wigs and a showmanship that shocked some church elders. Clara wrote the song around 1951, in the years when gospel quartets and groups filled auditoriums across the North and South and were recorded by small independent labels.","The song’s theme of looking back over a hard road carries two meanings at once, personal salvation and the collective survival of Black Americans, and the second became unmistakable in 1963, when Mahalia Jackson sang it to the crowd at the March on Washington shortly before Martin Luther King spoke. Listen for how the melody sits low and speech-like before leaping: it was built for a singer to stretch.","The song quickly entered the repertoire of other gospel singers and became one of the most widely performed gospel compositions of the century, sung by Mahalia Jackson, Aretha Franklin and countless church choirs. Ward’s authorship is sometimes overshadowed by those famous versions, and historians of gospel have worked to restore her place as a composer as well as a performer.","Hear the Ward Singers’ recording first, for the raw group sound and Clara’s piano. Then listen to Mahalia Jackson’s slower, grander versions and notice how she treats each phrase as a sermon. Finally hear Aretha Franklin, who learned from Ward as a girl in Detroit. Listen throughout for the moment the song shifts from memory into jubilation."],"clara-ward-packin-up":["An up-tempo Ward Singers number led by Marion Williams, the group’s most thrilling voice. The song pictures a believer getting ready for the journey to heaven, gathering her things and waiting for departure, and the tempo drives like a train. Williams leaps into a high, bell-like register, growls in the low one, and throws in whoops and falsetto runs, while the other singers pound out a rhythmic chorus behind her.","In the mid-1950s the Ward Singers were at the height of their fame, and much of their excitement came from Marion Williams, who had joined from Miami and often took the leads while Clara arranged and played. The group’s records sold widely in Black communities and their concerts drew huge crowds. Tensions over pay and credit inside the group would lead Williams and others to leave in 1958 to form the Stars of Faith.","Listen to Williams’s ornaments: the high swoops and the little yodel-like breaks are techniques that rock and roll singers were absorbing at exactly this moment, and Little Richard openly named Williams as the source of his famous high whoop. The imagery of packing a bag for glory also turns death into a cheerful journey, a very old spiritual idea delivered at dance-floor speed.","It became one of the songs most closely associated with Williams, who kept singing it through her later solo career on concert stages in America and Europe. Gospel historians often cite the Ward Singers’ records of this period as the peak of the female group style, and they frequently credit Williams rather than Clara as the group’s greatest vocal talent, a judgment that has shaped how both women are remembered.","Listen for the lead voice first and try to follow every change of register; Williams moves from a shout to a whisper in a single phrase. Then focus on the backing singers and the piano, which keep the rhythm relentlessly steady. Afterwards play Little Richard’s Tutti Frutti and listen for the same whoop turned secular."],"bill-haley-crazy-man-crazy":["Two minutes of shouted enthusiasm from Bill Haley and His Comets. The band is essentially a western swing outfit, with steel guitar and slapped double bass, pushed into a hard backbeat, and the singers answer Haley with gang-chant responses borrowed from teenage slang about how wild the music is. A short guitar break and a steel guitar solo keep things moving, and the whole record rides on the clacking bass.","Haley had spent years as a country and western bandleader in Chester, Pennsylvania, before noticing that the rhythm and blues records played by local Black disc jockeys excited young audiences. He renamed his Saddlemen the Comets around 1952 and began writing songs aimed at teenagers. This one, written with the band’s bassist Marshall Lytle, was issued by the small Philadelphia label Essex in 1953.","The record is usually counted as the first rock and roll recording to reach the national pop charts in the United States, a year before Rock Around the Clock. Listen for the slap bass: Lytle slaps the strings against the fingerboard so that the bass works as a second drum, a country technique that gives the Comets their particular clattering drive in place of a heavy drum kit.","It reached the top twenty in 1953 and was used in a television drama, but it was quickly overshadowed by Haley’s later hits and is often forgotten in popular histories. Rock historians have since restored it as a milestone, though some argue that Black rhythm and blues records such as Rocket 88 deserve the title of first rock and roll record more than any of Haley’s work.","Listen first to the rhythm section rather than the voice: the slapped bass and the snare backbeat are the new thing. Then notice the chanted responses, a crowd sound built into the record. Compare it with Hank Williams on one side and Big Joe Turner on the other, and you will hear the two sources Haley was stitching together."],"bill-haley-shake-rattle-and-roll":["Haley’s version of the twelve-bar blues written by Jesse Stone under the name Charles Calhoun and recorded earlier the same year by Big Joe Turner. The Comets play it faster and brighter, with a honking tenor saxophone, crisp guitar fills and the band shouting the title in unison. Haley’s delivery is cheerful rather than knowing, and the whole thing bounces rather than rolls.","Signed to Decca in 1954 and working with the producer Milt Gabler, who had produced Louis Jordan’s jump blues, Haley recorded this within months of Turner’s rhythm and blues hit. Radio in the segregated United States was far more likely to play a white band’s version to a white audience, and Decca aimed it squarely at that market. It became a top-ten pop hit before Rock Around the Clock had broken through.","Compare the words with Turner’s and you will find that Haley’s version removes or rewrites the more suggestive verses, turning an adult song about a woman in bed into something closer to a teenage party chant. That quiet sanitising is the whole story of 1950s crossover in miniature. Listen also for the Louis Jordan-style arrangement, Gabler bringing his jump blues experience to the session.","It sold heavily and helped establish Haley as the first white star of rock and roll, especially in Britain. Later critics have often used it as a textbook example of a white cover softening a Black original, and Turner’s recording is now generally preferred. Yet musicians including the young British beat groups heard Haley’s version first, and it undeniably carried the song to places Turner’s could not reach.","Play Big Joe Turner’s version and Haley’s back to back. Notice the tempo, the saxophone and the voice: Turner leans back and growls, Haley pushes forward and smiles. Then listen for what the Comets add, the unison shouts and the slap bass. Afterwards try Elvis Presley’s later version, which splits the difference between them."],"celia-cruz-burundanga":["A fast, comic song in the Afro-Cuban style, sung by Celia Cruz with the Sonora Matancera. Written by Óscar Muñoz Bouffartique, it tells a tangled story of a chain of animals and a mischievous brew, built on nonsense words and Afro-Cuban vocabulary that are a delight to sing. The trumpets punch out short riffs, the chorus answers Cruz, and her dark, powerful voice rides the rhythm with complete authority.","In the early 1950s Cruz was the new female lead of the Sonora Matancera, Havana’s most popular conjunto, having replaced the singer Myrta Silva. Some fans had doubted a Black woman with a deep voice could succeed with the band; songs like this silenced them. The group recorded for the New York-based Seeco label, whose discs spread their sound across Latin America and the Caribbean.","The title is Cuban slang for a jumble or a confusing mess, and the song’s nonsense chain of animals plays on that idea. Listen for the Afro-Cuban words woven into the lyric, which point to the Yoruba-derived religious culture Cruz grew up near in Havana and which she would carry into all her later music, long after salsa made her an international star.","It became Cruz’s first big hit and is usually described as her first gold record, establishing her as the Sonora Matancera’s star rather than a replacement. Critics now see her 1950s recordings as the foundation of the style that, in New York two decades later, would be marketed as salsa, and many Cuban music historians prefer these sides to her later, glossier work.","Listen to how Cruz shapes the rhythm with her consonants; the song’s playful words work like extra percussion. Notice the trumpets’ short answering blasts and the chorus response. Then play something from Celia & Johnny, two decades later, to hear the same voice with a harder New York band. Compare with Beny Moré or any Havana conjunto of the period."],"schuller-seven-studies-klee":["Seven short orchestral pieces, each a musical response to a painting by Paul Klee: Antique Harmonies, Abstract Trio, Little Blue Devil, The Twittering Machine, Arab Village, An Eerie Moment and Pastorale. The first builds slow, glowing blocks of sound like Klee’s coloured squares; Little Blue Devil is a sly jazz walk with a pizzicato bass; The Twittering Machine chirps and splutters in pointillist fragments; Arab Village evokes North African flute and drum music. The whole set lasts about twenty minutes.","Schuller wrote the set on commission from the Minneapolis Symphony Orchestra, which premiered it under Antal Doráti in 1959. He was then a horn player turned composer who moved between the concert hall and jazz clubs, and who had coined the term Third Stream for music that joined the two. Klee himself was a trained violinist who thought about painting in musical terms, which made him a natural subject.","Each study tries to translate something visual into sound rather than tell a story. Antique Harmonies works with slowly shifting blocks of colour, much as the painting is built from soft rectangles; The Twittering Machine gives its mechanical birds to chirping woodwinds and has the percussion suggest the crank that drives them. Little Blue Devil quietly borrows the walking bass and blues inflections of jazz, Schuller’s other musical world breaking through the concert-hall surface.","The piece was an immediate success and quickly became Schuller’s most widely performed orchestral work, admired for its wit, its brevity and its orchestration. Some modernist critics thought it too pictorial, even light; audiences did not care. It remains a favourite for orchestras wanting a modern piece that listeners enjoy at first hearing, and a common teaching example of orchestral colour.","Look at the Klee paintings while you listen, one per study; the connections are surprisingly direct. Start with Little Blue Devil and The Twittering Machine, the two most vivid. Notice how short everything is: not one gesture is wasted. Then compare with Mussorgsky’s Pictures at an Exhibition, the older model of music about pictures."],"schuller-of-reminiscences-and-reflections":["A single-movement orchestral elegy of about twenty-five minutes. It begins in darkness and quiet, with low strings and hushed brass, moves through passages of intense, dense lament and moments of violent outburst, and returns towards stillness. The harmony is freely atonal and often thick, but the emotional line is easy to follow: grief, remembrance of happier times, anger, and finally a kind of exhausted acceptance.","Schuller wrote the work after the death of his wife, Marjorie Black, in 1992, after more than four decades of marriage. It was commissioned by the Louisville Orchestra and premiered in 1993, and it won the Pulitzer Prize for Music in 1994. By then Schuller was an elder statesman of American music, known as conductor, publisher, record producer, jazz historian and teacher as much as composer.","The title’s two words describe a structure as much as a mood: passages that recall earlier, more lyrical music sit alongside stretches that seem to look back on them from a distance, as if memory itself were being examined. Listen for moments when the brass briefly recalls the warmth of an older tonal style before the harmony clouds again; they are the reminiscences breaking in.","The Pulitzer brought Schuller belated recognition as a composer rather than an all-purpose musical polymath, though the piece is played far less often than its prize might suggest. Critics have praised its sincerity and orchestral craft, while others find its language characteristic of a late-modernist style that younger American composers had already moved past. It is increasingly heard as one of his most personal statements.","Listen in one sitting and in quiet, as you would to a requiem, following the rise and fall of intensity rather than any tune. Notice where the texture thins to a few instruments; those are the most private moments. Compare it with Berg’s Violin Concerto, another memorial written in a modernist idiom, and with Schuller’s own lighter Klee studies to hear his range."],"soul-stirrers-peace-in-the-valley":["Thomas A. Dorsey’s gospel song sung by the Soul Stirrers, with the young Sam Cooke on lead. The quartet sets a slow, rocking bed of close harmony, deep bass voice at the bottom, and Cooke sings the melody with an unusual lightness, gliding between notes rather than hitting them hard. The song describes a promised land where there will be no more sorrow and where even wild animals are gentle, and the group sings it with calm longing.","The Soul Stirrers, founded in Texas in the 1920s and shaped by the lead singer R. H. Harris, were the most influential male gospel quartet in America. When Harris left in 1950 the nineteen-year-old Cooke from Chicago replaced him, and this was among his first recordings with the group for Art Rupe’s Specialty label in Los Angeles in 1951. Dorsey had written the song for Mahalia Jackson in the late 1930s.","Listen to how Cooke handles long notes: instead of the heavy vibrato and shouted climaxes of older quartet leads, he adds a small turn or melisma and lets the note slide away. The quartet behind him uses the switch-lead approach Harris had pioneered, voices ready to take over the line. Red Foley and later Elvis Presley would record the song, carrying Cooke’s smoothness into country and pop.","It was well received in gospel circles, though some older listeners missed Harris’s power. With hindsight, historians hear in these early sides the sound that would soon make Cooke the first gospel star to cross into secular soul, and they credit the Soul Stirrers’ blend of harmony and individual lead as a template for doo-wop and soul vocal groups.","Listen first for the bass singer, who holds the floor steady, then for Cooke gliding on top. Notice the absence of instruments; everything is voices. Compare with Mahalia Jackson or Dorsey’s own version to hear how the song changes when a quartet sings it. Then play Cooke’s You Send Me and listen for the same slides."],"soul-stirrers-nearer-to-thee":["A slow, intensely devotional song by the Soul Stirrers with Sam Cooke on lead, built on the long tradition of hymns about drawing close to God. The quartet hums and sustains chords while Cooke sings the lead with rising urgency, repeating short phrases with small variations, and the gentle guitar keeps a steady pulse. By the end his voice is soaring above the group in long, bending lines, yet it never becomes harsh.","By the mid-1950s Cooke was a star in the gospel world, drawing young women to Soul Stirrers concerts in a way no quartet singer had before. Specialty recorded him often, and the group’s records sold well to church audiences. At the same time Cooke was being quietly urged to try popular music, a move that, in 1957, would scandalise part of the gospel community but make him a pop idol.","Listen to the way Cooke builds tension by repeating a phrase and changing only one note or one syllable each time, a preaching technique borrowed from the pulpit; it is the same technique he would later use in secular songs. The guitar is also worth noticing, since quartets had long sung unaccompanied, and its presence hints at the move towards rhythm-and-blues-style backing.","It became one of Cooke’s best-loved gospel sides and is often cited by writers on gospel as the fullest expression of his quartet style before his crossover. Some gospel purists later regarded his departure as a betrayal, but collectors and critics now treat the Specialty recordings as a peak of the quartet tradition and as essential background for soul music.","Begin by listening only to Cooke and count how many ways he finds to sing a single repeated phrase. Then listen to the voices behind him, which hold the harmony like an organ. Follow it with Touch the Hem of His Garment, then with A Change Is Gonna Come, and hear how the gospel sound survives in his secular masterworks."],"gerry-mulligan-bernies-tune":["The Gerry Mulligan Quartet with Chet Baker plays Bernie Miller’s brisk tune with no piano at all. Mulligan’s baritone saxophone and Baker’s trumpet state the theme together, then weave around each other in improvised counterpoint, one taking the lead while the other plays a supporting line, over walking bass and brushed drums. The sound is airy and transparent, with every line audible, and the whole track is over in about three minutes.","Mulligan had arranged for the Birth of the Cool sessions in New York and then moved to Los Angeles, where he began playing with Baker at a club called the Haig. The small room had no usable piano, or so the story goes, and the pianoless format was born partly of necessity. Richard Bock founded the Pacific Jazz label to record the quartet, and this was among its very first releases in 1952.","Without a piano to state the chords, the harmony is implied entirely by the bass and the interplay of the two horns, so listen for how Mulligan’s low lines often function as a second bass or a piano left hand. The counterpoint owes something to Bach and to Dixieland, where trumpet and clarinet also improvised together; West Coast cool quietly revived the oldest jazz texture.","The record sold well and made the quartet the most talked-about jazz group of 1952 and 1953, turning Baker into a star and West Coast cool into a movement. Some East Coast critics dismissed the style as polite or thin; others praised its clarity and wit. Today the quartet’s recordings are regarded as a high point of the cool era and a model for pianoless small groups since.","Listen with the two horns in separate ears if you can: follow Mulligan’s baritone for one chorus, then Baker’s trumpet. Notice how much space there is without a piano. Compare with a bebop quintet track to hear what has been taken away, and with Ornette Coleman’s pianoless quartet a few years later to hear who learned from it."],"gerry-mulligan-night-lights":["A late-night album of slow and mid-tempo pieces played by a sextet with Art Farmer on trumpet and flugelhorn, Bob Brookmeyer on valve trombone, Jim Hall on guitar, Bill Crow on bass and Dave Bailey on drums. Mulligan plays piano on the opening title track, a quiet original, and baritone elsewhere. The programme includes a bossa nova reworking of a Chopin prelude and standards such as Morning of the Carnival and In the Wee Small Hours of the Morning. Everything is soft-edged and unhurried.","By 1963 Mulligan had led quartets, a concert jazz band and many all-star sessions, and he was one of the best-known figures in American jazz. Bossa nova had arrived from Brazil the previous year and was sweeping American jazz, and this record reflects that fashion quietly. It was made in New York for Philips, with musicians who had played in his Concert Jazz Band and knew his writing well.","Listen to Prelude in E minor: Mulligan takes Chopin’s short, grieving piano prelude from Op. 28 and sets its descending chords to a gentle bossa nova rhythm, so the nineteenth-century harmony sits naturally inside a 1960s Brazilian groove. On the title track, notice that the leader is at the piano, not the saxophone, playing sparse chords with a composer’s patience rather than a pianist’s display.","It was received as a pleasant mood record and has only grown in reputation, becoming one of Mulligan’s most beloved and most reissued albums. Some critics regard it as the purest expression of his lyrical side; others find it too comfortable next to the adventurous jazz of 1963. The title track has become a quiet standard among jazz musicians and late-night radio hosts.","Play it after dark at a low volume, as intended. Start with the title track, then go straight to the Chopin, and compare it afterwards with a piano recording of the original prelude. Listen for Jim Hall’s guitar, which fills the space a piano would take. Pair it with Getz/Gilberto for bossa nova’s other American face."],"harry-belafonte-at-carnegie-hall":["A double album taken from two sold-out concerts in April 1959, laid out like the evening itself: a first half of Black American work songs and spirituals, a Caribbean section, and a closing tour of songs from around the world. Belafonte moves from the hard, driving rhythm of 'John Henry' to Irish and Hebrew songs and back to the islands, and the famous stretch is a long, loose 'Matilda', where he turns the whole hall into his chorus.","By 1959 Belafonte was the biggest-selling Black entertainer in America, riding the success of Calypso, a Hollywood career and a growing role in the civil-rights movement beside Martin Luther King Jr. RCA recorded the concerts with the new stereo equipment and put them out as a lavish two-record set, then a luxury item. Robert DeCormier, a folk-choral arranger from the left-wing tradition of the Weavers, shaped the choral and orchestral backing.","Listen to how the audience is recorded: RCA placed microphones out in the hall, so you hear the crowd learn a refrain and then carry it without help, until Belafonte teases them for singing it wrong. The running order is an argument in itself: work songs, then island songs, then songs of other nations, putting Caribbean music on the same stage as Irish, Israeli and American folk song rather than filing it away as novelty.","It was a huge commercial success and stayed on the American album charts for years, one of the first live albums to sell on that scale. Folk purists sometimes found the polish too smooth, and later writers argued over whether Belafonte flattened the island styles he popularised. The record is now seen as a model for the concert album and for folk music presented as a world-wide family.","Begin with 'Matilda' and let the crowd singing do its work, then go back to the opening work songs to hear how hard and rhythmic Belafonte could sound. Compare the Weavers' own Carnegie Hall concert of 1955 for the same folk-revival spirit on a smaller scale, and the Belafonte–Makeba record for where this work led."],"harry-belafonte-island-in-the-sun":["A slow, swaying ballad in a gentle island rhythm, sung by Belafonte in his warm, husky middle voice over guitar, soft percussion and a hushed backing choir. It is less a dance tune than a hymn to a place: the song describes the beauty of a Caribbean island, the dignity of the people who work its land and sea, and a singer's promise never to forget it. It lasts barely three minutes and never raises its voice.","Belafonte wrote it with Irving Burgie, the Brooklyn-born songwriter of Barbadian descent who supplied much of the Calypso album, as the title song for Robert Rossen's 1957 film Island in the Sun, in which Belafonte starred. The film, set on a fictional West Indian island, caused a scandal in the segregated United States by hinting at romance between Belafonte's character and a white woman played by Joan Fontaine, and some Southern cinemas refused to show it.","Notice what the song leaves out: there is no joke, no double meaning and no fast calypso patter, the things American listeners had come to expect from island music. Burgie and Belafonte wrote it closer to a folk anthem or a hymn, and its images of labour on land and sea echo the work songs Belafonte sang elsewhere. The choir's soft answering phrases give it the sound of a community singing, not a tourist admiring the view.","It was a big hit, especially in Britain, and quickly became a standard, recorded by many singers and loved across the Caribbean diaspora. Some critics have heard it as picture-postcard exoticism of the kind the film was selling; others note that it grants island people a gravity that Hollywood rarely did. It remains one of the songs most closely tied to Belafonte's name.","Listen first for the choir underneath, entering and falling away like waves, and for how little Belafonte pushes his voice. Then play it beside 'Day-O' from Calypso to hear the two sides of his island repertoire, the work song and the ballad, and beside Lord Kitchener's London calypso for a Trinidadian's view of leaving home."],"kander-and-ebb-kiss-of-the-spider-woman":["Two men share a cell in a prison in an unnamed Latin American dictatorship: Molina, a gay window-dresser jailed on a morals charge, and Valentin, a hard-line political prisoner. To survive the torture and boredom Molina retells the films of his favourite star, Aurora, and the stage keeps breaking open into her glamorous musical numbers. Aurora also plays the Spider Woman, a figure of death whose kiss the prisoners fear, and the score moves between cell-block grit and lush movie pastiche.","The show comes from Manuel Puig's 1976 novel and Hector Babenco's 1985 film. Terrence McNally wrote the book and Harold Prince, who had directed Cabaret, staged it. An early workshop in 1990 went badly, but the rebuilt show opened in Toronto and London in 1992 and on Broadway in 1993, with Chita Rivera as Aurora and Brent Carver as Molina, written in the years when AIDS was devastating Broadway's own community.","Kander writes Aurora's numbers in the style of 1940s studio musicals and Latin dance films, with sweeping strings and brassy tangos, while the prison music is harder and plainer; listen for the moment late in the show when Molina's own voice takes on the film-star sweep, as fantasy and reality finally merge. The anthem 'The Day After That', sung by the prisoners' families, was taken up at AIDS benefits in the 1990s, giving the show a second, unspoken subject.","Broadway critics were divided on the book but praised Rivera and Carver, and the show won the Tony for Best Musical in 1993, along with awards for score and book. Some felt the fantasy numbers smothered the prison drama; others saw that tension as the whole point, as in Cabaret. It has had fewer revivals than Kander and Ebb's two big hits, and many admirers think it is their most underrated score.","Start with the Broadway cast recording and play the title song and 'Where You Are', Aurora's invitation to escape into the movies, then the quiet cell scenes that follow. Listen for how each fantasy number answers a moment of pain. Pair it with Cabaret, the earlier show where a nightclub also comments on political terror."],"lee-konitz-with-warne-marsh":["Konitz's light, dry alto and Warne Marsh's airy tenor weave around each other through a set of standards and bebop tunes, including Charlie Parker's 'Donna Lee' and the swing number 'Topsy'. The two horns often play long, winding unison lines, then split into two independent voices that sound like a baroque duet. The rhythm section keeps a steady, quiet swing underneath, and nobody raises the temperature: the excitement is all in the length and logic of the lines.","Konitz and Marsh had both studied with the blind pianist Lennie Tristano in New York, whose school stressed long, even melodic lines, counterpoint and freedom from bebop clichés. By 1955 both were working away from their teacher, and this Atlantic session reunited them with a strong New York rhythm section led by Oscar Pettiford on bass and Kenny Clarke on drums, drummers and bassists far earthier than Tristano liked, which loosens the music.","Listen for passages where the two saxophones improvise at the same time rather than taking turns, each keeping its own line while fitting the other, a habit learned from Tristano's counterpoint exercises and from Bach. Many of the 'new' tunes are fresh melodies written over the chords of old standards, a Tristano-school practice, so a familiar song is often hiding underneath an unfamiliar title.","At the time it was filed under cool jazz and admired mainly by musicians. Later critics came to treat it as the best record of the Tristano school, warmer and more swinging than their earlier work because of Pettiford and Clarke. It is now a favourite of saxophonists, studied for its line-writing, and a strong argument that cool jazz was not merely soft.","Pick one saxophone and follow it through a whole track, then play it again following the other. 'Donna Lee' is the best way in; compare it with Charlie Parker's own version to hear how the Tristano players cooled bebop's heat without slowing it down. Then try Konitz's earlier 'Subconscious-Lee'."],"lee-konitz-motion":["A trio record with no piano: Konitz's alto, Sonny Dallas on bass and Elvin Jones on drums, playing standards such as 'I Remember You', 'All of Me' and 'You'd Be So Nice to Come Home To'. Konitz barely states the melodies; he starts improvising almost at once, spinning long, unbroken lines that bend and reshape each tune. Jones, soon to power John Coltrane's quartet, surrounds him with a rolling, busy pulse that pushes the alto forward.","By 1961 Konitz, once the model cool-jazz altoist, had been overshadowed by the hard boppers and by Ornette Coleman's free jazz. Producer Creed Taylor recorded this date for Verve in New York during the same months in which Jones was joining Coltrane. Removing the piano, as Sonny Rollins and Gerry Mulligan had done, left Konitz free of fixed chords and able to steer the harmony wherever he wanted.","Try naming each tune before Konitz lets you: often he suggests the melody only in passing fragments, trusting you to hear the song's chords under his new line. Listen too for the contrast of temperaments, the cool, level alto against Jones's churning triplets and cymbal crashes; the tension between them is what gives the album its name and its energy.","It sold modestly and was out of print for long periods, but musicians passed it around, and it is now widely rated Konitz's greatest record and one of the key piano-less trio albums. Critics hear it as proof that the Tristano approach could survive in the hotter climate of the 1960s. Expanded reissues with extra takes have only strengthened its reputation.","Start with 'I Remember You' and listen to how quickly Konitz leaves the melody behind. Keep one ear on Elvin Jones's drums, which almost form a second solo. Compare Sonny Rollins's piano-less trio on Way Out West for a more humorous, earthy take on the same format."],"stan-getz-jazz-samba":["Getz's soft, cloud-like tenor floats over Charlie Byrd's nylon-string guitar and a light, shuffling samba rhythm, playing new Brazilian tunes such as Jobim's 'Desafinado' and 'Samba de Uma Nota Só' (One Note Samba). The sound is intimate and quiet: brushes on the snare, a gentle bass, the guitar picking out harmonies. Getz barely raises his voice, yet his phrasing gives each melody a sighing, half-sad lilt that became the American sound of bossa nova.","Charlie Byrd had heard bossa nova on a State Department tour of South America in 1961 and brought records home to Washington. Producer Creed Taylor paired him with Getz, back from years in Europe, and the album was recorded in a single day in February 1962 in a Washington church hall chosen for its sound. Released that spring, it rode a new appetite for quiet, sophisticated music in the early 1960s.","The American players did not fully master the Brazilian beat; listen to the drums and you hear something closer to a soft jazz shuffle than João Gilberto's guitar rhythm, one reason Brazilians later preferred Getz/Gilberto. Also notice 'Desafinado', whose title means 'out of tune': Jobim wrote its odd, wandering melody as a joke about critics who called bossa nova singers off-key.","It reached the top of the Billboard album chart, a rare feat for jazz, and 'Desafinado' became a hit single that won Getz a Grammy. It launched the American bossa nova craze and a flood of imitations. Byrd later sued over royalties, feeling he had been under-credited. Critics now rate it slightly below Getz/Gilberto but credit it as the record that opened the door.","Start with 'Desafinado' and listen to how Getz handles the melody with barely any vibrato, then how Byrd's solo answers him on guitar. Play it next to João Gilberto's Chega de saudade to hear the Brazilian original, and then Getz/Gilberto to hear what happened when the two traditions met properly."],"cannonball-adderley-quintet-in-san-francisco":["A live set by Cannonball Adderley's new quintet: his big-toned, bubbling alto, his brother Nat's cornet, Bobby Timmons on piano, Sam Jones on bass and Louis Hayes on drums. The opener, Timmons's 'This Here', is a swaying, church-flavoured tune in a rocking triple time, full of handclap rhythms and blue notes. Around it come fast hard-bop pieces and ballads, stretched out in long solos, with Cannonball's warm spoken introductions setting the mood between numbers.","Adderley had spent two years in Miles Davis's sextet, playing on Milestones and Kind of Blue, and left in 1959 to lead his own band again. Producer Orrin Keepnews recorded the group at the Jazz Workshop club in San Francisco in October 1959. Timmons had just written 'Moanin'' for Art Blakey, and gospel-tinged jazz was in the air as Black church music moved into the clubs.","Listen to the audience: the club is loose and noisy, people clap on the backbeat, and the band feeds off it, which is part of the point. Cannonball's spoken introductions are worth hearing too, relaxed and witty, turning a jazz set into a warm community event. And notice how 'This Here' keeps its gospel feel even in three-beat time, an unusual mix at the time.","The album sold very well for a jazz record and made the quintet a popular act, with 'This Here' a jukebox hit. It helped define soul jazz. Some critics grumbled that the style became formula in lesser hands, and the Adderleys were occasionally accused of playing to the crowd. Most now see this record as fresh and joyful, a clear bridge between hard bop and soul.","Start with 'This Here' and clap along on the offbeats the way the crowd does. Then listen to how Cannonball's solo on a fast number flows in quick, singing phrases. Follow it with Horace Silver's 'The Preacher' and Blakey's 'Moanin'', then jump ahead to 'Mercy, Mercy, Mercy' to hear where the band went."],"eric-dolphy-far-cry":["A quintet record with Dolphy on alto sax, bass clarinet and flute, Booker Little on trumpet, Jaki Byard on piano, Ron Carter on bass and Roy Haynes on drums. The first side is a suite for Charlie Parker; there are original pieces full of wide leaps and sudden speech-like cries; and the album ends with 'Tenderly', played by Dolphy alone on alto, with no band at all. The music sits between bebop and the new free jazz, keeping chords but stretching them hard.","Recorded in one day in December 1960 at Rudy Van Gelder's New Jersey studio for Prestige's New Jazz label, it came during Dolphy's busiest year, when he was working with Charles Mingus, Ornette Coleman and John Coltrane. Booker Little was a gifted young trumpeter from Memphis who, like Dolphy, was drawn to bold intervals and darker harmonies. Their partnership was brief: Little died in 1961, aged 23.","Listen to 'Tenderly': unaccompanied saxophone was still rare (Coleman Hawkins had tried it in 1948), and Dolphy plays the ballad using huge jumps between high and low registers, as if two voices were talking. In the Parker tribute, listen for how Dolphy's alto recalls Bird's phrasing while leaping far outside it, homage and escape at once. His bass clarinet solos also helped establish the instrument in jazz.","At first some critics, including a few in Down Beat, found Dolphy's leaps and squeals ugly, and he was lumped in with the controversial new thing. Later judgment turned warmly, and Far Cry is now counted among his finest records, second only to Out to Lunch!. The Dolphy–Little partnership, cut short, is often mourned as one of jazz's great lost paths.","Begin at the end with 'Tenderly' and follow how Dolphy turns one familiar ballad into a conversation with himself. Then go back to the Parker suite and hear how the whole band honours bebop while bending it. Compare Hawkins's 'Picasso' for an earlier solo saxophone, and Out to Lunch! for where Dolphy went next."],"horace-silver-blowin-the-blues-away":["Horace Silver's quintet at full strength: Blue Mitchell on trumpet, Junior Cook on tenor, Gene Taylor on bass and Louis Hayes on drums, all playing Silver's own tunes. The title track charges in at speed; 'Sister Sadie' is a strutting, gospel-shouting blues with riffing horns; 'Peace' is a hushed, prayer-like ballad; and two trio pieces put Silver's percussive piano in front. Silver's style, short hammered phrases and churchy chords, drives everything.","Silver had co-founded the Jazz Messengers with Art Blakey and by 1959 had his own quintet, one of Blue Note's best-selling acts. The album was recorded at Rudy Van Gelder's studio in Hackensack, New Jersey, in the late summer of 1959, the same year as Kind of Blue and The Shape of Jazz to Come. Silver was writing tunes that pulled jazz toward gospel, blues and dance.","Listen to how Silver writes the horn parts in 'Sister Sadie': the trumpet and tenor answer each other like a preacher and congregation, a gospel call-and-response pattern turned into a jazz arrangement. In 'The Baghdad Blues', listen for the Middle-Eastern-sounding scale, an early sign of Silver's interest in music beyond America that came to the fore in Song for My Father, with its Cape Verdean roots.","It was well received and 'Sister Sadie' became a hit and a standard, later adopted by big bands. Critics now often rate it Silver's best album with this quintet, a perfect record of hard bop's balance between complexity and groove. Some purists thought soul jazz too commercial, but Silver's writing has aged better than most of the style, and 'Peace' is widely admired for its restraint.","Start with 'Sister Sadie' and notice how the horns riff behind each soloist, then play 'Peace' right after to hear the other side of Silver. Compare with Blakey's 'Moanin'' and Silver's own 'The Preacher', and then 'Song for My Father' to hear the same band sound opening up."],"chet-baker-chet":["An album of slow ballads on which Chet Baker does not sing but plays trumpet, softly and close to the microphone, in front of a cushion of New York musicians including Bill Evans on piano, Pepper Adams on baritone sax, Kenny Burrell on guitar and Paul Chambers on bass. The tempos hardly move, and songs such as 'Alone Together', 'It Never Entered My Mind' and 'How High the Moon' come out as late-night reveries, muted in colour and almost whispered.","Baker had been the golden boy of West Coast cool jazz in the early 1950s, but by the late decade his heroin addiction was damaging his career. Producer Orrin Keepnews recorded him for Riverside in New York around the turn of 1959, with East Coast players more usually heard in hard bop. The same season Bill Evans was working with Miles Davis on the sessions that became Kind of Blue.","Listen to Bill Evans behind the trumpet: his soft, clustered chords and quiet voicings set the mood almost as much as Baker does, and they are the same sound he brought to Kind of Blue a few months later. Notice too how Baker leaves space, playing fewer notes than any trumpeter of his generation; the silences between his phrases are part of the melody.","It was little noticed at release and sometimes dismissed as mood music. Later listeners, rediscovering Baker after his death in 1988, came to hear it as one of his most consistent records, and it is now valued as a showcase of his tone and as an early Bill Evans document. Some still find the ballad-only programme too even, and that is a fair complaint.","Play it late at night, quietly, and listen for the way Baker's trumpet almost breathes the melody instead of attacking it. Start with 'It Never Entered My Mind', then compare it with his singing on Chet Baker Sings and with Coltrane's Ballads for another horn player's approach to slow songs."],"ahmad-jamal-chamber-music-of-the-new-jazz":["A trio without drums: Ahmad Jamal on piano, Ray Crawford on guitar and Israel Crosby on bass, playing standards and originals as delicate, carefully arranged chamber music. Jamal plays very little, leaving wide spaces, then drops in a sudden crashing chord or a glittering run high on the keyboard. The rhythm is kept by Crosby's springy bass and Crawford's guitar, which sometimes taps the strings to imitate a bongo drum, giving the music a light, Latin bounce.","Jamal was a young pianist in Chicago, leading a trio in the city's clubs, when the local Chess company's Argo label issued this album. The title reached for the prestige of classical music, then a common move in 1950s jazz. Miles Davis heard Jamal in these years and told his own musicians to listen to Jamal's space and understatement, and later recorded tunes from Jamal's repertoire.","Listen for the silences: in a bebop age that prized many notes, Jamal leaves whole bars empty and lets the bass and guitar carry the tune, and those gaps are as arranged as the notes. The tune 'New Rhumba' became a feature for Miles Davis and Gil Evans on Miles Ahead in 1957, where Evans kept Jamal's lilting bass figure almost exactly.","It sold modestly at first and some critics called Jamal's style cocktail piano, too light for serious jazz. That judgment turned after Jamal's success with the Pershing recordings and as musicians talked about his influence on Miles Davis. It is now valued as the purest record of his early trio, and as a key source for the space and restraint of modal jazz.","Listen first for how much Jamal leaves out, then for Crawford's knocking guitar under the piano. After that, play 'New Rhumba' from Miles Ahead to hear Gil Evans's orchestra borrow the tune. Compare 'Poinciana' from a few years later, when drums joined and the trio found its hit."],"ahmad-jamal-the-awakening":["A trio album with Jamil Nasser on bass and Frank Gant on drums, mixing Jamal's own tunes, such as the title track and 'I Love Music', with pieces by Herbie Hancock, Oliver Nelson and Antonio Carlos Jobim. The sound is bigger and more percussive than Jamal's 1950s trio, with thunderous left-hand chords, long vamps on one repeated bass figure, and sudden quiet passages in which the piano plays alone like a meditation. Everything turns on dramatic contrasts of loud and soft.","By 1970 jazz was being pulled toward rock and electric instruments, and Miles Davis, once a disciple of Jamal's style, had just made Bitches Brew. Jamal stayed acoustic, recording this for Impulse! in New York in early 1970. He had also been through personal and business changes in the 1960s, and the album marked a rethinking of his style that he continued through the following decade.","Two decades later, hip-hop producers found this record: Pete Rock built the loop for Nas's 'The World Is Yours' out of the opening of 'I Love Music', and other producers mined the album too. Listen for that brief, rising figure and you will recognise it. Also notice how Jamal plays Hancock's 'Dolphin Dance' and Jobim's 'Wave', stretching them over long vamps that anticipate the slow build of later jazz trios.","It drew little notice at release, overshadowed by fusion and free jazz. Its reputation grew in the 1990s as sampling drew young listeners back to it, and critics now count it among Jamal's strongest later records. Some purists dislike hearing it through hip-hop, but most agree the album's grandeur and patience deserve attention in their own right.","Start with 'I Love Music', and listen for how Jamal takes several minutes to build it, then play Nas's 'The World Is Yours' to hear the loop in a new setting. Next try 'Wave', and compare Jobim's own version. Loud-soft contrasts matter here, so listen on a good system."],"clifford-brown-with-strings":["Twelve standards, each around three minutes, played by Clifford Brown over a lush string orchestra arranged and conducted by Neal Hefti: 'Yesterdays', 'Laura', 'What's New', 'Stardust', 'Smoke Gets in Your Eyes', 'Willow Weep for Me' and more. There are almost no fast choruses. Brown mostly states the melody, bending a note here, adding a quick turn there, and the one thing you recognise him by is the tone itself: round, warm, full in every register, never thin or brassy even at a whisper.","Recorded in New York in January 1955 for EmArcy, Mercury's jazz label, while Brown was co-leading the most exciting hard-bop quintet in the country with Max Roach. Strings albums were a commercial genre, a way to sell a jazz soloist to the dinner-party market, and Charlie Parker's had shown both the appeal and the risk. Brown's own quintet rhythm section sat in underneath the violins. Seventeen months later he was dead in a car crash on the Pennsylvania Turnpike, aged twenty-five.","Listen for how little he decorates. A bebop trumpeter who could run changes at blinding speed chose to play these tunes almost straight, and the art is in the placement: a phrase started a fraction late, a long note held with a slow swell, a single upward rip at the end of a bridge. The rhythm section of Richie Powell, George Morrow and Max Roach keeps a quiet swing going beneath strings that would otherwise float.","Some jazz critics filed it with the easy-listening strings records of the period and moved on to the quintet sides. Trumpeters never did: generations of players have studied it as a lesson in sound and in the discipline of melody, and it remains a standard recommendation to young players. Hefti's charts are sometimes called syrupy, and they are conventional, but the record has outlasted most of its kind because the soloist never condescends to the material.","Start with 'Laura' or 'Portrait of Jenny' and listen only to the trumpet's sound, as you would a singer's voice. Then play the same tune by a vocalist and notice how Brown phrases like one. Follow it with the quintet's 'Joy Spring' to hear what he was holding back, and with Charlie Parker's strings sessions for the model he was answering."],"little-walter-blues-with-a-feeling":["A slow twelve-bar blues, about three minutes long, in which Little Walter sings in his light, unforced voice and answers every line with harmonica. The harp is cupped around a cheap microphone and pushed through a guitar amplifier, so it comes out thick, distorted and sustained, closer to a saxophone than a mouth organ. The guitars lay down a patient walking figure while the harmonica swoops, bends and hangs on single notes until they almost sob.","The song was not his: it had been recorded in 1947 by the Los Angeles singer Rabon Tarrant with Jack McVea's band. Walter cut it in Chicago for Checker, the Chess brothers' subsidiary, in the run of hits that followed 'Juke' and made him, briefly, a bigger seller than his former boss Muddy Waters. His band then included the guitarists Louis and Dave Myers and the drummer Fred Below, the core of the South Side's tightest ensemble.","The subject is a man telling a lover he is leaving because she has made his blues feel real, but the real story is the space between vocal lines. Walter leaves long gaps in the singing and fills them with phrases that behave like a second voice, finishing his own sentences. Listen for the moments he holds a bent note while the band moves underneath it, turning one pitch from sweet to sour without touching it.","It sold well on the rhythm-and-blues charts and then became a harmonica player's rite of passage. Paul Butterfield put it on his first album in 1965, and it has been recorded by countless British and American blues-rock bands since, usually faster and louder. Historians now rank Walter's slow blues alongside his instrumentals as the core of his legacy, the evidence that amplified harp could carry emotional weight as well as drive.","Listen once for the voice and once for the harmonica alone, treating them as two singers. Notice how quiet the band stays. Then play 'Juke' for Walter at full speed, and Junior Wells's Hoodoo Man Blues for a pupil who took the same sound into the 1960s."],"muhal-richard-abrams-young-at-heart-wise-in-time":["Two long pieces, one per side of the original LP. 'Young at Heart' is Abrams alone at the piano for nearly half an hour, moving through stride, blues, church chords, dense clusters and passages of free time as if walking through the whole history of the instrument. 'Wise in Time' is a quintet performance in which trumpet and alto saxophone trade long, open improvisations over a rhythm section that stretches and compresses the pulse rather than keeping strict time.","Abrams had founded the Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians on Chicago's South Side in 1965, a co-operative that taught, organised concerts and insisted on musicians owning their own work. Delmark, a small Chicago label, recorded several AACM debuts in these years. The quintet side drew on younger members, including Leo Smith on trumpet and Henry Threadgill on alto, who would become leaders in their own right, at a time when the city's jazz clubs offered almost no work for this music.","The solo side is the key to Abrams's whole outlook. He was self-taught, grew up playing blues and bebop in Chicago bars, and the piece keeps returning to those roots even at its most abstract: listen for a boogie figure in the left hand surfacing under a storm of clusters, then vanishing. The AACM's idea that tradition and experiment were one continuous field is argued here in sound rather than in manifesto.","Reviewed in the jazz press as part of the puzzling new Chicago wave, it sold modestly and was overshadowed by the Art Ensemble of Chicago's more theatrical records. Later listeners have come to it for Threadgill's and Smith's early playing and for the solo side, now heard as one of the first extended free-piano statements outside Cecil Taylor's orbit, and a far more rooted one.","Take the solo side in one sitting, without skipping, and notice each time a recognisable style surfaces from the abstraction. Then hear the quintet side for the way the horns leave space for each other. Compare Cecil Taylor's Unit Structures for a very different, European-leaning freedom."],"muhal-richard-abrams-blu-blu-blu":["A big-band album by the Muhal Richard Abrams Orchestra, a New York ensemble of brass, reeds, strings of the electric kind and percussion, playing Abrams's own compositions. The pieces swing hard in places and dissolve into floating, many-layered textures in others, with written sections that sound like improvisation and solos that fit into the writing like planned episodes. The title track takes the plainest material in jazz, a blues, and bends it through dense, shifting orchestration without losing its grit.","By the late 1980s Abrams had lived in New York for over a decade, and his orchestra had become one of the few working large ensembles in creative jazz, drawing on players from the AACM diaspora and the downtown scene. Black Saint, an Italian label, was then the main home for this music, which American majors rarely recorded. The album came as the jazz mainstream was turning toward neoclassical repertory bands, making an original big-band book something of a counter-statement.","Abrams writes for the orchestra as a composer of concert music would, thinking in blocks of colour rather than in the usual trumpet, trombone and saxophone sections. Listen for passages where a melody passes through three or four instruments of different families within a single phrase, and for moments where the soloist and the written parts overlap so closely you cannot hear where one ends. That blurring was the point.","It was voted among the best records of its year in international critics' polls and is usually named as the finest of Abrams's orchestral albums. Some reviewers found it too dense to take in; others heard it as proof that the large jazz ensemble had a future beyond Ellington and Basie repertory. It has grown in standing as Abrams's reputation as a composer, rather than simply an organiser, has risen.","Start with the title track and listen for the blues underneath the elaborate writing, the way you might hear a folk tune under a symphony. Then play Ellington or Mingus for comparison, and Henry Threadgill's own ensembles for a pupil who went in a related direction."],"odetta-at-the-gate-of-horn":["A programme of work songs, spirituals, prison songs and ballads, sung by Odetta with her own strummed guitar and a string bass. The voice is the whole event: a trained, enormous contralto that can drop into a growl or open into operatic power, driven by a hard, percussive guitar stroke that sounds like a hammer on rail. Songs such as 'Take This Hammer' and 'He's Got the Whole World in His Hands' become rhythmic, almost physical performances rather than gentle folk settings.","The Gate of Horn was a Chicago folk club run by Albert Grossman, who became Odetta's manager and would later manage Bob Dylan and Peter, Paul and Mary. Tradition Records, the small New York label founded by Patrick Clancy of the Clancy Brothers, issued the album as the folk revival gathered pace. Odetta had trained in classical voice and musical theatre in Los Angeles before turning to folk, and she brought that training into songs born in fields and prison camps.","Listen to the guitar as a rhythm instrument. Odetta rarely picks melodies; she strikes the strings in a steady, heavy pulse that imitates the swing of an axe or hammer on the work songs, so the accompaniment itself carries the memory of labour. Her phrasing often pushes against that beat, which is where the tension comes from. The classical training shows in breath control on long held notes rather than in any polish.","It confirmed her as the most commanding voice of the young revival, and it was heard by a generation of musicians who later cited her: Dylan, Joan Baez, Janis Joplin and others. Some purists thought her too theatrical for traditional material; most listeners now hear exactly that dramatic force as her contribution, turning songs of Black labour and suffering into statements of dignity on the eve of the civil-rights movement.","Begin with a work song and feel the guitar's hammer stroke before the voice enters. Then compare Lead Belly's own recordings of the same kind of material, which she learned from. Play Odetta Sings Ballads and Blues next, and then early Joan Baez to hear who was listening."],"odetta-sings-dylan":["An album of Bob Dylan songs, among them 'Masters of War', 'Mr. Tambourine Man', 'Tomorrow Is a Long Time', 'Long Ago, Far Away' and 'Baby, I'm in the Mood for You', sung by Odetta with spare acoustic backing. Where Dylan's own versions are nasal, fast and conversational, hers are slow, weighty and sung out in full voice. The protest songs become grave, almost like spirituals, and the love songs acquire a patience they did not have in the writer's hands.","By 1965 the influence had reversed. Dylan had said that hearing an Odetta record in a Minnesota shop in the late 1950s led him to trade his electric guitar for an acoustic one; now the young songwriter was the revival's star, and Odetta, recording for RCA Victor, devoted a whole album to him. It was one of the first full-length records of anyone's Dylan covers, made as he was going electric and the folk scene was splitting over it.","Some of the songs were then barely known; several had not appeared on Dylan's own albums at all and circulated through publishing demos or other singers. 'Tomorrow Is a Long Time' is the one to watch: Elvis Presley recorded it in 1966, probably having learned it from this album, and Dylan later spoke warmly of Presley's version. A song passed from a white Minnesotan to a Black folk singer to the king of rock and roll.","It was well reviewed but overshadowed by the Byrds' electric Dylan covers of the same year, which pointed where pop was going. In time it has been reassessed as a reading that took Dylan seriously as a writer of songs for other voices, a view that has become standard. The slow 'Masters of War' in particular is often preferred by listeners who find the original too brittle.","Put Odetta's 'Masters of War' next to Dylan's from The Freewheelin' Bob Dylan and notice what slowing down does to anger. Then hear her 'Mr. Tambourine Man' against the Byrds' jangling single. Finish with Elvis's 'Tomorrow Is a Long Time' to follow one song across three traditions."],"george-jones-she-thinks-i-still-care":["A slow country ballad of under three minutes. The narrator insists, point by point, that he is over the woman who left him, and each denial is so specific that it gives him away. George Jones sings it in his close, controlled baritone, bending the ends of words and sliding between notes, with steel guitar and a soft vocal chorus behind him. The recognisable thing is the catch in his voice on the title phrase, restrained where a lesser singer would wail.","Written by Dickey Lee and Steve Duffy and produced by Jones's longtime mentor Pappy Daily, it was his first single for United Artists after years on Starday and Mercury, and it became a number one country hit in 1962. Nashville was moving toward the smoother Nashville Sound of strings and choirs, and Jones sat between that polish and the honky-tonk grit he had learned from Hank Williams and Lefty Frizzell.","The lyric is built on irony: every piece of evidence the man offers for not caring, from asking friends about her to lingering by her house, is the very proof that he does. Jones plays it straight, never winking, which is why it cuts. Listen to how he lands slightly behind the beat on the denials, as if he has to force each one out, and then relaxes when he finally names the feeling.","It is widely counted among his very greatest performances and among the defining country ballads of the 1960s. It was covered by many singers, including Elvis Presley and Anne Murray, whose version crossed into pop. Critics often point to it as the moment Jones's mature ballad style arrived fully formed, the style that would peak with He Stopped Loving Her Today eighteen years later.","Listen to it twice, once for the words and once for the voice alone, catching every slide and swallowed syllable. Then play Hank Williams's 'Your Cheatin' Heart' for where it comes from, and Patsy Cline's 'She's Got You', from the same year, for its closest cousin."],"ike-turner-a-fool-in-love":["The first Ike and Tina Turner record, under three minutes: a heavy, rolling rhythm-and-blues groove with a churchy call-and-response, backing singers answering the lead, and a vocal that sounds nothing like the smooth girl singers then on the radio. Tina Turner shouts, rasps and pleads about loving a man who treats her badly, while Ike's band keeps a thick, slow-rocking shuffle underneath. The rawness of the lead voice is what makes it instantly recognisable.","Ike Turner wrote the song for a male singer in his Kings of Rhythm revue in St. Louis; when that singer failed to show for the session, the young Anna Mae Bullock sang the guide vocal. Juggy Murray of Sue Records in New York heard it and wanted her voice, not a replacement. Ike renamed her Tina and launched the Ike and Tina Turner Revue around the record, which became a big rhythm-and-blues hit and crossed onto the pop chart.","Listen to how gospel it is. The call-and-response between Tina and the backing singers is lifted straight from the Black church, the same move Ray Charles had made a few years earlier, and it anticipates the soul music of the mid-1960s. The song's subject, loving someone who hurts you, reads darkly now that the couple's later history of abuse is public knowledge, and many listeners cannot hear it innocently.","It was a hit and a launch: critics now treat it as one of the records that pushed rhythm and blues toward soul, and as the arrival of one of the great voices in popular music. Ike Turner's role as writer, arranger and bandleader is acknowledged, but his reputation has rightly been shaped by Tina's account of the violence of their marriage, and the record now carries both histories.","Listen for the first entry of Tina's voice and compare it with any girl-group single of 1960. Then play 'Rocket 88' to hear Ike's band a decade earlier, and 'River Deep – Mountain High' for what Phil Spector made of the same voice. Otis Redding's Otis Blue shows where the style went."],"ike-turner-proud-mary":["Ike and Tina Turner's version of John Fogerty's Creedence Clearwater Revival song, a five-minute record in two halves. It opens with a slow, spoken introduction from Tina promising to start easy and then get rough, followed by a lazy, almost gospel-harmony verse sung low with Ike's bass voice. Then the band doubles the tempo and the whole thing explodes into a hard soul-rock stomp, with horns, the Ikettes, and Tina singing at full, rasping power about a riverboat rolling on.","Creedence had released the song in 1969 as a swampy rock single. By then the Ike and Tina Turner Revue had opened for the Rolling Stones on their 1969 American tour and was chasing a rock audience, covering songs by the Beatles, Sly Stone and Creedence in their live show. The studio version, released on Liberty in early 1971 and on the album Workin' Together, became their biggest American hit and won a Grammy for best rhythm-and-blues group vocal performance.","The slow-then-fast arrangement was the revue's stage routine, worked out in front of audiences before it was ever recorded, which is why the tempo switch feels like a show rather than a studio trick. Listen for Ike's deep voice echoing Tina in the slow section, one of the few places he is audible as a singer. The spoken introduction was, in effect, a statement of the band's whole identity.","It was an instant hit and became Tina Turner's signature, performed for the rest of her career long after she left Ike. Critics often prefer it to the Creedence original, a rare case of a cover eclipsing a famous source. Its reception today is coloured by knowledge of Ike's abuse, and it is usually heard as Tina's triumph more than his, though the arrangement was the band's.","Listen for the exact moment the tempo breaks, and notice how the band holds back until then. Play the Creedence original first to hear what was changed. Then try 'River Deep – Mountain High' and James Brown's 'Sex Machine' for the same years' other ideas of rough."],"james-cleveland-the-love-of-god":["A slow gospel song recorded by the Voices of Tabernacle, a Detroit church choir, with James Cleveland as lead singer and director. The choir sings the refrain in full, warm block harmony while Cleveland's rough, gravelly voice rises over it, testifying and stretching lines far beyond their written length. Piano and organ keep a steady, rocking pulse. What you recognise first is the contrast between the massed, smooth choir and the torn, preacher-like voice in front of it.","Cleveland had grown up in Chicago under the influence of Thomas A. Dorsey and Roberta Martin and was working in Detroit at Prayer Tabernacle, a church in the same city where C.L. Franklin preached and his daughter Aretha was learning gospel piano, partly from Cleveland himself. The record, issued on a small gospel label, became a hit in Black church circles and made Cleveland a star, at the moment when the gospel quartet was giving way to the mass choir.","The key is the pacing. Cleveland lets the choir establish the song plainly before he enters, then gradually pulls the performance into a sermon, repeating a phrase, slowing it, pushing it higher each time. Listen for the choir holding one chord while he improvises over it: the technique of the extended vamp that he would use for the rest of his career, and that soul singers took into secular music.","It sold strongly in the gospel market and established the template for Cleveland's choir records of the 1960s. Historians of gospel treat it as a turning point from the small-group era of the Soul Stirrers and Swan Silvertones to the choir-centred sound that dominates Black church music to this day. It is less known outside gospel than 'Peace Be Still', but insiders often name it as the start.","Wait for Cleveland's first entry and notice the texture change. Listen to how long the ending runs compared with the opening. Then play 'Peace Be Still' for the style at its peak, and Aretha Franklin's Amazing Grace, which he led, to hear his pupil in full flight."],"james-cleveland-i-dont-feel-noways-tired":["A long, slow-building gospel performance by James Cleveland with the Salem Inspirational Choir. The song, a statement that the believer has come too far to give up, begins almost quietly, with Cleveland half-speaking over piano and organ, and builds over several minutes as the choir enters, repeats, and rises in volume and pitch. By the end the choir is singing at full force while he shouts over it. The recognisable gesture is the patient build toward a climax.","Written by Curtis Burrell, it was recorded for Savoy, the label that had issued Cleveland's records for nearly twenty years. By the late 1970s Cleveland was gospel's elder statesman: founder of the Gospel Music Workshop of America, pastor of his own church in Los Angeles, and a mentor to hundreds of choir directors. Gospel was absorbing the rhythm sections and harmonies of contemporary soul, and a younger generation such as Andraé Crouch was pulling it toward pop.","Listen to the band. Beneath the traditional structure, the bass and drums are playing a 1970s soul feel, and the chords under the choir are richer than those of Cleveland's 1960s records. The old testifying style and the new rhythm section coexist, which is why the record spoke to two generations of churchgoers. The final minutes are close to improvised, the choir following his hand signals as much as a written arrangement.","It became one of the most sung songs in Black churches in America and a standard in the choir repertory, performed at countless services and funerals. Gospel historians usually treat it, with 'Peace Be Still', as Cleveland's defining record, and choir directors still teach its long build as a model. Outside gospel it remains little known, a reminder of how separate that market was.","Resist skipping ahead: the point is the length of the build. Notice when the choir first enters and how many times the phrase repeats before the peak. Then play Edwin Hawkins's 'Oh Happy Day' for the pop-gospel alternative, and Mahalia Jackson for the solo tradition he grew from."],"carl-perkins-honey-dont":["A fast, loose rockabilly song under three minutes, in which the singer complains playfully that his girl keeps saying yes and then no. Perkins sings in a relaxed Tennessee drawl over slapped upright bass and a skipping beat, and his electric guitar takes a trebly, twangy break that owes as much to country picking as to the blues. The song's hook is the chord change after the opening line, an unexpected slip that gives it a lopsided, grinning feel.","It was the flip side of 'Blue Suede Shoes', released by Sam Phillips's Sun Records in Memphis at the start of 1956, when Perkins, a sharecropper's son from west Tennessee, briefly looked set to rival Elvis Presley. Sun's small studio, slapback echo and a band built around Perkins and his brothers produced a sound halfway between hillbilly and rhythm and blues. A car crash in March 1956 halted his momentum just as the A-side was climbing the charts.","The odd chord move at the start, jumping somewhere a country song would not go, is what made it stick in young British ears. The Beatles played it on stage and Ringo Starr sang it on Beatles for Sale in 1964; it had been in their stage act since the Hamburg and Cavern years. Listen to Perkins's guitar fill after each line: short, bright, and almost exactly the kind of lick George Harrison would build a style on.","At the time it was simply a B-side, overshadowed by one of the biggest records of the decade. Its reputation grew through the Beatles, and rock historians now cite it as a model of the rockabilly song: casual, witty, rhythmically clever. Perkins is often described as the purest of the Sun rockabillies, and records like this, where he was not trying to be a star, are the evidence.","Play it straight after 'Blue Suede Shoes' and notice how much looser it is. Listen for the guitar break and the slapped bass. Then hear the Beatles' cover to see what they kept, and Elvis's 'That's All Right' for the Sun sound a year earlier."],"carl-perkins-matchbox":["A two-minute rockabilly reworking of an old blues, with the singer as a drifter sitting on his suitcase, broke and lovesick. The guitar riff that opens it, a low, stuttering boogie figure, is the thing you remember; after it comes Perkins's easy vocal, a rolling piano, and a short, stinging guitar solo. It moves at a medium, loping tempo rather than a sprint, closer to a blues shuffle than to the frantic rockabilly of the day.","Recorded at Sun in Memphis on 4 December 1956, the same day Elvis Presley dropped by and the impromptu Million Dollar Quartet jam followed. The piano player that day was a newly hired session man, Jerry Lee Lewis. Perkins had learned the song's idea from his father, and it grows from Blind Lemon Jefferson's 'Match Box Blues' of 1927, a verse that had drifted through southern music for decades, but his version reorganised it around a riff.","Its lineage is a small history of American song: a Texas street singer's blues from the 1920s, filtered through white country tradition, turned into a Memphis rockabilly single, then taken up by the Beatles, who released it with Ringo singing in 1964. Listen for Jerry Lee Lewis's piano behind the vocal, still restrained, a few months before he became a star in his own right with 'Whole Lotta Shakin' Goin' On'.","It was not a big hit, and Perkins's career never regained the heights of 'Blue Suede Shoes'. Musicians, though, treated it as a standard: the riff became a staple of rock-and-roll bands on both sides of the Atlantic. Critics now often choose it as the best illustration of how rockabilly was built from the blues, with the debt visible and the transformation audible.","Play Blind Lemon Jefferson's 'Match Box Blues' first, then this, and listen for which lines survived. Notice the riff and the piano. Then hear the Beatles' version, sung by Ringo Starr, to see how little they changed, and Jerry Lee Lewis's own records of the following year to hear that quiet session pianist let loose."],"loretta-lynn-dont-come-home-a-drinkin":["A brisk honky-tonk song of just over two minutes in which a wife tells her husband that if he comes home drunk expecting affection, he can forget it. Loretta Lynn sings in her bright, nasal Kentucky voice with steel guitar and a shuffling Nashville band, and she sounds more exasperated than wounded. The recognisable thing is the tone: plain speech, a hard downbeat on the key words, and the sense of someone who has had this argument many times.","She wrote it with her sister Peggy Sue Wells, drawing on her own marriage, and it was produced by Owen Bradley for Decca. It became her first number one country hit around the start of 1967. Country music at the time was full of songs about men drinking and cheating, usually told from the man's side; women's records mostly featured the patient, forgiving wife or the other woman.","The song flips a whole genre around. The honky-tonk drinking song belonged to men from Hank Williams to George Jones, and here the same shuffle and steel guitar are used to tell the man to stay out of the bedroom. Listen to how cheerful the band sounds: the arrangement is standard Nashville, which made the words go down on country radio when a more pointed sound might not have.","It established Lynn as the voice of working-class wives, and it opened the path to her later, more controversial records such as 'Fist City' and 'The Pill'. Feminist critics came to see her as a writer who made demands without the language of the women's movement, speaking from inside rural marriage rather than outside it. Some country stations were uneasy with her subjects, but she kept scoring hits.","Listen for the contrast between the jaunty band and the flat refusal in the words. Then play George Jones or Hank Williams for the male drinking songs she was answering, Tammy Wynette's 'Stand by Your Man' for the opposite argument, and 'The Pill' for where she went next."],"victor-jara-plegaria-a-un-labrador":["A short song built like a prayer that turns into a summons. Víctor Jara sings over quiet guitar with the vocal group Quilapayún behind him, their Andean flutes and charango giving the sound its colour. It begins low and almost private, addressed to a farm labourer as though to a saint, then the tempo quickens, the chorus enters in full voice and the prayer becomes an appeal to stand up, join hands and take the land's future into his own keeping. Three minutes, one long crescendo.","Jara wrote it in 1969, the year Chile's left was gathering behind Salvador Allende and land reform had become the country's central quarrel. He performed it at the first Festival de la Nueva Canción Chilena in Santiago that July, where it shared the top prize, and it appeared on his album Pongo en tus manos abiertas on DICAP, the label founded by the Communist Youth to record the movement. Jara was then also a respected theatre director, and the song's staging instinct shows.","The words borrow the cadence and some of the shape of the Lord's Prayer, the one text every Chilean peasant of the day would have known by heart, and bend it from asking heaven for bread towards asking the labourer himself to act. Listen to how the guitar keeps the same rocking figure throughout while only the voices change: the whole transformation is carried by breath, volume and speed, not by new harmony.","At the festival some on the Catholic right heard blasphemy, while many on the left heard exactly the fusion of Christian feeling and social demand that liberation theology was preaching in the same years. After Jara's murder in the Estadio Chile in September 1973, the song took on a martyr's weight it was never written to carry. Critics now tend to hear it less as slogan than as a carefully built piece of musical theatre.","Listen once only for the dynamics: mark the moment the chorus comes in and the moment the pulse quickens, and notice how little else changes. Then play Te recuerdo Amanda, from the same year, to hear the tender, private side of the same singer. Violeta Parra's late songs show where this idiom came from."],"victor-jara-el-derecho-de-vivir-en-paz":["The title song of Jara's 1971 album, a tribute to Ho Chi Minh and to the people of Vietnam under American bombing. It opens in the familiar nueva canción manner, voice and guitar, but soon an electric band slides in underneath with bass, organ and a rock drumbeat, and the song swells into something close to a hymn. The refrain claims a simple right, to live in peace, and returns each time with more weight behind it, as if the band were carrying the words forward.","By 1971 Allende's Popular Unity government was in power and Jara was one of its best-known cultural voices, recording for the DICAP label. The electric backing came from Los Blops, a young Santiago rock group, and the choice was pointed: in Chile, as elsewhere, some on the left distrusted rock as an American import. Jara used it anyway to salute a country fighting the United States, turning the imported sound against its source.","Ho Chi Minh had died in 1969 and the song treats him less as a political leader than as a poet and a gardener of his people, an image drawn from Latin American devotional song. Listen for the organ: it is the instrument that tilts the arrangement from rock towards church, which is where Jara's music always seemed to want to go. The album around it also mixes traditional forms with sharper, satirical pieces.","The song was admired at home but largely unknown abroad until long after Jara's death in 1973. In October 2019, during the mass protests in Chile, dozens of Chilean musicians recorded a new collective version and crowds sang it in the streets, which turned a Cold War solidarity song into a demand about Chile itself. Some listeners still find the Ho Chi Minh tribute awkward; the refrain has outgrown it.","Start by listening for the entry of the electric band and notice how it changes the song's mood without changing its tune. Then compare the 2019 collective recording to hear how a crowd reclaimed it. Pair it with Pete Seeger's Waist Deep in the Big Muddy for the North American view of the same war."],"freddie-king-the-stumble":["A two-and-a-half-minute guitar instrumental that became a set piece for every blues guitarist who came after. Freddie King plays a skipping, stop-start melody full of quick turns and sudden halts, as if the tune keeps tripping and catching itself, which is the joke in the title. Between the head statements he takes stinging single-note breaks over a steady shuffle, with piano and a small rhythm section keeping everything tight and danceable. It is bright, brisk and precise rather than slow and mournful.","King recorded for Federal, a subsidiary of Syd Nathan's King Records in Cincinnati, where pianist and bandleader Sonny Thompson ran his sessions. After the success of Hide Away in 1961, the label wanted more instrumentals that could sell to dance crowds and jukeboxes, and King cut a run of them in a short span. The Stumble was one of the best, issued as a single and gathered onto his all-instrumental album of that year.","King played with a plastic thumb pick and a metal pick on his index finger, a country-picking habit learned from Texas, and that is why his notes snap so hard and his fast passages stay clean. Listen to the breaks where the band stops dead: the tune depends on silence as much as on notes, and the timing of the restart is where the real skill lies.","It was never a big hit in the United States, but in Britain it became a rite of passage. Peter Green recorded a celebrated version with John Mayall's Bluesbreakers in 1967, and Eric Clapton, Mick Taylor and Stevie Ray Vaughan all learned from King's instrumentals. Historians now rank King beside B.B. and Albert King as one of the three models of modern electric blues guitar.","Count the stops. Each time the band halts, listen to how King re-enters exactly on the beat. Then play Hide Away to hear his first instrumental hit, and Albert King's Born Under a Bad Sign to compare a slower, bent-note style. Any good compilation of the Federal singles will do."],"gene-vincent-race-with-the-devil":["A two-minute sprint that is really a showcase for Cliff Gallup, the Blue Caps' lead guitarist. Gene Vincent sings about being chased by the devil and running flat out to stay ahead, his voice breathy and urgent, while the slap bass and drums drive a relentless beat. Then Gallup takes his solos: dense runs of notes, hammered and pulled, with a slapback echo that makes the guitar sound as if it is in two places at once. The whole thing feels like it might come off the road.","After Be-Bop-A-Lula made Vincent a star in 1956, Capitol hurried out follow-ups. The band recorded in Nashville for producer Ken Nelson, at the studio run by Owen Bradley, with local session men nearby but the Blue Caps themselves playing. Capitol had signed Vincent partly as its answer to Elvis Presley at RCA, and wanted another hit fast. This one was a modest seller in the United States but made a large impression overseas.","Gallup, a quiet, older musician who disliked touring, left the band before the end of 1956, so his playing survives only on a small number of records made within a few months. Listen for the way he mixes country finger-picking patterns with jazz-like chromatic runs, and for his use of the guitar's vibrato arm, which was unusual at the time. Those few sides became a private curriculum for later guitarists.","American critics long treated Vincent as a lesser Presley, but in Britain and France he was a hero, and the record's reputation grew with every generation of guitarists. Jeff Beck recorded a whole album of Gallup's parts in 1993 as a tribute, and Brian Setzer of the Stray Cats built a career on this sound. Today it is judged one of the purest recordings rockabilly produced.","Ignore the vocal on the first listen and follow only the guitar. Notice the echo on it, and count how many notes Gallup fits into each solo. Then play Carl Perkins's Blue Suede Shoes for a Memphis comparison and Eddie Cochran's Summertime Blues for where this energy went next."],"gene-vincent-bluejean-bop":["A short, springy rockabilly number about dancing in blue jeans, which in 1956 meant dancing like a teenager rather than like one's parents. Vincent's voice is soft and close to the microphone, almost whispered in places, then breaks into yelps. The Blue Caps play with a lighter touch than on the faster sides: slap bass, brushed and then driving drums, and neat, jazz-inflected guitar fills from Cliff Gallup answering each vocal line. It is both cool and nervous at once.","It was released in 1956 as a single and as the title song of Vincent's first album on Capitol. He was only twenty-one, walking with a painful limp after a motorcycle accident during his navy service, and the leather-clad, wounded image that followed made him a symbol of the unrespectable side of rock and roll. The song belongs to the moment when record companies discovered that teenagers, and their clothes, could be the subject of a song.","The drummer, Dickie Harrell, was a teenager and famous for screaming on the records; listen for the shouts buried in the mix, which were part of the band's live energy caught on tape. The album around the single also includes Vincent's slowed, crooning versions of older pop standards, a reminder that he admired smooth singers as much as wild ones and was never simply a shouter.","The single was a small hit in the United States but did well in Britain, where Vincent later lived and toured and where the Beatles and many other young musicians heard him as a model. The album is now considered one of the essential early rock and roll LPs, although for years it was hard to find in its original form. Its reputation rests as much on Gallup's playing as on Vincent's singing.","Listen for the contrast between Vincent's whispery phrases and the sudden shouts in the band. Then play Race with the Devil to hear the same group at full speed, and Buddy Holly's That'll Be the Day for a different, sunnier take on the same teenage world. An original mono mix sounds best."],"jerry-lee-lewis-live-at-the-star-club":["A live set recorded in a Hamburg club, and possibly the most violent-sounding rock and roll record of its decade. Jerry Lee Lewis pounds the piano and roars through a dozen or so songs almost without pause: his own hits, Chuck Berry, Ray Charles's What'd I Say, Little Richard, and a hard-driving country ballad or two. The British backing band, the Nashville Teens, strains to keep up. Tempos are fast, the crowd is loud, and the recording level often overloads into distortion.","Lewis's American career had collapsed in 1958 after the press revealed that he had married his thirteen-year-old cousin, and by 1964 he was playing small venues at home. In Europe he was still a star. The Star-Club was the same Hamburg venue where the Beatles had learned their trade, and in April 1964 Philips recorded him there. The album was issued in Europe and not properly available in the United States for many years.","The piano is doing the work of a whole rhythm section: listen to his left hand, which plays a pounding boogie bass throughout, and his right, which alternates chords, glissandos and hammered repeated notes. On several songs he changes tempo or key on the fly and the band audibly scrambles to follow. The distortion is not a flaw fixed in mastering; it is the sound of a small room at the edge of chaos.","For years it circulated as a collector's legend. When it became widely available, critics called it one of the greatest live albums ever made, and it regularly appears on such lists. Some hear in it a performer taking out his exile on the keyboard; others simply hear the most complete document of rock and roll as a physical act. Few dispute that it outclasses his studio records of the period.","Play it loud and start with Mean Woman Blues, then jump to Great Balls of Fire and compare it with the 1957 single. Listen to his left hand and notice it never lets up. Afterwards, try the Stooges' Fun House, a later live-in-the-studio record with the same appetite for overload."],"mercedes-sosa-en-argentina":["A double live album recorded at the Teatro Ópera in Buenos Aires, the concerts that marked Mercedes Sosa's return from exile. Her voice, deep and unhurried, moves through Argentine folk forms such as the zamba and the chacarera, songs by Latin American writers, and newer songs by the country's young rock and folk musicians. The accompaniment is modest, guitars and the bombo drum at the centre, and the audience is part of the sound: applause, singing along, long ovations.","Sosa had been arrested on stage in La Plata in 1979, along with her audience, and then left Argentina for Paris and Madrid. The military junta was weakening by early 1982, and she was allowed to come back and sing in February. The concerts sold out night after night. Within weeks the regime launched the Falklands war, and within two years it had fallen. The album became a document of that thaw.","The guest list is the quiet argument. By inviting younger musicians from the rock scene, notably León Gieco and Charly García, Sosa joined the folk tradition she represented to the generation that had grown up under the dictatorship. Listen to how she changes her phrasing on their songs, singing more plainly, and to the crowd's response when songs that had been banned or unplayable are heard in public again.","It was received at once as a national event rather than as a record, and it remains one of the best-selling and most cherished albums in Argentine music. Critics have praised how it avoids slogans and lets the choice of songs speak. Some purists regretted the pop and rock material; most now see that mixture as the point, and as the start of her later role as a bridge between generations.","Treat it as a concert and listen straight through the first side to feel the room warm up. Notice the audience singing and the silences before certain songs. Then compare Gracias a la vida, recorded a decade earlier, to hear how the voice darkened. Miriam Makeba's Soweto Blues offers a parallel from another exile."],"mighty-sparrow-dan-is-the-man-in-the-van":["A comic calypso that is really an attack on colonial schooling. The Mighty Sparrow lists the stories and nursery rhymes he was made to learn as a boy in Trinidad, one absurd example after another, and concludes that such an education was designed to keep a child foolish rather than make him think. The band plays a bright, bouncing arrangement with horns punctuating each verse, and Sparrow's delivery is sly and conversational, pausing to let each ridiculous example land.","Trinidad and Tobago became independent in August 1962, and Sparrow, already the most popular calypsonian in the Caribbean, turned his wit on what the empire had left behind. The target was the readers used in West Indian schools for decades, compiled in Britain and filled with English children, English weather and nonsense verse. The song came out at the height of the debate over what a newly independent nation should teach its children.","Calypso was always a form of news and commentary, and the humour here hides an argument that later scholars of colonial education made at length: that the most lasting power of empire was in the schoolbooks. Listen for the way Sparrow imitates a child reciting by rote, flattening his voice, before switching back to his own sharp adult tone for the punchline of each verse.","It was an immediate favourite in Trinidad and across the region, and it has stayed on school syllabuses, which Sparrow might have enjoyed as an irony. Literary critics have treated it as a text of postcolonial thought alongside the essays of the period. Some listeners outside the Caribbean hear only a novelty song; within it, the song is regarded as one of the sharpest things Sparrow wrote.","Listen first for the rhythm of the storytelling: each verse sets up a school example and the refrain delivers the verdict. Then play Jean and Dinah for Sparrow's earlier social satire, and Chuck Berry's School Days for an American song about the classroom that argues something very different."],"mighty-sparrow-federation":["A political calypso about the collapse of the West Indies Federation. Over a bouncing band arrangement with brass answering his lines, Sparrow tells the story as a family quarrel: Jamaica walks out, the smaller islands look to Trinidad to pay the bills, and Trinidad decides it will not be left holding the purse. His tone mixes mockery, grievance and resignation, and the chorus sums up the end of a dream in a few plain words.","The Federation, formed in 1958, was meant to lead ten British Caribbean colonies to independence as one nation. In September 1961 Jamaicans voted in a referendum to leave, and Trinidad's premier Eric Williams then withdrew as well, ending it in 1962. Calypsonians were the island's editorial writers, and Sparrow, whose songs were heard by nearly everyone in Trinidad, gave the popular reaction its voice just as Carnival season arrived.","Behind the jokes lies a real division: Sparrow takes Trinidad's side and is rough on Jamaica, and some verses sound less generous today than they did in 1962. Listen for how he frames the islands as relatives, a calypso habit that lets him say sharp things with a wink. The song is also a reminder that calypso was a regional industry, and Sparrow's own career depended on audiences across all ten islands.","It was hugely popular in Trinidad and much discussed elsewhere in the Caribbean, where not everyone enjoyed Sparrow's view. Historians of the Federation often cite it as evidence of how ordinary Trinidadians felt about the break-up. It has lasted less as a sing-along than as a historical document, and as proof that a dance-band calypso could carry a whole political argument.","Follow the story as you would a newspaper cartoon: each verse introduces a character, and the refrain delivers the moral. Then play Dan Is the Man in the Van from the following year to hear Sparrow turn the same satirical eye on colonial schooling. Lord Kitchener's London Is the Place for Me gives the view from the emigrant's side."],"albert-ayler-spirits-rejoice":["A short album that sounds like a brass band marching through a revival meeting. Albert Ayler's tenor saxophone, huge and trembling with vibrato, leads simple, anthem-like tunes, and his brother Donald's trumpet and Charles Tyler's alto join in raw unison before everyone breaks into collective shouting. Two bassists, Gary Peacock and Henry Grimes, rumble beneath, and Sunny Murray's drums shimmer rather than keep time. One track, Angels, is a quiet duet for saxophone and harpsichord, a surprise in the middle of the storm.","Recorded in Judson Hall in New York in September 1965 for ESP-Disk, the small independent label that documented much of the new free jazz. Ayler had come back from Europe and was forming a band with his brother, moving away from the trio of Spiritual Unity towards a sound drawn from marches, hymns and folk tunes. The civil rights movement and Black churches were the air he breathed; the title is meant sincerely.","The title track opens with a fanfare that clearly borrows from La Marseillaise, and other melodies sound half-remembered from bugle calls and gospel songs. Ayler was deliberately returning to music older than bebop, to the brass bands of New Orleans and to the church. Listen for the moment when the tidy unison melody dissolves: that break from order into chaos and back is the shape of every piece here.","Many listeners in 1965 heard it as either parody or incompetence, and the marching tunes still divide people. Others, including John Coltrane, regarded Ayler as a prophet. Later critics have come to hear the folk-march material as a deliberate, even radical, return to roots, and the album is now often recommended as a friendlier way into Ayler than his earlier trio records.","Start with the title track and listen for the familiar French anthem at the opening, then for the moment the band erupts. Next play Angels, the harpsichord duet, to hear Ayler's tone up close. Compare Coltrane's Ascension, from the same year, then Charles Ives's Variations on America for a composer who also loved brass-band chaos."],"buddy-guy-a-man-and-the-blues":["Buddy Guy's first real album, made with a Chicago band and the great blues pianist Otis Spann. The title song is a slow, smouldering blues that shows off his range: whispered lines, sudden cries and guitar phrases that jump from quiet to piercing in a single bar. Around it are shuffles, an instrumental jam, a country blues played electrically, and a version of the nursery rhyme Mary Had a Little Lamb turned into a jump tune. The sound is live and loose.","Guy had spent most of the 1960s at Chess Records, largely as a session guitarist, where his wilder style was usually kept in check. Moving to Vanguard, a New York label that had built its name on folk music and the blues revival, gave him an album-length format and a new audience of young white listeners. By 1968 Jimi Hendrix and Eric Clapton were speaking openly of his influence, and this record let people hear why.","Listen to the dynamics on the title track: Guy drops almost to silence, then explodes, a trick he took from gospel preaching and that Hendrix borrowed from him. Otis Spann's piano fills every gap with rolling runs, and it is worth listening only to him for one song. Mary Had a Little Lamb was a comic Guy original built on the rhyme, and Stevie Ray Vaughan's 1983 cover made it famous again.","It was well received by the blues press and has remained the most admired of his early records. Some purists found Guy's volume and showmanship excessive, a criticism that followed him for decades; others hear in it the missing link between Chicago blues and the rock guitar of the late 1960s. It is now usually listed as essential alongside Junior Wells's Hoodoo Man Blues, on which Guy also played.","Begin with the title track and follow the volume: mark each drop and each explosion. Then listen to Otis Spann's piano behind him. Compare Junior Wells's Hoodoo Man Blues for Guy as sideman, and Hendrix's Are You Experienced for what young rock guitarists took from him. The original stereo release is fine."],"don-cherry-symphony-for-improvisers":["Two long suites, one per side, each stitching several short themes into a continuous flow. Don Cherry's pocket cornet sings bright, folk-like melodies, and the band follows him from one theme to the next without stopping: Gato Barbieri's tenor burns hot, Pharoah Sanders's tenor and piccolo scream and whistle, Karl Berger's vibraphone adds shimmer, and two basses with Ed Blackwell's drums keep everything in motion. The effect is of a group improvising together rather than taking turns.","Cherry had played with Ornette Coleman in the late 1950s and had since lived in Europe, where he met Barbieri from Argentina, Berger from Germany and the French bassist Jean-François Jenny-Clark. Recorded in New York in September 1966 for Blue Note, the album mixed those European friends with Americans, including Henry Grimes on bass and a young Pharoah Sanders fresh from Coltrane's band. It was an international free jazz band before the term existed.","The suites are built from short themes that return in different disguises, and part of the pleasure is spotting them. Listen for the way Cherry cues each change with a brief phrase on cornet, a signal the others pick up; the form is written as much as improvised. Two basses play at once, one often bowed, one plucked, doubling the texture beneath the horns.","It was admired by the jazz press and has become the best loved of Cherry's Blue Note albums. Some listeners find it more joyful and less abrasive than other free jazz of 1966, which is exactly why it is often recommended as an entry point. Critics now see it as the moment Cherry began turning free jazz into something global and melodic.","Put on the first side and simply follow Cherry's cornet as a guide through the themes; each time it plays a new tune the band follows. Then listen for Sanders's piccolo. Compare Complete Communion, recorded the year before, and Coltrane's Ascension for a heavier kind of collective improvisation."],"don-cherry-mu":["A duo between Don Cherry and the drummer Ed Blackwell, issued in two volumes. Cherry plays pocket trumpet, but also piano, bamboo flute, bells and his own voice, and the pieces move from jubilant horn melodies to quiet, meditative chants. Blackwell's drumming, rooted in New Orleans parade rhythms and West African patterns, is so melodic that the pair never sound like only two players. Tunes are short and song-like, often built on repeated phrases that feel closer to folk music than to bebop.","Recorded in Paris in August 1969 for BYG's Actuel series, which gathered American free jazz musicians in France that year. Cherry was living in Sweden with the artist Moki Cherry and had been travelling widely, studying music from India, Africa and Asia. Mu marks the turn from his New York free jazz towards the global, spiritual music he would pursue for the rest of his life.","Blackwell and Cherry had played together with Ornette Coleman a decade earlier, and their rapport shows in how they finish each other's phrases. Listen for the melodic tuning of Blackwell's drums and for the way he plays with his hands and sticks, suggesting talking drums. Cherry's flute and vocal pieces are not interruptions but the heart of the album, pointing towards his later work.","It was little noticed at first, as BYG's catalogue was poorly distributed. Over the decades it has become a cult classic, praised for its warmth and simplicity, and it is often cited as one of the first recordings of what would later be called world jazz. Some jazz critics found the chants naïve; many listeners now find them the most moving part.","Start with the first volume and focus on how two players can fill a room. Listen to Blackwell's drums as though they were a second melody instrument. Then compare Coltrane's Interstellar Space, another saxophone-and-drums duo, which is fiercer, and Pharoah Sanders's Karma for another spiritual jazz record from the same year."],"alice-coltrane-ptah-the-el-daoud":["Four long pieces for a quintet built around Alice Coltrane's piano and harp, with two tenor saxophonists, Pharoah Sanders and Joe Henderson, who also double on alto flute, over Ron Carter's bass and Ben Riley's drums. The title track is a slow-burning modal blues that lets both saxophonists stretch out; 'Turiya and Ramakrishna' is a gospel-tinged piano trio; 'Blue Nile' floats two flutes over rippling harp; 'Mantra' builds from quiet invocation into a storm. The sound you remember is the harp glissando washing under everything like water.","Recorded in January 1970 at the Coltrane family home in Dix Hills, Long Island, less than three years after John Coltrane's death. Alice was raising four children, deepening her study with the Hindu teacher Swami Satchidananda, and working out what a band of her own should sound like. Impulse! still had the prestige of the Coltrane name and let her record with top players. Ptah is the Egyptian creator god; 'El Daoud' means 'the beloved', and the Egyptian and Indian titles map her growing interest in sacred traditions beyond the church.","Sanders and Henderson are opposites, and the album is quietly built on that contrast: Sanders tears and overblows, Henderson winds long, fluid, logical lines. On the title track listen to how each solo takes the same modal ground somewhere different. 'Turiya and Ramakrishna', despite its Hindu title, is steeped in gospel and blues feeling rather than Indian music, a reminder that her Detroit church upbringing underlies even her most mystical records.","Reviewers in 1970 often filed Alice Coltrane under her husband's legacy, and some treated her harp as decoration rather than a voice. Later reassessment turned that around: this album is now heard as the moment she became a bandleader with her own grammar, calmer and more spacious than late John Coltrane. Many listeners rank it as the most approachable of her Impulse! records, the one where free intensity and serene devotion are held in balance.","Start with 'Blue Nile': harp, two flutes and bass, nothing else, and you will hear her idea of sacred sound in six minutes. Then play the title track and follow the handover between the two tenors. Compare it with Journey in Satchidananda, made a year later, where the drone and the Indian instruments come to the front. A good stereo remaster makes the harp's shimmer much clearer."],"badalamenti-mulholland-drive":["Angelo Badalamenti's score for David Lynch's film is mostly slow, dark synthesizer and string washes: long sustained chords that hang over Los Angeles like smog, a low drone that makes daylight scenes feel unsafe, and a yearning main theme that rises and never quite resolves. Around this sit pastiche period pop, a jazzy lounge cue, and the Club Silencio sequence, where Rebekah Del Rio sings 'Llorando', a Spanish version of Roy Orbison's 'Crying', unaccompanied until she collapses and the voice keeps going.","Mulholland Drive began as a television pilot for ABC in 1999; the network rejected it, and Lynch, with French money from Canal+, shot new material to turn it into a feature, released in 2001. Badalamenti had worked with Lynch since Blue Velvet in 1986 and Twin Peaks in 1990, and their method was conversational: Lynch described moods, Badalamenti improvised at the keyboard, and fragments were stretched, layered and reused. Some cues were made from slowed and extended Badalamenti material rather than written fresh for scenes.","Watch closely and you will see Badalamenti himself on screen: he plays Luigi Castigliane, the mobster who tastes an espresso with elaborate ceremony and spits it into a napkin. The Club Silencio scene is also a lesson in the whole score's method. The host announces that there is no band, that everything is a recording, and the film's music keeps asking the same question about what is real and what is playback.","The film divided audiences at first but won Lynch the best director award at Cannes in 2001, and it has since topped critics' polls of the century's best films. The score was praised for its restraint, though some critics hear it as Badalamenti refining the Twin Peaks palette rather than breaking new ground. That argument misses how structural it is: the drones carry the story's shift from dream to nightmare when the plot deliberately refuses to.","Listen first to the main title theme and notice how little moves: one sustained chord shifting a single note at a time. Then find the Club Silencio sequence, ideally watching the film, and hear how a song heard alone can wreck you. Compare the score with Twin Peaks, which is warmer and more melodic, and with Bernard Herrmann's Vertigo, the older Hollywood score about doubles and obsession it echoes."],"archie-shepp-the-magic-of-ju-ju":["The record is dominated by its title track, which fills the whole first side: about eighteen minutes of Archie Shepp's tenor saxophone roaring, crying and preaching over a bank of drums and hand percussion, with no piano to fix the harmony. The rhythm never lets up; it is closer to a ceremony than a tune. Side two is shorter and more familiar in shape, with the brass joining for blues-rooted, swaggering pieces, but the sound anyone remembers is that single horn riding a thunderstorm of drums.","Shepp made this for Impulse! in 1967, the year John Coltrane, his champion at the label, died. Black American musicians were looking to Africa for rhythm, spirituality and political identity, and the Black Arts movement treated free jazz as a cultural statement rather than entertainment. Shepp, a playwright and poet as well as a saxophonist, was among the most outspoken of them. The title's appeal to African ritual power is part of that argument about where the music's roots lie.","Listen to how the title track is organised: not by chord changes or a melody but by the percussion's shifting density. When the drums thin out, Shepp moves toward long cries; when they thicken, he breaks into fragments and honks. The horn and the drums are having a call-and-response conversation that echoes African drumming traditions more than any bebop model, and Shepp's rough, breathy tone quotes older swing tenor players even at his most ferocious.","At the time, some jazz critics heard the title track as monotonous, eighteen minutes on one idea; others heard it as the most complete expression of the African turn in the new jazz. Its reputation has grown steadily, helped by reissues and by DJs and younger players drawn to its trance-like rhythm. It is now often ranked with Fire Music as Shepp's strongest Impulse! record, less literary but more physically overwhelming.","Give the title track its full length without interruption, and stop listening for a tune: follow the drums and let Shepp's horn become one more percussion voice. Turn it up. Then play Fire Music to hear how differently he used the same anger two years earlier, and Pharoah Sanders's Karma for the calmer side of the same African and spiritual reach."],"merle-haggard-sing-me-back-home":["A slow, plain country waltz-feel ballad in which Haggard tells of a prisoner being led to his execution who asks a fellow inmate with a guitar to play him one last song, something that will take him back to his mother and his childhood. The arrangement is Bakersfield lean: a gently picked electric guitar, weeping steel, soft brushed rhythm and a high harmony voice in the chorus. Haggard sings it almost conversationally, and the restraint is the point; the song never raises its voice.","Haggard had served time in San Quentin from 1958 to 1960 and was in the audience when Johnny Cash performed at the prison at the end of the decade. By 1967 he was a rising Capitol Records star in the Bakersfield scene, with his band the Strangers and producer Ken Nelson. He drew the song from memories of a fellow inmate who was executed; it went to number one on the country chart early in 1968, one of the prison songs that made his reputation.","The narrator is not the condemned man but the witness, a detail that changes everything: the song is about survivors, and Haggard is telling us he was the one who lived. Notice also that the requested song is never named. Whatever tune the guitarist plays stays private, so the listener fills it with their own idea of home, which is precisely how the song works on a jukebox or a radio.","It was an immediate country hit and set the template for Haggard's image as the genuine ex-convict among country singers, an image he later said sat awkwardly with him. Critics regularly place it among his finest songs, alongside Mama Tried, for its compassion without sentimentality. It has been covered widely, from the Everly Brothers, who recorded it on their 1968 album Roots, to the Grateful Dead, which shows how far its plainness travelled beyond country.","Listen to the first verse only for Haggard's phrasing: how he lets words fall slightly behind the beat, like a man remembering. Then listen for the harmony voice entering in the chorus, which turns one man's request into a prison choir. Follow it with Mama Tried, a year later, and Johnny Cash At Folsom Prison, to hear two very different ways of singing from behind bars."],"everly-brothers-cathys-clown":["The song opens with no instrumental introduction at all: the two brothers' voices in close harmony, stating the chorus straight out, with Don taking the lower line and Phil the higher one. Then a heavy, thudding drum pattern kicks in and the verse drops to Don alone. The subject is a young man realising that his girlfriend has made him a figure of fun, and deciding he will not play the part any longer. At under two and a half minutes it is compact, dark and almost stern.","Written by Don and Phil Everly, it was their first single for Warner Bros., after they left Cadence Records in 1960 for what was reported as a record-breaking contract. Recorded in Nashville with the town's top session players, it became their biggest hit, reaching number one in both the United States and Britain. It arrived in a lull: Elvis was in the army, Buddy Holly was dead, and rock and roll's first wave was fading into softer pop.","That drum sound is not quite what it seems. The pounding pattern was reportedly built up in the studio using a tape loop of the drum part, an unusually mechanical trick for 1960 that gives the track its relentless weight. Listen too for the harmony itself: the two voices sometimes move in opposite directions rather than in parallel, one holding while the other slides, the move a generation of British bands later copied.","It was a huge seller and a turning point, proving the Everlys could thrive beyond the Cadence songwriting partnership of Felice and Boudleaux Bryant. Critics now see it as one of the bridges between 1950s rock and roll and 1960s British pop; Paul McCartney has spoken of the Everlys' harmony as a model for the early Beatles. Some fans still prefer the airier Cadence hits, but this record's gravity has worn better.","Listen to the opening seconds on headphones and try to separate the two voices: they are so closely matched that they seem to become a third one. Then notice how the drums change the mood from choirboy to something bleaker. Compare it with All I Have to Do Is Dream for the softer Everlys, and then the Beatles' early harmony singing or the Byrds to hear where the sound went."],"wanda-jackson-fujiyama-mama":["A hard-driving rockabilly boast in which the singer compares her own temper and power to a volcano and to the most destructive weapons of the age, warning any man who crosses her. Wanda Jackson attacks it with the growl and rasp that became her trademark, a sound no other woman in rock and roll was then using so openly. The band plays a stomping, riff-based shuffle with a guitar break, and the whole thing is over in little more than two minutes.","Jackson was a teenage country singer from Oklahoma who toured with Elvis Presley in 1955 and 1956; he urged her to try rock and roll, and she began recording it for Capitol alongside her country sides. The song was written by Jack Hammer and first recorded by the R&B singer Annisteen Allen in the mid-1950s. Jackson's version sold modestly at home but became a major hit in Japan, where she toured to large crowds in 1959.","The song's imagery refers, in a couple of lines, to the atomic destruction of Japanese cities in 1945, delivered as swagger rather than protest, which makes its popularity in Japan little more than a decade later all the more strange. Listen for how Jackson roughens individual words: she saves the growl for particular syllables rather than using it throughout, which makes it sound like temper breaking through rather than a vocal effect.","In the 1950s American radio largely ignored it, and a woman singing this aggressively was often treated as a novelty. The rockabilly revival of the 1970s and 1980s, and punk and garage musicians hunting for female forebears, rediscovered her; the record became central to her billing as the Queen of Rockabilly. Today its nuclear imagery makes it uncomfortable for some listeners, and it is often discussed as a document of how casually the bomb entered pop.","Listen first just to Jackson's voice: compare the sweetness of her country records with the rasp here, and you will hear a performer deciding to be dangerous. Then set it next to Let's Have a Party and Elvis's Hound Dog, and then to later women in punk, to hear the lineage. The original Capitol mono single has the most punch."],"wanda-jackson-right-or-wrong":["A slow country ballad, written by Jackson herself, in which a woman pledges to stand by the man she loves whatever he does and whatever anyone says about him. Everything that made Fujiyama Mama wild is set aside: her voice is smooth, controlled and aching, with only a small catch at the ends of phrases. The arrangement leans toward the polished Nashville pop-country of the day, with gentle backing voices, soft piano and a restrained steel guitar framing the melody.","By 1961 Jackson's rock and roll had finally charted in the United States, with Let's Have a Party, and Capitol and producer Ken Nelson steered her back toward country, where she had begun. Right or Wrong reached the country top ten and crossed into the pop chart, and its follow-up, In the Middle of a Heartache, did similar business. It was a period when Patsy Cline was proving that a woman's heartbreak ballad could top both markets.","Few listeners realise Jackson wrote this herself; female songwriters were rare on country hit lists in 1961, and her authorship is a large part of the song's authority. It also shows her two careers overlapping: listen to the bass line and you can hear a softened rock-and-roll pulse under the country ballad. The song had a long afterlife as a country standard, and George Strait took it back to number one in 1984.","It was a solid hit that consolidated Jackson as a country artist, though for decades her rock-and-roll sides overshadowed it among critics. Country historians now value it as part of the early-1960s moment when women's voices and viewpoints moved to the centre of the genre. Its stand-by-your-man loyalty reads differently today, and some listeners find its devotion unsettling rather than romantic, which the performance's quiet strain arguably invites.","Play it straight after Fujiyama Mama to hear the same singer in two utterly different characters, and notice how she keeps a trace of grit even here. Then compare it with Patsy Cline's I Fall to Pieces from the same year, the benchmark for this style, and with George Strait's later version for how a man's reading changes its meaning."],"alton-ellis-rock-steady":["A dance record that names a genre. Over a slow, rolling groove, Alton Ellis invites everyone to the floor and describes the new dance and how to do it, easy, cool, without the jerky energy of ska. The bass has become the lead instrument, playing a melodic, looping line; the guitar chops lightly on the offbeat; the horns are held back to short phrases. Ellis sings with the sweet, pleading tone he learned from American soul, and the record feels relaxed and confident.","Kingston in 1966 and 1967 was changing: ska's fast tempos gave way to a slower, sweeter style, reportedly helped by a hot summer when dancers wanted less exertion, and by the influence of American soul vocal groups. Ellis, recording for Duke Reid's Treasure Isle label with the studio's house musicians, was at the centre of the change. The rude-boy violence of Kingston's streets was also a live topic, and Ellis had recorded songs urging youths to calm down.","Listen to how much space there is. Where ska filled every beat, this record leaves gaps, and the bass and drums lock together to fill them in a way that points straight to reggae and dub. Ellis himself argued that his earlier Girl I've Got a Date was the first true rocksteady record; this one gave the style its name, which is why historians argue about which came first.","It was a big dance hit in Jamaica and has been treated ever since as the style's anthem, the record that fixed the word in popular use. Critics outside Jamaica came to it late, via reissues and reggae histories, and it is now a standard text in accounts of how ska became reggae. Debate continues over who first played the rocksteady rhythm, but Ellis's place as its leading voice is not seriously questioned.","Put it on and move to it; it is instruction as well as music. Listen for the bass line first and try humming it, since it carries the song. Then play the Skatalites to hear the faster ska it replaced, the Paragons' The Tide Is High for Treasure Isle's vocal-group side, and Toots and the Maytals' Do the Reggay to hear the next change a year later."],"alton-ellis-im-still-in-love-with-you":["A gentle rocksteady love song in which the singer admits that, despite everything that has passed between them, he still loves the woman who left, and hopes she feels the same. Ellis sings in his tender, soul-inflected tenor over a slow, swaying rhythm; the bass plays a warm, looping figure with a distinctive rise and fall, and the guitar and keyboard answer lightly on the offbeat. It is simple, patient and quietly devoted.","Recorded in Kingston in 1967, at the height of rocksteady, when Jamaican singers were absorbing American soul ballads from radio stations in Miami and New Orleans and reshaping them for the island's slower dance rhythm. Ellis was the style's leading singer, known as 'Mr Soul of Jamaica'. In Jamaica, producers owned the backing tracks, called riddims, and reused them freely, a practice that would give this recording a second and third life.","The rhythm under this song became one of the most reused in Jamaican music. In 1977 Althea and Donna's Uptown Top Ranking, a British number one in 1978, rode a version of it, and in 2003 Sean Paul and Sasha revived the song itself as a dancehall duet. Listen for the bass line: once you know it, you will hear it in dozens of later records, a small melody carrying a whole tradition.","It was a Jamaican favourite rather than an international hit on release, and its fame outside the island came indirectly, through the records built on its rhythm. Reggae historians now treat it as a standard of the rocksteady love song and an example of how Jamaican music circulated through riddims rather than songs. Ellis's original is often rated above the later versions for the simple warmth of his vocal.","Listen once for Ellis's voice, especially how softly he lands the ends of lines, then again for the bass alone. Then play Uptown Top Ranking and Sean Paul's version to hear the same foundation in two later worlds. Compare it with Rock Steady, from the same year, and with Sam Cooke to hear the soul singing Ellis loved."],"eddie-cochran-cmon-everybody":["A party song: the singer's parents are out of town, the house is free, and he calls his friends round to dance. Everything is built on a stop-start rhythm, a hard strummed guitar chord, a pause, then the band crashing back in, with handclaps and Cochran's confident, slightly sneering voice riding on top. The guitar part is simple enough for any teenager to learn, which is part of its point, and the whole record lasts barely two minutes.","Cochran, only twenty in 1958, was unusual among early rock and rollers: a skilled guitarist who worked in Hollywood studios, overdubbed his own parts and took an active hand in production. He wrote this with his manager Jerry Capehart for Liberty Records, following Summertime Blues. It was a moderate American hit but a bigger one in Britain, where Cochran was idolised; he died in a car crash there in April 1960, during a tour.","Listen to how the song is built: the verses are almost silent between the guitar stabs, so the energy comes from what is left out. Cochran probably played several of the guitar parts himself through overdubbing, a studio technique still novel for a rock and roll singer then. The song's picture of teenagers with their own space and their own music is the market that record companies had just discovered.","In America it was seen as a lesser follow-up; in Britain it was an instant classic, and Cochran's reputation there has always been higher. It has been covered by many, from the Sex Pistols, with Sid Vicious singing, to countless rockabilly bands, and returned to the British charts in 1988 via a television advertisement. Critics now hear it as a model of economical rock and roll songwriting and a direct ancestor of punk's three-chord bluntness.","Count the gaps: notice how the band stops dead between phrases, and try tapping the rhythm. Then play Summertime Blues to hear the same stop-start trick used for complaint rather than celebration. Follow it with the Sex Pistols' Never Mind the Bollocks for the punk inheritance, and Chuck Berry's Sweet Little Sixteen for the teenage world both men were describing."],"eddie-cochran-twenty-flight-rock":["A comic rockabilly number about a young man whose girlfriend lives on the twentieth floor of an apartment block where the lift is broken; by the time he has climbed all the stairs he is too worn out to dance with her. Cochran sings with hiccuping, Elvis-influenced delivery over a bright, bouncy rhythm, a walking bass and short, snappy guitar breaks. The joke is told in two and a half minutes, and the energy never flags even as the singer claims to be exhausted.","Cochran performed the song in the 1956 Hollywood film The Girl Can't Help It, a rock and roll musical comedy that also featured Little Richard and Gene Vincent, and his appearance made him a star before he had a hit record. Liberty released the recording as a single in 1957. The song is credited to Ned Fairchild, with Cochran's name added in some credits. Rock and roll was barely two years old as a mass craze, and films were how teenagers outside America saw it.","The song's biggest fame came in Liverpool. On 6 July 1957, at a church fete in Woolton, the teenage Paul McCartney met John Lennon and played this song for him, knowing every word and chord, and that performance helped win him a place in Lennon's group, the Quarrymen. So in a real sense, the Beatles began with this record. Notice too how the song mocks the fitness of its hero, a gentle joke against rock and roll swagger.","It was never a major American hit, and at first it was known mainly through the film. Its legend has grown through the McCartney story and through British rockers, who treated it as a test piece. Critics now value it as one of the purest documents of mid-1950s rockabilly and as evidence of how film carried rock and roll abroad. It remains a staple for revival bands and guitar students.","Watch the clip from The Girl Can't Help It if you can: Cochran's cool, sideways stage manner is half the appeal. Then listen for the guitar breaks, short and clean. Compare it with Gene Vincent's Be-Bop-A-Lula and Little Richard's Long Tall Sally from the same film era, and then early Beatles covers of American rock and roll to hear what the young McCartney took from it."],"etta-james-tell-mama":["A hard-swinging Southern soul record in which the singer offers a man comfort and devotion, telling him to bring her whatever is troubling him and she will make it right. Etta James sings with enormous, gritty authority, growling and shouting over a tight rhythm section and punchy, stabbing horns. The groove is lean and driving, with the horns answering her lines. It is short, confident and built to burst out of a car radio.","By 1967 James's career at Chess Records in Chicago had stalled amid personal struggles, including heroin addiction. Leonard Chess sent her to Rick Hall's FAME Studios in Muscle Shoals, Alabama, where the local session players had just helped make Aretha Franklin's breakthrough. The song was reworked from Clarence Carter's 'Tell Daddy', with its viewpoint changed. Released on Chess's Cadet label, it became her biggest hit in years and reached the pop top thirty.","Turn the single over: its B-side was I'd Rather Go Blind, now arguably the more famous song, so one single carried both her most extrovert and most devastated performances. Listen too for the band. The Muscle Shoals players were mostly white Southern musicians working in a segregated state, which surprised many who assumed from the sound that the band was Black, and their playing is deliberately spare to leave room for her voice.","It revived James's career and was treated as proof she could compete with the new soul of Stax and Atlantic. Critics now rank the Tell Mama sessions as among her finest work and a key document of the Muscle Shoals sound. Some debate lingers over the recasting of a man's song into a woman's, which some hear as strong and nurturing, others as a traditional role; James's performance makes it unmistakably her own.","Listen for the moment her voice cracks into a growl on the chorus, and for the horns answering every line. Then flip to I'd Rather Go Blind to hear the same singer and band at a crawl. Compare it with Aretha Franklin's I Never Loved a Man, made in the same studio months earlier, and with At Last to measure how far her voice had travelled."],"lee-morgan-search-for-the-new-land":["Five Morgan originals played by a sextet that reads like a roll call of mid-sixties Blue Note: Wayne Shorter on tenor, Grant Green on guitar, Herbie Hancock on piano, Reggie Workman on bass and Billy Higgins on drums. The fifteen-minute title track opens the record with slow, suspended chords and a hymn-like trumpet line that seems to scan a horizon before the band settles into a loose, searching pulse. After it come a brisk blues-inflected tune, a dedication to Jomo Kenyatta, a ballad and a swaggering closer.","Morgan recorded the session in February 1964, only weeks after The Sidewinder, which became the label's surprise best-seller. Blue Note, suddenly chasing another boogaloo hit, shelved this more ambitious music and released it only in 1966. Morgan was twenty-five, newly back in New York after a period away from music, and listening closely to the modal experiments of Miles Davis and John Coltrane. The title and the Kenyatta tribute point to the civil-rights and African-independence mood of the moment.","Listen to how rarely the title track states a steady beat in its opening minutes: Higgins plays colour on the cymbals while Hancock voices open, unresolved chords, so the time feels floated rather than counted. Grant Green, usually a blues-and-organ-trio player, sounds unusually spare here, leaving gaps instead of filling them. The contrast with the dance-floor strut of The Sidewinder, cut the same winter, shows two sides of one musician at once.","Because it appeared two years late and without a hit single, the record drew modest attention on release and was long treated as a footnote to The Sidewinder. Reappraisal came with CD reissues and with critics tracing Morgan's growth beyond hard bop; many now rank it among his most personal statements. The main debate is whether the long title track sustains its length or drifts, which depends largely on how much a listener values atmosphere over tunefulness.","Start with the title track and give it the full fifteen minutes; the reward is the slow way the solos emerge from the mist of the opening. Then play Mr. Kenyatta for Morgan's crackling brilliance at speed. Compare Shorter here with his own JuJu from the same year, and put the album beside The Sidewinder to hear the road Blue Note did not market."],"lee-morgan-live-at-the-lighthouse":["A double album recorded over a July weekend at the Lighthouse club in Hermosa Beach, California, by Morgan's working quintet: Bennie Maupin on tenor saxophone, bass clarinet and flute, Harold Mabern on piano, Jymie Merritt on bass and Mickey Roker on drums. There are only four long pieces, each a band member's composition rather than Morgan's own, and they stretch out to twenty minutes and more. The sound is dark, driving and exploratory, with Merritt's hypnotic bass figures carrying whole sections.","By 1970 hard bop's commercial moment had passed, rock dominated the market and Blue Note had been sold to a larger corporation. Morgan, clean after years of heroin addiction, had assembled a band that absorbed modal jazz, free playing and a growing interest in African and Black nationalist ideas; Merritt's tune titles such as Nommo reflect that. Less than two years later Morgan was shot dead at Slugs' Saloon in New York, aged thirty-three, which turned this record into his late testament.","The famous drum-and-bass groove that underpins much of the set comes from Jymie Merritt, a former Jazz Messenger who had been developing a theory of rhythm and pitch he called Forerunner; his compositions give the band its long, circling ostinatos. Listen too for Maupin switching to bass clarinet, a sound he had just brought to Miles Davis's Bitches Brew sessions. The spoken introductions and audience noise preserve the intimacy of a small beach club.","The original double LP sold modestly and received little notice amid the fusion boom. Its standing rose steadily, and a greatly expanded multi-disc edition in 1996 brought many more hours from the engagement, persuading critics that this was one of the great live small-group documents of its era. Some prefer the tighter original selection; others argue the complete set shows better how the band built momentum across a night.","Begin with Absolutions or Nommo and let the bass pattern settle into your body before the solos arrive. Follow Morgan's tone: rounder and more patient than on his 1950s records, but still able to flare into bright, half-valved cries. Compare it with Art Blakey's Free for All for the older hard-bop fire, and with Bitches Brew for where the jazz world was heading."],"prince-buster-madness":["A short, bouncing ska single built on the classic pattern of the style: piano and guitar chopping on the offbeat, a walking bass, a stiff shuffle on the drums and a punchy horn line between verses. Buster sings in a relaxed, half-declaimed voice about a world that has lost its senses, with a chorus that simply repeats the title as a kind of wry diagnosis. It is less a story than a chant, the kind of record built to be played loud on a sound system.","Cecil Bustamante Campbell had risen from street tough and sound-system enforcer to producer and label owner in Kingston, running his own Voice of the People shop and competing hard with Coxsone Dodd and Duke Reid. Ska was the new national beat of a Jamaica that had become independent in 1962. His records were licensed in Britain to the Blue Beat label, where they found a devoted audience among West Indian migrants and young mods in London clubs.","The song's afterlife is its main secret: in 1979 a group of young north London ska fans named their band Madness after it, and their debut single, The Prince, was a tribute to Buster, backed with a cover of this very song. That makes this three-minute record the seed of the whole British Two Tone revival, which carried Jamaican rhythm into the British pop charts a second time.","In its own day the single was one hit among many for a prolific producer and drew little critical comment beyond the sound-system world. Its reputation is largely retrospective, shaped by the Two Tone generation who treated Buster as a founding father. Discographers still disagree about exact release dates and pressings, a common problem with early Jamaican singles issued by several labels on two sides of the Atlantic.","Listen first to the rhythm guitar and piano hitting only the offbeats; that single gesture is ska. Then notice how little Buster sings and how much he leaves to the band. Play it next to Madness's own 1979 recordings and to the Skatalites' Guns of Navarone to hear the Jamaican original and its British echo side by side."],"prince-buster-judge-dread":["A courtroom drama in three minutes, set over a slowed-down, heavy rocksteady rhythm. Buster plays a merciless judge who hauls a line of rude boys before the bench, hands out absurdly long prison sentences and floggings, and refuses every plea as the defendants break down weeping in court. Spoken dialogue, gavel-like accents and sobbing effects turn the record into a piece of radio theatre, half comic sketch and half stern lecture.","In mid-sixties Kingston the rude boy, a sharp-dressed, often violent young man from the poor districts, was both a folk hero and a public menace, and many singles either celebrated or condemned him. Buster answered the rude-boy anthems of rivals with this mock trial, which played on real anxieties about gang violence in West Kingston before the 1967 elections. It was released as the beat was slowing from ska into rocksteady.","The record spawned a whole sequence of answer songs, including Buster's own follow-ups in which the judge relents or is himself put on trial, a typical Jamaican conversation carried on between singles. The name then travelled: a white English singer of bawdy reggae took Judge Dread as his stage name in the 1970s, and the British comic character Judge Dredd is often said to owe his name to it as well.","Opinion has always split over its tone. Some heard it as a moral stand against violence that devastated poor communities; others found it heavy-handed or simply funny, and it was a strong seller in Jamaica and among British skinheads and mods. Later critics treat it as a key document of the rude-boy debate and an early example of the producer as dramatist, using studio effects to tell a story.","Hear it as a radio play first: follow the voices, the sobbing and the judge's stern delivery rather than any melody. Then notice how slow and heavy the rhythm has become compared with Al Capone from a few years earlier; this is rocksteady arriving. Pair it with Desmond Dekker's 007 (Shanty Town), which views the same rude boys from the street."],"blind-boys-of-alabama-i-can-see-everybodys-mother-but-mine":["The record that made the group's name: a slow, grieving gospel number sung by a male quartet with little or no instrumental backing. Clarence Fountain's lead voice carries the song, a son's lament at seeing other people's mothers at church and in the world while his own is gone, and the group answers him with close, humming harmony that swells and falls behind each line. Its force comes from restraint, then from the sudden rasping intensity Fountain unleashes near the end.","The singers had met as boys at the Alabama Institute for the Negro Deaf and Blind in Talladega in the late 1930s and toured the Southern church circuit as the Happyland Jubilee Singers. Around 1948 a promoter billed them against the Five Blind Boys of Mississippi, and the new name stuck. This period was the golden age of the gospel quartet, when groups competed in programmes that were as fiercely theatrical as any secular show.","Listen to the division of labour: the background singers keep a near-constant blanket of sound, while the lead is free to bend, delay and break words across the beat. That call-and-response technique, the lead driving and the group answering, passed straight from quartet gospel into doo-wop and soul; singers such as Sam Cooke and Bobby Womack came up in exactly this world before crossing over.","It was a substantial hit on the gospel circuit and set up the group's long career, though it received no attention from the mainstream press, which barely covered Black religious recordings at the time. Historians of gospel now cite it as a classic example of the hard, emotional quartet style of the postwar years. Exact recording and release details vary between sources, as they often do for small independent gospel labels.","Play it in a quiet room and follow Fountain's voice from gentle to raw; the emotional arc is the whole piece. Then compare the Five Blind Boys of Mississippi's Our Father, their rivals' signature, and the Soul Stirrers' Jesus Gave Me Water. Finally jump fifty years to Spirit of the Century to hear the same group, and the same instincts, in old age."],"blind-boys-of-alabama-higher-ground":["The veteran quartet sings a set of mostly secular soul and rock songs reread as spirituals, backed by Robert Randolph and the Family Band, whose sacred-steel pedal guitar wails and slides like a second lead voice. Songs by Stevie Wonder and Curtis Mayfield sit beside traditional pieces, and Ben Harper joins on several tracks. Clarence Fountain, Jimmy Carter and George Scott still trade leads in the old quartet manner, but the rhythm section gives the record a loose, churchy funk.","After Spirit of the Century revived their career in 2001 and won a Grammy, the Blind Boys, then all in their seventies, became unexpected stars of the Americana and world-music festival circuit through Peter Gabriel's Real World label. Pairing them with Randolph, a young player from the House of God church tradition of sacred steel, linked two Black gospel lineages. The album won the Grammy for Best Traditional Soul Gospel Album.","The method is the point: by singing Stevie Wonder's Higher Ground, a song about spiritual rebirth, or Curtis Mayfield's civil-rights anthems in straight quartet style, the group reveals how much of soul music already came out of the church. Listen for Randolph's pedal steel doing the job a fifth singer would, answering each vocal line. It is the sacred-steel sound of Pentecostal services that most listeners had never heard before this period.","It was warmly received and sold well for a gospel record, confirming the group's crossover. Some gospel purists felt the secular repertoire and rock production diluted the tradition, while roots-music critics praised the way it made old quartet singing sound urgent. It is generally seen as the second, somewhat busier half of a revival that Spirit of the Century began.","Start with the title track, then go back to Stevie Wonder's original on Innervisions and hear how the meaning shifts when old men of faith sing it. Listen for the moments when the band drops out and the voices stand alone. Follow with Curtis Mayfield's People Get Ready and the Staple Singers' Freedom Highway, the soul-gospel line this album celebrates."],"pharoah-sanders-tauhid":["Sanders's first album for Impulse! is built from three long pieces. Upper Egypt and Lower Egypt fills the first side: a quiet, rumbling opening of percussion, piano and piccolo that slowly coheres into a swaying, chant-like vamp, then erupts when Sanders's tenor enters screaming in the upper register. A brief song, Japan, has Sanders singing a simple pentatonic melody. The second side is a suite moving from reverie to storm and back, with Sonny Sharrock's guitar slashing through the texture.","Sanders had spent the previous year in John Coltrane's band, where his overblown, ferocious tone shocked audiences, and Coltrane's advocacy won him this contract. The record was made in New York in November 1966, a few months before Coltrane's death. The title is an Arabic word for the oneness of God, and the Egyptian and Asian references reflect a wider search among Black musicians of the time for spiritual traditions outside the Christian church.","Sonny Sharrock, later famous for some of the most violent guitar in jazz, is used here mostly as colour: listen for his rippling, harp-like strums in the quiet stretches before he tears into the climaxes. The piccolo at the start is Sanders himself. The shape of Upper Egypt, calm to chaos to calm around a single repeated bass figure, became the blueprint for Karma and for much spiritual jazz after it.","Early reviewers who knew Sanders only as Coltrane's screaming partner were surprised by the record's patience and beauty. It sold modestly but is now regarded as the founding document of his own voice and an early landmark of spiritual jazz, sometimes overshadowed only by the more famous Karma. A few critics still find the free passages formless; most hear them as a release built carefully into the design.","Listen to Upper Egypt from the first note and resist skipping the slow opening: the long wait is what makes the tenor's entry hit so hard. Notice the bass line that anchors everything, a pattern you could hum. Then play Karma to hear the same form expanded, and Alice Coltrane's Journey in Satchidananda for the gentler branch of this family."],"pharoah-sanders-thembi":["A varied, colourful record of six shorter pieces rather than one long ritual. It opens with Astral Traveling, a floating ballad led by Lonnie Liston Smith's electric piano, then moves to a fierce saxophone workout, a lilting title track dedicated to Sanders's wife, a long unaccompanied bass feature by Cecil McBee, a gentle prayer and a closing dance for African percussion and balafon-like sounds. Violin, bells and hand drums give the whole album a warm, handmade texture.","By 1971 Sanders was Impulse!'s leading spiritual-jazz artist after Karma had found a wide audience, and his bands had become loose collectives drawing on African, Indian and Caribbean sounds. The period saw many Black jazz musicians looking to Africa for musical and political inspiration, and to non-Western instruments for new colours. Impulse! gave him studio time and freedom to shape each side as a suite of contrasting moods rather than a single extended piece.","Astral Traveling is often said to be the first time Lonnie Liston Smith played an electric piano, one he found in the studio and began exploring on the spot; its soft, bell-like shimmer later became his trademark in his own Cosmic Echoes band. Listen, too, for how Sanders withholds his tenor on several tracks, letting the ensemble colours carry the music rather than his famous scream.","Reviewers found it more accessible and more diverse than his long-form albums, and it has remained one of his most loved records. Some purists considered it less intense than Karma or Jewels of Thought. Its reputation grew strongly with the rediscovery of spiritual jazz by DJs and sample-minded listeners from the 1990s, when Astral Traveling in particular became a touchstone for ambient-leaning jazz.","Begin with Astral Traveling and let the electric piano set the mood; then hear the title track's lilting melody. Treat McBee's bass solo as its own small piece of chamber music. For contrast, follow with Tauhid's tumult, and for descendants listen to Floating Points' Promises, Sanders's late collaboration, and Kamasi Washington's The Epic."],"roscoe-mitchell-congliptious":["Credited to the Roscoe Mitchell Art Ensemble, the album divides in two. The first side gives each front-line player a solo showcase: Malachi Favors alone on bass, Lester Bowie alone on trumpet, Mitchell alone on saxophone. The second side brings the full group together with drummer Robert Crowder for a long, shifting ensemble piece that moves from scattered sounds to swinging passages and back. Silence, small sounds and sudden bursts replace the continuous roar of much free jazz.","Mitchell, Bowie and Favors were core members of Chicago's Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians, the cooperative founded in 1965 to present original music on its own terms in the South Side. Chuck Nessa, a young producer who had worked at Delmark, launched his own small label partly to record them. Within a year the players would leave for Paris and, with Joseph Jarman and later Famoudou Don Moye, become the Art Ensemble of Chicago.","Bowie's solo track is a piece of theatre as much as music: he mixes trumpet sputters, smears and fanfares with a spoken parody that asks whether jazz is dead, poking fun at the critics' debates of the day. The unaccompanied solo format itself was radical in 1968; Anthony Braxton's For Alto, a year later, would take the idea of a whole solo album to its extreme.","Released on a tiny label, it reached few listeners at first, but the few critics who heard it recognised a new approach to space and form in free jazz. It has since been reissued and is valued as the missing link between Mitchell's Sound and the Art Ensemble of Chicago's Paris albums. Some listeners find the solo side more compelling than the long group piece; others the reverse.","Start with the three solos as a set of portraits and notice how each player builds a shape from very few sounds. Then listen to the group side as a conversation, following who leads and who holds back. Compare it with Sound for the AACM's first recorded statement, and with People in Sorrow for where this quartet went next."],"roscoe-mitchell-nonaah":["A double album gathering several versions of Mitchell's composition Nonaah alongside other pieces. The best known is a solo alto saxophone performance recorded live in Switzerland, in which Mitchell repeats a short, jagged figure again and again, varying it by tiny degrees, for many minutes. Another version sets the same material for a quartet of alto saxophones, including Anthony Braxton and Joseph Jarman. Duets and further solos fill out a portrait of Mitchell as composer as much as improviser.","By the mid-1970s the Art Ensemble of Chicago was well established, and its members were building separate careers; Mitchell was working in Michigan and developing a body of written music for small groups. The solo recording comes from the Willisau Jazz Festival in 1976. European festivals were then among the main supporters of American experimental jazz, though their audiences did not always welcome it.","The festival crowd at Willisau reportedly grew restless and vocal during the solo Nonaah, expecting a conventional set, and you can hear the atmosphere in the room. Mitchell's response was to keep repeating his figure rather than give way, until the persistence becomes the point of the performance. Hearing the same material later scored for four saxophones reveals how carefully composed that apparently obstinate improvisation was.","Critics who admired the AACM praised the album's rigour and nerve; others found the repetitive solo exhausting. Over time it has become a touchstone for discussions about composition within improvisation and about the relationship between performer and audience in experimental music. It remains one of the most cited records of solo saxophone, often ranked alongside Braxton's For Alto.","Try the solo version first and listen as you would to minimalist music: notice how slight changes in accent, tone and length alter a phrase that seems fixed. Then play the saxophone-quartet version to hear the same idea as ensemble writing. Set it beside For Alto, and beside Steve Reich's Drumming for a very different use of repetition."],"smokey-robinson-shop-around":["A brisk, bouncing rhythm-and-blues single led by Smokey Robinson with the Miracles answering him in doo-wop-style harmony. The piano pounds out a boogie-woogie figure, the drums push hard on the backbeat and a saxophone fills the gaps. The words relay a mother's advice to her son: do not settle for the first girl who comes along, look around before you commit. Robinson sings it with a light, knowing grin in his voice rather than the aching falsetto of his later ballads.","Berry Gordy had founded his Detroit record business only in 1959, and Robinson, still barely twenty, was both his first star and his most trusted songwriter; they wrote this together. An early, slower version was first issued locally, but Gordy felt it dragged and called the group back to the studio to recut it faster. The new version became the company's first million-seller and reached the top of the rhythm-and-blues chart and number two in the pop chart early in 1961.","There are two genuinely different records under this title, and early pressings of the slow, bluesier take sometimes turn up under the same catalogue number, a collector's puzzle. Compare them and you hear Gordy's commercial ear at work: faster tempo, brighter piano, a more youthful bounce. That decision, favouring the version that would sound good on a car radio, is essentially the Motown method in miniature.","It was a major hit, and historians treat it as the moment Gordy's operation became a real company rather than a hopeful local label. Critics sometimes rank it below Robinson's later, subtler songs, finding the lyric simple and the production rough. Its importance, though, is rarely disputed: without its success, the funding and confidence behind the Motown sound of the mid-sixties would have been far less certain.","Listen to the piano first; it drives the song more than the vocal. Then notice how the Miracles' backing voices act as a chorus of gossiping friends. Hear it beside The Tracks of My Tears to measure how far Robinson's writing and singing matured in five years, and beside the Marvelettes' Please Mr. Postman, Motown's first pop number one."],"captain-beefheart-safe-as-milk":["Beefheart's debut album with his Magic Band is a set of short, punchy songs rooted in Delta and Chicago blues but bent out of shape: slide guitar riffs, lurching rhythms, doo-wop and soul touches, and Don Van Vliet's enormous growling voice, which leaps several octaves from a gravelly bass to a howl. Tracks such as Electricity add an eerie theremin, while Abba Zaba rides a jittery, almost African drum pattern. Among the oddities are tender, even pretty songs.","Don Van Vliet grew up in the Mojave Desert town of Lancaster, California, where he and his school friend Frank Zappa shared a passion for obscure rhythm-and-blues records. After a failed first deal with A&M, the band recorded this for Buddah, a label mostly known for bubblegum pop, in 1967, the year of psychedelia. The twenty-year-old Ry Cooder played guitar and helped arrange the songs, bringing discipline to Van Vliet's shifting ideas.","The theremin on Electricity was played by Samuel Hoffman, a veteran who had supplied the ghostly wails on 1940s and 1950s Hollywood film scores, an unexpected link between science-fiction cinema and garage blues. Van Vliet's voice was so powerful that a studio microphone is said to have failed during the sessions. Listen also for Grown So Ugly, a cover of the Louisiana bluesman Robert Pete Williams.","It sold poorly in America but found admirers in Britain, where John Peel championed Beefheart and John Lennon reportedly kept its promotional stickers at home. It was long seen mainly as the approachable prelude to Trout Mask Replica; later critics increasingly rank it among the great debut albums of the sixties on its own terms, a missing link between the blues revival and the art rock and punk that followed.","Start with Electricity for the theremin and the voice at full stretch, then Abba Zaba for the rhythm. Notice how blues riffs keep appearing but refuse to settle into twelve-bar comfort. Compare it with Howlin' Wolf, Van Vliet's clear vocal model, and then with Trout Mask Replica to hear how far he pushed these seeds in two years."],"captain-beefheart-lick-my-decals-off-baby":["Shorter and tighter than Trout Mask Replica, this album distils that record's method into fifteen compact pieces. Two guitars play interlocking, angular lines that seldom share a beat, the bass moves independently, and a marimba adds hard, glittering percussion to the mix. Van Vliet's voice, poetry and occasional free saxophone ride over the top. The songs cover desert landscapes, ecological worry, desire and absurd comedy, all in fractured, clattering textures that sound composed rather than jammed.","The Magic Band had rehearsed Trout Mask Replica under intense, sometimes harsh conditions in a small house in Woodland Hills, and several of the same players returned under Van Vliet's stage names: Zoot Horn Rollo on guitar, Rockette Morton on bass. Art Tripp, a classically trained percussionist who had played with Frank Zappa's Mothers of Invention, joined on marimba and drums. Van Vliet often said this was his own favourite of his albums.","Much of the guitar writing was worked out by Van Vliet on piano or by whistling and humming, and then transcribed and drilled by the musicians into fixed parts, which is why it sounds so unlike blues improvisation. The marimba is a deliberate link to twentieth-century percussion music; listen for how it doubles or contradicts the guitars like a third melodic voice. A surreal television advertisement made for the album was reportedly refused by many stations.","British listeners responded more warmly than American ones, and many critics considered it a more focused and approachable statement than its famous predecessor. For years it was out of print and hard to find, which made it a cult item. Today it is often recommended as the best starting point for Beefheart's avant-garde period, though some still hear Trout Mask Replica as the purer achievement.","Try the title track first, then listen to one guitar at a time on headphones and notice how the two lines interlock. Let the marimba guide you through the rhythm. Afterwards compare Varèse's Ionisation for music built from colliding percussion, and Devo or Pere Ubu-style post-punk for the bands that learned from these textures."],"corea-light-as-a-feather":["The second Return to Forever album, made by the same Latin-flavoured quintet as the first: Corea on Fender Rhodes, Joe Farrell on flute and saxophone, Stanley Clarke on acoustic bass, Airto Moreira on drums and percussion, and Flora Purim singing. It is sunny, melodic and rhythmically light, with Brazilian sambas, songs and a closing flamenco-flavoured tune called Spain. Also here are Captain Marvel and 500 Miles High, two Corea pieces that soon became jazz standards.","Recorded in London in October 1972, the album followed the success of the ECM Return to Forever record and moved the group to a bigger label and wider audience. Corea had recently left Miles Davis's electric band and turned away from free improvisation, partly under the influence of Scientology, which encouraged him to communicate directly with listeners. The result was jazz fusion at its most tuneful, before the band turned to louder, rock-based sounds with electric guitar.","Spain opens with a gentle Rhodes quotation of the slow movement of Joaquín Rodrigo's Concierto de Aranjuez, the same melody Miles Davis and Gil Evans had arranged on Sketches of Spain. Listen, too, for the tricky, hand-clapped break before the final theme, a small rhythmic puzzle that has tripped up many a later band. Flora Purim's wordless singing on several tracks is treated as another instrument in the ensemble.","It was well received and sold widely, helping to make fusion a commercial force. Some jazz critics found its sweetness lightweight next to the edgier work of Weather Report or the Mahavishnu Orchestra, while others praised its melodic freshness. Its songs have outlived its reputation: Spain in particular has been recorded countless times, from big bands to flamenco guitarists and vocal groups.","Go straight to Spain, and listen for the Rodrigo quotation before the tune explodes into a fast Latin groove. Then play 500 Miles High for Purim's voice and Clarke's singing bass. Compare it with the Aranjuez adagio itself and with Sketches of Spain, and then with Romantic Warrior to hear the same band turned into a rock-scale fusion group."],"desmond-dekker-007-shanty-town":["A rocksteady single with a loping, relaxed rhythm, bright piano chops on the offbeat and a catchy horn hook. Desmond Dekker sings in a high, sweet, slightly nasal voice, answered by his backing group the Aces. The song describes rude boys running riot in a Kingston shantytown, the police and army moving in and people fleeing through the streets, while the title nods to the James Bond films then showing in Jamaican cinemas. Its tone is part news report, part street celebration.","Dekker worked with the Chinese-Jamaican producer Leslie Kong at his Beverley's label, one of the most commercially successful studios in Kingston. By 1967 the fast ska rhythm had slowed into rocksteady, better suited to the heat and to the new electric bass. Rude-boy violence and political clashes in West Kingston were headline news. The record was also released in Britain, where it became the first of Dekker's British hits and a favourite among young mods and skinheads.","The title mixes two sources of imagery that ruled Kingston life in 1967: Hollywood spy films, whose gunmen and gadgets fascinated the young, and the real gang fights of the shantytowns. The song neither condemns nor glorifies its rude boys, which is part of its lasting ambiguity. Its later inclusion on The Harder They Come soundtrack placed it among the records that introduced Jamaican music to the wider world.","It was a hit in Jamaica and reached the British top twenty, paving the way for Israelites two years later. Critics treat it as one of the defining rude-boy records, notable for its vivid reportage. It has been covered and quoted by several later ska and punk bands, and appears regularly on lists of the best Jamaican singles of the sixties.","Hear the slower rhythm first: compare it with the faster ska of Prince Buster's Al Capone and you hear rocksteady being born. Then follow Dekker's voice against the backing chorus. Pair it with Buster's Judge Dread, which puts the same rude boys in court, and with The Harder They Come soundtrack as a whole."],"desmond-dekker-you-can-get-it-if-you-really-want":["An upbeat early-reggae single with a bouncing organ shuffle, rhythm guitar skanking on the offbeat and a chorus built for a crowd to sing. Dekker's bright voice delivers a message of determination: success is within reach for anyone willing to try, and to keep trying through setbacks. The arrangement is polished and joyous, with a mid-tempo bounce that sits between the rocksteady of his earlier records and the heavier reggae that followed.","The song was written by Jimmy Cliff, who also recorded it, and Dekker's version was produced by Leslie Kong, who worked with both singers. After Israelites, Dekker had moved to Britain, where Jamaican music was flourishing among West Indian communities and a young white audience. Released in 1970, this became his second-biggest British hit. Cliff's own performance later appeared in the film The Harder They Come, tying the song to that landmark soundtrack.","Cliff and Dekker were close musical partners: Cliff had introduced Dekker to Leslie Kong in the early sixties, and here the older friend's song gave the younger singer a hit. Listen to how Dekker's phrasing differs from Cliff's, lighter and more playful where Cliff is earnest. The optimistic lyric reads differently once you know Cliff wrote it amid the struggle to make a living in Kingston's music trade.","It was a big British hit and has become one of the best-known Jamaican songs internationally, used in films, commercials and political campaigns. Critics sometimes consider it more commercial than Dekker's rawer rude-boy records, and some prefer Cliff's version. Its reputation rests on its effortless tune and its message, which has made it a perennial on feel-good compilations.","Listen for the organ and the offbeat guitar that give the rhythm its lift. Then compare Dekker's version with Jimmy Cliff's own on The Harder They Come soundtrack, noticing how two singers shape the same words. Finish with Israelites and Many Rivers to Cross to hear the range of both artists."],"joan-baez-the-night-they-drove-old-dixie-down":["A marching, sing-along ballad in the voice of a poor Southern farmer at the end of the American Civil War, remembering the winter of hunger, the fall of Richmond and the death of his brother. Baez sings it brightly and squarely over a Nashville-style band of strummed guitars, piano and a drum beat that stamps like boots on a road. The chorus, with its massed voices and 'na-na' refrain, turns private grief into something a crowd can join.","Robbie Robertson wrote the song for The Band's second album in 1969, where Levon Helm sang it with a rough Arkansas edge. Baez, by then recording country-flavoured albums with Nashville session musicians for Vanguard, cut her version in 1971 and it became by far the biggest hit single of her career, reaching the American top five. It was an odd triumph for a singer known for civil-rights marches and anti-war campaigning: a pacifist from California singing a defeated Confederate's lament.","Listen closely and several words differ from The Band's recording: Baez learned the song by ear and misheard a few phrases, so a name and a couple of images in her verses change. She later admitted she had simply copied what she thought she heard. Compare the two records side by side and the song's meaning moves slightly between them.","Radio loved it and critics were divided. Some found Baez's clear soprano and upbeat arrangement too cheerful for a song of defeat, missing the bruised weariness Levon Helm brought to it; others heard the brightness as a deliberate, almost hymnlike distance. Later debate has focused on the song itself, whether it romanticises the Confederacy or simply gives voice to a poor man caught in history, and Baez's version sits in the middle of that argument.","Play The Band's original first, then Baez's: the same song, one sung from inside the mud, the other from a clear distance. Notice how her version builds the chorus into a communal chant, closer to a folk-revival singalong than a rock track. Then go back to her 1960 debut album to hear how far the voice travelled in eleven years."],"carole-king-writer":["Carole King's first solo album: twelve songs, most written with Gerry Goffin, sung in her plain, slightly husky voice from behind the piano with a small Los Angeles band. It mixes new songs with her own versions of Brill Building numbers, including 'Up on the Roof', which the Drifters had made a hit, and 'Goin' Back', recorded by Dusty Springfield and The Byrds. 'No Easy Way Down' and 'Child of Mine' show the gospel-flavoured piano writing that would carry Tapestry.","By 1970 King had left New York, divorced Goffin, and settled in Laurel Canyon, where she played in a short-lived band called The City. Lou Adler's new Ode label, distributed by A&M, gave her a solo contract, and Writer was made quickly and modestly. It sold poorly on release. The title was almost an apology: King still thought of herself as a songwriter who happened to sing, not a performer, and she was nervous on stage.","Listen to how she handles 'Up on the Roof': where the Drifters' 1962 single floated on strings, King's version is closer and more hesitant, a demo made public. That habit of taking back songs she had written for others, and singing them as private confessions, is the whole idea of Tapestry in rehearsal. The album charted properly only after Tapestry made her famous, when listeners went back to find it.","Reviews in 1970 were polite and few, and the record barely entered the charts. Its reputation is almost entirely retrospective, as the prologue to Tapestry, and critics generally judge it tentative: the voice not yet confident, some arrangements generic. But admirers argue it is the most direct record of King as a working songwriter, and that several of its songs, especially 'Child of Mine', stand with anything she wrote later.","Start with 'Up on the Roof' and play the Drifters' single straight after; the gap between a hit production and a songwriter's own reading tells you what the singer-songwriter movement was about. Then try 'Child of Mine' for the piano. Treat the album as a sketchbook, and move on to Tapestry to hear the finished picture."],"carole-king-music":["Twelve songs in the warm, unhurried style of Tapestry, but with more jazz in the chords and more room for the band. 'Brighter' opens with bouncing piano; 'It's Going to Take Some Time' is a quiet song about recovering from a relationship; 'Sweet Seasons', the single, is an easy, country-tinged hymn to a settled life; the title song lets saxophone and piano stretch out. King's voice sits close to the microphone throughout, and her left hand keeps up its rolling gospel bass.","Released in December 1971, barely ten months after Tapestry, while that album was still selling in enormous numbers. Lou Adler again produced it for Ode, with many of the same Los Angeles players, including King's husband Charles Larkey on bass. The pressure was obvious: follow the best-selling album of the era, and do it fast. Music went straight to number one in the United States, and for a while King held two albums in the top ten at once.","Listen to the harmony rather than the tunes. Several songs here drift into seventh and ninth chords of the kind King grew up with in jazz and Broadway, and the arrangements leave gaps where a saxophone or an electric piano improvises, much looser than the tight structures of her 1960s hits. The Carpenters took 'It's Going to Take Some Time' into the American top twenty the following year, polishing what King left deliberately rough.","Critics at the time mostly called it a gentle, likable sequel that lacked Tapestry's surprise, and that verdict has largely stuck. Its commercial success showed how completely the confessional singer-songwriter had become the centre of American pop by 1971. More recent listeners sometimes prefer its relaxed, jazzier grooves to Tapestry's familiar songs, and argue that the album sounds less like a sequel than like a musician enjoying her band.","Start with 'Sweet Seasons' and then 'It's Going to Take Some Time', one easy and one rueful. Then put on the title track and listen to the band rather than the voice: the conversation between piano and saxophone is the album's real subject. Hear it after Tapestry, as a contented postscript, and compare it with Joni Mitchell's Court and Spark for another singer-songwriter moving toward jazz."],"gilberto-gil-domingo-no-parque":["A song that works like a short film. It opens with the twang of the berimbau, the one-stringed bow of capoeira, and Gil's voice telling the story of two friends in Salvador, José and João, one a builder and one a street performer, who both fall for the same girl, Juliana, on a Sunday in the park. Images of a rose and an ice cream repeat and swirl, the orchestra and electric guitars crowd in, and the story ends in a jealous killing.","Gil performed it at TV Record's televised song festival in São Paulo in October 1967, the same night Caetano Veloso sang 'Alegria, alegria'. He was backed by the young rock trio Os Mutantes, with an orchestral arrangement by Rogério Duprat, and it took second prize. Electric guitars at a festival of Brazilian song were a provocation, since nationalist students saw them as American imperialism. The performance, with 'Alegria, alegria', is usually taken as the public birth of Tropicália.","Listen for the cutting: in the last section the words and the orchestra behave like film editing, jumping between close-ups of the rose, the ice cream, the knife and the faces, faster and faster, a technique Gil and Duprat took from cinema and from The Beatles. Underneath, the berimbau rhythm never leaves; the capoeira ring, where fights are also dances, is the song's hidden frame.","Festival juries and audiences were split: some heard a betrayal of Brazilian music, others its future, and the song's second place pleased no one entirely. Within a year it was treated as a founding text of Tropicália, and scholars of Brazilian culture now read it alongside Oswald de Andrade's idea of 'cultural cannibalism', swallowing foreign influence to make something Brazilian. It remains one of Gil's best known songs.","Seek out the studio version on Gil's 1968 album, then a clip of the festival performance if you can find one, with Os Mutantes grinning behind him. Follow the story first; on a second listen, ignore the words and track the berimbau against the orchestra. Play 'Alegria, alegria' next, the other half of the same night."],"gilberto-gil-refazenda":["A mostly acoustic, rural-sounding album: Gil's supple guitar and light voice with accordion, triangle and zabumba drum from the northeastern backlands, and occasional strings. The title song, a play on 'refazer' (to remake) and 'fazenda' (farm), is a gentle song about growing things slowly. 'Tenho sede' is a longing ballad; 'Jeca total' mocks the stereotype of the backwoods hick; 'Lamento sertanejo' is a spare lament for the northeastern migrant lost in the city.","Recorded in 1975 in Rio, still under military rule, when Gil was turning away from the electric experiments of his London exile toward the music of his Bahian and northeastern childhood, especially the baião of Luiz Gonzaga. The accordionist Dominguinhos, Gonzaga's protégé, is central to the sound. The album began a loose trilogy of titles starting with 'Re': Refazenda, then Refavela (1977), after a trip to Nigeria, and Realce (1979), which went toward disco.","Listen for the syllable games: Gil builds several songs out of rhyme and word-play in Portuguese, and the album's whole plan is announced by its title, a made-up word that splices 'remake' onto 'farm'. The accordion is not decoration but a quotation of a whole regional tradition, and in 'Lamento sertanejo' Dominguinhos and Gil share authorship: a Tropicalist and a forró musician meeting as equals.","It was received as a quieter, more rooted record than Expresso 2222, and some critics who had admired his rock-inflected work found it too gentle. Its standing has grown steadily; Brazilian critics now often place it among his finest, and the trilogy is read as a map of Gil's idea of Brazil, from the backlands through Afro-Brazilian cities to the dance floor. 'Refazenda' and 'Tenho sede' remain concert staples.","Start with the title song and 'Tenho sede', then listen for the accordion in 'Lamento sertanejo'. It is a morning record, best heard quietly. Follow it with Refavela to hear Gil turn the same method toward Africa, and with Luiz Gonzaga's baião records if you want the source of the accordion and zabumba sound."],"isaac-hayes-black-moses":["A double album of about an hour and a half: Hayes's deep baritone taking other people's love songs, including the Jackson 5's 'Never Can Say Goodbye', Bacharach and David's '(They Long to Be) Close to You' and 'Part-Time Love', and slowing them to a smoulder under lush strings, wah-wah guitar and female backing singers. Between them sit several spoken monologues called 'Ike's Rap', where he talks over a slow groove about love, loneliness and men and women, like a late-night preacher.","Hayes made it in Memphis for Stax's Enterprise label at the peak of his fame, months after the Theme from Shaft had topped the charts and would win an Academy Award. The title came from a nickname given to him as a figure of Black pride, and the original sleeve unfolded into a cross-shaped poster showing Hayes in robes like a biblical prophet. Stax was flush but overextended, and the album's sheer scale matches the company's ambition that year.","The 'Ike's Rap' tracks matter far beyond the album. Their slow, heavy grooves, built for talking over, were later sampled by producers in hip-hop and in Bristol trip-hop; the backing of 'Ike's Rap II' became the foundation of Portishead's 'Glory Box' in 1994. Listen for the way the spoken sections never rhyme or rise to a chorus: they are sermons, and the music waits for him.","It reached the top ten of the American album chart and topped the soul chart, but critics were less kind than to Hot Buttered Soul, finding the length indulgent and the covers too alike. The image of Hayes as prophet also drew mockery. Sampling rehabilitated it: once the raps became sources for 1990s records, listeners heard the album less as excess and more as a groundwork for slow, cinematic soul and later spoken-word R&B.","Do not start at side one. Begin with 'Never Can Say Goodbye' to hear how Hayes turns a bright pop song into a slow burn, then play one of the 'Ike's Rap' tracks and listen for the groove under the talk. After that, play Portishead's 'Glory Box' and listen for the loop. Take the rest in sides, as a late-evening record."],"milton-nascimento-travessia":["A slow ballad that climbs. Milton Nascimento's voice begins low and dark, almost speaking, over guitar and hushed strings, and gradually rises through a long, winding melody into a high, open falsetto that seems to come from a church choir loft. The words, by Fernando Brant, describe a man walking on after a love has ended, deciding that his life must go on through grief. The title means 'crossing', and the song itself sounds like one.","Nascimento, a young singer from Minas Gerais working in bars in Belo Horizonte, was entered in the Festival Internacional da Canção in Rio in 1967, reportedly by friends without his full consent. 'Travessia' took second place and he was named best performer, making him known across Brazil almost overnight. It became the title track of his first album. The same year, festival television was turning songwriters into national stars and battlegrounds for arguments about Brazil's identity.","Listen for the chords under the melody. They move in modal, unexpected ways, drawn from the Catholic hymns and processional music of Minas Gerais and from jazz, and they give the song its feeling of a long road rather than a verse and chorus. It was also Fernando Brant's first lyric; he became Milton's main partner for decades, and the Clube da Esquina circle grew around the pair.","Its festival success opened an international career: American jazz musicians took notice, and an English version called 'Bridges' was recorded by several singers. Some Brazilian critics at the time found it too solemn beside bossa nova's lightness, but it has become one of the most sung ballads of Brazilian popular music, and its range and slow build still test any singer. Milton has re-recorded it many times.","Listen once through without the words, only following the voice from its low start to the falsetto peak; it is a single long breath. Then find a later live version by Milton to hear how his voice aged into it. Follow with Clube da Esquina to hear the harmonic world the song opened, and with Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5 for another Brazilian voice soaring over strings."],"milton-nascimento-milagre-dos-peixes":["An album in which many songs have almost no words. Milton's voice, wide in range and sometimes wordless, moans, calls and soars over dense arrangements of percussion, organ, guitars and orchestra, often built on repeating, ritual-like figures. The effect is closer to a sung mass or a trance than to pop song. The title piece, 'The miracle of the fishes', has a rolling, hypnotic groove; other tracks move from near silence to full orchestral outbursts.","Recorded in 1973 during the hardest years of Brazil's military dictatorship. The federal censors rejected the lyrics of several of the songs Milton had prepared with his partners, including Fernando Brant and Márcio Borges. Rather than drop the songs, he recorded them anyway, singing melodies without the forbidden words or replacing them with vocal sounds. The album thus became an audible record of censorship, released a year after Clube da Esquina had made him a star.","Listen for the gaps where words should be. On the censored tracks the voice carries the full melody and the emotional shape of a text that is not there; the listener fills the hole. Musicians in his circle, including the pianist and arranger Wagner Tiso, fill out the arrangements with sounds that replace verbal meaning. A live concert version recorded shortly after with orchestra shows how the songs worked on stage.","Some listeners in 1973 found it difficult and dark after the sunny passages of Clube da Esquina. Its reputation has risen as the story of its making became widely known, and many Brazilian critics now treat it as Milton's most daring record, a protest carried not by slogans but by a voice denied its words. It is often cited in discussions of how artists worked around the dictatorship's censors.","Start with the title track and let the groove settle before you listen for the voice. Then play one of the wordless songs and ask what the melody seems to be saying without a text. It rewards headphones and a dark room. Compare it with Chico Buarque's 'Cálice' for the same censorship answered with a pun rather than silence."],"tammy-wynette-golden-ring":["A duet by Tammy Wynette and George Jones that tells the life of a wedding ring in about three minutes. It begins in a pawnshop in Chattanooga where a poor young couple buy it, follows them to the wedding and a small house, and then to the row that ends the marriage, when she leaves the ring behind and it returns to a pawnshop window. The two voices trade verses and then twist around each other in close harmony on the chorus.","Wynette and Jones had married in 1969 and divorced in 1975, after years of turbulence widely reported in the country press, but they kept recording together for Epic under their producer Billy Sherrill in Nashville. Bobby Braddock and Rafe Van Hoy wrote the song, and its release in 1976 was received as a public drama: a divorced couple singing the story of a failed marriage. It went to number one on the American country chart.","Listen to the harmony on the chorus: Jones slides and bends into notes while Wynette holds hers straight and clear, so the two voices seem to pull apart and come back together within one phrase, which is the song's story in miniature. Sherrill's arrangement, full of strings and a soft choir, is the Countrypolitan style at its most cinematic, a camera following one object through a life.","It was both a hit and a talking point, with fans hearing it as confession, while the singers insisted it was simply a good song. Critics generally rank it among the best country duets ever recorded, and the pairing of Jones and Wynette is still the standard against which country duets are judged. Some later writers note how neatly the story is told compared with the messier truth of the singers' lives.","Follow the ring: each verse is a new scene, so listen as though to a short story. Then listen again to the harmony alone on the chorus. Play Wynette's 'D-I-V-O-R-C-E' next for the same subject from a mother's point of view, and George Jones's 'He Stopped Loving Her Today' for where his voice went next."],"u-roy-version-galore":["An album of deejay records: U-Roy's excited voice chanting, whooping, half-singing and shouting catchphrases over rocksteady backing tracks from Duke Reid's Treasure Isle studio. Each track takes a well-loved song, lets the original singer's voice drift in and out, and fills the spaces with commentary, jokes and rhythmic phrases. 'Wear You to the Ball', over the Paragons' song, is among the best known. The backing is sweet and slow, with prominent bass and choppy guitar.","After the success of 'Wake the Town' and its companion singles in 1970, when U-Roy's records dominated the Jamaican charts, Duke Reid gathered his work into an album, also issued in Britain. Jamaican sound systems were at their peak, competing for crowds with exclusive records and star deejays, and producers were beginning to issue 'versions', instrumental B-sides meant to be talked over. The album's title names the method: a whole LP of versions.","Listen to how the original records are treated. Engineers could drop the vocal track in and out, so U-Roy often answers the singer, finishing a line or echoing a word, like a conversation across years. That practice of reusing a recording as raw material, rather than a finished product, is the seed of dub, of remixing, and of hip-hop's sampling; the album is a manual in miniature.","In Jamaica it confirmed U-Roy as the first superstar deejay, the 'Originator'. Outside, critics were slow to take toasting seriously, and for years it was treated as a novelty offshoot of reggae. Hip-hop changed that; historians now place U-Roy's Treasure Isle records at the start of the line that runs through Kool Herc's Bronx parties to rap, and Version Galore is the easiest way into them.","Start with 'Wear You to the Ball', and if possible hear the Paragons' original first, so you can hear what he adds. Listen for the moments where the singer's voice pokes through, and how U-Roy dances around it. Then play King Tubby's dub albums to hear the same backing tracks stripped further, and Grandmaster Flash to hear where the idea landed."],"u-roy-dread-in-a-babylon":["A roots reggae album built for the deejay. U-Roy chants, sings and exclaims over heavy, slow rhythms dominated by deep bass and crisp drums, with horns and organ drifting through. 'Runaway Girl' and 'Chalice in the Palace' are the best known tracks. Where his early records were joyful commentary on sweet love songs, the mood here is weightier and more spiritual, full of Rastafarian imagery and warnings about Babylon, the corrupt world of power and money.","By 1975 reggae had turned toward roots music, with Rastafarian faith and Black consciousness at its centre, and British labels were competing to sign Jamaican artists after Bob Marley's success. U-Roy recorded the album in Kingston with the producer Prince Tony Robinson, and it was issued in Britain by Virgin, then moving into reggae. It introduced him to a new, largely British rock audience, and its cover photograph of the dreadlocked deejay became famous.","Listen to the backing tracks on their own: they are new, full-band roots rhythms rather than recycled rocksteady hits, so for once U-Roy is working over music made for him. His phrasing changes accordingly, longer and more chant-like, closer to singing. Listen too for the gaps the arrangements leave, bars where the band thins to bass and drums precisely so the voice can enter.","The album sold well in Britain and was widely reviewed in the rock press, which was still learning what a deejay was. Critics praised its heavy sound and U-Roy's authority. It is usually ranked as his best album-length statement, and in the late 1970s it was among the reggae records that London's punk-era musicians and fans absorbed. Some purists prefer the rawer Treasure Isle singles.","Start with 'Runaway Girl' and then 'Chalice in the Palace'. Play it loud enough to feel the bass, which carries as much of the music as the voice. Compare it with Version Galore to hear how the deejay's art grew from party commentary to prophecy, and with Burning Spear's Marcus Garvey, another roots record from the same year."],"randy-newman-12-songs":["Twelve short songs, most under three minutes, played by Newman at the piano with a small, loose rock and blues band, including Ry Cooder's slide guitar. Instead of the orchestra of his first album the sound is dry and swampy. Newman sings in character, drawling and half-mumbling: a lonely man following a woman, a raucous party host in 'Mama Told Me Not to Come', a would-be arsonist in 'Let's Burn Down the Cornfield'. He closes with an old 1930s song, 'Underneath the Harlem Moon'.","Newman was a Los Angeles songwriter from a family of Hollywood film composers, known in the business for songs other singers recorded. His orchestral debut album of 1968 had sold very little. For this second album on Reprise, produced by Lenny Waronker, he stripped the sound down to a band. The same year, Three Dog Night took 'Mama Told Me Not to Come' to number one, so Newman's songs were famous before he was.","Listen for who is speaking. Almost every song is sung by a character Newman does not endorse, often someone unpleasant or deluded, and the music is friendly while the words disturb; the gap between them is the joke and the point. His choice to include 'Underneath the Harlem Moon', a period song full of racial caricature, sung without comment, is deliberate and uncomfortable, a preview of the method of Sail Away.","Critics adored it; Rolling Stone and others gave it rapturous reviews, and it established Newman as a songwriter's songwriter, though sales remained modest. It is now often ranked alongside Sail Away as his finest record. Discussion today centres on the character-song method: admirers see sharp satire of American voices, while some listeners find the irony too cool, or worry that audiences miss it.","Start with 'Mama Told Me Not to Come' and then play Three Dog Night's hit version; Newman's is sly and lazy where theirs is a romp. Then listen to 'Let's Burn Down the Cornfield' for the slide guitar. Ask on each song who the speaker is. Continue to Sail Away, where the same characters get an orchestra."],"chico-buarque-apesar-de-voce":["A buoyant samba that sounds like a party. Chico Buarque sings to an unnamed 'you', someone who gives the orders and has made life dark, and promises that in spite of this person, the title's meaning, a brighter day will come, and that 'you' will have to watch everyone celebrate. The rhythm section and a chorus build it into a joyful, clapping crowd song, with the anger held just under the surface of the good humour.","Chico had spent over a year in voluntary exile in Italy after the hard-line decree of December 1968 and came home in 1970, when the military regime under President Médici was at its most repressive. He released the song as a single, and the censors first passed it as a lover's quarrel. It sold heavily and was sung widely before the authorities realised it was addressed to the regime, when it was banned and copies were reportedly seized.","The trick is grammar. The song never names the regime, the general or politics; everything turns on the ambiguous 'you', which can be a woman or a government. Listen to how the most defiant promises are set to the happiest music, so that singing along becomes an act of protest disguised as celebration. The song was re-recorded on Chico's 1978 album, when the censorship began to loosen.","It became one of the defining protest songs of the dictatorship, and its banning made Chico a symbol of resistance and a constant target of censors, who later scrutinised everything he wrote. Scholars of Brazilian song hold it up as a model of the double meaning artists used to survive censorship. It returned to the streets during the campaign for direct elections in 1984 and has been sung at demonstrations since.","Listen first as if you know nothing, simply as a samba, and notice the lift of the chorus. Then listen knowing who 'you' is. Follow with Construção, from the next year, to hear the same anger turned grave, and with Pete Seeger's 'Waist Deep in the Big Muddy' for a North American song that also slipped past censorship by allegory."],"chico-buarque-calice":["A slow, heavy song that begins like a church chant, with a male chorus intoning, then builds into a grinding march for orchestra. Chico Buarque and Milton Nascimento alternate verses: Chico's voice dry and restrained, Milton's rising into a desperate, full-throated cry. The song borrows Jesus's prayer in Gethsemane that the cup might pass from him, and turns it into an image of a people forced to drink bitterness and keep silent. It ends in a clamour of voices and brass.","Chico wrote it with Gilberto Gil in 1973 to perform at Phono 73, a festival of the Phonogram record label's artists in São Paulo. The censors banned the lyrics, and when the two tried to sing the song anyway, humming the melody and repeating the title, their microphones were switched off one by one on stage. The song could only be recorded in 1978, as censorship eased; Milton took Gil's part on Chico's album that year.","The title is a pun. 'Cálice', the chalice or cup, sounds in Portuguese almost exactly like 'cale-se', 'shut up' or 'be silent', so the chorus is at once a prayer and an order to be quiet, and every repetition says both. Listen for how the words about silence are sung louder and louder: the performance acts out the thing it describes, and the 1973 microphones completed the metaphor.","Its release in 1978 was greeted as the lifting of a stone, and the story of Phono 73 became legend. It is now taught as the classic example of wordplay against censorship in Brazilian song, and is often ranked among the greatest Brazilian recordings. Critics also note its musical daring, closer to liturgy and art song than to samba. Gil and Chico have both performed it since, each in their own way.","Hear it first for the voices: Chico's quiet menace against Milton's anguish. Then listen again for the pun, every time the chorus returns. Play it after Milton's Milagre dos peixes, where censorship produced silence, and after 'Apesar de você', where it produced a party; here it produces a funeral march. Then hear Gil's own later live performances of it, slower and more hushed."],"jimmy-cliff-vietnam":["A bright, bouncing early-reggae groove carries one of the darkest stories in popular song. Over a skipping organ shuffle, a chiming guitar and a warm, rounded bass, Jimmy Cliff tells of a young soldier who writes home promising his mother he will soon be back, and of the telegram that arrives instead. The tune is almost cheerful; Cliff's high, pleading tenor supplies the grief, and a repeated call to end the war turns the last verses into a chant.","Cliff cut the song in Kingston with producer Leslie Kong, the Chinese-Jamaican shopkeeper-turned-producer who had launched him as a teenager, and it appeared on his 1969 album for Trojan in Britain. The Vietnam War was at its height and dominating world news; Jamaica had no soldiers in it, which makes the song an act of solidarity from the outside. Reggae was only a year or two old as a name, still thought of abroad as dance music.","The contrast is the point: the arrangement never darkens, so the horror arrives in the words while the band keeps smiling, a device reggae would use for decades. Listen, too, for how the narrative is told at one remove, through a letter and then a telegram, so the soldier never speaks in the present tense. The same Kingston players and sound drew Paul Simon to Jamaica to record 'Mother and Child Reunion' in 1971.","Bob Dylan is widely reported to have called it the best protest song ever written, and the remark has followed the record ever since. In Britain and Jamaica it circulated widely; in America, where the war was still bitterly divisive, it made little impression on radio at the time. Later listeners have ranked it with Marvin Gaye's work as proof that a protest song can be tender rather than strident.","Play it once just for the groove, then once for the story; the gap between the two is the experience. Notice how early reggae sits halfway between rocksteady and ska here, the offbeat still quick and light. Follow it with 'Many Rivers to Cross' from the same period, then with Marvin Gaye's What's Going On to hear the same war seen from Detroit."],"peter-tosh-legalize-it":["Peter Tosh's first solo album after leaving the Wailers is a lean, slow-rolling roots record built on heavy one-drop drumming, a deep bass that carries the melody as much as any singer, and Tosh's rich baritone. The title song is a mid-tempo campaign for cannabis reform; around it sit hymns ('Igziabeher'), a sly courtship song ('Ketchy Shuby'), a funeral-refusing anthem ('Burial') and 'Why Must I Cry', a song he wrote with Bob Marley.","Tosh had walked out of the Wailers in 1974, angry at how Island Records packaged the group around Marley. Legalize It appeared in 1976 on Columbia in America and Virgin in Britain, the first sign that major labels beyond Island would bet on reggae. In Jamaica the title song was kept off the radio, which only made it more famous; Tosh was repeatedly harassed and arrested by police in these years.","Behind Tosh, the harmony singers and the rhythm section draw on the same Kingston circle that made Marley's records, so the album is in some sense a Wailers record without Marley. Listen for Tosh's guitar: he was the Wailers' most inventive rhythm player, and the choppy, percussive upstrokes that open several tracks are his signature. 'Burial' returns to a song from his Wailers years, and its defiance of death sets up the militancy of Equal Rights.","Reviewers in 1976 heard it mainly as a novelty built on a slogan, and the title track still overshadows everything else. Reggae writers have since argued that the album's real weight lies in its devotional and domestic songs and in Tosh's singing, which is warmer here than on his later, angrier records. It is now generally counted, with Equal Rights, as the core of his solo work.","Start with 'Igziabeher' or 'Why Must I Cry' rather than the famous title song; they show the singer at his most open. Then listen to the bass on the title track as if it were the lead vocal. Compare Bob Marley's Catch a Fire to hear the band Tosh came from, and Equal Rights to hear where he went next."],"peter-tosh-bush-doctor":["Peter Tosh's third solo album is his most polished, driven by the rhythm section of Sly Dunbar on drums and Robbie Shakespeare on bass, whose tight, springy grooves would soon define a whole decade of reggae. It opens with a reggae remake of the Temptations' '(You Gotta Walk) Don't Look Back', sung as a duet with Mick Jagger, and moves through defiant songs of self-assertion, healing and faith ('Pick Myself Up', 'I'm the Toughest', 'Creation').","Tosh had opened for the Rolling Stones and signed to their own label, and Bush Doctor was the label's big reggae release of 1978. The same year he appeared with Jagger on American network television, and at the One Love Peace Concert in Kingston he lectured Jamaica's prime minister and opposition leader from the stage about poverty and police violence. The album sits between that political fury and a bid for the rock audience.","'Don't Look Back' was written by Smokey Robinson and Ronald White for the Temptations in 1965, a reminder that Jamaican singers of Tosh's generation grew up on American soul harmony groups. Listen to Sly Dunbar's hi-hat and rimshot patterns across the record: this is an early document of the precise, almost machine-like feel that Sly and Robbie took into the 1980s with Black Uhuru and Grace Jones.","The Jagger duet became Tosh's biggest international hit, and some reggae purists have never forgiven it, hearing the album as a softening after the militancy of Equal Rights. Others hear the opposite: Tosh using a rock star's reach to smuggle serious songs to a new audience. The consensus now places it just below his first two solo albums, prized chiefly for its rhythm section and its closing hymn.","Begin with 'Don't Look Back', then play the Temptations' original to hear what reggae does to a Motown song. After that, put on headphones for 'Creation', a slow, devotional closer where Tosh sounds most himself. For the Sly and Robbie sound in full flower, move on to their later 1980s work; for Tosh at his angriest, go back to Equal Rights."],"townes-van-zandt-townes-van-zandt":["Townes Van Zandt's third album, named simply after himself, is the one that fixed his sound: a gentle, slightly cracked Texas voice over fingerpicked acoustic guitar, with only light touches of bass and backing. The ten songs include 'For the Sake of the Song', 'Waiting 'Round to Die', 'Lungs', 'Colorado Girl', 'I'll Be Here in the Morning' and '(Quicksilver Daydreams of) Maria' — ballads of drifting, despair, weather and devotion, sung with almost no ornament at all.","Van Zandt, a Houston folk-club singer from a wealthy Fort Worth family, recorded for Kevin Eggers's small Poppy label. His first album, from 1968, had been dressed in arrangements he felt smothered the songs, so this record deliberately re-cut several of them in plainer form. The late 1960s singer-songwriter boom was under way, but Van Zandt belonged to the Texas song tradition of Lightnin' Hopkins and Hank Williams more than to Los Angeles.","Because several songs appeared on the first album too, comparing versions shows exactly what Van Zandt thought his music needed: less. 'Waiting 'Round to Die', one of the first songs he wrote, tells a whole life of losses in a few verses with the flat calm of a man reading a list. 'Lungs', with its bleak, riddling images of breath and fire, shows the other side of his writing: compressed, almost surreal, closer to Symbolist poetry than to country song.","The album sold very little and Van Zandt stayed a cult figure for most of his life, known mainly through other singers' covers. Since his death in 1997 it has been widely treated as one of the purest records of American songwriting, and writers including Steve Earle have named him their model. Some listeners still find the production too thin; admirers say that thinness is exactly the point.","Listen in a quiet room and let the words lead; the guitar is there to keep time with the thought. Start with 'For the Sake of the Song', then 'Waiting 'Round to Die'. Compare Nick Drake's Five Leaves Left, made the same year in London, for a similar hush. Then hear these songs again, live and rawer, on Live at the Old Quarter."],"townes-van-zandt-live-at-the-old-quarter":["A double album of one man, one guitar and a small, rapt crowd. Van Zandt runs through nearly all of his best songs — 'Pancho and Lefty', 'If I Needed You', 'Tecumseh Valley', 'Kathleen', 'To Live Is to Fly', 'Waiting 'Round to Die' — along with a few old blues numbers, stopping between songs to tell dry, deadpan jokes. His playing is precise fingerpicking, his voice worn but steady, and the tempos are unhurried throughout.","The recordings come from a run of shows in July 1973 at the Old Quarter, a tiny bar in downtown Houston that was a home base for Texas songwriters. Van Zandt's studio albums were selling poorly and his label situation was precarious; the tapes sat unreleased until 1977, when Tomato Records issued them. By then Emmylou Harris had recorded 'Pancho and Lefty', and a wider audience was starting to ask who wrote it.","The room is part of the recording: you can hear the crowd's closeness and the little sounds of a working bar, and the laughter after his jokes tells you how well this audience knew him. Listen to how the spoken patter, much of it bleak humour about drinking and bad luck, frames songs of real despair, so the evening never tips into self-pity. The guitar work is more intricate than on his studio records.","Many admirers, and many songwriters, rank this above any of his studio albums, arguing that the stripped setting is where his songs make sense. It is regularly listed among the great live folk records. The only real dissent concerns length: ninety minutes of one voice and guitar is a commitment, and some listeners prefer the tighter studio versions. Its reputation has grown with every decade since his death.","Treat it like an evening in a small club: put it on and do not skip. If you need a way in, start with 'Pancho and Lefty' and 'If I Needed You', then 'Tecumseh Valley'. Listen for how he lets the last guitar figure hang after each song ends. Compare Johnny Cash at Folsom Prison for another live record where the audience is part of the story."],"anthony-braxton-three-compositions-of-new-jazz":["Anthony Braxton's debut album as leader has no bass and no drummer keeping time. Instead, Braxton on alto saxophone and a battery of other reeds, Leroy Jenkins on violin and Leo Smith on trumpet share out small percussion instruments, sirens and bells between long passages of spare, pointillist playing; Richard Abrams adds piano and cello on one piece. The three pieces are named by diagrams and codes rather than titles, and they sound like chamber music assembled from quick, fragile gestures and silences.","Braxton had joined Chicago's Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians, the South Side collective founded in 1965 around Muhal Richard Abrams to let Black musicians control their own composition, performance and presentation. Delmark, a local blues and jazz label, recorded several of its members. The AACM's ethos, like Braxton's, looked as much to Stockhausen and Cage as to Charlie Parker, which in 1968 was a provocation on both sides of the jazz-classical line.","Listen to how often the ensemble stops. Silence works as a structural device, much as in Webern or Feldman, and each re-entry is placed rather than thrown. The small instruments are not colour for its own sake: when horn players pick up a bell or a whistle, the texture thins to single sounds, and the piece's architecture becomes audible. Braxton's diagram titles began a naming system he kept for the rest of his career.","Critics in 1968 were divided between those who heard a new, cerebral kind of freedom and those who heard jazz losing its swing and its blues. The debate over whether Braxton's music is 'really jazz' began here and never ended; Braxton himself rejected the category's boundaries. Today the record is valued as a foundational AACM document, less monumental than For Alto but the first statement of his composer's ear.","Do not wait for a groove; listen instead as you would to a piece of new chamber music, following one instrument through the silences. Jenkins's violin is a good guide. Then hear Roscoe Mitchell's Sound and Abrams's Levels and Degrees of Light to place it among its Chicago siblings, and Braxton's For Alto to hear him alone a year later."],"anthony-braxton-creative-orchestra-music-1976":["Braxton's first large-ensemble record is a set of six pieces for a big band of about twenty, and it keeps changing shape. One piece is a lurching, off-kilter march that sounds like a brass band slipping on ice; another is a swinging big-band chart turned slightly sideways; others dissolve into sustained clouds of brass, slow collective tones and passages where soloists move through pre-composed blocks. The sound is bright, dense and constantly surprising.","In the mid-1970s Arista, a new major label, signed Braxton and briefly gave an avant-garde composer real budgets. He gathered AACM colleagues and New York players, among them trumpeter Kenny Wheeler, trombonist George Lewis, Roscoe Mitchell, Leo Smith and Muhal Richard Abrams. Braxton was openly drawing on marching bands, Ellington's orchestra, Sun Ra's Arkestra, Ives and Stockhausen, a lineage he argued was one American tradition rather than several.","The march is the key to the whole record: it is both a tribute to the brass bands behind early jazz and to Ives's collisions of parade music, and Braxton's writing lets sections drift slightly out of step on purpose. Listen, too, for how the pieces use the orchestra as a set of textures rather than as a machine for backing soloists — the direction his later works for multiple orchestras would take to extremes.","Reviewers at the time were impressed and bewildered in equal measure; some jazz critics called it academic, while others heard the most original big-band writing since Mingus. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is now often cited as the best introduction to Braxton, precisely because its range, from march to swing to sound mass, shows how wide his idea of the jazz tradition was.","Start with the march; it is funny, strange and immediately memorable. Then go to the swinging piece and listen for the moment it bends away from the expected big-band arrangement. Compare Ellington's 'Ko-Ko' for the orchestral lineage, and Ives's Fourth Symphony for the colliding bands. Headphones help separate the thick brass voicings."],"burning-spear-man-in-the-hills":["The follow-up to Marcus Garvey keeps the same slow, rolling, heavy sound — deep bass, hand percussion, a mournful horn section — but turns from history to the countryside. Winston Rodney's voice, raw and incantatory, repeats short phrases until they become chants, while harmony singers answer him. The title song pictures a simple life of farming and self-sufficiency in the Jamaican hills as a moral ideal; other songs deal with struggle, repatriation and faith.","Burning Spear was then a vocal trio led by Rodney, from Saint Ann's Bay on Jamaica's north coast, the birthplace of both Marcus Garvey and Bob Marley. Island Records, riding Marley's success, released the album internationally in 1976. It was again produced by Jack Ruby, a north-coast sound-system operator, with a band of local players, which gives both records their unhurried, rural feel far from Kingston studio polish.","Rodney's lyrics say little and repeat a lot, and that is the technique: like a preacher or a work-song leader, he uses repetition to make a phrase gather weight. Listen to the horns, which rarely play hooks; they swell and fade like a congregation's response. The hills of the title are not only scenery — many listeners also hear in them the Maroons, escaped slaves who lived free in Jamaica's interior.","Coming after Marcus Garvey, it was inevitably judged against it and generally found slightly less urgent. Reggae historians now tend to treat the two records as a pair, the peak of Burning Spear's partnership with Jack Ruby, and the title song is among the most loved of his catalogue. Some critics prefer this album's warmer, more pastoral mood to its famous predecessor's intensity.","Let the tempo slow you down; this is music for sitting still. Start with the title song and notice how few words it needs. Then listen for the horn section's long, sighing lines. Play it next to Marcus Garvey to hear the difference between historical fury and pastoral longing, and next to Culture's Two Sevens Clash for another roots group of the same moment."],"burning-spear-garveys-ghost":["The dub version of Marcus Garvey: the same ten rhythm tracks, stripped of most of Winston Rodney's singing and remixed at the desk. What remains is the band's heavy bass and drums, with horns, guitar and keyboard fading in and out, drenched in echo and reverb, and occasional ghostly scraps of voice. Each track keeps its original's mood but becomes more spacious and hypnotic, as though heard from the far side of a valley.","By the mid-1970s dub — remixing a record into an instrumental version on the mixing desk — was a standard Jamaican practice, pioneered by engineers such as King Tubby and Lee Perry for the B-sides of singles. Island Records took the unusual step of releasing a whole dub album of a major roots record internationally in 1976, betting that listeners in Britain and beyond would want the rhythms on their own. It was an early such release for a mass audience.","The title is a pun with a point: the dub makes Garvey literally a ghost, his vocal present only as a trace. Listen for the few sung phrases the mix leaves in, and notice how a single surviving word can carry the whole meaning of the lost verse. Compare the horn lines too; in dub they often get echoed so heavily that a single phrase trails off into a long, repeating shadow.","Reviewed modestly at first, it has become one of the most admired dub albums, valued because the source record's rhythms are so strong. Dub specialists sometimes rank it below the more radical studio work of King Tubby or Lee Perry, since the mixing is comparatively restrained. Others hear that restraint as respect: the album keeps the gravity of the songs while opening them out.","Listen to Marcus Garvey first, then play the two albums track by track side by side; the exercise teaches what dub actually does. Use good speakers or headphones with real bass. Then move on to King Tubby Meets Rockers Uptown and Lee Perry's Super Ape, both from the same year, to hear other engineers' approaches."],"elis-regina-arrastao":["A song about fishermen hauling in a dragnet at dawn, written by Edu Lobo with words by the poet Vinicius de Moraes. It starts quietly, like a work song or prayer to the sea, then swells into a surging, brassy chorus as the net comes in full. Elis Regina, twenty years old, sings it with enormous projection and rhythmic drive, pushing far past the soft, intimate delivery that bossa nova had made the Brazilian norm.","Regina sang it in 1965 at the first Festival de Música Popular Brasileira, broadcast by TV Excelsior in São Paulo, and it won. Televised song festivals were becoming the arena where a new generation of Brazilian songwriters competed, and after the 1964 military coup they also became a place where a coded, national-popular spirit could be expressed. Her victory made her a star and helped name the new music: MPB.","Listen to how the song borrows from Afro-Brazilian coastal devotion — the fishermen's appeals to the sea echo the religious culture of Bahia — while the orchestration is big-band television. That mix of northeastern folk roots and modern arrangement is exactly the MPB formula in miniature. Her performance style, whirling her arms as she sang, earned her the nickname 'Eliscóptero' in the press.","The victory was controversial among bossa nova loyalists, who found her singing too loud and theatrical; others heard Brazil's popular music finally raising its voice. History has sided with the festival: 'Arrastão' is routinely named as the starting point of the MPB era, and it launched both Regina's career and Edu Lobo's. Her extrovert style remained divisive for years before becoming the national benchmark.","Listen first for the build: from near-silence to the full chorus in under three minutes. Then compare João Gilberto's hushed Chega de saudade to hear what Regina was reacting against. Follow it with Elis & Tom, from nine years later, to hear how much subtler and freer her voice became, and Caetano Veloso's 'Alegria, alegria' for the next festival revolution."],"laurie-spiegel-unseen-worlds":["An hour of slowly unfolding electronic pieces, some serene and chiming, others dark and drifting, made largely with software Spiegel wrote herself. Harmonies shift gradually, melodic patterns grow and turn back on themselves, and the textures move between glassy, bell-like tones and dense, murmuring clouds. It feels less like a set of tracks than a series of landscapes, and it is gentler and more tonal than most academic computer music of its time.","After her years at Bell Labs in the 1970s, Spiegel wrote Music Mouse (1986), one of the first 'intelligent instrument' programs for home computers such as the Macintosh, Amiga and Atari: the user moves a mouse and the software supplies harmony and pattern. Unseen Worlds grew out of that work and was issued on a small independent label in 1991, when personal computing was making electronic composition possible outside institutions for the first time.","Many of these pieces are partly improvised: Spiegel plays her own algorithms like an instrument, steering processes rather than writing every note. Listen for moments where a pattern seems to decide its own next step; that is the software's contribution, and the composer's art lies in when she intervenes. The title points to her lifelong interest in phenomena too slow or too large to perceive directly.","Barely noticed on release, the album became a cult item among electronic musicians and was reissued in 2019, when a younger generation of ambient and synthesizer artists was rediscovering Spiegel. Critics now hear it as a missing link between 1970s computer music and 1990s ambient and IDM. Some still find its long pieces too even-tempered; admirers call it among the most humane computer music ever made.","Play it as an album, at low to moderate volume, and let it run. Start with a piece that catches you and follow a single repeating figure as it changes. Compare The Expanding Universe to hear how far her tools had come in a decade, then Aphex Twin's ambient work and Brian Eno's Music for Airports for kindred approaches."],"tony-williams-life-time":["Tony Williams's first album as leader is not a drummer's showcase but a set of open, exploratory compositions for shifting small groups. The opening two-part suite pairs Sam Rivers's tenor saxophone with two basses; later pieces feature Herbie Hancock's piano and Bobby Hutcherson's vibraphone in spare, floating textures, and one track is a quiet dialogue with almost no steady beat. The drumming is responsive and colouristic rather than propulsive.","Williams was eighteen and already the drummer in Miles Davis's new quintet, the youngest and most radical player in it. Given his own Blue Note session, he chose the freest music he knew, drawing on Ornette Coleman, Cecil Taylor and contemporary classical ideas. It was recorded in 1964 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio, the same year Williams played on Eric Dolphy's Out to Lunch!","Listen for how Williams uses cymbals: he plays them as washes and shimmers rather than keeping time, the approach he was also bringing into Miles Davis's band. The two-bass pairing on the opening suite lets one bass anchor while the other roams. The album's title later became the name of his fusion group, Lifetime, so this quiet record is the root of a very loud one.","Critics were startled that a teenage prodigy would make such abstract, undemonstrative music, and some found it cold or unfinished. Later writers have seen it as one of Blue Note's boldest mid-1960s releases, a bridge between hard bop's label identity and free jazz. It remains less known than the Davis quintet records, and is often recommended as the key to understanding Williams's thinking.","Do not listen for drum solos; listen for space and for how the players leave room for one another. Start with the opening suite, following Rivers's saxophone against the two basses. Then play Out to Lunch! from the same year, and Hancock's Empyrean Isles, to hear the circle Williams belonged to. Finish with Emergency! to hear where the name went."],"tony-williams-believe-it":["The New Tony Williams Lifetime was a tight electric quartet, and Believe It is its first album: short, hard-hitting instrumentals built on complex riffs, fast unison lines and explosive drumming. Allan Holdsworth's guitar is the other star, playing long, fluid, legato runs that sound more like a saxophone than a rock guitar. Alan Pasqua's keyboards and Tony Newton's bass keep the grooves funky and dense, and pieces such as 'Fred' and 'Proto-Cosmos' are compact and sharply composed.","Williams's first Lifetime, the trio of Emergency!, had been too raw and too loud for most listeners, and the band had dissolved. By 1975 jazz-rock fusion was commercially established — Mahavishnu Orchestra, Weather Report and Return to Forever were filling halls — and Columbia backed a more polished version of his idea. The British guitarist Holdsworth, then little known in America, was the key recruitment.","Listen to Holdsworth's lines closely and you will hear almost no picked attack: he slurs notes together, and his harmonic choices come from jazz rather than blues. Williams, meanwhile, often plays a different accent pattern against the riff, so the rhythm feels as if it is leaning forward. 'Fred' was written by Holdsworth and became something of a fusion standard among guitarists.","Critics who had championed the wild first Lifetime sometimes found this record too slick, a fusion product rather than an experiment. Musicians disagreed: guitarists in particular treat it as a holy text, and Holdsworth's playing here influenced generations of rock and metal players. It is now counted among the best-crafted fusion albums of the decade, if not among the most daring.","Start with 'Fred' and follow Holdsworth's solo, then go back and listen only to the drums. Compare Emergency! to hear how much tighter and brighter Williams's idea had become. Then put on Mahavishnu Orchestra's Birds of Fire and Return to Forever's Romantic Warrior to place it among its rivals. Loud playback is appropriate."],"van-morrison-brown-eyed-girl":["A bright, three-minute pop single opening with one of the best-known guitar riffs of the 1960s, a chiming figure that climbs and falls over a light, Latin-tinged beat. Van Morrison sings, with a soul singer's phrasing, a nostalgic song remembering young love out of doors — running, hiding, laughing by a stream — and then leads a wordless, sing-along refrain with female backing voices. It is compact, sunny and instantly memorable.","After leaving the Belfast band Them, Morrison went to New York in 1967 to record for Bert Berns's Bang Records. Berns, co-writer of 'Twist and Shout' and 'Hang On Sloopy' and producer of Them's 'Here Comes the Night', produced this session with New York studio musicians. The single reached the American top ten, but Morrison later described the Bang contract as a trap that paid him almost nothing, and he spent years disowning the song and its style.","The song was reportedly first called 'Brown Skinned Girl', which would have made it an interracial love song in 1967 America; the title change removed that. A line alluding to a teenage romance's physical side was edited out on some radio versions. Listen for the loose, almost jazzy vocal phrasing in the verses, which hints at the long, free-flowing style of Astral Weeks the following year.","At first a modest radio hit, it has become one of the most played songs on American radio, a fixture of oldies radio, weddings and films. Morrison's own coolness towards it has become part of its story. Critics tend to see it as a perfect pop single and as a misleading guide to an artist whose real ambitions lay elsewhere.","Listen first to the guitar riff and how it is layered between two parts. Then listen to Morrison's voice and how he stretches phrases across the beat. Play Astral Weeks straight after to hear the same singer, a year later, abandoning pop form altogether. For the 1960s New York pop world it came from, try the Ronettes and the Shangri-Las."],"pran-nath-earth-groove":["Extended raga performances sung by Pran Nath with nothing more than the drone of tanpuras and, in the faster sections, tabla. Each begins with an unmetred alap in which the voice settles onto a single note and moves away from it by the smallest possible steps, bending into each pitch rather than landing on it. The recognisable gesture is that slow, weighted glide: a note approached from below over several seconds, held until it seems to change colour, and only then released.","Pran Nath was a singer of the Kirana gharana, a lineage prized for its unhurried alap and its obsession with exact intonation, and he had spent years as a near-ascetic disciple of Abdul Wahid Khan. In the late 1960s he was little known outside Delhi. This LP, issued in America by Alan Douglas's small independent label, was one of the first chances Western listeners had to hear him, at the moment when psychedelia, drone and Indian music were all in the air of downtown New York.","Listen to how rarely he sings a note without first leaning on the one beside it. The Kirana style treats each pitch as a place to be reached, not a fixed point, and Pran Nath tunes his approach so finely that the tanpura drone seems to brighten when he finally arrives. That sensation, of harmony produced by one voice against a fixed hum, is exactly what La Monte Young and Terry Riley were chasing with electronics and just intonation in the same years.","The record made little commercial impression, and the title, with its nod to the counterculture, now reads as a label's attempt to sell a severe classical singer to a rock audience. Its importance is in who heard it. Pran Nath came to the United States in 1970 at the invitation of Young and Marian Zazeela, and the minimalists' long apprenticeship to him followed. Collectors now treat the LP as the first document of that crossing, and reissues have drawn fresh attention to it.","Do not wait for a melody. Give the alap ten minutes with the volume up and let the drone fill the room, then notice how each new note feels like an event. When the tabla enters, the change of energy will feel enormous. Afterwards play the opening of La Monte Young's Well-Tuned Piano and listen for the same patience."],"al-green-love-and-happiness":["Five minutes of Memphis soul built from almost nothing. A lone electric guitar picks out a quiet, circling figure; the band enters one instrument at a time; the horns hold back until well into the song. Al Green, who wrote it with guitarist Teenie Hodges, sings about how love can lift a person up and also wreck a household, moving from a murmur to a falsetto shout and back. The track never speeds up, yet it builds steadily, like a sermon gathering force over a steady beat.","It appeared on the 1972 album I'm Still in Love with You, cut at Royal Recording Studio in Memphis for Hi Records with producer Willie Mitchell. The house band was built around the Hodges brothers on guitar, organ and bass, with the Memphis Horns. Mitchell's formula was restraint: dry, close drums, a thin guitar, strings used sparingly, and a singer told to keep his voice soft. Green was at the height of his run of hits, and this song was not even a lead single.","The opening seconds are the lesson. The guitar is played so lightly it almost ticks, and the drums enter with the snare damped until it sounds like a hand on a table. Everything is kept small so that Green's sudden high cries land like thunder. Listen too for the organ: Charles Hodges holds long, soft chords under the verses that you barely notice until they drop out and the room suddenly feels empty.","At the time it was an album track; a single edit followed later. Its reputation has grown steadily since, and many critics now rank it above the bigger hits as the purest statement of the Green and Mitchell sound. Some hear in its warning about love's danger the tension that would soon lead Green away from secular music and into the pulpit, though that reading leans heavily on hindsight.","Play it loud enough to hear the space between instruments; that space is the arrangement. Follow the band entering layer by layer, then wait for Green's first break into falsetto. Afterwards try Let's Stay Together for the polished single version of the same sound, and Otis Redding for the rougher Memphis voice Green consciously avoided."],"gram-parsons-gp":["Gram Parsons's first solo album, eleven songs split between his own writing and country standards and covers. The sound is straight country, with pedal steel, fiddle and honky-tonk piano, played by musicians who also knew rock and roll. Its defining texture is the duet: Emmylou Harris, then almost unknown, sings harmony on many tracks, her clear voice sitting just above his frayed one. Songs like A Song for You and She are slow and mournful; Streets of Baltimore is a tidy story of a country couple undone by the city.","After leaving the Flying Burrito Brothers, Parsons spent time drifting, including a stay in France with the Rolling Stones while they made Exile on Main St. Reprise signed him, and he recorded in Hollywood in 1972 with several players from Elvis Presley's touring band, including guitarist James Burton. On a tip from Chris Hillman he went to hear Harris in a Washington club and brought her in. Country radio did not know what to do with a long-haired Californian; rock radio did not want pedal steel.","Listen to how Harris shadows him. She often sings the harmony a fraction behind, following his phrasing rather than locking to the beat, which is the old brother-duet habit of the Louvins and Everlys. The covers are pointed choices: a song co-written by George Jones and one by a Boston rock band sit side by side, as if to prove that his idea of Cosmic American Music was a matter of feeling, not genre.","GP sold poorly and failed to chart, and reviews were respectful rather than excited. Its standing rose after Parsons's death later in 1973 and rose again as Harris became a star and as alt-country bands of the 1990s claimed him as a founder. Some prefer the looser Grievous Angel; others find GP the more coherent country record. Either way, the partnership it introduced is the thing most listeners come for.","Start with We'll Sweep Out the Ashes in the Morning and She, the two tracks where the duet style is clearest. Listen for the pedal steel answering the voices at the end of each line. Then go back to the Byrds' Sweetheart of the Rodeo to hear where Parsons began, and forward to Emmylou Harris's Pieces of the Sky to hear where she took it."],"gram-parsons-safe-at-home":["The only album by the International Submarine Band, Gram Parsons's first group: a short record of honky-tonk and Bakersfield-style country played by young musicians who had grown up on rock and roll. Parsons's own songs, including Blue Eyes and Luxury Liner, sit beside Johnny Cash and Merle Haggard material and a medley joining Folsom Prison Blues to the Elvis Presley song That's All Right. The playing is clean, bright and a little stiff; the singing is sincere, sometimes shaky, and plainly in love with the genre.","Parsons, a Georgia-born Harvard dropout with a trust fund, formed the band in the mid-1960s in Cambridge and New York, then moved it to Los Angeles, where country was playing in bars a few miles from the psychedelic clubs of Sunset Strip. The album was made for Lee Hazlewood's small LHI label. Before it came out in 1968, Parsons had already joined the Byrds, and the band had effectively dissolved, so the record appeared with almost no one to promote it.","Notice what is missing: there is no irony. In 1967 a rock musician playing honky-tonk was expected to wink; Parsons sings Cash and Haggard as if they were Dylan, which is exactly the move he would make with the Byrds months later. The Folsom Prison medley is the thesis in miniature: Presley's Sun rockabilly and Cash's Sun country were cut in the same Memphis studio, and Parsons stitches them back together.","It sold almost nothing and went out of print, and for years it was a collector's curiosity overshadowed by Sweetheart of the Rodeo. Historians of country rock now often cite it as the first full album of the style, though some argue the claim is inflated and that the record is more tribute than fusion. Emmylou Harris's later recording of Luxury Liner as a title track helped rescue the song and the album's name.","Take it as a sketchbook. Listen for the pedal steel doing the work that lead guitar would do on a rock record, and for Parsons's voice already cracking in the right places on Blue Eyes. Then play the Byrds' Sweetheart of the Rodeo to hear the same idea with better players and a major label behind it."],"hector-lavoe-la-voz":["Héctor Lavoe's first album under his own name after years as Willie Colón's singer, and the record that earned him the nickname in its title. It keeps the rough New York salsa sound he had helped create: blaring trombones, a piano repeating a tight montuno pattern, congas and timbales driving underneath. Lavoe's high, nasal Puerto Rican voice, full of rural jíbaro turns, rides over the top and trades lines with the chorus. Mi gente, a Johnny Pacheco song, became his calling card in concert.","By 1974 Colón had stepped back from bandleading and handed his band to Lavoe, while staying on as producer. Fania Records, run by Pacheco and Jerry Masucci, was at its peak: the Fania All Stars filled stadiums, and salsa had become a badge of Puerto Rican and Latino pride in New York. Lavoe was already a star in the barrio, but also struggling with drug use, and his punctuality was notorious. A solo album was a bet that his voice alone could carry a record.","Listen to his soneos, the improvised lines he sings between the chorus's repeated refrain in the long second half of each track. They are where Lavoe was most himself: wisecracks, local references and bits of country décima phrasing from his native Ponce, delivered with perfect timing against the clave. The band leaves deliberate gaps for them, and the best moments are when he slides in a beat late and still lands exactly.","La Voz was a commercial success and established Lavoe as a solo headliner, with Mi gente becoming one of the anthems of the Fania era. Critics have sometimes rated it below his later Comedia, which has the larger songs, but many salseros hear it as the purest record of his voice. His later decline has coloured how the whole catalogue is heard, sometimes to the point of turning the music into biography.","Begin with Mi gente, then listen to any track twice: once for the arrangement, with its brass riffs, once only for Lavoe's improvised lines after the chorus comes in. Notice how the trombones are pitched rough and loud on purpose. Then go to El cantante to hear the later, more tragic Lavoe, and to Rubén Blades's Siembra for the next step in salsa."],"hector-lavoe-periodico-de-ayer":["A salsa song built on a single sharp image, written by the Puerto Rican composer Tite Curet Alonso: a love affair compared to yesterday's newspaper, sensational one morning and forgotten the next. The first half is the song proper, a slow-burning verse sung by Lavoe over a steady, heavy groove. Then the chorus takes up a short refrain and the track opens into a long montuno section of improvised vocal lines, brass riffs and percussion, which is where most dancers came alive.","It appeared on Lavoe's 1976 album De ti depende, produced by Willie Colón for Fania. Curet Alonso was a postal worker and journalist who became salsa's most sophisticated songwriter, known for social themes and elaborate metaphors, and he wrote for Cheo Feliciano, Ismael Rivera and Rubén Blades as well as Lavoe. The newspaper conceit suited the moment: salsa in the mid-1970s was beginning to prize lyrics that could be read as poetry, not just called over a dance band.","The song holds its metaphor all the way through: headlines, front pages, old print that ends up used for wrapping. Curet was a newspaperman himself, which gives the image a wry, insider edge. Listen too for how Lavoe sings the verses nearly deadpan, almost reporting the story, and saves his full voice for the montuno, as if the singer only admits his feelings once the band gives him cover.","It became one of Lavoe's signature songs and a staple of salsa radio, and its lyric is often cited as proof that 1970s salsa had grown up. Some purists prefer the rawer Colón and Lavoe records of the early 1970s, finding the later Fania sound more polished and arranged. Its long montuno has also been cut down on many compilations, which loses much of the point.","Find the full-length album version. Follow the verse for the story, then, when the chorus locks into its refrain, stop listening to words and follow the trombones and timbales pushing the tempo forward. Compare Rubén Blades's Siembra, where the same generation of writers turned salsa toward storytelling on a larger scale."],"shore-the-silence-of-the-lambs":["Howard Shore's score for Jonathan Demme's thriller is almost the opposite of a horror soundtrack. There are few shocks and no screaming strings. Instead the orchestra moves slowly in dark, close harmonies, with low strings, muted brass and soft woodwind, and a sad, rising melody that belongs to Clarice Starling rather than to the killers. Long cues crawl forward like someone walking down a corridor they would rather not enter. The climax in the basement is built on pulse and dread, not volume.","Shore had worked mainly with David Cronenberg, writing austere, unsettling scores for films like Videodrome and The Fly, and had been the first music director of Saturday Night Live. Demme's film, released in 1991, became a critical and popular sensation and swept the five main Academy Awards. Shore's music was not nominated, but it defined how the film felt: grave, sorrowful, and more interested in the young FBI trainee's fear and resolve than in the mechanics of suspense.","Pay attention to whom the music follows. The warmest, most melodic writing attaches to Clarice and to the victims, while Hannibal Lecter's scenes are often scored with near silence or a single sustained colour, which is part of why he seems so self-contained and so frightening. The orchestra writes like a Romantic symphony slowed to a crawl, and Shore uses that inherited weight to make a procedural film sound like a tragedy.","The score was admired by film-music writers from the start, but it was overshadowed by the performances and later by Shore's Lord of the Rings. Retrospectively many see it as a model of the restrained thriller score, the root of the sombre, textural sound Shore himself pushed further in Seven and that later Fincher films took up. Some listeners find it too uniformly grey on album, away from the images.","Start with the opening titles as Clarice runs through the woods, then the final basement cue. Listen for how rarely the music raises its voice. Compare Bernard Herrmann's Psycho, which scores terror with slashing strings, and Mica Levi's Under the Skin, which pushes dread into pure texture."],"run-the-jewels-run-the-jewels":["The first album from El-P and Killer Mike as a duo: ten short, loud tracks that sound like a boxing match between two friends. El-P's production is dense and abrasive, built from distorted synthesizers, rattling drum machines and bass that growls rather than booms. The two rappers trade verses quickly, often line by line, one voice Brooklyn and nasal, the other Atlanta and thunderous. Songs like Banana Clipper, with Big Boi, and 36\" Chain are pure bragging, but the energy is joyous rather than menacing.","El-P had been a key figure in 1990s underground rap with Company Flow and his Definitive Jux label; Killer Mike came up with OutKast's circle in Atlanta. In 2012 El-P produced Mike's solo album R.A.P. Music and they toured together, and the chemistry led to this collaboration. It was released as a free download in June 2013 through Fool's Gold, at a time when both men were in their late thirties and far from the commercial centre of rap.","Listen to how the beats are built around the two voices rather than decorating them: El-P leaves space in the low end for Mike's heavy delivery and fills the high frequencies with his own clipped, cluttered sounds. Notice too how the verses are written as dialogue: one rapper often finishes the other's thought, so the album feels less like two solo artists sharing tracks than a single two-headed voice, which became the group's identity.","Critics welcomed it as a surprise: two veterans making the most exciting rap record of the summer on no budget. Its reputation has been somewhat eclipsed by Run the Jewels 2, which added more politics and range. Some reviewers found it one-note in its aggression, but many fans still hear it as the purest version of the partnership, before the duo became a festival headline act.","Play it straight through; at barely half an hour it works as one long adrenaline rush. Notice how often the verses hand off mid-thought, and how El-P's beats change texture under each rapper. Then go to Run the Jewels 2 to hear the same pair turn their energy toward anger, and to Public Enemy for the noise tradition El-P inherited."],"run-the-jewels-rtj4":["The duo's fourth album, and their most direct: eleven tracks mixing El-P's thick, synth-heavy production with harder, more open drum sounds and even funk guitar. Ooh La La, with DJ Premier scratching and Greg Nice, is a party record; Walking in the Snow and JU$T, featuring Pharrell Williams and Zack de la Rocha, are furious arguments about police violence, prisons and money. The closing track, a slow build called A Few Words for the Firing Squad, ends the record in something close to mourning.","The album was completed before 2020's events, then released in early June, days ahead of schedule, as protests spread across the United States after the killing of George Floyd by Minneapolis police. The duo made it free to download, with an invitation to donate to a legal-aid group supporting protesters. Killer Mike had just given a widely shared speech in Atlanta urging calm and organisation, so the record arrived as part of a public conversation, not only as music.","One verse on Walking in the Snow describes a man choked by police who says he cannot breathe; it was written about Eric Garner's death in 2014, years before Floyd died with the same words, and the coincidence gave the song a terrible second meaning. Listen also to JU$T, which rides on a sample-like figure and a hook that turns American banknotes and slavery into the same argument.","Reviews were among the best of the duo's career, and it appeared on many year-end lists as the record that caught 2020's anger in real time. Some critics argued that its timing made it hard to judge as music at all; others noted that the lyrics were no more topical than on earlier albums, only newly heard. Its reputation has held as the peak of their political writing.","Start with Ooh La La for the fun, then Walking in the Snow for the anger, and hear how the same beat-making serves both. Pay attention to how Killer Mike slows down when the subject gets serious. Then try Public Enemy's Fight the Power and Kendrick Lamar's Alright, two other records that became protest songs in the streets."],"original-dixieland-jazz-band-tiger-rag":["A fast, rowdy New Orleans tune in several strains, played by five white musicians who called themselves the Original Dixieland Jass Band. Nick LaRocca's cornet leads, Larry Shields's clarinet runs around it in squealing lines, Eddie Edwards's trombone slides underneath, and piano and drums keep a relentless two-beat pulse. The famous last strain stops and starts while the trombone growls a low slide, meant to be a tiger's roar. It lasts barely three minutes, the length of a 78 rpm side.","The band came from New Orleans to Chicago and then New York, where their residency at Reisenweber's restaurant in 1917 made them a craze. Their first Victor record that year was the first jazz record ever issued, and they followed with this in 1918. They were not the originators of the style: Black New Orleans bands had played this music for years, but record companies had not recorded them. Acoustic recording, into a horn with no microphone, forced loud playing and simple drums.","The tune itself was older than the band. Jelly Roll Morton later demonstrated for the Library of Congress how he believed it grew out of an old French quadrille played in New Orleans, with its separate sections intact, and several other musicians claimed pieces of it. LaRocca copyrighted it in his own name. Listen for the quadrille shape: four distinct sections, each with its own key and character, strung together like a set of dances.","It became one of the most recorded tunes in jazz, from Louis Armstrong and Duke Ellington to Art Tatum's piano showpiece, and the ODJB version sold widely. Its reputation has since been complicated by LaRocca's later insistence that white musicians invented jazz, a claim historians reject. The playing is now heard as stiff next to King Oliver's band, yet it remains the recording that carried the tune around the world.","Listen through the surface noise for the three horns weaving at once: that collective improvisation is the heart of New Orleans jazz. Wait for the final strain and the trombone's growl. Then play King Oliver's Snake Rag or Armstrong's Hot Fives to hear the same idea in looser, more swinging hands."],"original-dixieland-jazz-band-dixie-jass-band-one-step":["A brisk dance number for the one-step, the fast walking dance then sweeping American ballrooms. The five-piece band plays with high energy and a clipped two-beat rhythm, cornet on melody, clarinet darting above it, trombone filling in the bass line with slides. There is little solo improvisation in the modern sense; the excitement comes from all three horns playing at once in a tight, rehearsed tangle, and from breaks where the band stops dead and one instrument fills the gap.","Recorded for Victor in New York in February 1917 and issued as the other side of Livery Stable Blues, it was half of the first jazz record ever released. The disc sold in huge numbers and turned a regional New Orleans sound into a national fashion within months. The band was playing to white dancers at Reisenweber's, and Victor had chosen them partly because a white band seemed a safer commercial risk than the Black players who had built the style.","One strain of the tune was borrowed from That Teasin' Rag, a 1909 piece by the Black ragtime composer Joe Jordan. Jordan's side objected, and the credit on later issues was reportedly adjusted to acknowledge him. It is a small, telling moment: the very first jazz record already contained an unacknowledged debt to a Black composer, the pattern that would shadow the band's whole reputation.","Because Livery Stable Blues, with its animal noises, drew the attention and the lawsuits, this side has often been treated as the lesser half of the disc. Jazz historians now tend to find it the better music: more coherent, more danceable, closer to how the band sounded live. It is valued more as a document than as a performance, as the moment the music first reached a record.","Imagine a crowded dance floor in 1917 and listen for the tempo; this was music for moving fast. Focus on one horn at a time on repeat plays. Then compare Scott Joplin-era ragtime piano or James P. Johnson's Carolina Shout to hear the written ragtime this music was loosening."],"golden-gate-quartet-jonah":["The Bible story of Jonah and the whale told by four unaccompanied male voices in a fast, swinging style. The narrator half-sings, half-speaks the tale in a rhythmic patter, while the other three singers pump out short repeated syllables underneath, imitating a rhythm section: the bass voice walks like a string bass, the tenors chop out off-beat chords. There are no instruments, yet the record swings like a small jazz band. The tension of the storm and the relief of Jonah's rescue are carried by rhythm and dynamics alone.","The Golden Gate Quartet formed in Norfolk, Virginia, in the 1930s and were among the finest jubilee quartets, the rhythmic, upbeat branch of Black gospel singing that preceded the hard-gospel style of the 1940s. Their Bluebird recordings began in 1937 at a Victor field session in the South and sold widely. Within a year they were singing in John Hammond's From Spirituals to Swing concert at Carnegie Hall, and they later appeared at the White House.","The quartet's secret was that they sang like a swing band. Listen to the bass: he rarely sings words, but plays a walking line exactly as Walter Page did for Count Basie. The narrator's delivery, poised between preaching and scat, is an early relative of what would become rap-like storytelling in gospel and rhythm and blues. The Mills Brothers had done the same with pop songs; the Gates applied it to Scripture.","Their records were hits across both church and secular audiences, and some religious listeners found the jazzy style irreverent. Gospel historians now rank the Gates' Bluebird sides as the high point of jubilee singing, though the style was later overshadowed by the emotional intensity of the Soul Stirrers and Mahalia Jackson. Doo-wop groups of the 1950s drew heavily on their vocal techniques.","Listen once for the story, then again only for the bass voice, and a third time for the chopped chords of the tenors. Notice there is no instrument anywhere. Then compare the Fisk Jubilee Singers for the older, concert style of spirituals, and Count Basie's band for the rhythm the quartet was imitating."],"golden-gate-quartet-stalin-wasnt-stallin":["A wartime topical song, sung in the quartet's usual swinging jubilee style: a narrator tells the story while the other voices keep a chugging rhythm behind him. The subject is not a Bible story but the Second World War. The song praises the Soviet Union as America's ally, casting Hitler as a figure of devilish pride who breaks his pact with Stalin and gets beaten back. The style treats current events the way the Gates treated Scripture, as a parable with a moral and a punchline.","Released in 1943, when the Soviet Union was an American ally and Hollywood and the government encouraged friendly images of the Russians. The quartet had become national figures, singing for President Roosevelt and appearing on radio and in films, and they were closely linked to the left-leaning circle around John Hammond and Café Society in New York. Wartime shellac rationing meant fewer records were made, which gave each release more weight.","Listen to how the structure is borrowed from the sermonising spirituals: a biblical-style narrative of pride and downfall, with Hitler in the role usually taken by Pharaoh or the Devil. The song is credited to the group's baritone and narrator Willie Johnson, whose preacher-like delivery carries it. The form is so old that the political content slides in almost unnoticed.","It was a wartime novelty and sold reasonably, but within a few years the Cold War turned it into an embarrassment, and it was rarely reissued. Later listeners have treated it as a historical curiosity that shows how swiftly the politics of popular song can flip. The singing itself is as polished as anything the group recorded, which is why it is still anthologised.","Listen for the jubilee machinery rather than the politics: the narrator's patter, the bass walking, the chords snapping on the off-beat. Then play the group's Jonah and notice the same method applied to a very different story. Woody Guthrie's topical songs of the same years make a useful comparison."],"john-prine-bruised-orange":["Ten songs in a warm, unhurried band sound, Prine's cracked Illinois voice sitting right in front. The record swings between comedy and grief without warning: a sly fantasy about the child film star Sabu wandering alone through Minneapolis, a shuffling hymn to getting by called Fish and Whistle, and then the title song, a slow meditation on how sorrow gets passed hand to hand. The gentle opener, That's the Way That the World Goes 'Round, sets the tone of shrugging, tender fatalism.","Prine had signed to Asylum after his Atlantic years, and his previous album, Common Sense, had been a muddy, uncertain record. For this one he went home to Chicago and asked his old friend and fellow folk-club veteran Steve Goodman to produce. The sessions were slow and fraught; Prine, by his own later account, doubted almost every song. The late-1970s singer-songwriter market was crowded, and Prine was a cult name rather than a star, admired by Dylan and Kristofferson more than by radio.","The title song probably grows out of a childhood memory from the Chicago suburbs, a boy killed on the railway tracks, and Prine turns it into a parable about bitterness rather than a news story: the point is what the survivors do with the fruit of their grief. Listen too for the co-writing credit on If You Don't Want My Love, shared with Phil Spector, an unlikely pairing that yielded one of Prine's plainest love songs.","Critics received it as a return to form after Common Sense, and it has grown in stature ever since; many Prine admirers now rank it just behind the debut. Its modest sales at the time confirmed his place as a writers' writer. Later songwriters, from Jason Isbell to Kacey Musgraves, have named it as a model of how to be funny and heartbroken inside the same three minutes.","Start with That's the Way That the World Goes 'Round for its easy humour, then go straight to the title song and listen for how little happens in the arrangement, so that the words carry everything. Then hear Sabu Visits the Twin Cities Alone as Prine at his most absurd. Afterwards, compare with his 1971 debut to hear a young writer turned into a seasoned one."],"john-prine-the-tree-of-forgiveness":["A short, lean record of ten songs, most under four minutes, sung by a seventy-one-year-old whose voice had been roughened by two bouts of cancer. The arrangements are acoustic and close, with guests adding harmony here and there. The songs range from a sweet plea to an old flame to a gentle ballad about a town losing its young people, and it closes with When I Get to Heaven, a talking, then singing, comic vision of the afterlife that ends in a joyful singalong.","It was Prine's first album of new original songs in thirteen years, recorded in Nashville with the producer Dave Cobb, who had made his name with Jason Isbell and Sturgill Simpson. Prine had spent the gap touring, recovering from surgery for neck and lung cancer, and running his own label, Oh Boy. Several songs were built from fragments in old notebooks and finished with co-writers. The record became the highest-charting album of his career, and it proved to be his last: Prine died of COVID-19 in April 2020.","Listen for the guest voices tucked into the harmonies: Jason Isbell, Amanda Shires and Brandi Carlile all appear, three younger writers who openly claimed him as a teacher, so the record quietly stages a handing-on of a tradition. Summer's End, which sounds like a simple song of missing someone, gained a second meaning through its video, which tied it to families losing children to opioid addiction.","Reviews were warm to rapturous, many treating it as a late-career victory lap earned rather than granted. Some critics found it slight next to his classics, a set of sketches rather than a statement. Since his death, the closing song has been read as a self-written epitaph, and the album is now heard less as a comeback than as a farewell made with unusual good humour.","Begin at the end with When I Get to Heaven, which shows Prine's comedy and faith side by side and gives you the whole man in five minutes. Then go back to Summer's End and listen for the way the harmony voices come in only when the song needs lifting. Pair it with Johnny Cash's late American Recordings for another great voice facing mortality plainly."],"suzanne-ciani-lixiviation":["A collection of short pieces and fragments made across some fifteen years on the Buchla modular system and its successors. Some are abstract studies: bubbling sequences, glassy bell tones, voices swept through filters. Others are commercial work, sound logos and advertising music made for television, a few seconds long and startlingly vivid. The whole plays like an archive opened at random, bright and playful, and more varied than the calm new-age albums for which Ciani later became known.","Ciani trained as a composer at Berkeley, met Don Buchla in the late 1960s and became one of the first musicians to perform on his instruments. To fund her work she opened a studio in New York in the 1970s, making electronic sounds for commercials, video games and film, work that made her one of the most heard, if least credited, composers of the era. This compilation came from a British reissue label in 2012, just as a new generation of modular-synth players was looking for its forebears.","The advertising cues are the key. The famous sound of a soda bottle opening and pouring, used in television commercials, was made electronically in her studio, as were other brand signatures of the period. Hearing them beside her free pieces shows that the same ear was at work in both: a fascination with sounds that seem physical, wet or tactile, though no object ever made them.","The release played a large part in Ciani's rediscovery. Reviewers who had known her only as a new-age pianist and synthesist were surprised by how rough, witty and experimental her early work was. It fed a wider reassessment of women in early electronic music, alongside Laurie Spiegel, Pauline Oliveros and Wendy Carlos, and led to documentaries, festival bookings and new modular performances by Ciani herself.","Treat it like a sketchbook: listen in short bursts and let each piece be its own small world. Notice how often a sound seems to move around the room or change texture, the Buchla's speciality. Then play her Buchla Concerts 1975 to hear the same vocabulary stretched into long improvisation, and Laurie Spiegel's The Expanding Universe for a kindred mind working with computers."],"stanley-brothers-angel-band":["A slow gospel song sung in close three-part harmony, Carter Stanley leading and his brother Ralph soaring above in the high tenor that became the signature of their sound. The instruments barely move; a guitar marks the beat and a mandolin or fiddle fills the gaps. The song imagines a dying believer asking to be carried home by angels, and the brothers deliver it with the unshowy calm of people who sang such hymns in church every week.","The Stanleys came from the mountains of south-west Virginia and had built a bluegrass band on Bill Monroe's model in the late 1940s. By the mid-1950s they were recording for Mercury and leaning more heavily on sacred material, which suited their Primitive Baptist upbringing and sold steadily to rural audiences even as rock and roll ate into country music's market. The words are a nineteenth-century hymn by Jefferson Hascall set to a tune by William Bradbury, long sung in Southern churches.","Listen to how the harmony parts are placed: Ralph's line sits above the melody rather than below it, giving the chorus a keening, almost bodiless top that suits a song about leaving the body. The effect owes as much to Old Regular and Primitive Baptist lined-out singing, heard in the brothers' childhood churches, as it does to any commercial country model.","It was a modest record in its day, one of many gospel sides. Its fame came later, when the Coen brothers used it over the closing credits of O Brother, Where Art Thou? in 2000, bringing the Stanleys to millions of new listeners. Folk and bluegrass scholars now treat it as a model of mountain gospel harmony, and it has been sung at countless funerals.","Listen first just to Ralph's high voice in the chorus, then play it again and follow Carter's lead underneath. Notice how the tempo never pushes; the song is about arrival, not struggle. Then hear Rank Stranger for the brothers at their most haunted, and Emmylou Harris's Roses in the Snow for a later singer carrying this repertoire forward."],"glenn-branca-lesson-no-1":["About eight minutes of electric guitars strumming the same chords over and over, driven by a relentless drum beat, building slowly in volume and density until the overtones ringing off the strings seem to form new melodies of their own. There are no vocals and no solos. What sounds at first like a single riff held too long turns into something closer to a chorale, a cathedral of distortion built out of repetition and loudness.","Branca was a theatre-trained musician who had moved to New York in the mid-1970s and played in the no-wave bands Theoretical Girls and The Static. Downtown Manhattan at that moment mixed punk clubs, art lofts and the aftermath of minimalism, and Branca wanted to fuse them. This piece was the first release on 99 Records, a small label run from a record shop, and it put him on the map as a composer rather than a band member.","The piece is less about the notes played than about the notes produced by the collision of several guitars at loud volume: listen past the strumming and you will hear high singing tones that no one is playing directly. That discovery, that amplified guitars could generate harmonic clouds, drove all of Branca's later work, including the symphonies for massed guitars.","Downtown critics hailed it as a bridge between rock energy and minimalist process, and it circulated widely among young musicians. Its influence came mostly through players who passed through Branca's ensembles, above all Thurston Moore and Lee Ranaldo of Sonic Youth. Some minimalist composers were dismissive of its bluntness; today it is seen as a founding text of noise-rock and of guitar-based drone music.","Play it loud, since at low volume the overtones never appear. Listen for the moment, a few minutes in, when the chords seem to blur and a higher shimmer rises over them. Then follow the thread forward to The Ascension and to Sonic Youth's Daydream Nation, and back to Terry Riley's In C for the minimalist ancestor."],"glenn-branca-symphony-no-3":["Subtitled Music for the First 127 Intervals of the Harmonic Series, the symphony trades the massed guitars of Branca's earlier work for home-made instruments tuned to the natural overtones of a single fundamental. The result is a huge, ringing, slowly shifting wall of sound, closer to bells and organs than to rock, over which drums pound. There are several long movements, each stacking and swelling overtones until the air itself seems to vibrate.","By the early 1980s Branca was running a large ensemble of downtown musicians and presenting symphonies in New York's alternative venues and festivals. After his first two symphonies for multiple guitars, he became interested in the acoustics of the overtone series, the physical basis of harmony that composers from Harry Partch to La Monte Young had explored through alternative tunings. He designed and built instruments to play these exact pitches, a practical feat as much as a musical one.","The subtitle is literal: each new pitch belongs to the natural series of overtones above one low note, so the harmony moves not by chords but by climbing the series. As the music reaches high, closely spaced overtones, the pitches become unfamiliar and slightly sour to Western ears, and that strangeness is the point, a sound older than any scale.","Its premiere confirmed Branca as a serious composer to some and a noise merchant to others; the arguments echoed those about Partch and Young. John Cage was famously hostile to Branca's music, reading it as authoritarian. Recordings have made the piece better known than its rare live performances, and it is now valued for its acoustic ambition as much as its volume.","Listen on speakers rather than earbuds and let the sound build around you. Do not look for a melody; follow instead how the texture thickens and brightens as the harmonic series climbs. Then compare it with La Monte Young's The Well-Tuned Piano, which explores similar pure tunings in near-silence rather than at full volume."],"ruben-blades-buscando-america":["Salsa without its horns. Blades's new band, Seis del Solar, replaced the blaring trumpet and trombone section of New York salsa with synthesizer, vibraphone and a tight rhythm section, making room for long, story-telling songs. The album includes a portrait of a secret policeman setting off to make an arrest, a song about a priest and an altar boy gunned down in church, a lament for the disappeared of Latin America's dictatorships, and a title song searching for a continent that has lost itself.","Blades had become the thinking man's salsa star at Fania with Willie Colón and Siembra, but he chafed at the label's business practices. Buscando América was his move to Elektra, a major rock label, and a bid to reach English-speaking listeners without giving up Spanish. Central America was in the headlines: civil wars in El Salvador and Guatemala, the killing of Archbishop Óscar Romero in 1980, and the continent-wide practice of forced disappearance.","El Padre Antonio y el Monaguillo Andrés never names Romero, but its story of a priest shot at the altar is transparently his; Blades shifts details so that the song stands for every murdered cleric. Listen also for how the missing horns change the music's grammar: the vibraphone takes the answering phrases a brass section would play, lending the protest songs a cool, almost jazz-like distance.","It was widely hailed as a landmark of socially conscious Latin music and earned a Grammy nomination, while some salsa purists missed the horns and dance-floor heat. The album cemented Blades as a political songwriter, a path that later took him into Panamanian politics, including a presidential run and a spell as tourism minister. It is now a staple of lists of the greatest Latin albums.","Start with Desapariciones and listen for how the voice builds from quiet reportage to a chant. Then hear El Padre Antonio for its long narrative shape and the choral refrain that turns grief into a public ritual. Play Siembra next to hear what Blades left behind, and Víctor Jara for the Latin American protest song he extends."],"ruben-blades-maestra-vida":["A two-part salsa opera, originally issued as two separate LPs, that follows a working-class couple, Carmelo and Manuela, from courtship through marriage, poverty, the raising of a son, old age and death in a Latin American barrio. Blades wrote the songs and sings the central roles, with Willie Colón's horn-heavy band supplying the music and guest voices taking other parts. Dance numbers alternate with ballads, spoken scenes and choral refrains.","After Siembra became the best-selling record in salsa's history, Blades and Colón had the leverage to attempt something longer and more ambitious. Fania was a New York label built on dance hits, and a story album about ordinary lives, with no single obvious hit, was a risk. Blades had a law degree and literary ambitions, and he had been writing sketches of barrio life, drawn from his Panamanian childhood, for years.","The title means 'Life, the Teacher', and time is almost a character: listen for how the musical style ages with the couple, from bright, youthful dance tunes in the first part to slower, more reflective writing as the second part moves toward bereavement. The recurring chorus frames the whole as a lesson about what life does to people who never make the news.","It sold far less than Siembra, and some reviewers found it uneven, with stretches of narrative that stall the dancing. Over time, though, it has been reassessed as one of salsa's most ambitious projects. The partnership of Blades and Colón broke up in recrimination soon after, which gives the record an added sense of an ending.","Take it one side at a time and listen as you would to a radio play, following the lives rather than waiting for hits. Begin with the opening songs of Part One to meet the characters, then jump to the last scenes of Part Two. Compare Weill and Brecht's The Threepenny Opera for another popular-song theatre of the poor."],"t-bone-burnett-truth-decay":["Burnett's first proper solo album is a spare, dry, small-band record rooted in rockabilly, country blues and early rock and roll, with his nasal Texan voice singing clever, moralising songs about vanity, advertising, lust and self-deception. The playing is lean and live-sounding, with guitar lines that could come from a Sun single of 1956. Every track is short, and the whole album has the feel of a sermon delivered by a man who suspects he is also the sinner.","Burnett grew up in Fort Worth, worked as a producer in Texas, and then joined Bob Dylan's Rolling Thunder Revue in 1975 and 1976. Afterwards he co-founded the Alpha Band, which made three admired, commercially unsuccessful albums. By 1980 he was a professing Christian writing songs of faith for a secular audience, and he made this record for Takoma, a small label better known for folk guitarists, as punk and new wave were reshaping rock.","The title is a pun that sets the whole agenda: the songs return again and again to the idea that modern life, and especially advertising and the entertainment business, wears truth away. Burnett's faith is present everywhere yet almost never named; he writes as a satirist in the Old Testament tradition rather than as a preacher, which let him avoid the Christian-rock ghetto.","Critics liked it, and it has had a quiet cult reputation ever since, particularly among songwriters. Sales were small. Its larger importance became visible later, when Burnett's love of stripped, early American sounds shaped his work as a producer, from Elvis Costello and Los Lobos to the O Brother soundtrack and Robert Plant and Alison Krauss's Raising Sand.","Listen to it as a record of lean guitar sound first, noticing how much empty space surrounds each instrument. Then attend to the words, which reward close hearing for their jokes and barbs. Pair it with Elvis Presley's Mystery Train to hear the Sun Records sound Burnett adored, and with the O Brother soundtrack to see where that love led."],"t-bone-burnett-the-criminal-under-my-own-hat":["A darker, more textured record than Burnett's early work, with acoustic guitars, murky low percussion and echoing electric guitar colouring songs about self-knowledge, public morality and the confusions of love. Burnett sings in a hushed, conversational voice, often half-speaking. The sound anticipates the dusky, reverberant Americana he would later give to other artists: close-miked voices, thudding drums, a sense of songs heard from the far side of a dark room.","By the early 1990s Burnett was better known as a producer than as a performer, having worked on records by Elvis Costello, Los Lobos, Roy Orbison and Sam Phillips, his wife at the time. This was his first album for Columbia and turned out to be his last solo record for fourteen years. The alternative-rock boom was under way, and his brooding, literate songs sat awkwardly beside grunge on the radio.","The title confesses the album's theme: the enemy is inside one's own head. Many songs turn their accusations back on the singer, and Burnett uses his production skills to mirror that, placing his voice unusually low in the mix so that you lean in, like a listener overhearing a confession rather than receiving a performance.","It earned a Grammy nomination for Best Contemporary Folk Album and good reviews, but sold modestly, and Burnett largely withdrew from making his own records. Critics now hear it as a key to his later production style; the sound world of O Brother and of Raising Sand is already here, though applied to his own sardonic songs rather than old tunes.","Listen on headphones, which reveal the careful placement of the low drums and the soft voice. Start with the opening tracks to catch the sound, then follow the words across the album as a single argument about pride and conscience. Afterwards play Raising Sand to hear the same sonic ideas serving other singers."],"the-weavers-wimoweh":["A buoyant, chanting record in which the four Weavers repeat a single rhythmic word over a driving vocal bass, while Pete Seeger rises above in a high, wordless falsetto melody. Gordon Jenkins's orchestra and chorus fill out the sound with brass and strings, turning a South African choral song into American pop. It is almost entirely repetition and harmony, with the excitement coming from the overlapping parts and Seeger's soaring line.","The song began as Mbube, recorded in Johannesburg in 1939 by Solomon Linda and his Evening Birds, a Zulu vocal group, and became a hit in South Africa. Alan Lomax passed a copy to Pete Seeger, who adapted it for the Weavers, then at the peak of their fame at Decca with Goodnight, Irene. Within a year, the group would be blacklisted for their left-wing associations during the McCarthy era.","The title is a mishearing: Seeger heard the Zulu chant of Linda's chorus and rendered it as a nonsense word. The soaring falsetto line he sang is Linda's own improvised melody. A decade later the Tokens added English words and turned it into The Lion Sleeps Tonight, and the song's long life in pop made fortunes for others while Linda died poor in 1962.","It was a hit and helped introduce African music to a mass American audience. Later, it became a case study in the ethics of folk borrowing: a South African journalist's 2000 exposé of Linda's lost royalties led to a 2006 settlement for his heirs. Seeger himself, who had sent some money to Linda's family, came to regret the copyright arrangements behind the song.","Listen first for the bass chant underneath, then for Seeger's falsetto, and notice how the orchestra adds glamour but not substance. Then find Solomon Linda's original Mbube and hear the older, rawer version from which everything grows. Finish with Miriam Makeba to hear South African song in the voice of a South African star."],"bruce-springsteen-born-in-the-usa":["Twelve songs by Springsteen and the E Street Band, polished into big, bright 1980s rock: booming snare drums, a ringing synthesizer riff on the title track, and glossy keyboards. Beneath the arena sound, many of the songs are as bleak as those on Nebraska: laid-off workers, a Vietnam veteran shut out by his country, a small-town life closing in. Lighter songs such as Glory Days and Dancing in the Dark balance the darker material with humour and restlessness.","Many songs came from the same 1982 sessions as Nebraska; Springsteen had tried the title song as a solo acoustic piece before cutting it with the full band. The album arrived amid Reagan's reelection campaign, MTV's rise and a booming market for blockbuster albums like Thriller. It became one of the best-selling records of the decade, tying a record with seven Top Ten singles, and turned Springsteen into a global stadium star.","The title song is the famous case of an anthem heard upside down. Its chorus sounds like celebration, but the verses tell of a veteran who comes home to no job and no help. During the 1984 campaign, a columnist and then President Reagan praised Springsteen's message of hope, prompting him to point audiences to the song's actual story.","Reviews were strong and sales colossal, but critics and fans have argued since about the production, which some find dated and overblown next to Darkness on the Edge of Town or Nebraska. Springsteen himself has expressed mixed feelings. Others insist that smuggling hard stories into radio hits was the point. The political misreading has become a textbook lesson in how music is heard.","Start with the title song, and listen to the verses rather than the chorus. Then play Nebraska's version of the same worries, stripped bare, to see what the band adds and hides. Listen to Glory Days for the E Street Band's party side, and My Hometown for the quiet ending, where the album's heart lies."],"tom-waits-small-change":["Eleven songs of late-night Los Angeles, sung in a voice already turned to gravel, over a small jazz combo of piano, upright bass, brushed drums and tenor saxophone, with strings on a few tracks. Some are ballads of drunk, lonely people; some are spoken, jazzy monologues, such as Step Right Up, a machine-gun parody of a salesman's pitch. The opener, Tom Traubert's Blues, sets the tone of mourning, using a borrowed Australian song as its chorus.","Waits had arrived at Asylum as a sensitive singer-songwriter and drifted steadily towards a persona of the barfly poet, living in a Hollywood motel and absorbing Kerouac, jazz and old standards. Small Change was made with the producer Bones Howe and a group of seasoned jazz players, including the drummer Shelly Manne. It became his first album to enter the Billboard Top 100, though barely, and its success confirmed his image as a nocturnal chronicler of the down and out.","Tom Traubert's Blues borrows the chorus of Waltzing Matilda, the Australian bush ballad, and turns it into a song about being lost and drunk far from home; knowing this reveals the wanderer theme across the album. Also notice how the persona is a performance: Waits, then in his mid-twenties, deliberately sounds like a man twice his age.","Critics praised the writing and the jazz sophistication, though some found the character act mannered. It is now widely held as the finest album of his early Asylum period. Rod Stewart's 1992 cover of Tom Traubert's Blues brought the song to a mass audience, and the album is frequently named as the bridge between his early ballads and the experimental later work.","Begin with Tom Traubert's Blues and listen for the moment the familiar Australian refrain enters. Then try Step Right Up for Waits's verbal comedy, and The Piano Has Been Drinking for his gift of bar-room absurdity. Finally play Swordfishtrombones to hear how completely he later abandoned this polished nightclub sound."],"jaco-pastorius-word-of-mouth":["Jaco Pastorius's second solo album is less a bass player's record than an orchestrator's. Six pieces move from the churning free-for-all of 'Crisis' through the long, patient 'Three Views of a Secret', the brass-heavy funk of 'Liberty City', a solo-bass reading of Bach's Chromatic Fantasy that dissolves into the Beatles' 'Blackbird', and a closing tone poem for strings and voices. The sound is huge and warm: massed horns, Caribbean steel pan and harmonica floating over his singing fretless bass.","Pastorius made it in 1980 and 1981 while still the bassist of Weather Report, newly signed to Warner Bros. as a leader. Much of it was built by layering: he recorded rhythm parts first and had guests such as Herbie Hancock, Wayne Shorter, Jack DeJohnette, Peter Erskine, Michael Brecker and Toots Thielemans add to them, sometimes in separate sessions and cities. The album's title became the name of the big band he toured with, and the record appeared as his life was beginning to unravel.","On 'Crisis', the players reportedly heard only Jaco's racing bass line while recording their own parts, so nobody was answering anybody: the chaos is assembled, not jammed, which is why it sounds so strangely airless. Listen too for the join between Bach and 'Blackbird' — he treats the Beatles tune as if it were another Baroque chorale, and the bass harmonics make the switch almost seamless.","Reviewers in 1981 were puzzled that the most famous bassist in jazz had made a record where the bass often sits in the background, and sales were modest. The album's standing has risen steadily since: arrangers now cite 'Three Views of a Secret' and 'Liberty City' as big-band writing of real ambition, and the record is usually heard as proof that Pastorius's gift was compositional as much as technical.","Start with 'Liberty City', his joyful portrait of a Miami neighbourhood, and follow the way the horn chords pile up over a simple, bouncing bass figure. Then go back to 'Three Views of a Secret' and let Thielemans's harmonica carry the melody. Compare the writing with Gil Evans, whose big-band colours Jaco openly loved."],"jaco-pastorius-invitation":["A live big-band album in which the Word of Mouth ensemble finally sounds like a working band rather than a studio construction. The title track takes Bronisław Kaper's standard at a furious tempo; 'Soul Intro / The Chicken' turns a greasy funk riff into a shout chorus for the whole brass section; 'Continuum' floats his fretless bass over a hushed backdrop. Trumpet, saxophones and trombones, Othello Molineaux's steel pan and Toots Thielemans's harmonica trade the spotlight with the leader.","The music was recorded on the Word of Mouth band's tour of Japan in 1982, where Pastorius was treated as a star, and released by Warner Bros. the following year. The band included Randy Brecker, Bob Mintzer and Peter Erskine. It was one of his last major releases: by then his drinking and a then-undiagnosed bipolar disorder were making his behaviour erratic, and within a few years he was playing on the streets and in pickup bands in New York and Florida.","Listen to how much of the ensemble writing is built from bass-player thinking: the horn lines often move like his own walking and funk patterns, just orchestrated for ten mouths. The steel pan is not decoration either; Molineaux often doubles the bass line an octave or two up, giving the band its bright, Caribbean top edge, a sound Pastorius knew from growing up in South Florida.","Critics tend to prefer Invitation to its studio predecessor for its energy, and many bassists and big-band players regard it as the best record of the Word of Mouth band. Japan also received a fuller two-volume release, Twins, and some listeners argue that set gives the truer picture of the concerts. The debate about how much of his later decline is audible here remains unresolved.","Play the title track first, loud, and notice that the bass and drums never let the tempo sag even as the arrangement grows dense. Then compare 'Continuum' with the version on his 1976 debut: the tune is the same, but here it sits inside a live room. Follow with Weather Report's Heavy Weather to hear where the sound began."],"modern-jazz-quartet-fontessa":["The Modern Jazz Quartet's first album for Atlantic sets pianist John Lewis's composed pieces beside ballads and bebop tunes. It opens with 'Versailles', a small fugue in which vibraphone and piano chase the same subject, and centres on the title suite, roughly eleven minutes long, whose short sections sketch characters from the Italian commedia dell'arte. Around them sit 'Angel Eyes', 'Over the Rainbow', 'Willow Weep for Me' and Milt Jackson's blues, all played with chamber-music quiet and swing underneath.","By 1956 the quartet had settled into its classic lineup of Lewis, Milt Jackson on vibes, Percy Heath on bass and the newly arrived Connie Kay on drums, and had moved from Prestige to Atlantic. They performed in dark suits on concert stages rather than in clubs, a deliberate bid for the respect given to classical ensembles. Lewis had studied European music seriously and wanted jazz to borrow its forms without losing the blues.","In 'Versailles' the fugue is real: each instrument enters with the theme in turn, and Heath's bass takes its own entry rather than simply keeping time. Listen too to Connie Kay's kit, which includes small bells and a triangle alongside brushes; those tiny metallic sounds become part of the Fontessa suite's commedia masks, giving each character a colour as much as a tune.","The album was widely praised and helped make the quartet one of the best-selling jazz groups of its time, but it also sharpened a lasting argument. Admirers heard jazz gaining form and polish; detractors heard stiffness and a courtier's bow to Europe. Today most listeners notice how hard the group swings under the surface, and that Jackson's blues playing keeps the refinement honest.","Begin with 'Versailles' and try to follow the fugue subject as it passes between vibes and piano. Then listen to 'Bluesology' to hear the same musicians relax completely. Compare the Fontessa suite with Stravinsky's Pulcinella, another modern piece dressed in commedia costumes, and with Miles Davis's Birth of the Cool."],"lucinda-williams-sweet-old-world":["Lucinda Williams's fourth album is a record about loss, played by a supple roots band of guitars, bass, drums, with touches of accordion and fiddle. Several songs circle death: 'Pineola' recounts the funeral of a friend who took his own life, and the title song addresses someone who has died, listing the ordinary pleasures they gave up. Around them sit tough love songs like 'Hot Blood' and 'Six Blocks Away', and a cover of Nick Drake's 'Which Will'.","Released in 1992 on Chameleon, a small label distributed by Elektra, it followed her self-titled 1988 record and a long, frustrating struggle with labels over how her songs should sound. She co-produced with Gurf Morlix and Dusty Wakeman in Los Angeles. The Americana and alternative-country scenes were only just taking shape, and radio had no format for a Louisiana-raised songwriter who mixed country, blues and folk with a poet's eye.","Williams's father was the poet Miller Williams, and the songs work like poems: concrete place names, objects and weather rather than abstractions. 'Pineola' is named after a real crossroads, and the grief in it comes almost entirely through details of the burial day. Listen for how the band stays deliberately plain there, letting the drawl and cracks in her voice carry the weight.","The album was admired by critics and fellow writers more than it was bought, but its songs travelled: Emmylou Harris recorded the title track on Wrecking Ball in 1995, and other singers have since taken up her songs from it. It is now seen as the bridge between her early records and Car Wheels on a Gravel Road, and some prefer its looser, sadder spirit to that album's polish.","Hear 'Pineola' first and pay attention to what she describes rather than what she says she feels. Then play the title song beside Emmylou Harris's version to hear how the same words shift in another voice. Follow with Car Wheels on a Gravel Road, where the same eye for detail meets a harder band sound."],"the-drifters-save-the-last-dance-for-me":["Ben E. King sings lead over a light Latin rhythm, a Brazilian-style baião beat on bass and percussion, with soft strings sweeping behind and the other Drifters answering in close harmony. The song's speaker tells a partner to enjoy the dance, laugh and flirt with anyone, but to remember who is taking them home and who gets the final dance. It is short, graceful and built around one long, patient melody that keeps returning to its promise.","The song was written by Doc Pomus and Mort Shuman in New York's Brill Building world and produced for Atlantic by Jerry Leiber and Mike Stoller, who had already brought strings into rhythm and blues with 'There Goes My Baby'. Pomus, who had polio and used crutches, is said to have drawn it from his own wedding, watching his wife dance while he could not. It reached number one in the United States in 1960.","Knowing Pomus's story turns the lyric from possessive to generous: it is less a warning than a man's permission to his partner to take the joy he cannot share, with one small request at the end. Musically, listen to how the Latin rhythm never pushes; it simply sways, which is why the song sounds more like a waltz of patience than a dance-floor stomp.","It was the Drifters' biggest hit and Ben E. King's last major record with them before going solo with 'Stand by Me'. Critics have long cited it as the peak of the uptown soul sound, where rhythm and blues met orchestral pop. The later discovery of Pomus's background made it one of the most discussed songs of its era, and it has been recorded many times since.","Listen first just to the bass and percussion pattern, which carries the whole Latin lilt, then to how the strings enter under King's voice. Compare it with 'There Goes My Baby' to hear the formula become smooth and assured, and with Phil Spector's early productions, which took the same strings-and-rhythm idea and turned it into a wall."],"augustus-pablo-java":["An instrumental built on a slow, heavy reggae rhythm with the bass deep in front, over which Augustus Pablo plays a minor-key melody on the melodica, a small keyboard wind instrument usually found in school classrooms. The tune winds and repeats with a slightly Eastern, modal cast, and the thin, reedy tone floating over the thick bass is what makes the record unmistakable. There are no vocals, just the tune and the groove.","Pablo, born Horace Swaby, was still a teenager when he recorded it in Kingston around 1971 at Randy's studio for the young producer Clive Chin. Jamaican music was moving from rocksteady into reggae, and producers were issuing instrumental and dub versions on the backs of singles. Nobody had treated the melodica as a lead instrument; Pablo had taken it up almost by accident and made it his voice.","The record helped fix Pablo's idea of what he called a Far East sound, a mood of mystic minor keys he pursued for the rest of his career. Listen for how sparse the arrangement really is, with guitar and organ reduced to tiny off-beat touches. Clive Chin later issued a dub album built around versions of this tune, a sign of how quickly Jamaican producers treated a hit as raw material.","A hit in Jamaica, Java made Pablo's name overnight and put the melodica permanently into reggae. It is now regarded as a foundation stone of instrumental and dub reggae, a record that let later producers think of a rhythm as a landscape to be explored. Its influence runs through Pablo's own work with King Tubby and through later dub and electronic music.","Play it on speakers with real bass, and listen to the space around the melodica rather than just the tune. Then go straight to King Tubby Meets Rockers Uptown to hear the same sensibility dissolved into echo. U-Roy's toasting records of the same years show another way Jamaican musicians were reshaping the single."],"steve-earle-train-a-comin":["An all-acoustic album with no drums: Steve Earle's worn voice and guitar, flatpicking, mandolin and upright bass, and harmony singing. The songs mix new writing with pieces he had carried for years, among them the tender 'Goodbye', the Civil War story song 'Ben McCulloch', and covers of Townes Van Zandt's 'Tecumseh Valley' and the Beatles' 'I'm Looking Through You'. The mood is quiet, reflective and bruised, closer to a front-porch session than a Nashville record.","Earle had spent the early 1990s in addiction, with no records released for years, and he served time in 1994 on drug charges before getting clean. This was his first album afterwards, made quickly for the small label Winter Harvest with bluegrass and folk veterans Norman Blake, Peter Rowan and Roy Huskey Jr., and Emmylou Harris singing harmony. It announced one of the most celebrated comebacks in American roots music.","Several of the songs predate his collapse; he had written some before his first major-label deal and never recorded them, so the album is partly a return to who he was at the start. Listen to the absence of drums: the bass and guitars have to keep time themselves, which makes the playing lean in closer and gives every string noise and breath its place.","Critics welcomed it as proof that Earle had survived with his gifts intact, and it earned a Grammy nomination in the folk category. Emmylou Harris recorded 'Goodbye' on Wrecking Ball the same year, bringing the song a wider audience. It now reads as the hinge of his career, the first of a run of records in the later 1990s that many rank as his best work.","Start with 'Goodbye' and notice how little he does with the voice; the restraint is the point. Then hear Harris's version on Wrecking Ball, with Daniel Lanois's atmospheric production, for a striking contrast. Go back to Guitar Town to hear the brash young man this older singer is looking back on."],"afrika-bambaataa-renegades-of-funk":["Credited to Afrika Bambaataa and the Soulsonic Force, the track is a dense collage of electronic drums, synthesizer stabs, crashing sampled-sounding hits and shouted group rapping. Its message is a roll call: the rappers claim a lineage of rebels and visionaries, naming figures from Sitting Bull and Thomas Paine to Martin Luther King and Malcolm X, and cast hip-hop's young innovators as the next in that line. It is louder and more aggressive than Planet Rock, with little room to breathe.","It followed Planet Rock on Tommy Boy Records in New York, again produced with Arthur Baker and keyboardist John Robie. Bambaataa, a former gang member from the Bronx River Houses, had founded the Universal Zulu Nation to turn street energy toward music, dance, graffiti and knowledge. The record carried that programme into the lyrics just as rap was beginning to reach beyond the South Bronx block parties.","The rhythm is not straightforward electro: the drum pattern keeps shifting and breaking, and many of the hits are layered orchestral and noise sounds that anticipate the sampler-driven collage of the later 1980s. Listen for how the list of historical names functions like a history lesson set to a beat; it is one of the first rap records to present the genre as a political tradition rather than a party.","Less of a sales event than Planet Rock, it has grown in stature as one of the first politically conscious rap records, and its noisy production is often cited as a bridge to Public Enemy's Bomb Squad. Rage Against the Machine covered it in 2000. Bambaataa's later disgrace, after accusations of abuse made public in 2016, has complicated how the record and his role are celebrated.","Play it right after Planet Rock and notice how much harder and more jagged it is. Follow the list of names the rappers call out and think of it as a manifesto. Then jump to Public Enemy's Fight the Power to hear where this blend of noise and history ended up."],"the-shirelles-tonights-the-night":["Shirley Owens sings lead in a soft, slightly uncertain voice over a gentle rhythm-and-blues shuffle, with the other three Shirelles answering behind her and strings adding a sweet, cinematic frame. The song's narrator faces a night she knows will change things with her boyfriend, and she swings between excitement and nerves, unsure whether she is ready. The arrangement is light and quick; the whole thing is over in about two minutes.","The Shirelles were four schoolfriends from Passaic, New Jersey, signed to Florence Greenberg's Scepter label, where producer Luther Dixon shaped their sound. Owens and Dixon wrote the song together, and it reached the American top forty in 1960. It came out just before Will You Love Me Tomorrow, the Carole King and Gerry Goffin song that would become the group's first number one and the first by a Black girl group.","Heard next to Will You Love Me Tomorrow, the two records form a pair: here the question is whether to go ahead, there it is what will happen the morning after. That sequence, written partly by a teenage singer herself, shows how early girl-group pop let young women voice doubt and desire on their own terms. Listen for the way Owens's voice wavers slightly on purpose; the hesitancy is part of the story.","It was a modest hit on first release, overshadowed by the Shirelles' number ones, but historians of the girl-group era now cite it as one of the first records in which the young woman's inner debate is the whole subject. Its co-writing credit for Owens is also noted, since singers in that world rarely got one. The song was widely covered afterwards.","Listen to it immediately before Will You Love Me Tomorrow and hear the story continue. Notice how the strings stay in the background, giving the song polish without drowning the voice. Then compare the Ronettes' Be My Baby to hear how Phil Spector turned the same girl-group scenario into a roar."],"marshall-jefferson-ive-lost-control":["Released under the name Sleezy D, the track pairs a relentless drum machine with a squelching, bending bass-synthesizer line that grows more distorted as it repeats. Over it a voice mutters and cries out about losing control, sounding less like singing than a nervous breakdown on the dance floor. There is almost no melody; the tension comes from the bass line twisting in tone while the beat never lets up. It feels dark, hypnotic and slightly frightening.","Marshall Jefferson produced it in Chicago with Derrick Harris, the dancer and scene figure known as Sleezy D, who supplied the vocal, and it came out on Trax Records in 1986. That year Chicago house was moving from disco-rooted records to rawer drum-machine tracks. Cheap Roland equipment, discarded by musicians who found it useless, was being bought secondhand and pushed into strange new sounds by young producers.","The squelching bass is generally identified as a Roland TB-303 bass-line machine, the device at the heart of acid house, which makes this one of the earliest released records to use it in that way; histories argue over exactly which came first. Listen for the gradual change in the bass tone as knobs are turned during the take, the gesture that acid house would build a genre on.","It was a cult record in Chicago clubs rather than a hit, and its reputation grew as acid house exploded in Britain in 1987 and 1988. Historians now place it with Phuture's Acid Tracks at the origin of acid house, and argue over which reached dance floors first. It also shows Jefferson's range beyond the joyful piano house of Move Your Body.","Play it loud and follow only the bass line, noticing how its colour keeps changing while the notes stay much the same. Then play Phuture's Acid Tracks to compare two early experiments with the same machine. Finish with Move Your Body to hear the same producer at his most celebratory."],"marshall-jefferson-someday":["Credited to the singer Ce Ce Rogers, this is a house record built for a church-trained voice. A steady drum machine and warm synthesizer chords lay down a slow-burning groove, and Rogers sings in full gospel style about a better world to come, where people live in peace and the struggle ends. The long arrangement builds patiently, letting the voice climb and testify over the beat until the track feels closer to a sermon than a club tune.","Marshall Jefferson produced it in 1987 after the success of Move Your Body had made him one of Chicago house's leading names. Released by Atlantic, it was among the first house records put out by a major American label, at a time when the music lived on small local imprints like Trax and DJ International. Its hopeful message reflected the Black and gay club communities where house was born.","Jefferson has spoken of wanting house to carry real songs and real singing, not only tracks; this record is his clearest statement of that aim, and it points toward the deep house of Larry Heard and the gospel house of the 1990s. Listen to how the arrangement leaves space for the voice, with the drum machine kept almost polite, very different from his raw acid productions.","It was not a large pop hit, but it became a club anthem and is regularly cited as a founding record of deep and gospel house. Critics and DJs point to it as proof that house could carry spiritual and social meaning, and it remains in DJ sets decades later. Some purists at the time distrusted the major-label move; that argument has faded.","Hear it as a gospel song first and a dance record second: follow Rogers's voice as it rises. Then compare it with Frankie Knuckles's Your Love for another early marriage of emotion and machine. Mahalia Jackson's Move On Up a Little Higher shows the church tradition this voice comes from."],"the-ronettes-baby-i-love-you":["Two and a half minutes of Wall of Sound built as a follow-up to Be My Baby. Ronnie Spector's voice, nasal, pleading and slightly ragged at the top, rides over a slow, rolling beat thickened by several pianos, doubled guitars, castanets, sleigh-bell-like percussion and a wash of strings. The song is almost nothing but a declaration repeated with growing insistence; the recognisable gesture is the long, climbing vocal cry on the title words, answered by a choir that seems to fill the room behind her.","Written by Phil Spector with the Brill Building husband-and-wife team Jeff Barry and Ellie Greenwich, the record was cut at Gold Star Studios in Los Angeles in late 1963, while Be My Baby was still on the radio. Spector ran Philles as a singles label, and each release had to sound like an event. The Ronettes were touring, and the session is usually said to have been completed with Ronnie alone, the familiar harmony parts filled by Los Angeles session singers rather than her sister Estelle and cousin Nedra.","Listen to the backing voices: they are denser and more gospel-shaded than the thin, girlish blend of Be My Baby, which fits the account that other singers, reportedly including Darlene Love, stood in for the group. Notice too how little the drums do; Spector let the pianos and percussion carry the pulse, so the rhythm seems to come from the whole room's echo rather than from any one player.","It was a solid but smaller hit than its predecessor, and critics have long treated it as the lesser twin of Be My Baby. Its reputation grew through its afterlife: Spector himself produced a lush version for the Ramones on End of the Century in 1980, a meeting of punk and girl-group pop that still divides fans. Many now hear the Ronettes' original as one of Spector's most tender productions, less of a manifesto and more of a love letter.","Play it straight after Be My Baby and listen for what Spector kept and what he softened: the beat is gentler, the strings warmer, the voice more exposed. Then hear the Ramones' 1980 version for the odd pleasure of Joey Ramone inside a Spector string arrangement. Seek the original mono single; later stereo mixes lose the fused, single-instrument roar."],"the-ronettes-walking-in-the-rain":["The record opens with a crack of thunder and the hiss of falling rain, and the storm never quite leaves. Over a slow, swaying rhythm of pianos, guitars and strings, Ronnie Spector sings of a boy she has not yet met: she lists the qualities he must have, above all that he should love walking in the rain as much as she does. The effects rise and fall between the verses, so the weather becomes a second instrument, dark and romantic, against the hopefulness of the song.","Spector wrote it with Barry Mann and Cynthia Weil, the Brill Building team who had just given him You've Lost That Lovin' Feelin'. It was recorded at Gold Star in Los Angeles in 1964, with engineer Larry Levine building the storm from sound-effect material mixed into the echo chambers. By then the British Invasion was crowding American girl groups off the charts, and the Ronettes, who had toured Britain with the Rolling Stones, were finding hits harder to come by.","The thunder is not decoration laid on top: listen to how its low rumble sits in the same echo as the drums, so on a mono player the storm and the rhythm section blur together. The sound design won the record a Grammy for best sound effects, an unusual honour for a pop single and a sign of how far Spector had moved the studio itself to the centre of the music.","It was only a modest chart success, and it marked the start of the Ronettes' commercial decline, yet among producers and critics it has grown steadily in stature. Many rank it beside Be My Baby as the group's most atmospheric side. The debate it raises is the familiar Spector one: whether the singers were artists or instruments in his orchestra. Ronnie's calm, yearning vocal is the best argument that she was the former.","Use headphones and a mono source if you can, and listen for the moment the thunder returns under the final chorus. Then compare the Shangri-Las' Remember (Walking in the Sand), from the same year, which uses seagulls and surf in the same spirit, and Beethoven's Pastoral storm for a much older idea of weather as music."],"the-supremes-stop-in-the-name-of-love":["A sustained organ chord and a sudden shout of the title word open the record, and that command returns like a traffic signal at every chorus. Diana Ross sings as a woman who knows her partner is slipping off to see someone else and asks him to think again before he breaks her heart. Behind her, Florence Ballard and Mary Wilson answer in smooth harmony, while the Funk Brothers' bass, tambourine and snare keep Motown's four-on-the-floor stomp driving underneath the heartbreak.","Holland, Dozier and Holland, Motown's in-house writing and production team, wrote it in early 1965 while the Supremes were on an unbroken run of number ones that had begun with Where Did Our Love Go. Berry Gordy's Detroit company was pushing hard into white America and Britain, polishing the group with etiquette lessons and gowns. The song became their fourth consecutive American number one, and it reached British audiences through television appearances during Motown's first UK tour.","The record's most famous element is not on the record at all: the raised-palm stop gesture the three women made on television, a routine reportedly devised backstage with help from one of the Temptations. Listen, too, to the song's structure, which begins with its chorus rather than a verse, a Holland-Dozier-Holland habit that let a hook grab radio listeners in the first second.","It was received as pure product, a Motown factory hit, and for years critics used the Supremes as shorthand for soul made safe for the mainstream. Judgment has since shifted: the writing is now admired for its tension between a pleading lyric and a dance beat, and Ross's restrained delivery is heard as a deliberate style rather than a lack of grit. Later slow, dramatic covers showed how much anguish lay underneath the bright surface.","Start with the opening seconds and notice how the organ and the shouted title set up the whole record before any verse arrives. Then listen for the baritone saxophone and the tambourine in the groove. Compare it with Where Did Our Love Go to hear the Supremes' sound tightening, and watch a 1965 television clip to see the gesture that made it a dance."],"ike-and-tina-turner-its-gonna-work-out-fine":["A playful rhythm-and-blues duet built as a conversation. Tina Turner sings, half teasing and half imploring, while a low male voice answers in speech, gruff and reluctant, as she tries to talk her man into settling down. A loping shuffle beat, a sharp, twangy guitar and backing voices keep it light. It is a small domestic comedy in three minutes, and the recognisable thing is Tina's rasp, already huge, pressing against the spoken replies.","After A Fool in Love in 1960 turned Ike Turner's St. Louis revue into a national act, the pair recorded this follow-up for Juggy Murray's New York label Sue Records. The song came from outside writers, Rose Marie McCoy and Sylvia McKinney, and fitted a fashion for talking duets popularised by Mickey and Sylvia. It gave the duo their strongest pop showing of the early 1960s and earned a Grammy nomination for best rock and roll recording.","The speaking voice is usually attributed not to Ike but to the guitarist Mickey Baker, of Mickey and Sylvia, who also played on the session; Ike, who rarely sang, is widely thought to have stepped aside. Knowing what Tina later revealed about the marriage, the record's premise, a woman coaxing a sullen man toward promises, now carries an irony nobody heard in 1961.","It was admired at the time as a lively novelty, and its Grammy nomination was an early sign that the revue could cross over from Black radio to the pop charts. Later writers have tended to treat it as a stepping stone between A Fool in Love and the Spector session for River Deep, yet it remains the clearest record of Tina as a comic actor in song, a side of her often lost behind the later power.","Listen first for the exchange itself, how Tina's singing and the man's grumbled answers trade places like lines in a play. Then play A Fool in Love to hear where the rasp comes from, and River Deep to hear how far it would travel. A good early-years compilation from Sue Records will give you the best transfers."],"ike-and-tina-turner-nutbush-city-limits":["A tough, clipped funk-rock groove: a stuttering guitar riff, a bass that locks tight with the kick drum, and a squealing synthesizer line cutting across the top. Tina Turner half sings, half barks a portrait of the tiny rural town where she grew up, a place with a church house, a gin house, a school house and hardly anything else, where the rules are strict and the speed limit is low. It is fast, bossy and funny, and it moves like a machine.","Tina wrote it herself, her first major songwriting credit, about Nutbush, the cotton-farming community in Haywood County, Tennessee, where she was born Anna Mae Bullock. Ike produced it at Bolic Sound, the couple's own studio in Inglewood, California. By 1973 the marriage was near collapse, and within three years Tina would leave. The single did modestly in America but was a big hit across Europe and in Australia.","Listen for the synthesizer, still a novelty on a soul record in 1973, used not for atmosphere but as a rude, buzzing lead voice. The lyric is also sharper than its party reputation suggests: under the list of buildings lies a clear picture of a small Southern town policed by church and law, and of a young woman who had every reason to leave it.","American critics paid it limited attention at first, but in Europe and Australia it became a standard, and in Australia a line dance called the Nutbush became a fixture of school discos and weddings, taught to generations who may not know its origin. Later writers have recognised it as a turning point, the moment Tina's own voice as a writer surfaced from inside Ike's operation, anticipating her independent rock career.","Start with the first eight seconds of guitar and notice how little is happening: the groove is almost all space. Then follow the synthesizer's entrances. Put it beside Ike's Proud Mary arrangement to hear the revue's rock turn, and beside Sly and the Family Stone's Thank You to hear kindred funk minimalism. The original single mix hits hardest."],"larson-tick-tick-boom":["A chamber rock musical for three performers and a four-piece band, played straight through in about ninety minutes. Jon, a composer about to turn thirty, waits tables in a New York diner while trying to get his futuristic musical produced; his girlfriend Susan wants to leave the city, and his best friend Michael has swapped acting for a well-paid job. The numbers range from anxious pop-rock to a Sondheim-style diner fantasy, Sunday, to the aching ballad Why.","Larson first performed the piece himself in 1990 as a solo rock monologue, originally called Boho Days, drawing on his own frustration after years spent on Superbia, a dystopian musical that got a workshop but never a production. After his sudden death in January 1996, the night before Rent's first off-Broadway preview, the playwright David Auburn reshaped the monologue into a three-actor musical, which opened off-Broadway in 2001.","Sunday is a loving parody of the first-act finale of Sondheim's Sunday in the Park with George, rewritten for a Sunday brunch rush, and Sondheim, who had mentored and encouraged Larson, is a real presence in the story. The ticking of the title recurs in the score as a nervous pulse, and the whole show reads now as a draft of Rent's themes: friends with HIV, rent money, and the cost of art.","The 2001 production was warmly received and won an Outer Critics Circle award, though some reviewers found it slight beside Rent. Its stature grew over two decades of small-scale productions and grew again with the 2021 film directed by Lin-Manuel Miranda, which restored some of Larson's original monologue feeling. Debate continues over how much of the show is Larson's and how much Auburn's structure; both versions are now performed.","Begin with the opening number, 30/90, to catch the anxiety, then go straight to Sunday if you know Sondheim, and to Why for the emotional centre. The 2001 off-Broadway cast album is the core recording; the 2021 film soundtrack is fuller. Follow it with Rent and Sunday in the Park with George to hear the lineage."],"scientist-heavyweight-dub-champion":["A full album of dub versions: reggae rhythm tracks stripped to drum and bass and then rebuilt at the mixing desk. Instruments drop out and crash back in, a single rimshot is thrown into a long tail of echo, a guitar chop repeats until it dissolves, and the bass stays huge and patient under everything. The mood is dry, tight and slightly menacing, the sound of the new dancehall rhythms of 1980 rather than the rootsy 1970s.","Scientist, born Hopeton Brown, was barely twenty and working under King Tubby at his small studio in the Waterhouse district of Kingston. The rhythms came largely from producer Henry 'Junjo' Lawes's sessions with the Roots Radics band at Channel One, the same tracks that were carrying the early dancehall singers. The London label Greensleeves issued the album in Britain, where a dedicated dub audience bought these records as eagerly as Jamaican sound systems did.","Listen to the snare drum, which Scientist treats as the lead instrument: every fourth hit or so is sent into a different reverb, so the drummer seems to move around a changing room. The boxing-title boast is a statement of rivalry with his own teacher's generation, and marks the moment the mixing engineer became the star of the record.","It was received as dance-floor material and sold steadily to sound-system followers; serious critical attention came later, as dub was rediscovered by post-punk, trip-hop and techno musicians. Collectors now rank it among Scientist's strongest sets, less gimmicky than the themed albums that followed. The one enduring argument about his work concerns authorship: how much credit belongs to the mixer, and how much to the Roots Radics players.","Play it loud, with real bass, and give your attention to what disappears rather than what is added: the drop of the guitar, the moment everything but the kick drum vanishes. Then compare King Tubby's 1970s dubs to hear the teacher's warmer sound, and the Rids the World album to hear where Scientist went next."],"scientist-meets-the-space-invaders":["Ten or so dub mixes of Roots Radics rhythms, dressed up as an interplanetary battle. The music itself is Scientist's lean style: cavernous bass lines, snare shots flung into echo, keyboard stabs and guitar skanks cut in and out, sudden holes of near silence. The track titles and the comic-book cover do the storytelling; the sound supplies an eerie, spacious atmosphere that makes the science-fiction premise seem almost reasonable.","Released by Greensleeves in 1981, at the height of the arcade craze that Space Invaders had set off in 1978, the album was one of a run of themed Scientist records that paired dub with pulp imagery: vampires, football, video games. The rhythms again came from Henry 'Junjo' Lawes's productions with the Roots Radics, mixed at King Tubby's studio in Kingston, and the cartoon cover was drawn in the style of a pulp comic.","Listen for the sound effects Scientist lets bleed into the mix: shrill tones and electronic swoops that echo the arcade machines, blended into the reverb so they seem to come from inside the instruments. The joke of the concept hides a serious point, that a mixing desk in a small Kingston studio was as much a machine for building new worlds as the computers of the space age.","The themed albums were long dismissed as novelty packaging, and some purists still prefer the plainer early sets. Yet the covers made Scientist famous far beyond Jamaica, and later electronic musicians, from jungle to dubstep producers, cited these records as formative. The music has aged better than its gimmick, and reissues have treated the album as a core document of early-1980s dub.","Enjoy the cover, then close your eyes and forget it: follow the bass line and wait for the moments when the whole band drops away and a single echo hangs in the air. Compare Kraftwerk's Computer World from the same year for a European version of machine music, and Burial for dub's long afterlife in London."],"the-crystals-then-he-kissed-me":["A bright, swinging Wall of Sound single that opens with a clean, chiming electric guitar figure before the full orchestra of pianos, castanets and strings crowds in. La La Brooks tells a small story in order: meeting a boy at a dance, his asking her home, the first kiss, meeting the parents, the proposal. The strings swell at each step, so the arrangement climbs with the romance, and the lead voice stays youthful and sure.","Phil Spector wrote it with Jeff Barry and Ellie Greenwich and recorded it at Gold Star in Los Angeles in 1963, a year when the name Crystals meant whichever singers Spector chose. He's a Rebel had actually been sung by Darlene Love, but La La Brooks, a genuine Crystal, sang Da Doo Ron Ron and this follow-up. The Wrecking Crew musicians supplied the backing, with Jack Nitzsche arranging.","Listen to the opening guitar alone: that crisp, repeating figure is one of the few moments in a Spector record where a single instrument is heard clearly before the wall closes over it. The Beach Boys later recorded it with the genders reversed, and Martin Scorsese used the original in Goodfellas under a single long Steadicam shot through the Copacabana nightclub, where its innocence becomes a kind of seduction.","A major hit on both sides of the Atlantic, it was praised as a nearly perfect pop narrative. Critics have questioned whether the Crystals were a group or a brand, since Spector freely swapped singers and names, but this record is secure as their finest. Its storybook romance has been read both as sweetly naive and as a portrait of the narrow future on offer to young women in 1963.","Follow the story line by line and listen for how the strings rise at each new stage. Then hear the Beach Boys' reworking on Summer Days (And Summer Nights!!) to hear Brian Wilson's debt to Spector, and watch the Copacabana scene in Goodfellas to hear what film can do to a pop song's meaning."],"the-crystals-uptown":["A slow, Latin-tinged pop song, wistful in mood, led by a plucked Spanish guitar, clicking castanets and swooping strings. Barbara Alston sings about her man, who spends his days downtown being ordered around and belittled at work, and comes home each evening uptown to a tenement where, with her, he is somebody. The record is short and restrained, a small social drama rather than a teenage party song.","Barry Mann and Cynthia Weil wrote it in New York's Brill Building world, and Spector recorded it in Manhattan before he moved his operation to Gold Star in Los Angeles. The model was the string-laden, Latin-rhythmed productions Leiber and Stoller had made for the Drifters. Uptown, in this context, meant Harlem and Spanish Harlem, and the song's picture of a working man's humiliation had a quiet racial and class edge unusual in 1962 pop.","Listen for the flamenco-style guitar and the castanets, which borrow directly from the baion rhythm that Leiber and Stoller used on There Goes My Baby and Spanish Harlem. Spector, who had apprenticed with them, is here still working as their student; the massive Wall of Sound had not yet arrived, and you can hear each instrument clearly in a way his later records never allow.","It was a solid hit and established the Crystals, but it was soon overshadowed by He's a Rebel and the louder records that followed. Critics have since singled it out as one of the most socially observant songs of the girl-group era, and Mann and Weil's writing, which would lead to their protest-flavoured work, is often dated from here. It remains a favourite among collectors of early Spector.","Hear it next to the Drifters' Up on the Roof, from the same year, for another song of the city as refuge. Notice the guitar's Spanish colour, then how little percussion there is. Then play Da Doo Ron Ron to hear how dramatically Spector's sound expanded within a year."],"the-righteous-brothers-unchained-melody":["Bobby Hatfield sings alone here, without Bill Medley, in a soaring tenor over a slow triple-time piano pulse and gradually thickening strings. The song is a lover's plea across distance and time, a wish to be reunited and to be told that love has waited. It builds for almost four minutes toward a final climb in which Hatfield's voice leaps into a sustained high note that has become one of the most imitated moments in pop singing.","Alex North and Hy Zaret wrote the song in 1955 for Unchained, a prison film, and it was already a standard, recorded by many singers, before the Righteous Brothers took it on. Their version was released in 1965 on Spector's Philles label as the B-side of Hung on You, but disc jockeys flipped the single and made this the hit. Medley has said that he, not Spector, actually produced the track.","Listen to how sparse the first minute is: little more than piano and voice, with the famous Spector density held back almost entirely until the end, which is a clue to Medley's hand. The song's second life came in 1990, when the film Ghost used it in a scene at a potter's wheel, and the old single returned to charts on both sides of the Atlantic.","Many earlier versions had been hits, but the Righteous Brothers' recording became the definitive one, and Hatfield's final high note is now the part other singers are judged against. Critics have sometimes found it overwrought, and the Ghost revival turned it into wedding and karaoke music, blunting its effect. Yet as vocal craft it is widely admired, a blue-eyed soul performance that owes much to Black gospel phrasing.","Wait for the last minute: count how long Hatfield holds back before the climb, and notice the strings swelling underneath. Then listen to an earlier 1950s version, such as Al Hibbler's, to hear how the song changed shape. Compare the Righteous Brothers' You've Lost That Lovin' Feelin' to hear the duo together."],"the-righteous-brothers-soul-and-inspiration":["A slow-building duet in which Bill Medley's deep baritone begins alone and Bobby Hatfield's tenor enters to push the song upward. The singer begs a lover not to leave, saying that without her he has nothing to live for. Behind them, a huge arrangement of strings, horns, piano and choir mounts in waves, with a drum pattern that thumps like a heartbeat. The record is a deliberate reconstruction of the Spector sound, down to the echo.","Barry Mann and Cynthia Weil had begun the song as a follow-up to You've Lost That Lovin' Feelin', but by the time it was finished the Righteous Brothers had left Spector's Philles for Verve. Medley produced it himself, consciously copying Spector's methods, and it became their second American number one in 1966, a better commercial result than anything Spector was then achieving with his own acts.","Compare the structure with You've Lost That Lovin' Feelin': the low solo opening, the tenor's entrance, the long crescendo to a shared climax. It is almost a remake in disguise, by the same writers and singers without the same producer. The success showed that the Wall of Sound could be learned, a fact that stung Spector and hastened his retreat after River Deep's American failure the same year.","Reviewers noticed the resemblance to Lovin' Feelin' at once, and some have always regarded it as a knowing imitation. Others hear it as proof that the duo's voices, especially Medley's, were central to the old records. It sits now as a high-water mark of blue-eyed soul, influential on the stormy British productions of the Walker Brothers and on later power ballads.","Play it right after You've Lost That Lovin' Feelin' and listen for what Medley kept: the low start, the echo, the choir. Then notice the differences, particularly the heavier drums. Follow with the Walker Brothers' The Sun Ain't Gonna Shine Anymore for the style carried across the Atlantic."],"toots-and-the-maytals-54-46-thats-my-number":["A tight, springy rhythm, somewhere between rocksteady and the new reggae beat, carries Toots Hibbert's rough, gospel-hot voice through a song about being arrested and jailed for something he says he did not do. His harmony partners Raleigh Gordon and Jerry Mathias answer him in close, churchy responses. The famous passage near the end has Toots call out to the band for hits, one after another, and the band answering with sharp accents, like a sermon turned into a game.","Toots was jailed in the mid-1960s after a conviction for marijuana possession, which he always said was a frame-up, and he came out to write this song about the experience. It was produced by Leslie Kong, the Chinese-Jamaican producer of the Beverley's label, who also recorded Desmond Dekker and had cut Bob Marley's first single. Jamaica was barely six years independent, and the rude boy and the prisoner were becoming heroes of its popular song.","The title is a prisoner's number, though Toots reportedly said it was not his own. Listen closely to the call-and-response section: it comes straight out of Pentecostal and revival church practice, where a preacher calls and a congregation replies, and Toots, raised singing in church, transfers it to a secular band. The bass line, rolling rather than walking, points forward to the reggae beat Toots would name a year later.","It was a big hit in Jamaica and has been re-recorded by the group several times, most famously in live versions that stretch the band section. Critics now treat it as one of the foundational reggae songs, alongside Do the Reggay, and it has been covered widely. The debate is only of labels: whether it is late rocksteady or early reggae, a boundary this record helps to define.","Wait for the call-and-response ending and count along as the band responds to each cue. Then listen for the organ's short chops on the offbeat. Play Do the Reggay next to hear the style christened, and Desmond Dekker's 007 (Shanty Town) for another story of Kingston life from the same moment."],"whitney-houston-whitney-houston":["A debut album of ten songs that moves between glossy synthesizer pop, adult ballads and upbeat dance numbers. The voice is the whole point: huge in range, gospel-trained, clean and exact, capable of a whisper at the start of a line and a full-throated climb at its end. The ballads, Saving All My Love for You and Greatest Love of All, are built as showcases, while How Will I Know has a bright, bouncing dance-pop surface.","Whitney Houston, daughter of the gospel and session singer Cissy Houston and cousin of Dionne Warwick, was signed to Arista by Clive Davis in 1983, and the album was prepared carefully for two years with several producers, including Michael Masser, Kashif, Narada Michael Walden and Jermaine Jackson. It was released in 1985 and climbed slowly; its singles kept coming, and it finally reached number one in America in 1986.","Greatest Love of All was not new: George Benson had recorded it for the 1977 Muhammad Ali film The Greatest. Listen too for the duets, including one with Teddy Pendergrass, which placed the newcomer beside established soul singers. And listen to how tightly the vocals are controlled; the power is often held in reserve until the final chorus, a choirgirl's discipline beneath the pop gloss.","It became one of the best-selling debut albums by any artist, and Saving All My Love for You won a Grammy. Some critics found the production bland and too eager for adult radio, and the question of whether her talent was served or smothered by the polish has followed her career. Its influence on the vocal showcase ballad, and on the singers of the 1990s, is not disputed.","Start with How Will I Know for the joy, then Saving All My Love for You for control, then Greatest Love of All for the full voice. Listen for the moments she chooses not to belt. Compare Madonna's Like a Virgin from the same period to hear two very different ideas of the 1980s pop star."],"the-skatalites-eastern-standard-time":["A three-minute instrumental led by Don Drummond's trombone, the great melancholy voice of Jamaican ska. The rhythm section keeps the ska engine running underneath: guitar and piano chopping on the off-beat, a walking bass, drums snapping at the back of the bar. Over it the horns state a slow, brooding theme in a minor key, and Drummond's solo moves with a heavy, sliding, almost reluctant grace. It is dance music that sounds as if it is thinking about something sad.","Kingston in the first years of independence, when producer Clement 'Coxsone' Dodd's Studio One was turning out a new record almost every day. The Skatalites were a pool of jazz-trained players, several of them schooled in the brass band of the Alpha Boys School, who backed singers in the morning and cut instrumentals in the afternoon. Releases were often credited to whichever horn player led the tune, so this one usually appears under Drummond's own name, with the band implied.","Listen to how rarely Drummond lands exactly on the beat. He tends to start his phrases a little late and lean behind the rhythm, which gives the tune its weary, dragging feeling against a band that never slows down. Notice, too, how much of the arrangement is quiet space: the horns drop out and leave the guitar and bass alone, so that the return of the full section lands like a change of weather.","Drummond was revered by other musicians as the most original composer on the Kingston scene, and pieces like this are why. His career ended in tragedy within a year or two, with a killing and his confinement in a psychiatric hospital, where he died in 1969, and that story has sometimes overshadowed the music. Collectors and the later two-tone revival restored his reputation as a composer in the line of the jazz writers he admired.","Start by tapping the off-beat with the guitar, then let your attention drift up to the trombone and hear how it pulls against that steady chop. Compare it with the brighter, faster Guns of Navarone to hear the range of the same band. Any good Studio One reissue will do; the thin, bright original sound is part of the charm."],"the-skatalites-foundation-ska":["A two-disc gathering of the instrumentals the Skatalites cut for Studio One in their brief life as a working band, among them Guns of Navarone, Eastern Standard Time, Man in the Street and Freedom Sounds. Heard in bulk, the sound is unmistakable: a four-piece horn section playing jazz-style heads in close harmony, then taking turns to solo, over a rhythm section that places every guitar chop and piano stab on the off-beat. Tempos are brisk, the mood swings from jubilant to mournful.","The band formed in 1964 out of Kingston's best session players, among them Tommy McCook and Roland Alphonso on tenor, Johnny Moore on trumpet, Don Drummond on trombone, Jackie Mittoo on piano, Lloyd Brevett on bass and Lloyd Knibbs on drums. They broke up in 1965. Their records were issued piecemeal on singles for sound-system dances, often under individual names, so the band's work only became visible as a body decades later, through compilations like this American reissue.","Listen to the drums. Knibbs is often credited with the ska beat itself, placing heavy accents where an American rhythm-and-blues drummer would leave space, and the whole later history of Jamaican rhythm, from rocksteady to reggae's one-drop, grows from shifting those accents around. Many of the tunes are also adaptations: film themes, pop songs and jazz standards reworked for the dance floor, a practice that anticipates the version culture of dub.","For years the Skatalites were known outside Jamaica mainly through cover versions by British two-tone bands. Reissues like this one, with proper notes, made the argument that they were a great jazz band as well as a dance band, and critics now rank them with the founding ensembles of Caribbean music. The only complaint usually raised is sound quality, since some masters survive only in worn copies.","Do not play it straight through; take one side at a time, as the original dancers heard these records one by one. Listen first for the rhythm guitar on the off-beat, then follow each horn solo as you would in a hard-bop record. Afterwards, hear King Tubby's dubs to see where these rhythms went."],"love-love":["Love's debut is a short, loud, jangling record of fourteen songs, most of them under three minutes. Chiming electric guitars in the manner of the Byrds meet a rougher garage-band snarl, and Arthur Lee's voice swings between a crooner's smoothness and a shout. The opener is a sped-up cover of Burt Bacharach and Hal David's My Little Red Book; the album also has a racing version of Hey Joe and the slow, bleak Signed D.C., about addiction.","Love were the house band of the Sunset Strip in 1965–66, playing clubs such as Bido Lito's, and one of the few racially mixed rock groups in Los Angeles, led by Arthur Lee with guitarist Johnny Echols. Elektra, until then a folk label, signed them as one of its first rock acts, and Lee's enthusiasm reportedly helped bring the Doors to the same label. The album was made quickly and cheaply, essentially the live set on tape.","Hear how the Bacharach song is transformed: a sophisticated film-song melody is hammered into two chords and a garage-rock beat, a small act of translation that told listeners Love were not a folk-rock band like the others. Listen to the quieter songs by Bryan MacLean, the band's second writer, whose gentler melodies point towards the orchestral delicacy that would come on Forever Changes.","It sold modestly and was quickly overshadowed by the two albums that followed. Critics long treated it as a promising first step, notable mainly for its speed and for predating Jimi Hendrix's slow Hey Joe by some months. More recently it has been heard as a missing link between folk rock and the punk of the following decade, and its raw energy has won it admirers of its own.","Play My Little Red Book loud and then play the Bacharach original to hear the argument. Then go to Signed D.C. for the opposite pole. Treat the whole record as a club set and let it rush past; it is a snapshot of the Sunset Strip before psychedelia arrived, and a starting point for hearing how far the band travelled."],"love-da-capo":["Two albums in one. Side one is six compact songs that range from the jazz-waltz Stephanie Knows Who to the harpsichord-and-flute ballad The Castle and the gentle Orange Skies, plus the ferocious single 7 and 7 Is, two minutes of frantic drumming that ends in a sound like an explosion. Side two is a single track, Revelation, a loose blues jam of nearly twenty minutes built for the stage.","By late 1966 Love had grown to seven players, adding a flautist and saxophonist and a new drummer so that their old drummer could move to keyboards and harpsichord. Elektra gave them more studio time, and the band had absorbed jazz and chamber textures from the Los Angeles scene. The title is a musical instruction meaning 'from the beginning', a sly nod to the classical score. Released early in 1967, it arrived months before the psychedelic flood.","The explosion at the end of 7 and 7 Is is followed by a brief, calm blues, as if the world has ended and the band has wandered back to play in the ruins. The Castle and Orange Skies already use the harpsichord and woodwind colours that would become the orchestral palette of Forever Changes. And Revelation is often cited as one of the first rock tracks to fill an entire album side.","7 and 7 Is became the band's biggest single, and the first side is widely admired. Revelation, by contrast, divides listeners: some hear a historical first, most a long jam that loses focus away from a live room. The usual verdict is that Da Capo is half a great record and the necessary bridge to Forever Changes, which appeared the same year.","Start with 7 and 7 Is and count how few seconds the drummer rests. Then hear The Castle straight after, for the contrast between violence and filigree. Take Revelation later, as a document of what the band was like in a club. Pair the album with Forever Changes to hear the same musicians move from jazz-rock to chamber pop within months."],"slick-rick-childrens-story":["A single rap told as a bedtime story. Slick Rick, in his London-tinged drawl, agrees to tell two children a tale before sleep, then narrates the fate of a teenager who drifts into robbery and dies after a frantic police chase. The beat is light, bouncy and almost cheerful, a drum-machine pattern with bright musical hooks, which makes the grim story more unsettling. Rick changes voices for each character, from the sleepy children to the panicking young robber.","It appeared on The Great Adventures of Slick Rick, Def Jam's big release of late 1988, and became one of his best-known singles. Hip-hop at the time was split between party records and the new political and street-reporting styles of Public Enemy and the West Coast. Rick, born in London to Jamaican parents and raised in the Bronx, offered something different: rap as spoken fiction, with a plot, a narrator and a moral, told with the timing of a stand-up comedian.","Listen to the frame. The story opens and closes with the narrator and the children, so the tale of crime is always held at one remove, told by an adult who is plainly performing. Rick never raises his voice even at the climax; the tension comes from the rhythm of his lines, which speed up as the chase does. The track is also an early case of a rapper playing every part in a scene.","It was a hit on radio and in clubs and has been reused ever since: Montell Jordan built This Is How We Do It on it in 1995, and Mos Def and Talib Kweli rewrote it as a parable about the music industry in 1998. Critics count it among the founding texts of rap narrative, and Rick's calm, melodic delivery is often cited as a model by later storytellers from Nas to Snoop Dogg.","Listen once only for the story, as you would to a radio play, and follow the chase. Then listen again to the voice itself: the accent, the pauses, the way Rick turns each couplet like a joke's punchline. Compare it with Schubert's Erlkönig, another tale in which one performer voices a whole cast and a child's world ends badly."],"the-doors-strange-days":["The Doors' second album is darker and stranger than the first. Ray Manzarek's organ and keyboard bass, Robby Krieger's slide-inflected guitar and John Densmore's jazz-trained drumming frame Jim Morrison's baritone in ten songs, including the hits People Are Strange and Love Me Two Times. The centre of gravity is at the ends: a title track with an electronically treated voice, a spoken poem over a storm of noise in Horse Latitudes, and the eleven-minute closing epic When the Music's Over.","Released in autumn 1967, only about eight months after the debut, it was recorded at Sunset Sound in Hollywood with producer Paul Rothchild and engineer Bruce Botnick, who now had more tracks to work with and time to experiment. Many songs predated the first album, written in the band's Venice Beach beginnings. The group was famous by now, and the record was made in the shadow of Sgt. Pepper, with overdubs, effects and studio tricks newly expected.","The piano on Unhappy Girl was played backwards and reversed on tape, giving it a sucking, inhaled sound. On the title track an early Moog synthesizer bends Morrison's voice. Horse Latitudes, a poem about horses thrown from becalmed ships, is one of Morrison's earliest texts. And look at the cover: circus performers in a New York street, with the band present only as a poster on the wall.","It sold well but less than the debut, and reviewers at first called it a sequel. Opinion has since grown: many critics, and some of the band members, have called it their most complete record, with the most coherent sound. Its theatrical spoken passages remain the dividing line between those who find Morrison a poet and those who find him pretentious.","Start with People Are Strange for the band's cabaret side, then jump to When the Music's Over and follow how Manzarek's organ builds and drops the tension across eleven minutes. Listen for the moment the band falls silent behind the voice. Compare it with the debut's The End; this is the same idea, more controlled."],"the-doors-la-woman":["A loose, bluesy, road-weary album recorded almost live. The Doors return to the blues that had always been under their sound, with a hired bass player and a second guitarist filling out the band. The long title track rolls like a car on a freeway; Love Her Madly was a hit; Crawling King Snake is a John Lee Hooker cover. The last track, Riders on the Storm, is a slow jazz-tinged ballad in which Manzarek's electric piano glitters over recorded rain and thunder.","By 1970 the band was worn down by Morrison's arrest and trial after a Miami concert and by his heavy drinking. Paul Rothchild walked away from the sessions, reportedly unhappy with the new songs, so the band co-produced with engineer Bruce Botnick in their own rehearsal room in West Hollywood, recording quickly with Elvis Presley's bassist Jerry Scheff. The album came out in April 1971; Morrison had left for Paris, where he died that July.","Morrison sang many of his parts in a small bathroom next to the rehearsal room, for its natural echo, and the vocals have a dry, close sound unlike the earlier records. In the slow middle section of the title track he repeats a chant whose letters are an anagram of his own name. On Riders on the Storm, listen for a second, whispered vocal track shadowing the main one.","It was received as a return to form after two uneven albums, and Morrison's death three months later turned it into an epitaph. Critics prize its looseness; some hear the band at its most musical, others a group running on memory. Riders on the Storm has become one of their most played recordings, a quiet end to a loud career.","Put on the title track and listen for the moment it slows to half speed and then accelerates back; that is the whole band breathing together. Then play Riders on the Storm in the dark. For contrast, go back to the first album: the same four people, five years younger, and much less tired."],"the-meters-look-ka-py-py":["A short instrumental built from four interlocking parts and almost nothing else. Zigaboo Modeliste's drums stutter and skip across the beat, George Porter Jr.'s bass answers in short phrases, Leo Nocentelli's guitar picks tight, clipped figures, and Art Neville's organ adds dabs of colour. There is no melody in the usual sense; the tune is the groove, and the joy comes from how each player leaves space for the others.","The Meters were the house rhythm section for producers Allen Toussaint and Marshall Sehorn in New Orleans, backing singers such as Lee Dorsey, and in 1969 they began releasing their own instrumentals on the Josie label. This single followed Cissy Strut and gave its name to their second album. It was the era of James Brown's hard funk, but New Orleans had its own rhythm tradition, drawn from street parades, Mardi Gras and the piano of Professor Longhair.","Listen to the drums as a parade band would. Modeliste distributes a New Orleans second-line pattern around a small kit, so the bass drum, snare and hi-hat each seem to be played by a different marcher. The patterns are asymmetrical and never quite repeat as you expect, which is why the track feels unstable yet perfectly steady. The title apparently means nothing at all; it is there for its sound.","A modest hit on the rhythm-and-blues charts, it was little known outside funk circles for years. Its reputation rose with hip-hop, when producers mined early Meters records for their breaks, and drummers now study Modeliste's playing here as a textbook of syncopation. Critics treat it as the purest statement of the early Meters sound: four players and no fat.","Listen four times, each time following only one instrument, then listen once to all four together and hear how they lock. Compare it with James Brown's Cold Sweat to hear two answers to the same question, Brown's tighter and more military, the Meters' looser and more like a street parade."],"gza-beneath-the-surface":["GZA's second album for a major label trades the cinematic gloom of Liquid Swords for a drier, brighter sound. Beats built on chopped soul and orchestral samples, with harder drums, frame long verses delivered in GZA's calm, precise voice, packed with internal rhymes, chess and science imagery and street reportage. Wu-Tang members appear as guests, notably on Crash Your Crew and Breaker, Breaker. The texture is steady rather than dramatic; the attraction is the words.","By 1999 the Wu-Tang Clan had expanded into a business empire of solo records, clothing and affiliates, and the group's second-wave releases were being judged against their famous 1993–95 run. RZA, stretched thin, handed much of the production to producers from the Wu-Tang circle, among them Mathematics and True Master. Hip-hop had meanwhile moved towards glossy, sample-cleared radio production, and this album stands between the two worlds.","Listen to how GZA builds a verse. He rarely uses a hook-driven chorus; instead he strings a single metaphor across many lines, and the end of a verse often answers something set up at the start. Several tracks run their beats with almost no variation for minutes, a deliberate backdrop that leaves all the movement to the rhymes, much as a jazz rhythm section might support a soloist.","It charted well and got respectful reviews, but it was inevitably measured against Liquid Swords and usually found less haunting. Later assessments have been kinder, hearing a more technically refined rapper and some of GZA's densest writing. It remains the natural next step for anyone who has absorbed Liquid Swords and wants more of GZA the writer rather than RZA the director.","Read the words while you listen, at least once; GZA's writing rewards close attention. Then play a track from Liquid Swords next to one from here to hear the change in production from shadowy to sharp. If you like the verbal density, carry on to Raekwon and to the later work of Kendrick Lamar."],"gza-labels":["A single verse over a stark, looping beat, with no chorus to relieve it. GZA builds his entire rap out of the names of record companies, stringing together dozens of them, from the big corporate majors to the small independent hip-hop labels, into sentences that tell a story about exploitation, bad contracts and the way the music industry treats young artists. It is a word game and an argument at the same time.","The track appeared on Liquid Swords, GZA's first album after the Wu-Tang Clan's breakthrough. GZA had made an earlier solo record, Words from the Genius, for Cold Chillin' Records, where he felt he had been poorly promoted. The Wu-Tang Clan's famous deal, which let each member sign solo contracts with different labels, was itself a statement about control of the industry, and this track gives that stance a sharp, comic voice.","The meaning is carried by double sense. Label names are chosen because they also form everyday phrases, so a line can be read as a list of companies or as a plain sentence. When his own former label passes by, the tone does not change, but the grievance is personal. The beat's minimalism is deliberate, keeping the ear on the words.","Listeners and critics singled it out at once as one of the album's most inventive tracks, and it became a favourite example of GZA's verbal control. It set a pattern for later concept tracks by rappers who build verses entirely out of brand names, band names or other lists. Its complaint about the industry has aged well.","Look up a list of record labels, or simply listen out for the names you know, and try to hear each line two ways at once. Then play the rest of Liquid Swords to hear how GZA's usual style is equally dense but less playful. A good companion is Nas's N.Y. State of Mind, another single, relentless verse."],"os-mutantes-mutantes":["The trio of Rita Lee and the brothers Arnaldo Baptista and Sérgio Dias, on their second album, move further from the Tropicália circle towards their own mix of psychedelic rock, Brazilian popular music, parody and sound collage. Dom Quixote lurches between pomp and psychedelic chaos; 2001, co-written with Tom Zé, sets a space-age text to the rustic sound of Brazilian country music; Caminhante Noturno and Fuga Nº II mix fuzz guitars with orchestral and choral colours.","The album appeared in 1969, months after the military government's Institutional Act No. 5 of December 1968 brought harsh censorship and the arrest and exile of Caetano Veloso and Gilberto Gil. The Mutantes had backed Veloso at the 1968 song festival, when he was booed for his song about prohibition. They were young São Paulo teenagers who listened to the Beatles and made their own equipment, working with a record industry dominated by television song contests.","Much of the sound comes from home-built equipment. Cláudio César Dias Baptista, the older brother of two members, built guitars, amplifiers and effects for the band, including a famous gold-plated guitar, because imported gear was unaffordable. Listen for how often a song abruptly changes genre mid-flow; the joke is always on the idea that musical styles belong to separate social classes.","In Brazil the album was a modest success and sometimes dismissed as imitation of British rock. Abroad the Mutantes were almost unknown until the 1990s, when musicians such as Kurt Cobain and David Byrne championed them and reissues appeared. Now critics place this album alongside the debut as the band's creative peak, and some prefer its more confident, stranger songs.","Start with 2001 and listen for the rural guitar sound suddenly meeting science-fiction imagery; that collision is the band in miniature. Then play Dom Quixote for its wild gear-changes. Pair it with the Beatles' Revolver for the model and with Caetano Veloso's early albums for the Brazilian context."],"os-mutantes-a-divina-comedia":["The third Mutantes album is heavier and more rock-based than the first two, with less orchestral decoration and more distorted guitar. Ando Meio Desligado, a floating psychedelic song about feeling vague and absent, became their best-known track. Ave Lúcifer mixes church solemnity with rock, Meu Refrigerador Não Funciona is a showcase for Rita Lee's raw blues voice, and a mocking version of the old serenade Chão de Estrelas fills a famous romantic song with sound-effect gags.","By 1970 the Tropicália movement had broken apart under dictatorship censorship, with Veloso and Gil in exile in London. The Mutantes survived by focusing on rock, touring and television, and they had begun to play abroad. The title, a joke combining Dante's Divine Comedy with a phrase about feeling 'somewhat disconnected', captures their mixture of high culture and irreverence. Brazilian rock was growing heavier in response to Hendrix and Led Zeppelin.","The Chão de Estrelas parody is more pointed than it seems. The original was a revered serenade of the 1930s, and the band punctures its sentimental images with slapstick noises, a comment on the sacred status of old Brazilian popular song that also reflects Tropicália's argument with nationalist tradition. Ando Meio Desligado is often read as a drug song, though it works just as well as a portrait of daydreaming.","Received as their most commercial rock record, it later became a favourite of foreign fans who discovered the band in the 1990s. Some critics find the shift towards hard rock a loss of the band's unique mixture; others hear the Mutantes at their most confident as players. Ando Meio Desligado is now a classic of Brazilian rock.","Start with Ando Meio Desligado for its dreamy drift, then play Meu Refrigerador Não Funciona to hear how hard Rita Lee could sing. Listen to the Chão de Estrelas parody next to a traditional version of the song to hear the joke. Afterwards try Gilberto Gil's Expresso 2222 for the exiles' side of the same moment."],"fleetwood-mac-rumours":["Eleven polished, melodic songs by three songwriters, Lindsey Buckingham, Stevie Nicks and Christine McVie, sung over the rock-steady rhythm section of Mick Fleetwood and John McVie. The sound is clear and warm: acoustic and electric guitars, piano and organ, close three-part harmony. The hits are Go Your Own Way, Dreams, Don't Stop and You Make Loving Fun, but the core is The Chain, the only song credited to all five, with its famous slow-building bass line and final surge.","The band had been re-formed in 1975 around the American duo Buckingham and Nicks, and its previous album had been a success. During the sessions of 1976, largely at the Record Plant in Sausalito, both couples in the band were breaking up: John and Christine McVie were divorcing, Buckingham and Nicks were splitting, and Fleetwood's marriage was failing. With producers Ken Caillat and Richard Dashut, they turned those tensions into songs sung to one another.","The songs are a conversation. Buckingham's Go Your Own Way and Nicks's Dreams address the same breakup from opposite sides, and each singer had to sing harmony on the other's song. The Chain was assembled from fragments, including an older Christine McVie song and a Buckingham piece. Christine McVie's Songbird was recorded alone at the piano in an empty Berkeley concert hall. And Nicks's Silver Springs was cut from the album, surviving as a B-side.","It was one of the best-selling albums ever made and won the Grammy for Album of the Year. Punk-era critics treated it as the smooth soft rock they despised; its reputation has grown steadily since, and it is now admired for its songwriting and craft. The debate now is about whether its polish tames the emotional chaos or makes it more poignant.","Hear Go Your Own Way and Dreams back to back, as an argument between two people. Then listen to The Chain and wait for the bass line in the middle, followed by the full band's return. Listen to the whole album in order; it is short, and its track sequence is carefully built. The standard remasters are fine."],"fleetwood-mac-tusk":["A double album of twenty songs, much stranger than Rumours. Christine McVie's and Stevie Nicks's songs, including Nicks's long, drifting Sara, carry on the band's warm sound, but Lindsey Buckingham's contributions are jagged, short and lo-fi, with tinny drums, whispered vocals and odd sound. The title track is a thundering, chanted rhythm piece with the University of Southern California marching band. The album alternates between polish and experiment, beauty and noise.","After the huge success of Rumours, the record company expected a sequel. Buckingham, inspired partly by punk and new wave, wanted to avoid repeating himself and recorded many of his parts at home, reportedly using unorthodox methods such as drumming on tissue boxes. The band spent months, and reportedly more than a million dollars, in a Los Angeles studio. It was released in 1979 as a double album with a high price.","Listen to Buckingham's songs for their sound as much as their melodies. He often used simple home equipment, deliberately rough drum sounds and heavily compressed vocals, recording one instrument at a time. The title track's marching band was recorded in Dodger Stadium, a massive sound that pairs with the song's tiny, obsessive riff. On many of his pieces Fleetwood and John McVie, the band's famous rhythm section, are barely present.","It sold millions, but far fewer than Rumours, and was seen as a commercial disappointment and self-indulgence. The reputation has changed completely: critics and indie musicians now praise it as the band's boldest record, and it is often cited as a model for independent rock musicians who value experiment over polish. Its unevenness is now seen as part of its character.","Start with the title track and then with Sara, for the band's two sides. Then play several Buckingham songs in a row, for example The Ledge and Not That Funny, and hear how odd they sound for a stadium band. Listen to the whole album over two sessions, as its original four sides."],"gillian-welch-the-harrow-and-the-harvest":["Ten songs sung by Gillian Welch with David Rawlings, accompanied only by their guitars, banjo and harmonica. The tone is dark and quiet: slow, spacious ballads about loss, poverty, stubbornness and time, sung in close harmony. Scarlet Town takes its setting from the old ballad Barbara Allen; Six White Horses is built on hand-clapped body percussion and harmonica; The Way It Will Be and The Way It Goes form a pair of opposites. Rawlings's guitar solos slip between the voices.","It was released eight years after the previous album, Soul Journey, after a long period in which the duo said they wrote songs they did not like and threw them away. In the meantime they toured, recorded with Rawlings as leader, and ran their own label and studio in Nashville. It appeared in 2011, when a new generation of Americana bands was bringing banjos and old songs to large festival audiences.","Many of the songs seem traditional but are original, written in the style and vocabulary of old ballads. Listen to how Rawlings shadows Welch's melody just above or below it, then drops out to leave her alone for a phrase. Six White Horses uses body percussion in the hambone tradition, an African-American practice of slapping the chest and thighs.","Reviews were very positive, praising its concentration and restraint, and it was nominated for a Grammy. Some listeners found it too slow and too uniform after the long wait; others hear the duo's deepest and most unsparing record. It is now seen as the late summit of their work together, proof that the long silence had been spent refining rather than losing the voice.","Listen at night, quietly, and in order. Start with Scarlet Town, then hear The Way It Will Be and The Way It Goes as a pair. Follow Rawlings's guitar as a second voice. Compare it with the Carter Family and with Time (The Revelator) to hear how old and new their style is."],"the-band-music-from-big-pink":["Eleven songs by five musicians who sound as if they have been playing together in a kitchen for decades, which is nearly the case. Robbie Robertson's guitar stays in the shadows; Garth Hudson's organ supplies the colour; Levon Helm, Rick Danko and Richard Manuel trade and stack lead vocals in rough, overlapping harmony. It opens with the slow lament 'Tears of Rage', passes through 'The Weight', and closes on Manuel's high, cracked falsetto in 'I Shall Be Released'. Tempos are slow, the sound is warm and woody.","The group had spent years as Ronnie Hawkins's Hawks and then as Bob Dylan's electric band on his booed 1966 tour. After Dylan's motorcycle accident they settled near Woodstock, New York, and played daily with him in the basement of a pink house in West Saugerties, the sessions that became the Basement Tapes. Producer John Simon then took them into studios in New York and Los Angeles in early 1968. Capitol released the record that July, in the same season as psychedelia's loudest excesses.","Look at the sleeve: the naive painting on the front is by Dylan himself, and the inside photograph, captioned 'Next of Kin', shows the players with their parents and relatives, a quiet manifesto against the generation war then raging. Three songs carry Dylan's hand as writer or co-writer. Before 'Chest Fever' lurches in, Hudson plays a long, churchy organ prelude that quotes Bach's manner more than any rock record of its year had dared to.","Sales were modest, but musicians heard it at once. Eric Clapton later said it persuaded him to leave Cream, and George Harrison went to Woodstock to spend time with the group. Critics came to treat it as the starting point of what is now called Americana: rock made from country, gospel, soul and old-time music rather than from the blues boom. Some still prefer the second, brown album for its tighter songs; few dispute which one changed the direction of the field.","Start with 'The Weight' and listen to how each verse passes to a different singer, then how the voices pile together on the chorus. Next play 'Tears of Rage' and notice how slowly it moves, with the horns leaning on every beat. Then 'I Shall Be Released', and compare Manuel's falsetto with Dylan's own version. Hear the Basement Tapes and The Band's second album next."],"the-band-the-last-waltz":["A farewell concert on record and film: The Band play their own songs with a horn section arranged by Allen Toussaint, then welcome guests who shaped them or whom they shaped. Ronnie Hawkins, Dr. John, Neil Young, Joni Mitchell, Van Morrison, Muddy Waters, Eric Clapton and Bob Dylan each take the stage; Emmylou Harris and the Staple Singers appear in studio segments. The sound is big, brassy and celebratory, closing with the whole company singing together.","The concert took place on Thanksgiving Day, 25 November 1976, at the Winterland Ballroom in San Francisco, promoted by Bill Graham, who served a turkey dinner to the audience before the music began. Robbie Robertson had grown tired of touring after sixteen years on the road and pushed for a formal ending. Martin Scorsese filmed it with several cameras, and the film and a triple album were released in 1978, framed as the end of an era of American rock.","Not everything you hear happened on the night: Robertson later admitted that parts of the performances were repaired in the studio, a common practice then but one that complicates the idea of a pure live document. Listen to Muddy Waters's 'Mannish Boy' and Van Morrison's 'Caravan' for moments where the guests outshine the hosts. The film also contains songs composed and recorded afterwards, including the country ballad 'Evangeline' with Emmylou Harris.","The film was quickly hailed as one of the great concert movies, and its reputation has only grown. Inside the group it was bitter: Levon Helm, in his memoir, attacked the way the film centred Robertson and ended a band the others did not want to end. Critics still argue whether it is a generous tribute to American music or a monument built by one member to himself. Either way, it defined how later rock farewells would be staged.","Watch the film before hearing the album, because the faces matter: the joy of Muddy Waters, the strain on Richard Manuel. Then listen for how Toussaint's horns fatten 'The Night They Drove Old Dixie Down' and 'Ophelia'. Follow the guests back to their own records afterwards. The expanded later editions add songs cut from the original release."],"fania-all-stars-our-latin-thing":["A documentary film and its soundtrack, built around the Fania All Stars' 1971 night at the Cheetah club in Manhattan. Between long performances by a band of bandleaders — Johnny Pacheco directing, Ray Barretto on congas, Willie Colón's trombones, Larry Harlow at the piano — the camera goes into the streets of the Lower East Side and East Harlem: children playing, a botánica, a cockfight, dancing in the clubs. Singers Héctor Lavoe, Ismael Miranda and Pete 'El Conde' Rodríguez take turns at the front.","Fania Records, founded in 1964 by Pacheco and the lawyer Jerry Masucci, had gathered New York's Puerto Rican and Cuban musicians into a single stable. Masucci hired Leon Gast to film the Cheetah concert and turned it into a feature that played in cinemas in Latin neighbourhoods and beyond. It arrived when young Nuyoricans were building a political and cultural identity of their own, and it gave that identity a soundtrack and a face.","The film is as much about place as music: notice how Gast cuts from the stage to the tenements, arguing that this sound belongs to specific blocks and people rather than to Havana or the dance-hall past. The concert audio overlaps with the Live at the Cheetah albums, but the film's editing and street footage make a different argument. Listen for Yomo Toro's cuatro, the small Puerto Rican guitar, a rural voice inside a city band.","It was a surprise success and has been credited with spreading the music far beyond New York, to Puerto Rico, Venezuela, Colombia and Africa, helping to fix the word 'salsa' as its name. Later writers note that Fania's marketing flattened a range of Cuban and Puerto Rican styles into a brand, and that the musicians saw little of the profits. As a film of a scene at its peak, it remains essential.","Watch it whole if you can; the street scenes change how the music sounds. Then compare a number here with the same night on the Live at the Cheetah albums. Listen for the moments when the horns stop and the percussion and chorus carry the song alone, then the brass returns. Follow with Leon Gast's later film Salsa or with Willie Colón and Héctor Lavoe's own records."],"fania-all-stars-live-at-yankee-stadium":["Two volumes of the Fania All Stars at their largest: the house band of New York salsa playing to a stadium crowd. Extended arrangements let each star singer take a turn — Héctor Lavoe's 'Mi Gente' is the best known, a call to the audience answered by thousands — while trumpets and trombones punch out short riffs and the percussion section drives underneath. The long percussion feature 'Congo Bongo', for conga and bongo, is the other centrepiece.","The concert took place at Yankee Stadium in the Bronx on 24 August 1973, the first time a salsa show filled a major American ballpark. Fania's owners wanted to prove the music could sell in the numbers of a rock concert. The show was cut short when the crowd poured onto the field, and the albums, issued in 1975, probably draw heavily on a concert given soon afterwards at the Roberto Clemente Coliseum in San Juan, Puerto Rico.","The title is partly a marketing claim: because the stadium performance was interrupted and the recording compromised, much of what you hear likely comes from San Juan, with studio work on top. Listen to 'Congo Bongo', the percussion duel associated with the moment the audience broke onto the field, and to how the chorus answers Lavoe in 'Mi Gente', written by Johnny Pacheco for exactly this call-and-response.","For many fans these albums are the definitive statement of the band in its prime, the moment salsa claimed arena scale. Collectors argue over the patchwork of venues and overdubs and whether it should really be called a live record at all. The event itself, rather than the discs, has become the legend, shown in Leon Gast's film Salsa and retold in every history of the Fania era.","Put on 'Mi Gente' first and listen to the crowd sing back to Lavoe; this is the music as a community ritual. Then follow the rhythm section in 'Congo Bongo', picking out the deep conga against the higher, sharper bongo. Compare with the smaller, sweatier Live at the Cheetah to hear how the band changed as the rooms grew."],"funkadelic-funkadelic":["The debut album of George Clinton's hard, strange band: seven long tracks where doo-wop harmonies from Clinton's old vocal group sit on top of fuzzed, heavy guitar and slow, grinding funk. It opens with 'Mommy, What's a Funkadelic?', nine minutes of spoken weirdness and wah-wah, and includes 'I Got a Thing, You Got a Thing, Everybody's Got a Thing', 'I'll Bet You' and 'Music for My Mother'. Eddie Hazel's guitar and Billy Nelson's bass are the sound you remember.","Clinton had led the Parliaments, a New Jersey vocal group with a hit in 1967, but a contract dispute took the name from him. He put the backing musicians forward as Funkadelic and signed them to Westbound, a small Detroit label. The records were cut in Detroit as the city's Motown era waned and its hard rock scene rose. Clinton wanted a black band that sounded like Jimi Hendrix and Sly Stone at once.","The same singers and players would soon appear as Parliament on another label; Clinton ran two names for one collective, a trick that became the P-Funk empire. Listen to 'Music for My Mother' for its country-blues harmonica and hollering, a nod to much older rural black music under the psychedelic surface. The opening track's spoken introduction announces the band as a kind of cosmic creed, the first sign of Clinton's later mythology.","Sales were small, and soul radio did not know what to do with it; rock radio mostly ignored a black band this loud. Its reputation rose with the later P-Funk records and with hip-hop producers, who found its grooves ideal for sampling. Most critics now place it just behind Maggot Brain, a rawer first draft of the same idea: that funk and psychedelic rock were one music.","Start with 'I Got a Thing, You Got a Thing, Everybody's Got a Thing' and listen to the vocal harmonies riding the heavy groove. Then 'Music for My Mother', slow and swampy. Play it loud, then follow with Free Your Mind and Maggot Brain to hear the band grow stranger, and with Sly and the Family Stone's Stand! to hear what they were answering."],"ll-cool-j-bigger-and-deffer":["LL Cool J's second album, heavier and slicker than his first. Most tracks are boasts over hard drum-machine beats with scratching: 'I'm Bad' opens with a police-radio scene and a relentless flow, 'Go Cut Creator Go' puts his DJ over a rock-and-roll guitar pastiche. But its most famous track is the odd one out: 'I Need Love', a slow, keyboard-washed song in which the rapper speaks softly about wanting a real relationship rather than conquests.","After Radio, produced by Rick Rubin in 1985, LL was a teenage star on Def Jam, the label that was making rap an album form. For the follow-up he worked with a young production team from Los Angeles, the L.A. Posse, rather than Rubin, who was moving into rock. The album came out in 1987, a crowded year that also brought Public Enemy's and Eric B. and Rakim's debuts, and it sold on a much larger scale than its rivals.","The title's initials spell BAD, and LL's name itself is an abbreviation for 'Ladies Love Cool James'; 'I Need Love' is where that promise turns sincere. Listen to how the beat on that song drops the drums to almost nothing, leaving pads and a gentle rhythm; nearly every rap love song since sits on that template. Notice too how the album alternates tough and tender, the dual persona LL kept for his whole career.","'I Need Love' topped the R&B chart and was called the first great rap ballad, while hardcore listeners accused LL of going soft, a charge he answered in songs for years. Critics now rank the album below Radio and Mama Said Knock You Out as a whole, but its commercial reach proved rap could fill pop radio. The debate about the love song as selling out never really ended; it just moved on to other rappers.","Hear 'I'm Bad' and 'I Need Love' back to back: the same voice, opposite moods. Then listen to 'Go Cut Creator Go' for the turntable work. Compare the drums here with Rubin's sparer sound on Radio, and follow the ballad's line forward into the R&B-rap duets of the 1990s."],"rush-moving-pictures":["Seven tracks in which a three-piece band compresses its long progressive suites into tight, dense songs. 'Tom Sawyer' opens with a growling synthesizer sweep and Neil Peart's famous drum fills; 'Red Barchetta' tells a story of a forbidden car drive in a future where such cars are banned; 'YYZ' is an instrumental built on a rhythm spelling the Toronto airport code in Morse; 'Limelight' reflects on fame. The long 'The Camera Eye' is the last of their extended pieces.","Rush recorded it in late 1980 at Le Studio in Morin-Heights, in the Quebec countryside, with their long-time producer Terry Brown. Having made side-long concept pieces such as 2112 through the late 1970s, they were absorbing new influences: synthesizers, reggae, new wave bands such as the Police. It was released early in 1981, just as MTV and arena radio were reshaping rock, and it became their biggest-selling studio album.","'Red Barchetta' was inspired by a science-fiction story about a man secretly driving an old car in a world that has outlawed them; the song's shifting tempos mimic the chase. In 'YYZ' listen for the opening pattern, the airport's call sign tapped out rhythmically, later doubled by all three instruments. 'Limelight' is Peart's frank account of his discomfort with fans, an unusual subject for a rock hit. 'Witch Hunt' began a series of songs on fear.","Critics who had mocked Rush as pompous found this one harder to dismiss, and fans consider it their peak. 'YYZ' was nominated for a Grammy for rock instrumental. Its reputation has grown with generations of musicians, especially drummers and bassists, for whom Peart's and Geddy Lee's parts became set texts. The old argument about Rush's lyrics, Peart's individualism and Ayn Rand phase, still colours how some listeners hear it.","Begin with 'Tom Sawyer' and follow the drums through the instrumental middle, where Peart's fills become melodies. Then play 'YYZ' and try to hear the three players locking onto one odd pattern. Finish with 'The Camera Eye' to hear the old long-form Rush one last time. Compare with 2112 to see how much they cut away."],"rush-permanent-waves":["Six tracks marking the turn from the long concept pieces of the 1970s to shorter, sharper songs. 'The Spirit of Radio' starts with a racing, glittering guitar figure and swerves briefly into reggae; 'Freewill' sets a statement about choice over tricky meters; 'Jacob's Ladder' paints a storm in slow instrumental blocks; 'Natural Science' is the one long piece, three linked parts about tide pools, technology and human arrogance. Synthesizers now sit beside guitar rather than decorating it.","The album was recorded in late 1979 at Le Studio in Quebec and released in January 1980. After the ten-minute-plus epics of Hemispheres, the band had decided to make tighter songs and to listen to the new wave and reggae around them. 'The Spirit of Radio' took its title from the slogan of a Toronto station, CFNY, that played adventurous new music, a subject that suited a song about radio's decline into formula.","Hugh Syme's cover combines a real newspaper front page from 1948, the famous premature 'Dewey Defeats Truman' headline, with a wind-battered seawall; the joke is about false certainties. In 'The Spirit of Radio', listen for the reggae bars near the end, a nod to the Police and to punk-era London. 'Jacob's Ladder' is almost a tone poem: the band depict cloud and sunlight without much of a tune at all.","It became their first American top-five album and brought in listeners who had never cared for progressive rock. Some long-time fans missed the epics; later critics see it as the hinge on which the band's whole later career turned. Its best-known song is still played on rock radio, the very format it grumbled about, an irony the band acknowledged with some amusement.","Play 'The Spirit of Radio' first and listen for how many sections fit into five minutes. Then hear 'Natural Science' as a miniature of the old epic style, and 'Jacob's Ladder' as weather painted in sound. Follow with Moving Pictures, where the new approach is completed, and compare with Hemispheres to hear what was left behind."],"flying-burrito-brothers-burrito-deluxe":["The second Burrito Brothers album, looser and more rock-leaning than the first. Gram Parsons and Chris Hillman still trade close country harmonies, Sneaky Pete Kleinow's pedal steel still wails, but the new guitarist Bernie Leadon adds a brighter, busier sound, and Hillman has moved to bass. Songs range from honky-tonk ('Lazy Days', 'High Fashion Queen') to gospel ('Farther Along'), with a gentle version of the Rolling Stones' 'Wild Horses' as the emotional centre.","Made in Los Angeles in early 1970 after an unhappy, money-losing tour, the album came as Parsons was spending more time with the Rolling Stones than with his own band. The line-up had changed: bassist Chris Ethridge had left and former Byrds drummer Michael Clarke had joined. Soon after its release Parsons was out of the group, and he would go on to record solo, with Emmylou Harris beside him.","Their 'Wild Horses' came out a year before the Stones' own recording on Sticky Fingers; Mick Jagger and Keith Richards let Parsons have it, a sign of how close he had grown to them. Listen to how slow and exposed his vocal is, set against Kleinow's steel. The traditional 'Farther Along', a gospel hymn, shows Parsons's roots in the Southern church music he had grown up hearing in Georgia and Florida.","Reviews were lukewarm, and even the band thought it weaker than The Gilded Palace of Sin; Parsons himself dismissed parts of it. Later listeners have been kinder, hearing it as a bridge between the Burritos' first record and the country-rock of the Eagles and others that followed. It is now valued mostly for 'Wild Horses' and for the Parsons–Hillman harmonies at their most relaxed.","Go straight to 'Wild Horses' and then play the Stones' version; hear how pedal steel and a Southern voice turn it into a country ballad. Then try 'Farther Along' for the gospel roots. Compare the band's sound with The Gilded Palace of Sin, and follow with Grievous Angel to hear where Parsons went next."],"flying-burrito-brothers-sin-city":["A slow country waltz-time song with a Southern gospel feel, sung in close two-part harmony by Gram Parsons and Chris Hillman. Over strummed acoustic guitar and a sighing pedal steel, the song describes Los Angeles as a gilded, corrupt city heading for judgment, with verses about the rich, the naive, and the vanity of the music business. It sounds like a hymn sung by sinners, which is exactly the effect intended.","Parsons and Hillman wrote it in 1968 while sharing a house in the San Fernando Valley, soon after both had left the Byrds, and it appeared on The Gilded Palace of Sin in 1969. They were building a style they called Cosmic American Music, dressing in rhinestone Nudie suits embroidered with drug and religious imagery, and trying to win hip rock audiences over to the country music most of them despised.","The harmony is modelled directly on the brother duets of the Louvin Brothers and the Everly Brothers, where two voices move almost as one. One verse, about a good man lost, is often read as a reference to the death of Robert Kennedy in Los Angeles in 1968. The mood of the verses, half moral warning and half joke on the band's own world, is what gives the song its double edge.","It was not a hit, but it became the song most associated with the Burritos and a touchstone for later alt-country writers. Emmylou Harris and many others have sung it, and it is often named alongside 'Hickory Wind' as Parsons's finest writing. Critics debate how much credit belongs to Hillman, who co-wrote it and whose harmony is essential.","Listen first to the two voices: how Hillman's harmony shadows Parsons's lead. Then notice the pedal steel filling each gap. Hear it next to a Louvin Brothers or Everly Brothers duet, then next to the Eagles' early records, to hear the line it opened between Nashville and Los Angeles."],"earth-wind-and-fire-all-n-all":["Earth, Wind & Fire at their most lavish: tight funk, soaring ballads and short instrumental interludes stitched into one flowing record. 'Serpentine Fire' rides a bouncing bass and stabbing horns; 'Fantasy' sets Philip Bailey's falsetto over a sweeping string-and-horn arrangement; 'Jupiter' is fast and brassy; 'Love's Holiday' and 'I'll Write a Song for You' are slow and tender. The instrumental 'Runnin'' shows off the band as jazz-inflected players, and brief Brazilian-flavoured interludes tie the album together.","Maurice White, the band's leader and producer, had travelled in South America before the sessions and came back with Brazilian music in his ears. The group was among the biggest acts in America, playing arena shows with magic tricks and pyramids. The album was released by Columbia late in 1977, as disco peaked, and its sleeve by the Japanese artist Shusei Nagaoka set Egyptian temples against space-age imagery.","The short 'Brazilian Rhyme' interludes draw on Milton Nascimento's song 'Ponta de Areia', a quiet act of homage to Brazil's Clube da Esquina musicians. Listen to the horn arrangements: they play short, clipped figures rather than long lines, which is why the grooves feel so buoyant. White's kalimba, the African thumb piano, appears as a signature colour. The mysticism of the sleeve is deliberate, part of White's aim to project uplift and spiritual pride.","It was a huge seller and won Grammy awards, and many fans rank it with That's the Way of the World as the band's best. Some critics at the time found the spirituality vague and the production over-rich; later listeners hear an unusually ambitious soul album that took ideas from jazz and Brazil. Its interludes and horn charts are much sampled and studied.","Start with 'Serpentine Fire' and follow the bass, then 'Fantasy' for Bailey's high voice over the orchestra. Listen straight through to hear how the interludes carry you between songs. Then compare with Milton Nascimento's own recordings and with Stevie Wonder's Songs in the Key of Life, released the year before."],"elliott-smith-elliott-smith":["Twelve quiet songs, mostly just Elliott Smith's hushed voice doubled on itself and his intricately picked acoustic guitar. The playing is fluent, full of moving inner lines, and the melodies are more Beatles-shaped than folk. The words are bleak and precise, touching on addiction in the city, damaged families and self-loathing without melodrama. 'Needle in the Hay' opens with an insistent, scratching strum; 'Christian Brothers' is the one song where a band briefly joins.","Smith was still a member of the Portland rock band Heatmiser when he made these solo recordings, mainly at home on modest equipment. Kill Rock Stars, an independent label in Olympia, Washington, known for punk and riot grrrl, released it in 1995. The Pacific Northwest was coming down from the grunge boom, and a songwriter whispering over acoustic guitar was a deliberate step away from the loud, distorted mainstream.","Almost every vocal is double-tracked: Smith sings each line twice, closely matched, which gives the whisper its ghostly thickness. Listen to how much harmony his guitar carries, moving bass lines and passing chords borrowed from pop rather than folk. The songs name real Portland places, and the narrators are often characters rather than simply the singer, a distinction later listeners tend to forget.","Little noticed at first beyond the indie world, it became central to Smith's reputation after his later fame and his death in 2003. Wes Anderson's use of 'Needle in the Hay' in The Royal Tenenbaums in 2001 introduced it to a wider audience. Some critics call it his purest record, before the larger productions of XO; others find it too narrow. It shaped a generation of bedroom songwriters.","Listen on headphones at low volume, so the double-tracked voice sits right beside you. Start with 'Needle in the Hay' and 'Clementine', then follow the guitar rather than the words on a second listen. Compare with Nick Drake's Pink Moon, then play Either/Or to hear the same voice with more colour around it."],"mehldau-elegiac-cycle":["Brad Mehldau's first solo piano album is a suite of his own compositions on the theme of loss, framed by an opening piece called Bard and a closing one called The Bard Returns. Between them come pieces with titles such as Memory's Tricks, Resignation, Ruins, Trailer Park Ghost and an elegy for William Burroughs and Allen Ginsberg. The sound is closer to a Romantic piano cycle than to a jazz club: rippling left-hand figures, long melodies sung in the inner voices, and improvisation used to develop themes rather than to run changes.","By 1999 Mehldau had made his name with a piano trio and a string of Art of the Trio records for Warner Bros., admired for their unusual repertoire and their introspection. He was also a serious reader of German literature and philosophy, and a devoted listener to Brahms and Schumann. Recording alone let him pursue that side openly. He supplied a long essay for the booklet about Romanticism, irony and elegy, the first of several such essays that made his albums read almost like programme books.","One piece is titled Rückblick, meaning a look back, the same title Brahms gave the intermezzo of his F minor Piano Sonata, Op. 5; it is a quiet signal of the lineage Mehldau is claiming. Themes also recur between pieces, so the returning Bard is not only a title but a musical reprise: the opening material comes back changed by everything heard in between, as in a Schumann cycle.","Reviewers were divided. Some heard a jazz pianist extending his language into a genuinely personal Romantic idiom; others found it earnest, over-explained by its essay, and short on swing. It has aged well, and later solo records such as Live in Tokyo and After Bach made clear it was the start of a long project rather than an indulgence. It is now often cited as the key to his whole temperament.","Listen straight through, as a cycle, and let the Bard's return land as an ending. Notice how Mehldau's two hands work independently, the left often carrying as much melody as the right. Then set it beside Schumann's Kinderszenen or Brahms's late piano pieces, and beside Keith Jarrett's Köln Concert, to hear the two traditions he is joining."],"mehldau-largo":["A studio record in the pop sense: Mehldau's piano surrounded by drum loops, overdubbed brass and woodwinds, treated keyboards and layered percussion. His trio partners Larry Grenadier and Jorge Rossy play on much of it, alongside drummers from the rock and session world. The programme mixes originals with a Beatles song, Dear Prudence, and Radiohead's Paranoid Android, stretched into a long, surging improvisation. The textures change from track to track, but the piano remains the storyteller at the centre of each.","The producer was Jon Brion, best known for his work with Aimee Mann, Fiona Apple and on film scores, who also ran a weekly club night in Los Angeles where Mehldau sometimes sat in. Mehldau had moved to Los Angeles in the late 1990s and was listening widely to rock and electronic music. Warner Bros. gave him room for a record built in the studio rather than captured live, something rare for a straight-ahead jazz pianist at the time.","Brion's studio tricks are easy to take for synthesizers: some of the odd, muffled or clanking sounds are the piano itself, altered by objects placed on the strings or by tape and effects, a pop cousin of John Cage's prepared piano. Listen too to how the drum parts are often edited loops rather than continuous playing, so the band is improvising over a fixed, slightly mechanical floor.","Jazz critics were uneasy; some thought the production swamped the pianist, while rock critics and younger listeners took to it quickly. In hindsight it is seen as a pivot: it widened what a jazz piano record could sound like and pointed toward later Mehldau albums built around arrangement and production, such as Highway Rider. Paranoid Android has become one of his signature performances.","Start with Paranoid Android and compare it with the Radiohead original on OK Computer: notice which parts of the song Mehldau keeps as landmarks and where he lets it run. Then play one of the Art of the Trio albums to hear the same musicians without the studio around them. Headphones help with the layering."],"parliament-funkentelechy-vs-the-placebo-syndrome":["Six long tracks that play as one comic-book war for the soul of dance music. Bop Gun opens with a slow, massive groove and chanting choir; Sir Nose d'Voidoffunk introduces the villain who refuses to dance; Wizard of Finance is a sweet doo-wop parody about managing money; the title track and Placebo Syndrome are sermons over funk; and Flash Light closes with the synthesizer bass line that became Parliament's most imitated sound. Horns, chanted hooks, cartoon voices and Bootsy Collins's elastic bass fill every gap.","George Clinton was running two bands, Parliament and Funkadelic, with a shared pool of musicians, and in 1977 he was at his commercial peak, touring the Mothership stage set with its landing spacecraft. Disco was taking over radio and clubs, and Clinton cast it as the enemy: the Placebo Syndrome is fake funk, music that sells a pulse without feeling. Bernie Worrell's keyboards and Fred Wesley and Maceo Parker's horns, both veterans of James Brown's band, gave the satire serious musical weight.","The title is a joke with a real philosophy underneath: entelechy is Aristotle's word for a thing realising its full potential, so funkentelechy means becoming fully yourself through the funk. The plot pays off in Flash Light, where the stubborn Sir Nose, hit by the light, finally dances. Worrell built that bass line from layered Minimoog parts, a keyboard doing the work of a bass guitar, and the idea spread through funk and later hip-hop.","It was a major hit and is regarded as the peak of the Parliament concept albums, tighter than Mothership Connection and funnier than what followed. Some listeners find the mythology exhausting; others think it is the point, a Black science-fiction allegory that anticipated Afrofuturism as a critical idea. The debate about whether Clinton was fair to disco, which was built largely by Black and gay dancers, continues.","Play Bop Gun loud and notice how slow the tempo is and how much weight it carries. Follow the story through to Flash Light and listen for the bass moving from guitar to synthesizer. Then play Dr. Dre's The Chronic to hear how deeply these grooves and synth leads fed West Coast hip-hop, and Chic's Good Times for the disco the album argues with."],"raekwon-only-built-4-cuban-linx-pt-ii":["A long, dense sequel to Raekwon's 1995 debut: more than twenty tracks of street narrative, with Ghostface Killah again his near co-star and almost every member of the Wu-Tang Clan dropping in. The beats come from many hands, RZA, J Dilla, Pete Rock, Dr. Dre and Marley Marl among them, but they share a palette of dusty soul samples, film dialogue, strings and hard, slow drums. House of Flying Daggers, a posse cut over a Dilla beat, is the track most listeners know first.","The sequel was announced years before it appeared and passed through label changes and delays, including a period when Dr. Dre was expected to oversee it. By 2009 the Wu-Tang Clan's reputation had been dented by uneven solo records and internal quarrels, and New York rap had lost its commercial centre to the South. Raekwon released it through his own label with EMI distribution, aiming squarely at listeners who remembered the original.","Beats came from J Dilla, who had died in 2006; their use here is a kind of posthumous meeting between the Detroit producer and the Wu-Tang sound he admired. The record also keeps up the first album's habit of giving each rapper a Mafia-film alias, so the cast list reads like a crime ensemble, and it deliberately opens with a spoken prologue as the original did.","Sequels to classic albums usually disappoint, and this one did not; critics welcomed it as the best Wu-Tang-related record in years, and many ranked it among the strongest hip-hop albums of 2009. The main reservations are length and the patchwork of producers, which makes it less unified than the RZA-built original. It remains the usual answer to whether a nineties New York rapper could return in full form.","Start with House of Flying Daggers and notice how each rapper enters with a different rhythm against the same loop. Then take the album in two sittings rather than one. Play the 1995 original first if you have not heard it, and listen for the returning characters and the way the storytelling, much of it about the drug trade, has become more reflective and weary."],"raekwon-ice-cream":["A single from Only Built 4 Cuban Linx, with Raekwon joined by Ghostface Killah, Cappadonna and Method Man, who sings the hook. RZA's beat is warm and hazy: a looped soul sample with a slow, lazy drum pattern and a sense of late-afternoon heat. The four voices take turns in a relaxed round, each with a distinct style, from Ghostface's excited rush to Cappadonna's tumbling, run-on phrasing. Among the album's grim crime stories, it is the moment of lightness.","Raekwon's solo debut, released in August 1995, followed Method Man's and Ol' Dirty Bastard's solo debuts, part of RZA's plan to sign members to different labels while keeping the production in-house. Ice Cream was chosen as a single to show the album's playful side and to put Method Man, the group's most famous voice, on radio. Its video helped fix the image of the Clan as a crew of friends rather than a set of rivals, an image that would soon be tested.","The song is a flirtation conceit: women of different complexions compared to flavours of ice cream, a metaphor handled more fondly than crudely, and one that later rappers borrowed. Cappadonna was not yet an official member of the group, and his verse here, alongside his turn on the album, is one reason he became one. Listen for how loosely RZA leaves the drums, almost slurring behind the beat.","Received as a charming change of pace and a showcase for the ensemble, it became one of the best-loved Wu-Tang singles. Some later listeners find its sexual framing dated, even if it is more affectionate than much rap of the period. Its reputation now rests mostly on the chemistry of the four voices and on Method Man's hook.","Listen to it as a relay race: notice where each rapper takes the baton and how the flow changes. Then play C.R.E.A.M., from the Clan's first album, for the same crew in a far darker mood, and the whole of Only Built 4 Cuban Linx to hear where this lighter moment sits in the story."],"suicide-alan-vega-and-martin-rev":["Suicide's second album trades the grimy organ and rattling rhythm box of the debut for cleaner, colder synthesizers and a studio sheen. Martin Rev's parts are still built from one or two repeating patterns, but now they glitter and pulse; Alan Vega's voice floats over them, crooning and murmuring more than screaming. Diamonds, Fur Coat, Champagne mocks glamour, Dance and Touch Me are hypnotic, and Harlem and Sweetheart show how tender the duo could be.","Ric Ocasek of the Cars, then one of the most successful new-wave musicians in America, was a devoted fan and produced the album in New York, giving Rev better equipment and a professional studio. It came out on ZE Records, the downtown label that also released no wave and mutant disco. After the violent audiences of their 1978 British tour, the duo were now being treated as pioneers by a new generation of synthesizer bands in Britain.","Ocasek's production is the hidden subject: listen for how much space and reverb surround Vega's voice, and how the synthesizers are panned and layered, compared with the mono-feeling rawness of 1977. The approach anticipates the clean electronic pop of the early 1980s. Several tracks are close to disco in tempo, a sign of how near ZE's dance roster sat to the duo's minimalism.","It sold little and was long overshadowed by the first album, and some fans felt Ocasek had smoothed away the menace. Its standing has risen steadily: synth-pop and later electronic rock acts point to it as much as to the debut, and reissues have presented it as the sound of Suicide becoming a pop group on their own terms. The argument over which album is truer to the band continues.","Play it straight after the first album and notice what the new synthesizers give and take away. Start with Dance and Harlem. Listen for Vega's Elvis-like croon becoming more intimate as the arrangements get cleaner. Then try Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine and New Order's Blue Monday, both nearby in time and sound."],"suicide-dream-baby-dream":["A single from 1979, recorded between the band's two albums and issued on its own. Martin Rev lays down a gentle, looping keyboard figure and a soft drum-machine pulse that barely changes for six minutes; Alan Vega repeats a handful of simple phrases over it, urging the listener, over and over, to hold on to their dreams. It is almost a hymn or a lullaby, all repetition and tenderness, the opposite of the band's confrontational reputation.","Ric Ocasek produced it for ZE Records, which released it as a single in Britain, where the duo's audience was growing among synthesizer musicians. In New York the punk scene had fragmented and electronic dance music was rising; the track sits on that border. It was not included on either original album and circulated for years mainly among collectors and musicians.","The whole song rests on one idea: the words hardly change, and the drama comes from Vega's small changes of tone and emphasis over a pattern that never moves. That repetition connects it more to Terry Riley and to Kraftwerk than to rock songwriting. Bruce Springsteen, who had admired Suicide since the late 1970s, closed shows on his 2005 solo tour with it, alone at a pump organ.","Little noticed at the time, it has become the most loved Suicide song, largely through covers. Springsteen's performances and his recorded version on High Hopes in 2014 carried it to a huge new audience, and many indie and electronic musicians have recorded it. Some fans prefer its fragile original, where the machines make the tenderness feel stranger.","Put it on and do nothing else for six minutes; let the repetition work. Notice how Vega's voice grows more urgent without the music changing. Then compare Springsteen's pump-organ version, and play Bruce Springsteen's Nebraska to hear how much of Suicide's bare, haunted feeling he had already absorbed."],"weather-report-weather-report":["The band's first album is closer to chamber music than to the funk-driven fusion they later became famous for. Joe Zawinul's electric piano, Wayne Shorter's soprano saxophone and Miroslav Vitouš's acoustic bass weave around each other with no one soloing over a fixed accompaniment. Alphonse Mouzon's drums and Airto Moreira's percussion shimmer rather than drive. Tracks such as Milky Way, Orange Lady, Morning Lake and Eurydice drift, breathe and dissolve; Seventh Arrow and Umbrellas are more urgent.","Zawinul and Shorter had both just played on Miles Davis's In a Silent Way and Bitches Brew, and Zawinul had written much of the former's atmosphere. They formed Weather Report in New York in 1970 with Vitouš, another former Davis sideman, around a principle they described as everyone soloing and no one soloing. Columbia, Davis's label, signed them at a moment when it was eager to sell jazz to rock audiences.","Milky Way, the eerie opening, reportedly contains no ordinary notes at all: the sounds come from Shorter's saxophone setting the strings of an acoustic piano resonating while Zawinul held the keys down silently, with the attacks removed. Orange Lady is a Zawinul melody Miles Davis had also recorded, so the two bands share a tune at the moment of their parting.","Critics greeted it warmly as a new direction for small-group jazz, and it is still admired as the band's most purely collective record. Later fans of Heavy Weather sometimes find it too diffuse; others hear the group's real originality here, before the demands of the rock market pulled it toward groove. It is often compared with the early ECM sound for its space and quiet.","Begin with Orange Lady and listen for how the melody passes from sax to piano to bass without a clear leader. Then go back to Milky Way with headphones. Compare Miles Davis's In a Silent Way, which it grows from, and Heavy Weather, six years later, to hear how far the band travelled from this spacious start."],"alison-krauss-every-time-you-say-goodbye":["Alison Krauss and her band Union Station at the point where their sound was fully formed: close, clean harmony singing, Krauss's high, weightless lead voice, and crisp acoustic picking. The programme mixes aching contemporary ballads, led by the title song, with fast instrumentals such as the old fiddle tune Cluck Old Hen and a gospel number or two. There is also a version of Shawn Colvin's I Don't Know Why, a sign of how far beyond bluegrass Krauss was already looking for material.","Krauss, from Champaign, Illinois, had been a fiddle prodigy and signed to the independent Rounder label as a teenager. By the early 1990s bluegrass was a small world of festivals and specialist labels, and country radio had little room for it. Union Station's line-up was settling into the group that would carry her to mainstream success, and this album, which won a Grammy for Best Bluegrass Album, was a big step toward it.","Listen to how rarely Krauss's voice pushes: bluegrass singing traditionally favours a hard, piercing high lonesome sound, and her soft, breathy approach is a quiet revolution in the style. The band's arrangements are equally restrained, with instruments dropping out under the vocals so that the ballads sound almost like chamber pop, while the instrumentals keep full bluegrass drive.","Bluegrass purists and newcomers both admired it, though some traditionalists felt the ballads leaned toward country-pop. Its reputation has only grown as Krauss became one of the most decorated musicians in Grammy history; it is now seen as the template for her later records and for the progressive bluegrass boom that followed O Brother, Where Art Thou? in 2000.","Start with the title song and then play Cluck Old Hen straight after, to hear the band's two faces. Notice how the harmony voices blend so closely they sound like one. Then compare the Stanley Brothers for the older high lonesome style Krauss softened, and Gillian Welch's Revival for another take on tradition from the same decade."],"mahavishnu-orchestra-apocalypse":["John McLaughlin's second Mahavishnu Orchestra plays with the London Symphony Orchestra, conducted by Michael Tilson Thomas. Five long pieces alternate between delicate orchestral passages, vocal sections sung by keyboardist Gayle Moran, and the band's familiar high-speed unison runs for guitar and violin, now played by Jean-Luc Ponty. The centrepiece, Hymn to Him, runs nearly twenty minutes, moving from meditative stillness to full-orchestra climax. The sound swings between film score, chamber music and hard fusion.","The original Mahavishnu Orchestra had broken up in 1973 amid personal tension. McLaughlin, then a devotee of the guru Sri Chinmoy, reformed the band with new players and a string section, and recorded in London with George Martin, the Beatles' producer, who had the orchestral experience the project needed. Michael Gibbs orchestrated McLaughlin's material. Rock bands from Deep Purple to Procol Harum had tried orchestras; this was a jazz-fusion group's attempt.","Listen for how the band's small string trio acts as a hinge between the rock band and the orchestra, so that textures pass across the stage without a hard seam. McLaughlin also writes some of the orchestral lines in the same odd, Indian-influenced rhythmic cycles as the band's riffs, which is why the LSO sometimes sounds as if it is playing fusion rather than accompanying it.","Reviews were mixed: some welcomed the ambition and the lyricism, others found the vocals and spiritual texts heavy and the orchestra underused. Fans of the first band often prefer Birds of Fire. Its reputation has risen with the revival of interest in orchestral crossover and progressive music, and it is increasingly heard as one of the more serious attempts at a symphonic fusion.","Go straight to Hymn to Him and let its long build play out, listening for the moment the orchestra hands the drive back to guitar and violin. Then play Birds of Fire for the original band's ferocity, Deep Purple's Concerto for Group and Orchestra for a rock parallel, and Stravinsky's Rite of Spring for the kind of orchestral violence McLaughlin was reaching toward."],"mf-doom-operation-doomsday":["The debut of MF DOOM, the masked villain persona of Daniel Dumile, who produced most of it himself. The beats are warm, lo-fi loops lifted from 1970s and 1980s soul, quiet storm and R&B, with snippets of old cartoon dialogue threaded between the songs. Over them Dumile raps in a slow, mumbling, conversational voice packed with internal rhymes, puns and sudden changes of subject. The title track, built on a sample from Sade, sets the melancholy, knowing mood.","In the early 1990s Dumile, as Zev Love X, led the group KMD with his younger brother DJ Subroc. In 1993 Subroc was killed by a car, and soon afterwards Elektra dropped the group and shelved their second album. Dumile disappeared for several years, then reappeared at New York open-mic nights with his face covered, first by a stocking and then by a metal mask. The album came out on Bobbito Garcia's small Fondle 'Em label.","The villain concept is borrowed from Marvel's Doctor Doom, and the cartoon interludes come from 1960s superhero animation, so the whole album reads as a comic in which the rapper casts himself as the bad guy wronged by the industry. Beneath the jokes is grief: the loss of Subroc hangs over the record, and the mask becomes a way of returning to music without showing the face that had been through it.","Barely noticed outside the underground on release, it became a cult classic as Dumile's reputation grew with Madvillainy and MM..Food. Critics now treat it as a founding record of 2000s underground hip-hop, admired for its writing and its casual, loose production. Some listeners find the sound murky and the pace slack; for others that is the charm.","Start with Doomsday and follow the rhymes rather than the story: notice how many syllables chime inside each line. Then listen to the whole album and let the cartoon interludes build the character. After it, play Madvillainy for Dumile with Madlib's beats, and J Dilla's Donuts for another producer building a world from soul records."],"mf-doom-born-like-this":["Credited simply to DOOM, this is his last solo album and a harder, darker record than MM..Food. The beats, mostly his own with contributions from J Dilla and Madlib, are gritty and compressed, with blaring horns, distorted soul loops and abrupt switches. Guests include Raekwon and Ghostface Killah. The word-play is still dense and comic, but the mood is colder, and a recurring sense of apocalypse runs through it, crystallised in a reading by the poet Charles Bukowski.","After MM..Food and Madvillainy in 2004, Dumile's reputation was at its height, but he had become notorious for sending masked impostors to perform his concerts. Born Like This came after a long gap, on the British independent Lex Records, which had released his collaboration with Danger Mouse. Underground hip-hop had lost some commercial ground, and many fans wondered whether he still cared. The album answered by sounding sharper and angrier.","The title comes from Bukowski's poem Dinosauria, We, whose opening words are the album's name; a recording of Bukowski reading it frames the track Cellz, turning a small-press poet into the album's prophet. Gazzillion Ear is built on a J Dilla beat made before his death in 2006. One track's cartoonish treatment of gay men drew criticism as homophobic, a rare blot in his catalogue.","Critics received it well, as a return to form after years of silence, though few ranked it with Madvillainy or Operation: Doomsday. Its reputation has held as a tougher, more abrasive late statement. Debate focuses on the homophobic track and on whether DOOM's persona excused, or merely disguised, its content. After his death in 2020 it gained added weight as his final solo record.","Start with Gazzillion Ear, then Cellz with Bukowski's reading. Notice how much harsher the drums and loops sound than on his earlier records. Then play Operation: Doomsday to hear the same voice ten years earlier, gentler and more wistful, and Raekwon's Only Built 4 Cuban Linx Pt. II, released the same year, for another veteran returning."],"snoop-dogg-gin-and-juice":["The best-known single from Doggystyle, produced by Dr. Dre. The beat is G-funk at its smoothest: a rolling bass line, a slow, loping drum pattern, a high, whining synthesizer line and a sung female hook. Over it Snoop Doggy Dogg narrates a house party that stretches into the next morning, fuelled by drinking and smoking, in his unmistakably relaxed, sing-song drawl. It is less a story than a mood, a picture of an endless Long Beach afternoon.","Doggystyle was released on Death Row Records in November 1993, after Snoop's star turn on Dr. Dre's The Chronic had made him the most anticipated debut in rap. Gin and Juice followed as a single early in 1994. Snoop was at the same time facing a murder charge, of which he was later acquitted, and the gap between his easy-going voice and the headlines became part of his image. MTV played the video heavily.","Listen for how little Snoop raises his voice: he places words slightly behind the beat and lets consonants slide, so that the menace in G-funk is carried by calm rather than aggression. Dre's arrangement keeps the synth lead in a narrow, nasal register that owes a clear debt to Parliament-Funkadelic's Minimoog sounds, the P-Funk inheritance that runs through all of Death Row's records.","A big hit and a Grammy nominee for Best Rap Solo Performance, it became one of the defining songs of 1990s West Coast rap. Critics have argued since about its celebration of intoxication and of a certain carefree gangster image, but its craft is rarely disputed. A bluegrass version by the Texas band the Gourds in the late 1990s showed how well the tune survives outside its genre.","Listen first to the groove alone: bass, drums and that high synth line, and notice how slow and spacious it is. Then follow Snoop's phrasing against it. Play Parliament's Flash Light to hear where the synthesizer sound came from, and Dr. Dre's Nuthin' but a 'G' Thang for Snoop's first turn in the spotlight."],"snoop-dogg-drop-it-like-its-hot":["A duet with Pharrell Williams, who produced it with Chad Hugo as the Neptunes. The track is startlingly bare: tongue clicks, a dry drum machine, a few muted synthesizer notes and long stretches of near-silence, with Pharrell singing the hook in a light falsetto. Snoop raps unhurriedly into the gaps, boasting and teasing. Nearly every sound is small, close and percussive, so the record works less like a groove than a clock ticking in an empty room.","By 2004 Snoop had left Death Row and No Limit and was reinventing himself as a mainstream entertainer. The Neptunes, the most successful production team in pop and rap, signed him to their Star Trak label through Geffen, and the single led his album R&G (Rhythm & Gangsta). Minimal, click-driven beats were in fashion after the Neptunes' own hits, and Snoop wanted a sound that owed nothing to his 1990s records.","The clicks are Pharrell's own mouth sounds, recorded and placed as percussion, so the most distinctive element of the track is a human body imitating a machine. Listen too for how often the bass drops out entirely; the silences are part of the rhythm. The track also nods back to the P-Funk-derived world of Snoop's early records only in attitude, not in sound, a deliberate break with G-funk.","It became Snoop Dogg's first number-one single in the United States, a decade after his debut,, and stayed on the charts for months. Critics praised the daring minimalism; some older fans missed the lush G-funk. It is now often cited as a high point of the Neptunes' style and as proof that Snoop could adapt to a new era.","Listen for the silences rather than the sounds: count how few elements are playing at any moment. Then play Gin and Juice to hear how differently Snoop sounded ten years earlier, and Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine for another pop record built on clicks, space and rigid machine rhythms."],"return-to-forever-hymn-of-the-seventh-galaxy":["Half an hour of loud, knotted electric jazz from a quartet that had just thrown away its flutes and Latin percussion. Chick Corea plays Fender Rhodes and organ, often through distortion; Bill Connors plays a snarling electric guitar; Stanley Clarke's bass leads as often as it accompanies; Lenny White drums like a rock player. Tunes such as the title track, Captain Señor Mouse, Space Circus and The Game Maker pack unison runs, sudden stops and swerving tempos into tight compositions, with solos squeezed between the written sections.","Only a year earlier Return to Forever had been a gentle, Brazilian-tinged band built around the voice of Flora Purim and the flute of Joe Farrell. By 1973 the ground had shifted: the Mahavishnu Orchestra was filling rock halls with volume and speed, and Corea, a Miles Davis alumnus from the Bitches Brew period, rebuilt his group along the same lines. This was its first record as an electric rock-scaled quartet, made in New York for Polydor, and aimed as much at rock audiences as at jazz clubs.","Listen to Stanley Clarke on After the Cosmic Rain, the one piece here he wrote: the bass is not holding the floor but singing the melody, bowed and plucked phrases standing out ahead of the band. It is an early glimpse of the solo bass voice that would make Clarke a star. And notice how much of the record is composed: the apparently wild passages are often doubled note for note by keyboard and guitar together.","It was received as the moment Return to Forever became a fusion band, and it still divides listeners. Admirers hear the most raw and urgent version of the group, before its music grew grander and more polished; critics then and since found the playing airless and hectic next to the lyricism of the Light as a Feather band. Bill Connors left soon after, and the record is often overshadowed by the Al Di Meola albums that followed, though guitarists still prize his tone here.","Start with the title track, which lays out the whole method in three and a half minutes: a solemn keyboard hymn that snaps into jagged, rapid unison figures. Then play Light as a Feather, the band's previous record, straight afterwards to hear how far the same leader, bassist and compositional instincts travelled in a single year. Follow with Mahavishnu's Birds of Fire, the record this one was answering."],"return-to-forever-where-have-i-known-you-before":["The first Return to Forever album with the teenage guitarist Al Di Meola, and the line-up that defined the band. Long band pieces such as Vulcan Worlds, The Shadow of Lo, Beyond the Seventh Galaxy and the sprawling Song to the Pharoah Kings alternate with three short solo-piano interludes whose titles all begin with the album's question. Corea now adds Minimoog and other synthesizers to the electric piano, and Di Meola answers with fast, clean, precisely picked lines that lock with Stanley Clarke and Lenny White.","Di Meola was about nineteen and still a student at Berklee when Corea hired him in 1974 to replace Bill Connors. The band was now touring rock venues and selling records on a scale jazz rarely reached; fusion had become a commercial genre. Corea was also deep in the cosmic and spiritual imagery that runs through his titles of these years, and this album sets out the space-opera atmosphere the group would keep until its break-up in the late 1970s.","The quiet solo-piano interludes are the key to the record, not filler. Played on acoustic piano, they are closer to the Corea of Now He Sings, Now He Sobs than to anything electric, and they set up the modal colours the band then blows up to full volume. Listen too for Clarke's bass on Earth Juice, a short feature that shows the slapping and chording he was developing for his own solo albums.","Fans often name it as the moment the classic quartet clicked, and Di Meola's arrival was treated as big news in guitar magazines. Jazz critics were cooler, some of them finding the long compositions overbuilt and the synthesizer tones already dated. The band's reputation was later consolidated by No Mystery and Romantic Warrior, and this record is now usually heard as the bridge to them rather than as a peak of its own.","Play Vulcan Worlds first for the band at full speed, then the solo piano piece that follows it to hear the contrast the album is built on. Save Song to the Pharoah Kings, the long closer, for last: it moves through a slow, modal opening, a synthesizer passage and a driving finale. If you like it, go straight on to Romantic Warrior."],"van-halen-1984":["A thirty-three-minute record that pulls in two directions at once. The short title track and Jump open it with synthesizer, not guitar: a bright, stacked keyboard riff over a steady rock beat. Then Panama, Hot for Teacher, Girl Gone Bad and House of Pain return to the band's home ground of fast, swaggering hard rock, with Eddie Van Halen's guitar, David Lee Roth's showman vocals, Michael Anthony's high harmonies and Alex Van Halen's drums. I'll Wait mixes both worlds.","It was the first album made at 5150, the studio Eddie Van Halen built at his home in the Hollywood Hills so that he could work without the band's producer and label pushing him to repeat himself. Ted Templeman still produced. The early eighties were the years of MTV, and the band's comic, colourful videos, above all Hot for Teacher, played constantly. It was also the last album with Roth for more than twenty years; he left in 1985.","Hot for Teacher opens with Alex Van Halen playing double bass drum under a tapped guitar figure, a passage drummers still treat as a test piece; it sounds like an engine revving before the song pulls away. And listen to how little guitar there is in Jump: Eddie takes only a brief solo, then hands the break straight to a synthesizer solo, a small scandal for the most imitated guitarist in rock.","Jump went to number one in America, and the album sold in enormous numbers, held off the top of the chart only by Michael Jackson's Thriller. Some guitar fans heard the keyboards as a betrayal, and Roth later made clear he had not wanted them. Time has been kind: the record is now heard as the band's most complete balance of hard rock and pop, and the model for the polished, keyboard-flecked rock of the rest of the decade.","Hear it in order: the synthesizer overture into Jump, then the jolt of Panama, then the chaos of Hot for Teacher. Notice how Michael Anthony's backing vocals thicken every chorus. Then play the band's 1978 debut for the rawer, all-guitar version of the same four people, and Thriller for the pop record this one was racing."],"van-halen-eruption":["A guitar solo of under two minutes that sits second on Van Halen's first album. After a short band flourish, Eddie Van Halen plays alone: fast runs, squealing harmonics, the whammy bar dragging notes down into a growl, and a deep tremolo. Then comes the passage everyone remembers, in which he reaches over the neck and taps the strings with a finger of his picking hand, producing cascades of notes so fast and even that they sound less like a guitar than a keyboard.","Van Halen had spent years playing Los Angeles clubs and backyard parties before Warner Bros. signed them in 1977. The solo was Eddie's party piece, a showcase he played live, and according to his own account producer Ted Templeman heard him rehearsing it in the studio and asked to record it. It went down quickly, and Eddie said for years that he heard mistakes in it. He had built his guitar himself from spare parts, the striped instrument fans later called the Frankenstrat.","Part of the tapped passage near the end appears to be adapted from an étude by Rodolphe Kreutzer, a violin teacher of Beethoven's era, whose studies Eddie and Alex knew from their classical music lessons as children. So the most famous rock guitar solo of its decade has a nineteenth-century practice exercise inside it. Tapping itself was not new, but no one had made it sound this fluid.","Guitarists heard it as a revolution almost at once, and within a few years tapping, whammy-bar dives and flashy speed had become the vocabulary of eighties metal and hard rock, for better and worse. Critics who disliked that shred era sometimes blamed Eruption for it. Its standing has never really fallen: it is still named near the top of every list of great guitar solos, and it made Eddie the most copied player since Hendrix.","Listen for the moment around the one-minute mark when the tapping begins and the sound turns liquid; then go back and hear how carefully the rougher opening sets it up. Compare it with Paganini's Twenty-Fourth Caprice for the same idea on violin, one instrument made to sound impossible. Hendrix's Star-Spangled Banner is the grandparent."],"devo-freedom-of-choice":["Twelve short, tight songs in which Devo's jerky art-punk becomes streamlined synth-pop. Stiff drum patterns, buzzing synthesizer bass lines and chopped guitar chords lock together like parts of a machine, and Mark Mothersbaugh's clipped, nasal voice rides on top. Whip It, with its whip-crack snare and loping synth riff, is the famous one, but Girl U Want, the title song, Gates of Steel and Cold War share the same bright, rigid sound. Almost nothing runs past four minutes.","Devo came out of Akron and Kent State, Ohio, where Gerald Casale and Mark Mothersbaugh had built a theory of de-evolution, the idea that humanity was sliding backwards into conformity. By their third album they had moved to Los Angeles, and producer Robert Margouleff helped them build a cleaner electronic sound. This is the record that introduced the red, stepped energy-dome hats, and Whip It, helped by its absurd video, became a surprise American hit in 1980.","Whip It sounds like a motivational jingle, and that is the joke: Casale has described the words as a pastiche of American self-help slogans, the can-do optimism the band found so empty. Once you hear it as satire, the whole album reads that way, including the title song, which describes a world with endless choices in which nobody really chooses at all.","Whip It gave Devo their only big American hit, and many listeners took them for a novelty act in funny hats, which the band both courted and resented. Critics were split between those who heard a sell-out from the sharper Q: Are We Not Men? and those who heard the concept finally matching the sound. It is now usually counted among their best albums and a key step towards eighties synth-pop.","Start with Girl U Want, the opener, to hear the new sound at its tightest, then Whip It, then Gates of Steel, the most urgent song on the record. Listen to how every instrument plays short, clipped notes, never a sustained chord. Then hear Kraftwerk's The Man-Machine for the sleeker European cousin of this idea."],"devo-jocko-homo":["Devo's manifesto in song. Over a stiff, lurching groove in an odd count of seven, Mark Mothersbaugh plays a lecturer or preacher explaining that humanity is devolving, and the band answers him in a chanted call-and-response. Midway the song switches into a faster, almost cheerleading passage and the chant turns into a crowd ritual. The sound is thin, harsh and deliberately mechanical: jabbing guitar, cheap synthesizer, drums that never swing.","The band released it themselves in 1977 on their own Booji Boy label, with Mongoloid on the other side, after years of being mocked in Akron. It came with a short film, The Truth About De-Evolution, made with Chuck Statler, which won a prize at the Ann Arbor Film Festival and got them noticed. The title comes from Jocko-Homo Heavenbound, a 1920s anti-evolution pamphlet the band had found, and the song's catechism borrows from the 1932 horror film Island of Lost Souls.","Count along: the verses are in seven beats, not four, which is why the groove feels like it keeps tripping over its own feet. That lopsided pulse is the idea made audible, a body that cannot quite march in step. The question and answer that made the band's name, and later the title of their first album, is lifted almost directly from the beast-men's creed in Island of Lost Souls.","It confused and annoyed audiences at first, as intended, and punk fans were not sure whether Devo were mocking them too. David Bowie and Iggy Pop championed the band, which led to their first album, where the song was re-recorded. The homemade single is now treated as one of the founding documents of American new wave, and the film as an early model for the music video.","Try to find the original 1977 single rather than the album remake: it is rougher and stranger. Watch The Truth About De-Evolution alongside it, since the song was made to be seen. Listen for the switch into the fast section, where a lecture becomes a rally, and then play Pere Ubu or Suicide for the rest of that raw Midwestern and New York moment."],"sean-paul-dutty-rock":["A long, crowded dancehall album built from riddims, the shared instrumental tracks that many Jamaican deejays voice in turn. Sean Paul rides them in his quick, slurred, half-sung patois, full of catchphrases and nicknames, while the beats run from sparse drum-machine clatter to glossier R&B-flavoured productions. The singles carry it: Gimme the Light, Get Busy on the lurching Diwali riddim, Like Glue, and the love duet I'm Still in Love with You with the singer Sasha. The mood is party, flirtation and swagger.","Sean Paul had built his name on Kingston sound systems and singles in the late 1990s. His second album came out through VP Records, the reggae label based in New York, with Atlantic handling the wider market, at a moment when American hip-hop and R&B were hungry for Caribbean rhythm. A Busta Rhymes remix of Gimme the Light helped open radio, Get Busy went to number one in the United States, and the album won the Grammy for Best Reggae Album.","Many of these beats were not made for Sean Paul alone. The Diwali riddim under Get Busy, built by producer Steven Lenky Marsden around a clapping, Indian-flavoured rhythm, carried several other hits by other artists in the same season. Hearing those alongside it shows how dancehall works: the rhythm is common property, and the deejay's job is to own it for three minutes.","It made Sean Paul the most commercially successful dancehall artist ever and put Jamaican rhythm onto pop radio across the world. Purists complained that it was dancehall smoothed for export, and that the lyrics rarely went beyond the dance floor. Others heard a faithful translation of real Kingston riddims into global pop, and its influence on later Caribbean-flavoured pop, from Rihanna onwards, is now widely recognised.","Do not play it straight through; it is long. Start with the four singles, then dip into the deeper cuts voiced over harder, more minimal riddims. Listen to how Sean Paul uses rhythm more than melody, clipping and stretching syllables against the beat. Then go back to Wayne Smith's Under Mi Sleng Teng to hear where digital dancehall began."],"sean-paul-temperature":["A fast, relentless dancehall single built on a bare riddim of handclaps, a thudding kick drum and a few stabbing synthesizer chords. Sean Paul barely pauses for breath, tumbling syllables in rapid patois through a song that is essentially an invitation to spend a cold night together. There is almost no melody in the usual sense; the hook is rhythmic, a bouncing chant, and the whole track is built to keep a dance floor moving without a break.","It came from The Trinity, Sean Paul's third album, released in 2005, and became his biggest American hit, reaching number one on the Billboard Hot 100 in 2006. The beat is the Applause riddim, produced by Rohan Snowcone Fuller in Jamaica, and like most riddims it was also voiced by other deejays. By then dancehall was a fixture on international pop and hip-hop radio, and Sean Paul was its best-known face.","Listen to the clapping pattern: it is the riddim's signature, and it is off-balance, landing slightly against where a pop listener expects the beat, which is what gives the song its forward tilt. The rest of the production is almost empty. Nearly all the melodic interest comes from the voice, which is used like a percussion instrument, darting between the claps.","It was a huge commercial success and a club staple for years, heard far beyond Jamaica and the United States. Critics have tended to treat it as a pure party record, enjoyable and slight, and roots-reggae fans saw it as dancehall at its most commercial. It remains one of the most played Caribbean records of its decade and a benchmark for dancehall-pop crossovers, the model many later island-flavoured pop hits followed.","Play it loud and follow the handclaps first, then the voice darting between them. Then look for other songs voiced on the Applause riddim to hear how the same beat carries different deejays. Compare it with Get Busy from three years earlier: the formula is similar, but this one is faster, sparer and harder."],"television-adventure":["Television's second album, eight songs in which the band's two guitars, Tom Verlaine's and Richard Lloyd's, weave around each other in clean, bright, carefully placed lines rather than power chords. The sound is softer and more polished than the debut, the tempos more relaxed. Glory and Foxhole are brisk and tuneful; Days is almost pastoral; Carried Away adds a hymn-like organ; The Fire and the long closing The Dream's Dream drift into slow, dreamy guitar interplay. Verlaine's high, strained voice sits on top.","It came out in spring 1978, a year after Marquee Moon had made Television heroes to critics and to the new punk scene in Britain, though it had sold little in America. The band, formed in New York around CBGB with Verlaine, Lloyd, Fred Smith on bass and Billy Ficca on drums, made it for Elektra under heavy pressure to follow a much-praised debut. It charted higher in Britain than at home, and the group split up that summer.","Put on headphones and separate the guitars: Verlaine usually in one channel, Lloyd in the other, each playing a part that only makes sense against its partner. On The Dream's Dream the two lines trade the lead without any clear hand-off, which is the band's whole method in slow motion. The instrumental closing stretch is where Television came nearest to their long live improvisations.","Reviewers mostly admired it, but it has always lived in the debut's shadow, and many found it too clean and cautious after the tension of Marquee Moon. Its reputation has risen steadily; admirers now hear a warmer, more varied record, and some prefer its quieter songs. It remains, with the debut, one of only two albums from the band's original run, which is why it matters so much to the story of New York rock.","Hear Marquee Moon first, then this, so you know what the band chose to soften. Start with Days for the gentlest guitar interplay, then Glory for the most direct song, and end with The Dream's Dream played on headphones. Afterwards, try Wire's Chairs Missing, a British record that learned a great deal from this kind of patience."],"blondie-call-me":["A driving, glossy rock single built on a pounding eighth-note pulse, snarling guitar and a bright synthesizer line, with Debbie Harry singing a tough, cool vocal about a lover who can be reached any time, day or night, on the terms she sets. The chorus lifts into a punchy shout of the title, and Harry slips briefly into Italian and French for the same idea. It is short, hard and relentless, disco's machinery with a rock band's attack.","Giorgio Moroder, the producer behind Donna Summer's I Feel Love, was scoring Paul Schrader's film American Gigolo, starring Richard Gere, and wanted a theme song. He sent Harry an instrumental track; she watched the film and wrote the words around its opening scenes of Gere driving through Los Angeles. Moroder produced it, and in 1980 it spent six weeks at number one in the United States, becoming one of the year's biggest singles.","The film's own soundtrack album carries a much longer version, around eight minutes, in which the instrumental sections stretch out into Moroder's familiar sequencer-driven style. Hearing that version shows how much of the single's drive comes from the machine underneath rather than from the band. Notice also how Harry's vocal is placed right on top of the beat, never behind it, a disco habit applied to rock.","It was a massive hit and confirmed Blondie as the band that could move between punk, disco, reggae and rap. Some early punk loyalists heard the Moroder gloss as further proof the band had left CBGB behind. Critics now tend to hear it as one of the great eighties film songs and a model for the synthesized hard pop that followed, and it is among the band's most covered and licensed recordings.","Play it next to Heart of Glass on Parallel Lines: both are Blondie meeting dance music, but this one comes at disco from the rock side, where Heart of Glass came from the club side. Then hear Donna Summer's I Feel Love to know Moroder's engine, and find the long soundtrack version when you want to hear the machine run on its own."],"j-dilla-welcome-2-detroit":["J Dilla's first solo album, released under the name Jay Dee, a varied hour of beats, raps and instrumentals from a producer who had until then worked mostly for others. Dilla raps on several tracks himself and gives others to Detroit friends such as Frank-N-Dank and Elzhi. Between them come instrumentals and genre excursions: a Brazilian-flavoured piece, a cover of Donald Byrd's jazz-funk Think Twice, and a clattering electro track. The drums are loose, slightly off the grid, and warmly muffled.","By 2001 Dilla was a producer's producer, known for his work with A Tribe Called Quest, The Pharcyde, Common, Erykah Badu and his own group Slum Village. The British label BBE invited him to make the first album in its Beat Generation series, a showcase for hip-hop producers. He used it to present Detroit, his city and its rappers, rather than to chase the stars he usually worked for, and he played some of the instruments himself.","The electro track's title, B.B.E., is spelled out as a Big Booty Express, a joke on Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express, and its hard, mechanical beat is a real tribute: Detroit's techno pioneers built their music on Kraftwerk, and Dilla is placing himself in that local line. Listen for how the drums on the other tracks deliberately avoid that machine precision, landing a fraction early or late.","It sold modestly and was noticed mainly by producers and crate-diggers, who treated it as a sampler of Dilla's range. Critics sometimes found it uneven, since the guest rappers vary and the instrumentals feel like sketches. Its standing rose sharply after his death in 2006, when listeners traced his influence back through it, and it is now valued as the first full statement of his own taste.","Start with the Donald Byrd cover, Think Twice, to hear Dilla as an arranger and player rather than a sampler, then the electro track, then the rap tracks with his Detroit friends. Listen to the kick and snare in any song and notice how they drag and push. Then play Donuts to hear where that feel ended up."],"j-dilla-the-shining":["A compact album of full songs, not beat sketches, in which Dilla's warm, swaying productions carry a series of guests. Common and D'Angelo meet on the soulful So Far to Go; Busta Rhymes, Pharoahe Monch, Black Thought, Madlib, MED and Guilty Simpson appear elsewhere, and Dilla raps and sings himself. The sound is lush and heavy: thick bass, crackling drums that lean behind the beat, and chopped soul samples that loop into something hypnotic.","Dilla was working on it during the illness that killed him in February 2006, at the age of thirty-two; he had been making beats from a hospital bed and a wheelchair. It was left unfinished, and his friend the drummer and producer Karriem Riggins completed it, with release on BBE that August, a few months after Donuts. It was the last album Dilla shaped himself, and it became part of a flood of posthumous tributes.","Riggins was careful to leave Dilla's decisions audible, and the record's rough edges, abrupt endings and sketch-like interludes are partly the traces of an unfinished project, not production choices. So Far to Go later reappeared in a different form on Common's album Finding Forever, which shows how a single Dilla beat could carry more than one song in more than one life.","It was received warmly and with grief, and inevitably measured against Donuts; many preferred Donuts' strangeness, while others heard in The Shining the more conventional song album Dilla had long wanted to make. Arguments continue about posthumous releases in general and how much of any of them is really his, but this one, completed by a close collaborator, is usually treated as the most faithful.","Hear Donuts first, then this: the same months, but a record built for voices rather than one made of fragments. Start with So Far to Go for Common and D'Angelo, then listen for the tracks where Dilla himself raps. Pay attention to the drums, which sit a hair late throughout, and compare them with D'Angelo's Voodoo, where live players learned that feel from him."],"lauryn-hill-mtv-unplugged-no-2-0":["A double live album of new songs performed by Lauryn Hill alone, singing and playing acoustic guitar, with long spoken reflections between them. The songs, including Mr. Intentional, Adam Lives in Theory, I Gotta Find Peace of Mind, Mystery of Iniquity and I Get Out, deal with spiritual crisis, betrayal, the music industry and personal freedom. Her voice is often raw and hoarse and the guitar is simple, a few chords repeated. She also sings Bob Marley's So Much Things to Say.","Hill recorded it in New York in July 2001 for MTV's Unplugged series, three years after The Miseducation of Lauryn Hill had made her the most celebrated singer in America and won her five Grammys. In between she had withdrawn from public life and said she was rejecting the image built around her. It was released in 2002 and turned out to be her last new album; no studio follow-up to Miseducation has appeared.","Mystery of Iniquity, a dense, fast song-sermon about corruption in the justice system, had a second life: Kanye West built his 2004 song All Falls Down around its melody, with the singer Syleena Johnson re-recording Hill's part. And listen to the spoken passages, not only the songs; they are part of the work, and she mentions in them that her voice is failing, which changes how the singing sounds.","Reviewers in 2002 were largely baffled or unkind, calling it self-indulgent, underproduced and closer to a breakdown than a concert; sales were far below Miseducation. Its reputation has grown a great deal. Listeners and critics now hear courage in its exposure and real songwriting in pieces such as Mystery of Iniquity, and some rank it as one of the most unguarded records a major star has ever released.","Do not start at the beginning: go first to Mystery of Iniquity, then I Get Out, then I Gotta Find Peace of Mind, and only then take the whole set with its talk. Listen to the voice cracking as a form of expression. Compare it with Bob Dylan's early solo records and Marley's Exodus, the two traditions Hill is drawing on here."],"sufjan-stevens-the-age-of-adz":["An abrupt turn from banjo and chamber folk to electronics. Glitching drum machines, buzzing synthesizers, distorted beats and bursts of brass and choir surround Sufjan Stevens's soft, high voice. Only the opening Futile Devices recalls his gentle folk records; after it come overloaded songs such as Too Much, the title track, I Walked, Vesuvius and I Want to Be Well. It ends with Impossible Soul, a suite of about twenty-five minutes that moves from anguish to an Auto-Tuned dance party to a quiet ending.","After Illinois in 2005, Stevens abandoned his plan to write an album for every American state, made an orchestral film piece, The BQE, and fell into a creative crisis. He has spoken of a debilitating viral illness affecting his nerves during that time. He turned to the art of Royal Robertson, a Louisiana sign painter who lived with schizophrenia and made apocalyptic, space-age visions; Robertson's work provides the cover and much of the album's imagery. It appeared on Stevens's own label in 2010.","Robertson's pictures were full of prophecy, comic-book spacecraft and furious lettering about betrayal, and once you know that, the album's alien noise sounds less like a style experiment than an attempt to enter his head. Listen too for the Auto-Tune in Impossible Soul: Stevens uses it not as polish but to make his voice sound strained and synthetic just as the song reaches its most emotional point.","Many fans of Illinois were thrown by it, and early reviews split between those who heard a brave reinvention and those who heard an exhausting mess. It sold well and grew in reputation, especially as later indie music caught up with its mix of orchestral and electronic sound. It is now often called his most daring album, though debate continues over whether Impossible Soul earns its length.","Begin with Futile Devices and then jump straight to Too Much, so you feel the shock of the change as first listeners did. Save Impossible Soul for a sitting on its own and follow its sections as you would a symphony's movements. Then play Kanye West's 808s & Heartbreak for another singer using Auto-Tune to express pain."],"chic-risque":["Chic's third album is seven songs and under forty minutes, and it opens with the most copied groove of its age, Good Times: Bernard Edwards's walking, springing bass line, Nile Rodgers's clipped rhythm guitar and Tony Thompson's dry, exact drums, with Alfa Anderson and Luci Martin singing in cool unison. The rest of the record is stranger than its reputation. My Forbidden Lover and My Feet Keep Dancing are sleek dance cuts, but Will You Cry and What About Me turn melancholy, with string lines that sound close to regret.","Edwards and Rodgers made Risqué at the Power Station in New York at the height of their powers, fresh from the enormous success of Le Freak and from producing Sister Sledge. It came out in the summer of 1979, the summer of the Disco Demolition Night riot at Comiskey Park in Chicago, when a crowd blew up disco records in the outfield. The backlash against the genre was organised, partly racial and partly homophobic, and Chic's commercial moment was suddenly closing.","Listen to the album as a whole and its title starts to read as irony. Beneath the glamour, several songs are about losing control, being used or being left: the elegance is a mask worn over unease. Rodgers has said that Chic wanted to smuggle what he called deep hidden meaning into dance records. Notice too how much of Good Times is empty space; the break in the middle, bass and handclaps almost alone, is exactly what hip-hop DJs would later loop.","Critics at the time were slow to take a disco band seriously as album-makers, and the backlash meant Risqué sold on the strength of one single. Its standing has climbed ever since. Good Times became the foundation of Rapper's Delight and the acknowledged model for the bass line of Queen's Another One Bites the Dust, and the album is now often called Chic's best: proof that Edwards and Rodgers were composers and arrangers of real subtlety, not merely hit-makers riding a fashion.","Play Good Times loud and follow only the bass for its full length; Edwards never plays it quite the same way twice. Then skip to Will You Cry, where the strings and the unison voices turn wistful. Compare the Sugarhill Gang record built on the same groove, and then Nile Rodgers's later guitar on Daft Punk's Random Access Memories. The original vinyl mix has the most air around the drums."],"robert-glasper-in-my-element":["A piano trio record, Glasper with Vicente Archer on bass and Damion Reid on drums, that sounds like a jazz trio listening to hip-hop. The drums sit behind the beat and repeat short, looping patterns; the piano works in long, circling chord cycles rather than bebop lines. The best-known track welds Herbie Hancock's Maiden Voyage to Radiohead's Everything in Its Right Place, and J Dillalude strings together tributes to the Detroit producer J Dilla, played live by acoustic instruments.","Glasper, from Houston, had come up in New York playing piano both for jazz leaders and for hip-hop and soul artists such as Mos Def and Bilal. In My Element was his second album for Blue Note, the label of Hancock and Monk, and it appeared a year after J Dilla's death in 2006, when young jazz musicians were openly admitting that Dilla's wobbling, unquantised beats had shaped their sense of time as much as Tony Williams had.","In the Dilla medley the drummer is imitating a machine that was itself imitating a human: Dilla programmed his beats slightly off the grid, and Reid reproduces those drunken placements by hand. In the Hancock and Radiohead track, notice that the two pieces fit because both float on the same kind of suspended, unresolved harmony; Glasper is making an argument about kinship, not just a mash-up for fun.","Jazz critics welcomed it as a fresh trio voice and recognised that the hip-hop feel was structural, not decoration. Some purists found the vamps long and the soloing restrained. In hindsight the record reads as the bridge to Glasper's later work: the argument he would make loudly on Black Radio, that jazz and hip-hop are one family, is already being made here quietly, by an acoustic trio.","Begin with the Maiden Voyage and Radiohead track, then play Hancock's original and Kid A side by side to hear what Glasper joined. Next try J Dillalude and listen only to the drums, noticing how each beat lands a hair late. Follow it with Dilla's own Donuts. A good pair of headphones helps; the bass is recorded close and warm."],"robert-glasper-double-booked":["A record deliberately split in two. The first half is Glasper's acoustic trio, with Vicente Archer on bass and Chris Dave on drums, playing jazz with hip-hop time. The second half is the Robert Glasper Experiment: Fender Rhodes and synthesizers, Derrick Hodge on electric bass, Casey Benjamin on saxophone and vocoder, and guest singers including Bilal and Mos Def. Between the halves, the sound changes from wood and felt hammers to electric keys and filtered voices.","By 2009 Glasper was leading two careers at once, the respected Blue Note jazz pianist and the keyboard player in demand for neo-soul and hip-hop sessions. The album's title and its framing interludes stage that problem directly: a musician booked for two gigs on the same night, trying to be in both places. Chris Dave, whose loose, off-kilter drumming carries both bands, was becoming one of the most imitated drummers in black music.","Listen to Chris Dave across the two halves: he is the hinge of the whole record, playing almost the same way with brushes behind a trio and with a hard backbeat behind the Experiment. The trio half includes a reworking of Hancock's Riot and a Monk tune, while the electric half pulls toward Dilla and D'Angelo. The spoken interludes are small comic scenes, but they carry the album's thesis.","Reviewers were divided along the same line the album draws. Jazz writers mostly preferred the trio half; younger listeners and R&B critics heard the Experiment as the future. That verdict was settled by Black Radio three years later, which grew straight out of the second half and won a Grammy. Double-Booked is now read as the moment Glasper publicly chose not to choose.","Listen straight through once, noticing the exact moment the acoustic piano gives way to the Rhodes. Then compare the trio half with In My Element and the Experiment half with Black Radio, to hear the album as a doorway between them. For the drumming, follow Chris Dave's hi-hat alone on one track; it rarely falls where you expect."],"husker-du-new-day-rising":["Fifteen short songs from a Minnesota trio, Bob Mould on guitar, Grant Hart on drums, Greg Norton on bass, played at hardcore speed but built on real melodies. The title track is almost nothing but the three title words chanted over a roaring guitar. Mould's guitar is a single wall of treble-heavy distortion that sounds like a jet engine and a twelve-string at once; above it, the two singers trade songs, Mould hoarse and furious, Hart higher and more tuneful.","Hüsker Dü recorded it in 1984 for SST, the California punk label of Black Flag and the Minutemen, only months after the double album Zen Arcade. The American hardcore scene prized speed and suspicion of anything pretty; the band came from the Twin Cities, far from the coastal centres, and toured in a van through a national network of halls and basements that bands and fans had built themselves.","Under the noise, these are pop songs with verses, choruses and hooks: Celebrated Summer even drops into a quiet acoustic passage before the storm returns, and The Girl Who Lives on Heaven Hill has a chorus the Byrds would have recognised. The famously murky production buries the vocals and cymbals in the guitar; listeners still argue whether that murk is a flaw or the whole point.","It was hailed in fanzines and by critics as the moment hardcore learned to write songs. Its reputation has only grown with time, since the melodic, distorted sound it pioneered became the basis of college rock and later grunge. The one lasting complaint is the mix, and a proper remaster has long been wished for; disputes between the band and its old label over rights and royalties kept the SST albums in limbo for decades.","Start with Celebrated Summer, listening for the moment the storm stops and an acoustic guitar appears, then the wall returns. Then play the title track and let the chant work on you. Hear it next to Zen Arcade for the ambition and next to the Pixies' Surfer Rosa for the legacy. Turn it up: the album is built to be felt as much as heard."],"husker-du-candy-apple-grey":["Ten songs that pull Hüsker Dü's roar toward clarity. The guitar wall is still there on Crystal and Dead Set on Destruction, but the production is cleaner and the voices are finally audible. Grant Hart's Sorry Somehow and Don't Want to Know If You Are Lonely are bright, organ-coloured pop songs; Bob Mould's Too Far Down and Hardly Getting Over It are almost bare, acoustic and slow, confessions of depression sung without any armour of noise.","In 1985 Hüsker Dü left SST and signed to Warner Bros., one of the first bands from the American independent punk network to join a major label. Many in the scene treated this as betrayal. Candy Apple Grey, released in early 1986, was the test: would a major-label Hüsker Dü still sound like itself? Tensions between Mould and Hart as songwriters were growing, and the band would break up within two years.","Notice how the album splits its ten songs almost evenly between its two writers, and how differently they handle despair: Hart sets his sadness to buoyant melodies, while Mould strips his down until nothing is left but a strummed guitar and a voice. Hardly Getting Over It, about the death of parents and the fear of it, is the quietest thing the band ever recorded, and one of the most frightening.","Some fans and critics called the record softened and compromised; others heard growth. Commercially it made little impression, and Warner's patience would not last long. Its real influence was as a precedent: when Sonic Youth and then Nirvana moved to majors a few years later, Hüsker Dü had already shown it could be done without surrender. The acoustic Mould songs pointed toward his later solo work.","Play Crystal and then Too Far Down back to back to hear the album's two extremes. Follow with Hart's Sorry Somehow, the band's nearest thing to a radio hit. Compare it with New Day Rising to hear how much the production changed, and then with Nirvana's Nevermind, another punk band's first major-label record, five years later."],"jason-isbell-something-more-than-free":["Eleven songs of working lives in the American South, told by one of the best short-story writers in song. The sound is warm and unhurried: acoustic guitars, a steady rhythm section, Amanda Shires's fiddle drifting around the edges, and Isbell's clear Alabama tenor right at the front. If It Takes a Lifetime opens with a man holding himself together by routine; the title song describes shift work as a kind of prayer; Speed Trap Town tells of a son leaving a dying father and a dead-end town.","It followed Southeastern, the 2013 album Isbell made after getting sober, which had rebuilt his career outside the Nashville mainstream. Produced by Dave Cobb in Nashville and released on Isbell's own label, it arrived as Americana was becoming a commercial force, and it topped several Billboard genre charts. It won the Grammy for Best Americana Album, confirming Isbell, once the young guitarist in Drive-By Truckers, as a leading songwriter of his generation.","The album is about work in both senses: manual labour, and the daily labour of staying sober and staying married. Listen for how often characters are grateful for tedium, because routine is what keeps them safe. To a Band That I Loved is a tribute to the Texas band Centro-matic, a rare public love letter from one songwriter to a group few listeners know.","Critics praised the precision of the writing, often comparing Isbell to John Prine and to short-story writers like Raymond Carver. A few found it gentler and less urgent than Southeastern, a contented man's record. That reading has softened: the album's calm is now heard as hard-won, and songs like Speed Trap Town and 24 Frames are regularly cited among his finest.","Start with Speed Trap Town and listen to it as a story, noticing how much is left unsaid about the father. Then play the title track, then 24 Frames, whose chorus warns about how quickly a life can be rebuilt or ruined. Follow with John Prine's first album to hear where this kind of plain, exact storytelling comes from."],"jason-isbell-the-nashville-sound":["Credited to Jason Isbell and the 400 Unit, this is his band record, louder and more electric than the albums before it. Anxiety and Cumberland Gap churn with distorted guitars; Last of My Kind and If We Were Vampires are close, quiet and acoustic. The most discussed song, White Man's World, examines the singer's own privilege as a white Southern man. If We Were Vampires, a love song about mortality, sung with fiddle and gentle guitar, is its emotional centre.","The title is a pointed joke. The Nashville Sound was the name for the smooth, string-laden country pop of the late 1950s and 1960s; Isbell's album, recorded with Dave Cobb at the historic RCA Studio A in Nashville, uses the name to claim the city for a different kind of music. It came out in 2017, months into the Trump presidency, amid bitter argument about the South, race and the white working class.","If We Were Vampires turns on a simple idea: love is precious only because it ends, and one partner will almost certainly have to bury the other. Amanda Shires, Isbell's wife at the time, plays the fiddle on it, which makes the song sound like a private conversation recorded in public. Hope the High Road answers the album's anger with a deliberately stubborn optimism.","It won two Grammys, Best Americana Album and Best American Roots Song for If We Were Vampires, and appeared on many year-end lists. White Man's World drew both praise and complaint, the latter from listeners who wanted country music to stay out of politics. The album is now seen as the moment Americana's leading songwriter decided his songs should speak directly about the present.","Begin with If We Were Vampires and listen for the fiddle, which answers each line like a second voice. Then hear Anxiety for the band at full volume, and White Man's World for the argument. Compare it with Something More Than Free, a solo record of the same period, and with Steve Earle's Copperhead Road, an earlier Southern writer's political turn."],"kassav-vini-pou":["Kassav' at full international strength: big, bright zouk with a relentless, tripping kick-drum pulse, bass locked tight to it, synthesizer pads and punchy brass stabs, and Jacob Desvarieux's clean, singing guitar weaving through. Several lead voices share the songs, including Jocelyne Béroard's warm alto and the high, supple tenors of Patrick Saint-Éloi and Jean-Philippe Marthély, all singing in Antillean Creole. The tracks are built for the dance floor, long and cyclical, rising to call-and-response choruses.","Founded in 1979 by Pierre-Édouard Décimus with Desvarieux and others, Kassav' had turned the carnival rhythms of Guadeloupe and Martinique into a studio-made pop music of their own, recorded mostly in Paris. Vini Pou, their move to the major label CBS, came after a string of hits and huge concerts in France, Africa and the Caribbean. Singing in Creole, not French, was itself a statement of Antillean identity.","Listen underneath the synthesizers for the old Caribbean drum patterns: the zouk pulse descends from gwo ka drumming in Guadeloupe and from carnival bands, translated onto drum machine and bass. Notice too how the guitar borrows the bright, looping style of Congolese soukous, a sign of how much traffic flowed between the Antilles and Central Africa.","In France and the Caribbean it confirmed Kassav' as the leading francophone Caribbean band; in West and Central Africa, where the band drew enormous crowds, zouk became the soundtrack of dance halls. Anglophone critics mostly ignored it, filing it under world music. Its long influence is felt in Angola, where zouk helped give birth to kizomba, and throughout Cape Verdean and Lusophone pop.","Put it on at dancing volume and follow the kick drum; the whole record rides on that rolling, slightly skipping pulse. Then pick out the voices one by one, noticing how the lead passes between singers. Compare it with Zouk-La Sé Sèl Médikaman Nou Ni for the band's first great hit, and with Franco's rumba for the guitar kinship."],"kassav-majestik-zouk":["The title is a boast, and the record lives up to it: polished, glossy zouk at its commercial height, made by a band at the top of its form. The familiar Kassav' machinery is all here, the rolling kick drum, tight electric bass, brass punctuation, synthesizer washes and bright guitar, with the lead passing among several singers. Some songs lean toward slower, romantic zouk-love, with softer keyboards and more space around the voice.","By 1989 Kassav' were among the biggest-selling acts in the French-speaking world, playing stadiums in Africa and large halls in Paris, and zouk had spread from the French Antilles to Haiti, Cape Verde, Angola and beyond. The band had become a kind of national institution for Guadeloupe and Martinique, proof that a Creole-language music from small islands could reach a mass international public on its own terms.","Notice the slower songs: the softer, romantic strain of zouk heard here would travel furthest, feeding Cape Verdean and Angolan styles that grew into kizomba. Behind the gloss the arrangements are intricate, with guitar, bass and keyboards each playing separate interlocking figures, a way of building a groove shared with Central African guitar bands that the members admired.","It was greeted in the francophone world as a triumph and a statement of Antillean pride, though some critics felt the sound had become too polished and predictable. Outside French-speaking markets it received little critical attention. Today Kassav' are recognised as one of the major popular bands of the Caribbean, and records from this period are cited as the classic zouk sound.","Listen first to how the rhythm section breathes: the kick drum and bass lock together while the percussion fills every gap. Then contrast an up-tempo track with one of the slow romantic ones. Compare with Vini Pou from two years earlier, and with Arrow's Hot Hot Hot for a neighbouring island's party music. It works best at volume."],"lin-manuel-miranda-in-the-heights":["A two-act musical about three hot summer days in Washington Heights, the largely Dominican neighbourhood at the top of Manhattan. Music and lyrics are by Lin-Manuel Miranda, book by Quiara Alegría Hudes. The score mixes hip-hop, salsa, merengue, bolero and Broadway ballad: the opening number is rapped by Usnavi, the bodega owner who narrates, while songs like Breathe and When You're Home are soaring show tunes. A winning lottery ticket, a blackout and a death drive the plot.","Miranda began the show as a student at Wesleyan University around 1999 and developed it for years with Hudes, the director Thomas Kail and the orchestrators Alex Lacamoire and Bill Sherman. It opened off-Broadway in 2007 and on Broadway in 2008, with Miranda himself as Usnavi. Very few Broadway shows had centred Latino characters written by Latino artists, and almost none had used rap as the natural language of a musical.","Listen for how the score assigns musical styles to generations: Usnavi and his friends rap and sing R&B, while the older characters, above all Abuela Claudia in Paciencia y Fe, sing in bolero and older Latin forms. Paciencia y Fe itself, her memory of emigrating from Cuba, is the show's most complex number, shifting tempo and style as her life passes before her.","It won the 2008 Tony Awards for Best Musical, Best Original Score, Best Choreography and Best Orchestrations, and a Grammy for its cast album. Some critics found the book sentimental and the conflicts mild. After Hamilton it was re-read as the rehearsal for Miranda's later triumph, and the 2021 film by Jon M. Chu brought praise and also a debate about the casting of darker-skinned Afro-Latino performers.","Start with the opening number, noticing how Usnavi's rap introduces the whole street one character at a time. Then play Paciencia y Fe and follow its changes of style. Compare 96,000, a big ensemble number about the lottery, with the stacked-voice climaxes of West Side Story and Hamilton. The original Broadway cast recording is the best way in."],"lin-manuel-miranda-we-dont-talk-about-bruno":["An ensemble number from Disney's animated film Encanto, sung by the Madrigal family about the uncle whose prophecies have made him an outcast. It moves through a chain of solos: a nervous opening over a Latin groove, a salsa-flavoured montuno section, a slinky reggaeton-tinged verse, and a darker, whispered passage. Each family member describes Bruno in a different musical style. At the end all the melodies return at once, stacked on top of each other.","Miranda wrote the songs for Encanto, set in a magical house in the Colombian mountains, while Germaine Franco composed the score. The film was released in late 2021, and the song spread online weeks later, especially on TikTok. In early 2022 it reached number one on the Billboard Hot 100, the first Disney animated song to do so since A Whole New World from Aladdin in 1993, even though Disney had not chosen it for the Oscar campaign.","The finale is a quodlibet, an old technique in which several different melodies are sung simultaneously and fit together. Miranda learned it from musical theatre, from the Tonight quintet in West Side Story, and had used it in Hamilton; it goes back to Bach and beyond. Listen too for how each character's style reveals personality: the anxious one gets fast patter, the charming one gets romance.","Its chart success surprised everyone, including Disney, and it became one of the most-streamed film songs ever. Critics praised the construction and the comic characterisation; others observed that its styles are a pan-Latin blend, with Cuban montuno and reggaeton as prominent as Colombian forms, while the film's more specifically Colombian music lives in other songs and the score. It is now a set piece for school and amateur choirs, a sign of how completely it has entered common culture.","Listen once for the story, then again focusing only on the finale and try to follow one voice through the stack. Then play the Tonight quintet from West Side Story and Non-Stop from Hamilton to hear the same trick in other hands. The film version, with the animation, shows who is singing which line."],"rem-losing-my-religion":["A rock single built on a mandolin. Peter Buck's circling, minor-key mandolin figure opens the song and runs through it, over acoustic guitar, Mike Mills's melodic bass and Bill Berry's restrained drumming. A small string section swells in the later verses. Michael Stipe sings a long, anxious confession of unrequited feeling, of having said too much and fearing he has been seen. There is no big guitar solo; the song simply rises in intensity until the strings and voice peak together.","It was the lead single from Out of Time in 1991, R.E.M.'s second album for Warner Bros., after a decade of steady rise from Athens, Georgia, through college radio. Buck wrote the riff while teaching himself mandolin. The phrase in the title is a Southern expression for losing one's temper or reaching the end of one's rope, not a statement about faith. The single became the band's biggest American hit, reaching the top five.","The mandolin changes the song's whole character: a folk instrument playing what would, on guitar, be an ordinary rock riff, making the song sound older and more fragile. Stipe has said the lyric is about unrequited love and obsession, not religion. The video, directed by Tarsem Singh, used religious imagery inspired by painters such as Caravaggio, which is why many listeners still hear it as a song about faith.","It won two Grammys and the MTV award for Video of the Year, and turned R.E.M. from a critics' band into a global one. Some old fans saw the success as the end of the band's underground integrity. Today it is regarded as one of the defining rock songs of the 1990s and proof that alternative rock could reach the mass market without grunge's distortion.","Listen to the first thirty seconds and follow only the mandolin; notice how it keeps repeating as everything builds around it. Then listen for the moment the strings arrive. Play Murmur next to hear how far the band travelled, and Automatic for the People, which followed, for the same acoustic, string-coloured sound taken further."],"alicia-keys-the-diary-of-alicia-keys":["Keys's second album is warmer and more rooted in 1960s and 1970s soul than her debut. It opens with a short solo piano prelude, Harlem's Nocturne, then moves through classic-soul ballads and hip-hop grooves. If I Ain't Got You, a piano ballad about valuing love above wealth, and You Don't Know My Name, built on a sampled 1970s soul record and featuring a spoken phone-call interlude, are its two most famous songs. Diary features the vocal group Tony! Toni! Toné!.","Keys, a classically trained pianist from Hell's Kitchen in New York, had become a star at twenty with Songs in A Minor in 2001. For the follow-up she worked with her longtime collaborator Kerry Brothers and a young producer named Kanye West, who co-produced You Don't Know My Name before his own debut album appeared. The record debuted at number one on the Billboard 200, with one of the largest first-week sales by a female artist at that time.","Harlem's Nocturne announces Keys's classical training: its title and style tip a hat to Chopin, whom she studied as a child, recast with blues-tinged harmony. You Don't Know My Name uses a speeded-up soul sample in the way Kanye West was pioneering, and the long spoken section in which the singer calls a man she admires recalls the spoken raps of classic 1970s soul records.","It won Grammys including Best R&B Album, and If I Ain't Got You became one of the most-played R&B songs of the decade, a standard for singing contests. Some critics wanted more risk and found the retro sound safe. It is now widely regarded as Keys's finest album, a bridge between neo-soul and mainstream pop, and a model of the piano-playing soul singer.","Start with If I Ain't Got You and listen to the piano introduction, then how the voice builds from quiet to full gospel power. Then play You Don't Know My Name for the sample and the phone call. Compare with Songs in A Minor, with Stevie Wonder's Songs in the Key of Life, and with Kanye West's The College Dropout for the production style."],"beastie-boys-check-your-head":["Twenty tracks that lurch between rap, loose instrumental funk jams, and short blasts of hardcore punk, all played largely by the three Beastie Boys themselves: Adam Yauch on bass, Mike D on drums, Ad-Rock on guitar, with Money Mark Ramos-Nishita on a battered Hammond organ. Vocals are shouted through cheap, overdriven microphones, so the rhymes arrive fuzzy and hot. The sound is a garage full of vintage gear rather than a crate of records: dusty, rough, deliberately unfinished.","After the sample-dense Paul's Boutique sold poorly in 1989, the trio stayed in Los Angeles and built their own studio, G-Son, in Atwater Village, working again with producer Mario Caldato Jr. They dug their instruments out of storage, since they had begun in 1981 as a New York hardcore band, and relearned them by jamming for months. The album came out in April 1992 on Capitol through their new Grand Royal imprint, just as alternative rock was taking over American radio.","The punk tracks are not a joke: Time for Livin', a Sly and the Family Stone song, is torn through at hardcore speed, a nod to the band's teenage records. Listen too for how many of the funk instrumentals were simply edited out of long jam tapes, cut and looped the way they had once cut other people's records. The studio famously held a basketball court, and the loose, playful feel of the sessions is audible everywhere.","Reviews in 1992 were mixed, with some critics hearing a band that had lost its focus, and early sales were slow. Its standing rose steadily as So What'cha Want and Pass the Mic found college radio and the Lollapalooza crowd, and the record went platinum. Today it is usually seen as the hinge of their career, the moment a rap group reinvented itself as a live band and opened the way to Ill Communication.","Start with So What'cha Want for the distorted vocals and organ, then Pass the Mic for the band trading lines over a heavy live groove. Let the instrumentals, such as Lighten Up and In 3's, play without skipping; they set the mood. Compare the live-played drums here with the sampled breaks of Paul's Boutique to hear what changed."],"kamasi-washington-heaven-and-earth":["A double album of about two and a half hours, split into two halves: Earth, about the world as it is, and Heaven, about the world as it might be. Washington's tenor saxophone leads a large band with two drummers, upright and electric bass, piano and synthesizers, over which a wordless choir and a string orchestra swell. It opens with a version of the theme from the Bruce Lee film Fist of Fury, sung and shouted, and never lowers the stakes afterwards.","The Epic of 2015 had made Washington, a Los Angeles saxophonist who had played with Kendrick Lamar and Snoop Dogg, the most visible jazz musician of his generation. Heaven and Earth, released in June 2018 on the British label Young Turks, was made with the same circle of childhood friends, the West Coast Get Down, and recorded in Los Angeles. It arrived amid a broad revival of interest in spiritual and cosmic jazz among young listeners who came to it through hip-hop.","The physical editions hid a further short album, The Choice, sealed inside the packaging so that buyers had to cut the sleeve open to find it. On the main record, listen for Hub-Tones, a Freddie Hubbard tune, and for how often a melody first heard on the Earth side returns transformed on the Heaven side, the choir taking over what the horns stated plainly.","Reviews were largely warm, praising the scale of the arrangements and the sense of purpose, and it was widely listed among the best records of 2018. The standing objection, also raised against The Epic, is length and grandeur: some critics hear too much uplift, too many climaxes and not enough of the grit or risk of the Coltrane and Pharoah Sanders records it recalls. Admirers answer that the maximalism is the point.","Do not try it in one sitting. Take the Earth half first, starting with Fists of Fury, and notice how the two drummers lock together under the choir. Later, try the Heaven half as a separate evening. For the lineage, follow it with Pharoah Sanders's Karma or Alice Coltrane's Journey in Satchidananda."],"kamasi-washington-harmony-of-difference":["A suite in six parts lasting about half an hour. The first five, Desire, Humility, Knowledge, Perspective and Integrity, are short pieces, each with its own melody and mood, from soft ballad to Latin-tinged strut. The sixth, Truth, runs some thirteen minutes and gathers all five melodies together, played at the same time, while a choir and strings slowly lift the whole weave upward. That final convergence is the idea of the whole record made audible.","Washington wrote the suite for the 2017 Whitney Biennial in New York, where Truth was shown with a film by the director A. G. Rojas and paintings by his sister, Amani Washington. It came out as an EP in September 2017, between The Epic and Heaven and Earth. At a moment of sharp political division in the United States, he framed it plainly as music about difference held together rather than erased.","Truth is built with the old technique of counterpoint, several independent melodies sounding at once, the same device a Baroque composer uses in a quodlibet. Try listening to Truth after hearing each of the five short pieces separately: you can then pick out each tune inside the texture as it enters, rather like finding a familiar voice in a crowd. The title is a literal description of the method.","Critics generally preferred its concision to the sprawl of The Epic, and many singled out Truth as the finest single piece Washington had recorded. Some felt the five short pieces were slight on their own, useful mainly as material for the finale. It has held its place as the clearest demonstration that his ambition is compositional, not just a matter of scale and volume.","Play it in order, once, and do not skip the short pieces: they are the ingredients. When Truth begins, listen for which melody comes in first and try to follow two at once as they stack up. The choir entrance near the middle is the turning point. Pair it with Coltrane's Meditations for another suite built on a single spiritual idea."],"pantera-cowboys-from-hell":["Twelve tracks of tight, mid-tempo metal built on Dimebag Darrell's guitar riffs, which stop and start in syncopated jolts rather than galloping at thrash speed. Vinnie Paul's drums, with a clicking, triggered bass drum, lock onto every accent; Rex Brown's bass shadows the guitar; Phil Anselmo moves between a hard bark and a high, piercing scream. The title track opens with a swaggering riff you could almost call country, and the long Cemetery Gates adds a slow, clean-guitar introduction and a soaring solo.","Pantera had spent the 1980s in Texas as a glam-metal band, releasing four albums on their own label. Anselmo's arrival in 1986 pushed them toward harder music, and after years of rejections they signed to Atco, a Warner imprint, releasing Cowboys from Hell in July 1990 with producer Terry Date. It arrived as thrash was peaking and the major labels were looking for heavier bands to follow Metallica's success.","The band later all but erased its first four records from the official discography, so Cowboys from Hell was presented as a debut. Listen for the traces of the old style, especially the melodic solos and ballad pacing of Cemetery Gates, and for Dimebag's pinched harmonics, the squealing high notes he pulls from the strings by touching them with his picking thumb.","It sold modestly at first but built a big following through touring, and it is now credited with fixing the groove metal sound: riffs that swing and punch rather than race. Some critics hear the band still finding itself, sitting between thrash, glam leftovers and what came next; most rank it just below Vulgar Display of Power, which sharpened the formula two years later.","Begin with the title track and notice how the riff leaves gaps for the drums to fill. Then play Domination and wait for the slowed, crushing section near the end, the passage that made the band's live reputation. Compare it with Metallica's Master of Puppets to hear the move from speed to weight."],"pantera-far-beyond-driven":["Pantera's heaviest and most abrasive album: guitars tuned lower than before, the mix dry and close, Anselmo's voice now mostly a hoarse roar. Tracks such as Strength Beyond Strength and Slaughtered hit at near-thrash speed, while Becoming and I'm Broken rest on short, lurching riffs repeated until they feel physical. Then the record ends in total contrast with a hushed, drifting cover of Black Sabbath's Planet Caravan, all soft percussion and murmured vocal.","Released in March 1994, two years after Vulgar Display of Power, it again used producer Terry Date and was made in Texas. The band had toured relentlessly and, without radio hits, had built a huge audience. The album debuted at number one on the Billboard 200, a startling result for music this extreme in the year grunge dominated. Its original cover, a drill bit entering a skull, was replaced in stores with a less graphic design.","On Becoming, the stuttering high guitar squeals come from a pitch-shifting pedal that throws the note up an octave and back as Dimebag rocks it, a signature sound of the album. Much of the lyric writing concerns anger, pain and dependency; Good Friends and a Bottle of Pills refers to drug use and betrayal in a spoken, sneering delivery that sounds nothing like the rest.","The chart debut was reported as proof that uncompromising metal could beat the mainstream on its own terms. Critics admired the sheer force but some found it monotonous and bleak compared with the variety of Vulgar Display. It is now seen as the band's commercial peak and the start of its decline, as tensions over Anselmo's health and behaviour grew.","Start with Becoming for the guitar effect and I'm Broken for the riff, then jump to Planet Caravan to feel the shock of quiet. Play at volume: the low end is the point. Follow with the Black Sabbath original on Paranoid to hear what Pantera admired in their elders."],"slayer-south-of-heaven":["Ten songs that deliberately slow down after the relentless speed of Reign in Blood. The title track opens with a creeping, dissonant guitar figure over a steady beat, and Tom Araya often sings rather than shouts. Kerry King and Jeff Hanneman still trade chaotic solos, and Dave Lombardo still drives the fast passages, but tracks like Mandatory Suicide and Behind the Crooked Cross move at a menacing mid-tempo, with more space and more echo in the sound.","Reign in Blood of 1986 had lasted under half an hour and set a speed benchmark for thrash. The band and producer Rick Rubin, whose Def Jam label released both albums, decided they could not outrun it and should go another way. South of Heaven came out in July 1988, the same year as Metallica's ...And Justice for All and Megadeth's So Far, So Good... So What!, at the height of the Los Angeles and Bay Area thrash boom.","The closing track, Dissident Aggressor, is a Judas Priest song from 1977, one of the few covers Slayer ever recorded, and a clue to where their twin-guitar style came from. Mandatory Suicide ends with Araya speaking rather than singing, a flat description of battlefield death delivered with the band dropping away beneath him; it is one of the most chilling passages in their catalogue.","Some fans and critics at the time felt betrayed by the slower tempos and the relative polish. Over the decades opinion swung strongly the other way: the title track and Mandatory Suicide became concert staples, and the album is now often ranked with Reign in Blood and Seasons in the Abyss as part of a peak trilogy. The band itself later said it had rushed some of the songwriting.","Put on the title track and listen to the first minute alone: the slow, crooked guitar line sets a mood thrash rarely attempted. Then compare Mandatory Suicide with Angel of Death from Reign in Blood to hear the change in pace. Listen for the heavier reverb, which makes the band sound as though it is playing in a cavern."],"slayer-seasons-in-the-abyss":["Ten tracks that fuse the two sides of Slayer: the blinding speed of Reign in Blood and the brooding weight of South of Heaven. War Ensemble opens at full velocity with Dave Lombardo's double bass drums, while the title track closes the album with a slow, ringing introduction before a heavy, almost stately riff. The guitars are clearer and fuller than on earlier records, and Tom Araya's vocals are more varied in pitch and phrasing.","Released in October 1990 on Def American, Rick Rubin's new label after he left Def Jam, and co-produced with Andy Wallace. Thrash was about to be overtaken commercially by grunge and by Metallica's slower 1991 album, and Slayer were touring on the Clash of the Titans bill with Megadeth and Anthrax. The Gulf crisis was building as the album appeared, and war is its constant subject.","The video for the title track was filmed in Egypt, near the pyramids at Giza, an unusual journey for a metal band at the time. Dead Skin Mask, a slow and eerie track, is written from the viewpoint of a notorious real-life killer, treated neutrally here, and ends with a child's voice pleading, which makes it one of the most unsettling songs in thrash.","Well received on release, and its standing has only grown: many fans consider it the band's most complete album, balancing the speed and the atmosphere of the two before it. It was the last studio record of the classic line-up until Lombardo's return in the 2000s, which gives it a sense of closure. War Ensemble and the title track remained in their setlists until the band's farewell tour.","Start with War Ensemble for speed and the title track for atmosphere, and notice how the band uses the same tools for both. Listen closely to Lombardo's drumming, which carries much of the drama. For contrast, play Megadeth's Rust in Peace from the same year, where precision rather than menace is the goal."],"sonic-youth-goo":["Eleven songs that fold Sonic Youth's alternate guitar tunings and feedback into tighter, catchier shapes than before. Dirty Boots opens with chiming guitars that build to a roar; Kool Thing has Kim Gordon's cool, deadpan vocal over a strutting riff; Tunic drifts through a dreamy haze. Between them sit short, abrasive pieces of pure noise such as Mildred Pierce, which starts as a pop song and collapses into screaming guitars within a couple of minutes.","After Daydream Nation, the band left the independent scene and signed with DGC, a Geffen imprint, releasing Goo in June 1990. Moving to a major label was a charged decision in the American underground, where independence was a point of honour. The band used bigger studios and producers Nick Sansano and Ron Saint Germain, and its presence on Geffen helped persuade the label to sign Nirvana the following year.","Kool Thing grew out of Gordon's awkward interview with the rapper LL Cool J for a magazine, and it features Chuck D of Public Enemy in a spoken exchange that playfully questions how white rock fans consumed Black music. Tunic is sung in the imagined voice of Karen Carpenter after her death. The cover, by Raymond Pettibon, is a drawing based on a 1960s press photograph from a British murder trial.","Some longtime fans heard a sellout and missed the sprawl of Daydream Nation; others praised it as proof that the band could be both difficult and inviting. Kool Thing became their first real MTV hit. Critical opinion now tends to rank it just below Daydream Nation, and to credit it with opening the door through which the whole alternative-rock boom of the 1990s passed.","Start with Dirty Boots and listen for how the two guitars, in unusual tunings, ring against each other like bells. Then Kool Thing, following Gordon's flat, ironic delivery against Chuck D. Try Mildred Pierce loud to hear the band's noise roots. Follow with Nirvana's Nevermind to hear who was listening."],"oneohtrix-point-never-eccojams-vol-1":["A cassette of loops cut from 1980s soft rock and pop, slowed down, drenched in echo and repeated until they turn strange. A single line from a familiar song, such as Toto's Africa, is caught mid-phrase and cycled over and over, its pitch dragged lower, so a bright hit becomes a hazy, melancholy fragment. The pieces run together as one continuous stream, like a radio picked up through a wall in a dream.","Daniel Lopatin, who records as Oneohtrix Point Never, first posted these experiments on YouTube in 2009 under a separate account, then issued them in 2010 as a limited cassette under the alias Chuck Person. It came out of a Brooklyn scene reviving cassettes and synthesizers, just as online video had made the whole of 1980s pop instantly available for free, a situation that made nostalgia itself a subject for art.","Lopatin called the technique echo jams: the aim was not to sample a hook for a new track but to trap a passing moment and stretch it until the listener hears its emotional core, or its emptiness. The record, unnoticed at first, became the direct model for vaporwave, a movement of internet musicians who slowed and looped corporate and commercial music of the 1980s and 1990s.","Barely reviewed at the time, it became a cult object as copies traded online, and critics later placed it at the root of vaporwave and of hypnagogic pop. Debate continues about whether it critiques consumer nostalgia or simply indulges it; Lopatin has been wary of being tied to the movement. Its reputation now exceeds that of many of his official albums.","Listen in one pass with headphones, and do not try to name every source; let the loops settle before judging them. Notice how slowing a phrase changes its feeling from cheerful to mournful. Then compare Brian Eno's tape loops on Discreet Music, and Oneohtrix Point Never's later Replica, which applies the same method to old television adverts."],"megadeth-peace-sells-but-whos-buying":["Eight songs of fast, intricate thrash with an edge of sarcasm. Dave Mustaine's riffs twist through sudden changes of tempo and meter, while Chris Poland's lead guitar adds jazzy, slippery solos. The title track opens with David Ellefson's walking bass line, one of the most recognisable openings in metal, before Mustaine's snarling vocal lays out a sour view of politics. The album also includes a swaggering cover of Willie Dixon's I Ain't Superstitious.","Mustaine formed Megadeth in Los Angeles after being thrown out of Metallica in 1983. This second album was recorded for the independent Combat label, but Capitol bought the contract and had it remixed before release in 1986. Reagan-era Cold War anxiety supplied its subjects; the cover shows the mascot Vic Rattlehead outside a ruined United Nations building, painted by Ed Repka.","Poland and the drummer Gar Samuelson came from jazz fusion, and their loose, swinging touch, especially Samuelson's cymbal work, sets this record apart from the stiffer thrash around it. The title track's bass introduction was used for years as the theme of MTV News, so many people knew the riff without knowing the band.","It was the band's breakthrough, praised as more musically ambitious than most thrash of its year, and it stands with Master of Puppets and Reign in Blood among the defining metal albums of 1986. Discussion now centres on the production: the Capitol remix sounds thin to some ears, and later remasters have altered the balance, not always to fans' approval.","Start with the title track for that bass opening and the sarcastic chorus, then Wake Up Dead for the tangled riffs. Listen to the drums on Good Mourning/Black Friday, where the cymbals swing. Compare with Metallica's Master of Puppets, released the same year, for two views of how thrash could grow."],"megadeth-countdown-to-extinction":["Eleven songs that trade the complex, breakneck riffing of Rust in Peace for shorter, slower, heavier structures with clear choruses. Symphony of Destruction marches on a simple, pounding riff; Sweating Bullets has Mustaine half-speaking a paranoid internal monologue; Skin o' My Teeth keeps some of the old speed. Marty Friedman's melodic lead guitar, with its bends drawn from Japanese and Asian scales, is the brightest colour on the record.","Recorded with producer Max Norman and released in July 1992, it followed Metallica's hugely successful 1991 album in pointing thrash bands toward radio. The line-up of Mustaine, David Ellefson, Friedman and the drummer Nick Menza was the band's most stable. It reached number two on the Billboard 200, their highest placing, at a moment when grunge was pushing most metal off the charts.","The title track attacks the practice of canned hunting, in which animals are shot in enclosures, and it earned the band an award from an animal-protection society, an unusual honour for a thrash group. Symphony of Destruction's lyric imagines a puppet leader controlled by hidden powers, and its music video, depicting an assassination, was restricted on MTV.","It was the band's commercial peak and sold several million copies. Some fans saw the simplification as a retreat after Rust in Peace; others praised the clarity and song craft. That debate, speed and complexity against accessibility, is the same one that surrounded Metallica's black album, and the two records are usually discussed together as the moment thrash went mainstream.","Begin with Symphony of Destruction and notice how few notes the main riff uses. Then play Sweating Bullets for Mustaine's twisted spoken vocal. Listen for Friedman's solos, which tend to sing a melody rather than shred. Afterward, play Rust in Peace to hear how much was stripped away."],"guns-n-roses-use-your-illusion":["Two separate albums, released the same day, holding thirty songs and about two and a half hours of music. Alongside the fast, snarling hard rock of the band's debut there are long ballads with piano and orchestra, such as November Rain and Estranged; a ten-minute track, Coma; horn sections and gospel backing singers; and covers of Bob Dylan's Knockin' on Heaven's Door and Paul McCartney's Live and Let Die. It is sprawling, extravagant and uneven by design.","After the enormous success of Appetite for Destruction, the band spent years touring, fighting and recording, with long delays largely blamed on Axl Rose. The drummer Steven Adler was fired and replaced by Matt Sorum; keyboardist Dizzy Reed joined. The two volumes came out on Geffen in September 1991 and entered the chart at numbers one and two, just days before Nirvana's Nevermind began to change what rock sounded like.","The covers are details from Raphael's fresco The School of Athens, reworked by the painter Mark Kostabi, whose painting gave the album its title. You Could Be Mine, on Volume II, had already been heard as the theme of Terminator 2. The rhythm guitarist Izzy Stradlin, author of many of the band's sharpest songs, left soon after release, and his leaner tracks show how much the balance was shifting.","Reviews praised the ambition while complaining about length, self-indulgence and Rose's attacks on critics and the press in several songs. The accompanying world tour, with riots and cancellations, fed the legend. Opinion remains split: some hear the last great arena-rock statement, others a bloated record that hid a single strong album inside two. The ballads, above all, have outlasted the arguments.","Do not start at track one. Try Civil War and Estranged first, for the band at its most ambitious, then You Could Be Mine for the old ferocity. Build your own single album from the best twelve songs, as many fans do. Compare with Appetite for Destruction to hear how far they had travelled."],"guns-n-roses-november-rain":["A nine-minute power ballad built on Axl Rose's piano. It begins quietly with piano chords and strings, grows through verses about love that cannot last and the need to hold on, and reaches Slash's first guitar solo, played slowly and melodically. After a final section with orchestra and backing choir, the song closes on a second, longer and more frantic solo and a big orchestral ending. The piano figure and the tolling strings mark it instantly.","Rose had been working on the song since the band's earliest days in Los Angeles in the mid-1980s, and it was already known among fans before the band recorded it. It appeared on Use Your Illusion I in September 1991 and became a single in 1992, reaching the top five of the Billboard Hot 100 despite its length. Its lavish video, directed by Andy Morahan, made it a fixture of MTV.","The video, with its wedding, sudden funeral and rain, is loosely based on a short story, Without You, by the band's friend Del James. Much of the orchestral sound was reportedly built by Rose on synthesizers and arranged with keyboards, not a full orchestra in the studio. Listen for how the second solo is framed by the band stopping and restarting, like a second ending.","It was an immediate hit and the video became one of the most expensive and most discussed of the era; in 2018 it became the first 1990s music video to pass a billion views on YouTube. Critics have long argued over whether it is moving or overblown. Many listeners who never cared for the band know it well, and it now outlives most of the album around it.","Listen once for the shape: quiet piano, growing band, solo, full orchestra, climax. On a second hearing follow only the strings and hear how they move from background to lead. Compare Slash's two solos, the first singing, the second racing. For another rock ballad pushed to operatic length, try Queen's Bohemian Rhapsody."],"phuture-your-only-friend":["A dark, slow-burning acid track: a squelching Roland TB-303 bass line coils around a stark drum-machine beat while a voice, pitched so low it barely sounds human, speaks over the top. The voice is that of a drug, cocaine, addressing the listener as a friend who will never leave, and gradually revealing itself as controlling and destructive. The effect is less a dance track than a menacing radio play with a beat.","It appeared on Phuture's 1987 twelve-inch record for Chicago's Trax label alongside Acid Tracks, the piece that defined acid house. Phuture were three young Chicagoans, DJ Pierre, Earl Smith Jr. and Herb Jackson, who had found the TB-303, a cheap bass machine abandoned by the musicians it was designed for, and learned to make it squeal by twisting its filter controls while it played.","The track is an anti-drug message in the form of the drug's own seductive monologue, a device that was unusual for early house music, which rarely told stories. The deep, slowed voice works like the menacing narrator of a horror film. Listen for how the 303 line changes timbre as the knobs turn, the same technique as Acid Tracks put to darker use.","It was overshadowed at first by Acid Tracks, but it has been widely reissued and is now cited as an early example of house music carrying a clear social message. DJs and historians of Chicago house value it as proof that acid's alien sound could be used for storytelling, not only for the dancefloor.","Listen after Acid Tracks and hear the same machine turned from euphoria to threat. Pay attention to the slowly shifting 303 line beneath the voice, and to how sparse the drums are. Then try Marshall Jefferson's Move Your Body for the brighter, piano-led side of Chicago house."],"bert-jansch-rosemary-lane":["Rosemary Lane is Bert Jansch alone: one voice, one acoustic guitar, a dozen songs and instrumentals recorded so close that you hear the fingers on the strings and the breath before each phrase. Traditional ballads such as the title song and Reynardine sit beside his own quiet originals and a small instrumental in the manner of an Elizabethan lute piece. The guitar never merely strums; it runs counter-melodies and low drones under the voice, so every song sounds like a duet for one man.","Jansch made the record in 1970 and 1971, while Pentangle, the band he shared with John Renbourn, Jacqui McShee, Danny Thompson and Terry Cox, was touring hard and selling well. The album was a retreat from that bustle: unhurried sessions away from the studio routine, released on Transatlantic, the small London label that had carried his work since 1965. British folk was splitting into electric folk rock and a purer revival; Jansch quietly stood outside both, making the most private record of his career.","Listen to how the guitar treats the traditional tunes. Rather than accompany Rosemary Lane with chords, Jansch plays a moving bass line and a broken, almost harpsichord-like figure that echoes Renaissance lute writing, the same world he nods to openly in the short instrumental Alman. The borrowing runs both ways: the ballad's old modal melody bends his picking toward open, unresolved sounds, and the song ends without the neat cadence a pop ear expects.","Released with little fanfare, the album was overshadowed at the time by Pentangle's success and sold modestly. Its reputation grew over the following decades as guitarists such as Johnny Marr and Bernard Butler, and younger folk singers, named Jansch as a model. Many admirers now rank it beside his 1965 debut as his finest solo work; the only real argument is whether its hush is a strength or a limitation for listeners who come to it expecting drive.","Play it late and quietly, all the way through; it is built as one mood. Start with the title song, then Reynardine, and follow the guitar rather than the words: notice how the bass keeps walking while the upper line decorates. Compare Jansch's own Jack Orion for the brighter, more assertive player, and then Nick Drake's Pink Moon, a later record in the same bare spirit."],"scott-walker-tilt":["Tilt is nine songs of stark, disorienting sound: Scott Walker's operatic baritone, now pitched high and keening, set against sudden orchestral swells, bare organ drones, industrial percussion and long stretches of near-silence. Nothing here resembles a verse and chorus. Farmer in the City opens with a slow, grieving string arrangement; later tracks lurch between hammering noise and a single sustained note. The closing Rosary strips everything away to voice and a lone electric guitar.","Walker had been a 1960s teen idol with the Walker Brothers and a cult solo singer at the end of that decade. By 1995 he had released only one album, Climate of Hunter, in eleven years, and lived largely out of public view in London. Fontana issued Tilt with little promotion. He wrote slowly and oblique, drawing on history and politics: Pier Paolo Pasolini's murder, courtroom trials, Latin America, and the stripped vocabulary of European modernist composers.","Farmer in the City carries a subtitle dedicating it to the memory of Pasolini, the Italian film-maker and poet murdered in 1975, and its fragments of Italian and its images of a body are drawn from his world. The Cockfighter braids together two unrelated trials centuries apart. Listen for how often Walker places a loud event with no warning: the arrangements are built on shock and space rather than development, closer to Penderecki than to pop.","Reviews in 1995 were split between reverence and bafflement; some critics called it pretentious and unlistenable, others the bravest record by a former pop star. It sold little. Over the next decade its standing rose steadily, helped by admirers such as David Bowie and Radiohead, and it is now read as the hinge of Walker's late trilogy with The Drift and Bish Bosch. Whether it is song at all remains an open and useful question.","Begin with Farmer in the City, the most approachable track, and let its strings carry you; then play the whole album at a good volume with no distractions. Do not wait for hooks. Treat it like a song cycle by a modern composer, listening for the silences as much as the sounds. Afterwards, go back to Scott 4 to hear how far the same voice travelled."],"scott-walker-the-drift":["The Drift is ten long pieces of dread, set to Scott Walker's high, wavering baritone. The arrangements move between near-silence and violent eruptions: massed strings holding a cluster for minutes, a guitar chord struck once and left to decay, the thud of a percussionist striking something heavy. There are no choruses to hold onto. Clara, Jesse and Cossacks Are are the landmarks; each feels less like a song than a scene from an opera staged in the dark.","It arrived eleven years after Tilt, released by 4AD, the independent label, when Walker was in his early sixties. He had spent the gap on a film score and producing other artists while working slowly on these songs with his long-time co-producer Peter Walsh. The same year a documentary, Scott Walker: 30 Century Man, brought his reclusive later career to a wider public, and the album was widely reviewed as a major event.","The sounds are often literal. For Clara, which concerns the deaths of Benito Mussolini and his mistress Clara Petacci, a percussionist punched a side of meat to make the rhythmic thuds. Jesse links Elvis Presley's stillborn twin brother to the fall of the two New York towers, twinning absence with absence. And in The Escape a cartoon duck's voice appears amid the horror, a deliberately grotesque intrusion that is easy to take for a studio error.","Reception was intense: many critics called it the most frightening record they had ever heard and placed it high in year-end lists, while others found it bleak to the point of self-parody. It has settled as the centre of Walker's late work, admired by experimental rock and contemporary classical listeners alike. The debate is less about quality than about whether art this deliberately harrowing can be listened to more than a few times.","Start with Jesse, which is the shortest route in: a slow, hollow guitar figure and Walker's voice alone in a huge space. Then try Clara at night with headphones, noticing where the orchestra enters and where it abruptly stops. Take it in halves rather than all at once. Pair it with Ligeti's Lontano or Penderecki's Threnody to hear the orchestral language Walker was reaching for."],"lloyd-webber-evita":["Evita is a sung-through musical in two acts about Eva Perón, the actress who became first lady of Argentina and died at thirty-three. There is almost no spoken dialogue. The score mixes Latin dance rhythms, rock band, Catholic requiem and grand ballad, while a narrator called Che comments sarcastically on everything. It opens at her funeral and plays the life in flashback. Its signature is the balcony anthem Don't Cry for Me Argentina, sung to a crowd from the Casa Rosada.","After Jesus Christ Superstar, Andrew Lloyd Webber and lyricist Tim Rice again launched a show as a concept album first, released in 1976 with Julie Covington as Eva; its big ballad became a British hit single. Harold Prince directed the London stage premiere at the Prince Edward Theatre in 1978, with Elaine Paige, and Broadway followed in 1979 with Patti LuPone and Mandy Patinkin. Argentina was then under military rule, which gave the story of populism and power an uncomfortable edge.","The famous tune is heard long before the balcony scene. Early in the first act Che sings the same melody with contemptuous new words in Oh What a Circus, so by the time Eva sings it sincerely the audience already knows it as mockery. Lloyd Webber also frames the whole show with a Latin requiem chant for the dead Eva, borrowing the liturgical world of the opera house to set a political funeral.","Critics praised the score's energy and Prince's staging but many argued the show glamorised a manipulative figure, and the Che character's shifting identity puzzled audiences. It became one of the most successful British musicals of its era. Alan Parker's 1996 film with Madonna and Antonio Banderas added a new song, You Must Love Me, which won an Academy Award. Debate continues over whether Rice's lyrics critique Eva or seduce us into her myth.","Start with the original 1976 concept album to hear the score as Lloyd Webber first imagined it, then the 1978 London cast with Elaine Paige. Listen for how Che's voice interrupts every public triumph, and compare Another Suitcase in Another Hall, a quiet folk-like song given to Perón's discarded mistress, with the brass of Rainbow High. Set it beside Jesus Christ Superstar to hear the same method refined."],"ozzy-osbourne-diary-of-a-madman":["Ozzy Osbourne's second solo album is eight tracks of heavy metal built around Randy Rhoads's guitar. Over the Mountain charges in on a drum pattern and a tight riff; Flying High Again is a bright, swaggering single; Believer grinds on a slow, menacing bass line. The seven-minute title track closes the record with a nylon-string guitar introduction, sudden shifts of metre, a choir and strings, and a sense of genuine unease. The solos everywhere are fast, melodic and precisely composed.","Recorded in 1981, only months after Blizzard of Ozz, the album confirmed that Osbourne, sacked from Black Sabbath in 1979, had a real solo career. Rhoads, a guitarist from California who had studied classical guitar and taught at his mother's music school, co-wrote the material with Osbourne and bassist Bob Daisley. Lee Kerslake played drums. In March 1982, on tour in Florida, Rhoads died in a light-aircraft accident at the age of twenty-five, which made this his final studio album.","The title track opens on classical guitar with harmonies Rhoads took from his lessons, and it is worth noticing how the whole piece is through-composed, more like a short tone poem than a rock song. Another detail sits on the sleeve: the original credits and photographs named a different rhythm section from the players you actually hear, a slight that led Daisley and Kerslake to years of dispute over credit and royalties.","It was well received and sold strongly, and it has grown in stature with Rhoads's reputation as one of metal's most influential guitarists. A 2002 reissue controversially replaced the original bass and drum parts with newly recorded ones after the royalty dispute; fans objected, and the original performances were restored on a 2011 edition. Many listeners now place it level with or above the debut.","Start with Over the Mountain and the title track, which show the two faces of the record: tight propulsion and ambitious drama. Listen to how Rhoads builds his solos from scales and arpeggios with a clear shape rather than simply playing fast. Seek the 2011 remaster or an original pressing, not the 2002 version. Compare Bach's violin partitas, whose patterns Rhoads openly admired."],"ozzy-osbourne-no-more-tears":["No More Tears is a big, polished hard-rock album with Zakk Wylde's thick, squealing guitar at its centre. Mr. Tinkertrain and I Don't Want to Change the World are hard and fast; Mama, I'm Coming Home is a slow power ballad with an acoustic opening. The seven-minute title track is the landmark: a creeping bass figure, a sinister verse, a sweeping orchestral-sounding middle section and a long guitar solo, closer to a mini-drama than a single.","By 1991 Osbourne was in his early forties, newly sober and announcing that the tour for this record would be his last, a retirement that did not last. Wylde, a young guitarist from New Jersey, had joined for the previous album and now shaped the sound. Lemmy of Motörhead wrote lyrics for several songs, including Mama, I'm Coming Home. It appeared the same year as Nirvana's Nevermind, just as grunge began to sweep hair-metal gloss off the radio.","The bass line that opens the title track, one of the most recognisable in 1990s rock, came from Mike Inez, who later joined Alice in Chains. Listen also to Wylde's pinched harmonics, the squealing notes made by touching the string with the thumb as it is picked; they appear on nearly every solo and became his fingerprint. Mama, I'm Coming Home is addressed, despite its title, to Osbourne's wife Sharon.","The album was a major commercial success and restored Osbourne's standing after a patchy late 1980s. A live version of I Don't Want to Change the World later won a Grammy. Critics are divided on its gloss: some hear it as the last great arena-metal record before grunge, others as overproduced. The title track is generally regarded as among the strongest songs of his solo career.","Begin with the title track at full volume and follow the bass through its creeping verses into the wide middle section. Then play Mama, I'm Coming Home to hear the ballad side. Compare Randy Rhoads on Diary of a Madman: Wylde is heavier and bluesier where Rhoads was classical and precise. For the context, set it beside Metallica's black album from the same year."],"richard-thompson-1952-vincent-black-lightning":["A narrative ballad for one voice and one acoustic guitar. Richard Thompson tells the story of James, a young outlaw with a prized motorcycle, and Red Molly, the girl he wins with it; after a robbery goes wrong he is shot, and on his deathbed he gives her his bike. The guitar is the song's engine: a restless fingerpicked pattern that plays bass, rhythm and a running melody all at once, galloping under the verses like the engine he sings about.","It appeared on Rumor and Sigh, a 1991 album produced by Mitchell Froom for Capitol, recorded after Thompson had moved to California and was building a solo career in the long shadow of his work with Fairport Convention and with Linda Thompson. The album is mostly full-band rock; this song is its bare centre. The Vincent Black Lightning of the title was a rare, very fast British motorcycle built in Stevenage just after the Second World War.","The song borrows the shape of the old broadside and outlaw ballad, the kind Thompson played in Fairport: a hero, a doomed deed, a deathbed bequest, a moral left unspoken. Listen to how the guitar never repeats exactly; small fills and bass runs answer each line of the story. The picking is fiendishly hard, and the fact that one man plays all of it while singing is easy to forget on first hearing.","It was never a hit, but it steadily became Thompson's most requested song and a standard among folk and bluegrass musicians; the Del McCoury Band's version helped carry it into American roots music. Guitarists study it as a benchmark of fingerstyle playing. Some listeners find its romance of the doomed outlaw sentimental, but most hear a modern traditional ballad that sits comfortably beside the old ones.","Listen once for the story, start to finish; then listen again only to the guitar, and notice how the thumb keeps a steady bass while the fingers work above it. Seek a solo live recording as well as the studio version; Thompson varies the instrumental breaks every time. Afterwards try Fairport Convention's Liege & Lief for the traditional ballads it descends from."],"nick-cave-the-mercy-seat":["The Mercy Seat is seven minutes of relentless build. Nick Cave and the Bad Seeds set a man awaiting execution in the electric chair against a grinding, ever-thickening backing: pounding drums, scraping string-like drones, and a riff that never lets up. The chorus repeats again and again with only tiny changes of wording, while the band grows louder and more chaotic. There is no release, no bridge, no relief; the song simply climbs until it stops.","It opened Tender Prey, released by Mute in 1988, when the Bad Seeds were based partly in West Berlin and Cave was writing a novel set in the American South, And the Ass Saw the Angel. The song was co-written with Mick Harvey, the band's musical anchor, and Blixa Bargeld of Einstürzende Neubauten played guitar. Cave was deep in addiction at the time, and the Old Testament imagery of judgment ran through his writing.","The title is a pun with deep roots: in the Hebrew Bible the mercy seat is the golden lid of the Ark of the Covenant, the throne of God's judgment, and here it is also the electric chair. Listen to the repeated chorus closely. Its wording shifts slightly on each return, and by the last repetitions the condemned man's claim to be telling the truth has quietly turned into an admission that he may be lying.","It was not a hit, but it soon became the Bad Seeds' signature live song and a touchstone for gothic and post-punk rock. Johnny Cash recorded it in 2000 for his American Recordings series, a version that brought it to an audience far from Cave's own, and Cave said he admired it. Critics now rank it among his greatest songs, though some prefer the sparer piano versions he later performed.","Play the original loud and do not skip: the whole effect is accumulation. Count the returns of the chorus and listen for the moment the change in its wording lands. Then hear Johnny Cash's version, which trades noise for weary calm, and compare Cave's later solo piano performances. Follow it with Leonard Cohen or Townes Van Zandt for other songs of judgment and doom."],"the-chieftains-irish-heartbeat":["Irish Heartbeat pairs Van Morrison's voice with the Chieftains' traditional ensemble in ten songs, most of them old Irish airs. You hear uilleann pipes, fiddles, flute, harp and bodhrán frame Morrison's unpredictable, stretching delivery. Slow airs such as She Moved Through the Fair, Carrickfergus and My Lagan Love sit beside rollicking street songs like I'll Tell Me Ma and the Scottish Marie's Wedding, plus two Morrison originals. One track, Tá Mo Chleamhnas Déanta, is sung in Irish.","By 1988 the Chieftains, led by piper Paddy Moloney, had spent twenty-five years taking Irish traditional music from pub sessions to concert halls worldwide, and had begun collaborating across genres. Morrison, from Belfast, had long drawn on Celtic mysticism in his soul-inflected records. The album was a meeting of equals across the political border, released on Mercury while the Troubles continued in Northern Ireland, and presenting a shared song culture that belonged to both parts of the island.","Raglan Road sets Patrick Kavanagh's poem about a lost love to a traditional air, The Dawning of the Day, a pairing the poet himself had suggested; listen to how Morrison bends the old melody as if it were a soul ballad. Moloney's arrangements often keep the instruments in unison or octaves, as in session playing, rather than building chords underneath, so the voice floats over a single line.","Critics welcomed it as a warm and serious record rather than a novelty, and it helped launch the Chieftains' long run of star collaborations, culminating in The Long Black Veil. Traditionalists sometimes find Morrison's phrasing too free for the slow airs; admirers say his improvising restores their emotional urgency. It remains one of the most loved crossover records of Irish music.","Start with Raglan Road and She Moved Through the Fair, and listen to how the pipes and harp answer Morrison's long held notes. Then play the lively I'll Tell Me Ma for contrast. Afterwards try the Chieftains' own early albums for the instruments without a singer, and Morrison's Astral Weeks to hear where his Celtic longing began."],"goldie-saturnz-return":["Saturnz Return is a two-disc album whose first disc is a single hour-long piece, Mother: an orchestral and electronic suite of slow strings, choir-like pads, spoken fragments and passages of drum and bass, built as a meditation on Goldie's relationship with his mother. The second disc is a set of shorter tracks, harder and more varied, with guest appearances by David Bowie on Truth, Noel Gallagher on Temper Temper and the rapper KRS-One on Digital.","Timeless, in 1995, had made Goldie the public face of jungle and drum and bass. He followed it on FFRR, a major-label imprint, at the height of his celebrity, with money and ambition to spare. The title refers to the astrological Saturn return, the passage of about twenty-nine years said to mark a reckoning; Goldie, who had spent much of his childhood in children's homes and foster care in the West Midlands, had just passed that age and was looking back.","Mother is less about drums than about time: the breakbeats arrive late and withdraw, and long stretches have no beat at all, which is where its real music lies. Listen for how strings and synthesized textures are layered so the orchestra seems to dissolve into electronics and back. The track's hour-long span was, for a dance music producer, a deliberate provocation against the three-minute single and the club edit.","Reviews were harsh or bewildered in 1998; many critics called Mother self-indulgent and the album bloated, and it sold far below Timeless. Drum and bass itself was changing toward harder, darker styles and many fans saw the record as out of step. Later listeners have been kinder, hearing Mother as an honest, flawed attempt to make electronic music on a symphonic scale; it remains his most contested work.","Give Mother one uninterrupted hour, lying down with headphones, and treat it as ambient music with a heartbeat rather than a dance record. Notice where the drums enter and how little they are used. Then dip into the second disc for Truth with Bowie. Compare Goldie's own Timeless for the tighter version of the same ambition, and Brian Eno's ambient albums."],"cream-fresh-cream":["Cream's debut is a power trio learning what it can do. Eric Clapton on guitar, Jack Bruce on bass, harmonica and lead vocal, and Ginger Baker on drums play loud versions of Delta and Chicago blues, among them Spoonful, Rollin' and Tumblin', Four Until Late and I'm So Glad, alongside Bruce's own pop-leaning songs such as N.S.U. and Sleepy Time Time. The album ends with Toad, a drum showcase for Baker built around one of the first extended drum solos on a rock record.","The trio formed in London in 1966, drawn from the best of the British blues scene: Clapton from John Mayall's Bluesbreakers, Bruce and Baker from the Graham Bond Organisation. Robert Stigwood managed them and released the album on his own Reaction label late that year. The name reflected their reputation as the cream of British players. In Britain, young white musicians were reverently studying American blues records, and amplification had grown loud enough to fill theatres.","Their version of Skip James's I'm So Glad earned the long-neglected Delta bluesman, rediscovered in 1964 and in poor health, the largest royalties of his life. Listen, too, to how Bruce's bass does far more than hold the root note; he plays counter-melodies against Clapton, a jazz-trained habit that turned the trio format into a three-way conversation rather than guitar and accompaniment.","It sold well in Britain and the United States and was received as the calling card of the first rock supergroup. Critics have since noted that the studio sound is tame beside their live performances, and that Bruce's pop songs sit oddly beside the blues covers. Still, it set the template for the heavy blues rock that led to Led Zeppelin and Black Sabbath, and it remains the clearest view of their roots.","Start with I'm So Glad and Spoonful, then play Skip James's and Howlin' Wolf's originals to hear what the trio added: volume, speed and interplay. Listen to the bass as a lead instrument. Then jump to the live half of Wheels of Fire to hear the same material stretched and set on fire. Choose a stereo edition that includes I Feel Free, the single added to the American release."],"cream-wheels-of-fire":["Wheels of Fire is a double album split in two: a studio record and a live one. The studio half opens with White Room, a dramatic wah-wah guitar song with an unusual lurching introduction, and adds blues covers, Jack Bruce's sardonic Politician and odd experiments like Pressed Rat and Warthog. The live half catches the trio on stage at full volume: a fast, tight Crossroads and a sixteen-minute Spoonful where the three instruments improvise together for long stretches.","Produced by Felix Pappalardi, who also played viola, trumpet and other instruments on the studio tracks, it was recorded in London and New York in 1967 and 1968, with the live tracks taped in San Francisco at the Fillmore and Winterland ballrooms. By its release in 1968 the trio were exhausted and at odds, and they had already announced they would break up after a farewell tour. It is commonly cited as the first double album to sell a million copies.","The opening of White Room is in five beats to the bar rather than the usual four, which is why it seems to lurch before the steadier verse arrives. On the live Crossroads, Clapton's two solos are among the most studied in rock; notice that he builds them on repeated short phrases and the rhythm section pushes the tempo forward. Bruce and Baker came from jazz, and on stage treated songs as frameworks for improvising.","It was a huge commercial success, topping the American album chart, yet it also crystallised the argument against Cream. Admirers heard virtuoso improvisation; critics, and later Clapton himself, heard indulgence, especially in the long solos. The live Crossroads is almost universally admired, while the long Spoonful and Toad divide listeners. It sits at the head of the tradition of heavy, improvising blues rock.","Start with White Room and then jump to the live Crossroads; those two tracks explain the band. Then give the live Spoonful your full attention and follow Bruce's bass as it trades ideas with Clapton. Compare Robert Johnson's original Cross Road Blues for the source, and Jimi Hendrix's Electric Ladyland, from the same year, for another answer to what a trio could do."],"soft-machine-the-soft-machine":["The first Soft Machine record is a trio album with no guitar: Mike Ratledge's fuzz-toned organ does the work a lead guitar would do elsewhere, Kevin Ayers plays bass and sings in a lazy baritone, and Robert Wyatt drums and sings in a high, boyish voice. Its short songs run into one another without gaps, so the record feels like a single set, lurching from whimsical pop tunes to sudden bursts of jazz-inflected drumming and long organ solos that sound as if the instrument is being pushed through a broken amplifier.","The band came out of the Canterbury scene of students and bohemians, and by 1968 they were touring North America as support to the Jimi Hendrix Experience. They cut the album in a few days in New York that April, with Hendrix's manager Chas Chandler and the producer Tom Wilson, who had worked with Dylan and the Velvet Underground. It came out on the ABC label's Probe imprint in the United States, and British listeners mostly had to find it on import for years.","Listen to the drums rather than the songs. Wyatt is already playing like a jazz drummer, answering the organ instead of keeping a rock backbeat, and on the quieter tracks you can hear him singing and drumming at once in a way that makes the vocal lines swing oddly. Ayers's contributions, whimsical and a little absurd, point toward his later solo records, and the group was already splitting between his song-craft and Ratledge's instrumental ambitions.","On release the album was barely noticed outside the underground press, and the band itself had doubts about the rushed sessions. It has since become the founding document of the Canterbury sound, prized for catching the group before it turned into an instrumental jazz-rock unit. Some listeners prefer it to the later records because the humour and songs are still there; others hear it as a sketch of what Third would realise properly.","Play it straight through as one continuous set, since the tracks are designed to bleed together. Notice how the organ tone changes from a churchy drone to a snarling, distorted lead. Then play Robert Wyatt's drumming against a Hendrix record from the same year, and afterwards jump to Third to hear how far the band travelled in two years. Any remastered edition will do; the original mix is rough by design."],"soft-machine-volume-two":["Volume Two is built as two long suites rather than a list of songs. The first, gathered under the title Rivmic Melodies, strings together about ten short pieces, some under a minute, that swerve from nursery-rhyme singing to fuzz-organ freak-outs and brisk jazz drumming. The second side mixes short songs with Esther's Nose Job, a sequence of dense organ riffs in odd metres. Hugh Hopper's distorted bass and Robert Wyatt's high, conversational voice give the record its particular playful and slightly unhinged character.","Kevin Ayers had left after the exhausting 1968 American tour, and the band's former road manager Hugh Hopper, an old Canterbury friend, took over bass; his brother Brian added saxophone on some tracks. Recorded in London early in 1969, the album caught the moment when British rock musicians were discovering modern jazz and the avant-garde, and it was the last Soft Machine record on which Wyatt's songs and humour balanced the band's growing appetite for long instrumental pieces.","In one short piece Wyatt sings the alphabet forwards and then backwards, and elsewhere he sings in Spanish, a sign of how seriously the band took not taking itself seriously. A fragment titled Thank You Pierrot Lunaire tips a hat to Schoenberg. Esther's Nose Job borrows its title from an episode in Thomas Pynchon's novel V., and its riffs in uneven metres are the first real sketch of the long, repeating structures that dominate the next album.","The record reached few buyers but has grown in stature as the hinge between the psychedelic trio and the jazz-rock ensemble of Third. Critics now tend to hear it as the most distinctively Canterbury of all the Soft Machine albums, witty, English and technically daring at once. Some find the fragmentation wearing; others argue that the rapid cutting between tiny pieces anticipates the collage methods later used across progressive rock.","Take side one in a single sitting and try to count the changes of piece without looking at the track list. Listen for how Hopper's fuzz bass locks with the organ to form one thick instrument. Then follow Wyatt's voice through the album and on into his solo work. A good remaster brings out the drums, which are the real engine; headphones help with the stereo tricks."],"genesis-foxtrot":["Foxtrot opens with Watcher of the Skies, whose slow wall of Mellotron chords rises like an organ voluntary before the band locks into a stuttering rhythm, and it closes with Supper's Ready, a song suite of about twenty-three minutes in seven sections. Between them come story-songs about property developers, a television talent contest and a brief classical-style acoustic guitar solo. Peter Gabriel's voice shifts between characters, while Tony Banks's keyboards and Steve Hackett's guitar trade lines like the parts of a small orchestra.","It was the second album by the line-up of Gabriel, Banks, Mike Rutherford, Hackett and Phil Collins, made for Tony Stratton-Smith's Charisma label in 1972. Genesis were building a following in Italy and Belgium before Britain, and this record gave them their first real chart success at home. On the tour that followed, Gabriel began appearing in costume, including a red dress and a fox's head drawn from the cover painting, turning concerts into theatre.","Hackett's short acoustic piece Horizons is modelled on the Prelude of Bach's First Cello Suite, a rare moment where the band's school-chapel background shows openly. Inside Supper's Ready, the section called Apocalypse in 9/8 runs over a limping nine-beat pattern while Banks solos across it; then the opening melody returns in triumph, so the long piece closes the way a symphony finale reprises its first theme.","Contemporary reviews were mixed, with some British critics finding it arch and overlong, but Supper's Ready quickly became the fans' touchstone and the piece other progressive bands were measured against. It is now widely rated the best of the Gabriel-era albums alongside Selling England by the Pound. Debate continues over whether its whimsical English storytelling is its charm or its limit, and punk-era writers used it as a symbol of everything they rejected.","Begin with Watcher of the Skies at high volume to feel the Mellotron swell, then give Supper's Ready your full attention in one sitting, following its changes of scene like acts in a play. Listen for the moment the quiet opening tune comes back transformed near the end. Compare Horizons with a cellist playing Bach's Prelude. The Steven Wilson-era remasters are clearer, though some prefer the original vinyl mix."],"jethro-tull-stand-up":["Stand Up is the record on which Jethro Tull stopped being a blues band and became something of their own. Ian Anderson's breathy, growling flute and acoustic guitar lead short, tightly arranged songs that move between heavy blues riffs, folk dances and a jazz arrangement of Bach. Martin Barre's electric guitar gives the heavier tracks their weight, while mandolin, balalaika and hand drums colour the lighter ones. At under forty minutes, it is compact, varied and full of sudden changes of mood.","Guitarist Mick Abrahams, the band's blues purist, left after the first album, and Anderson took full control of the writing. Barre joined in time for sessions in London in 1969, and the album, issued on Island, went to number one in Britain that summer. The original sleeve was a gatefold that opened into a cardboard pop-up of the band, a piece of packaging that told buyers this was a group with ambitions beyond the blues clubs.","Bourée is a jazz treatment of the Bourrée from Bach's Lute Suite in E minor, a piece long familiar to classical guitarists; listen to how Glenn Cornick's bass solo takes the tune through walking jazz lines before the flute returns. On Fat Man Anderson plays mandolin and Barre balalaika, sounds almost unheard in British rock of the day, and they foreshadow the folk turn the band took later in the decade.","Critics welcomed it at the time as a big step beyond the debut, and many fans still regard it as the band's best album, preferring its concision to the long concept records that followed. Bourée became a concert favourite and helped fix Tull's image as the classical-leaning rock band. Some later writers find the blues numbers dated, but the record is usually cited as the moment Anderson's distinct voice arrived.","Start with Bourée, then the opening track A New Day Yesterday, to hear the two poles of the band: Bach on one side, heavy blues on the other. Listen for the flute being overblown so that Anderson's voice and the instrument sound together. Compare the Bach original on classical guitar. The Steven Wilson remix clarifies the acoustic textures; the original mix has more grit."],"ltj-bukem-demons-theme":["Demon's Theme sets fast, crisp breakbeats beneath slow, warm washes of synthesiser pads and soft keyboard chords, so that the track seems to move at two speeds at once: the drums racing, the harmony drifting. There is little of the sirens, rave stabs or heavy bass drops that filled most jungle of its moment. Instead the piece builds by gentle layering over eight or so minutes, closer to jazz-funk and ambient music than to the hardcore dance floors it came from.","In the early 1990s British hardcore rave music was splitting into darker and more aggressive strands. Danny Williamson, a DJ from Watford working as LTJ Bukem, went the other way, founding his own label, Good Looking Records, to release music that drew on the soft keyboards of 1970s fusion. Demon's Theme was one of the records that announced this direction, and Bukem's club night Speed, and later Logical Progression, grew up around the sound.","Listen to how the drum programming is edited: the chopped breakbeats keep shifting their accents, so the rhythm rarely repeats exactly, while the pads above barely change. That contrast is the whole method of what became known as atmospheric or intelligent drum and bass. Its chords owe more to the electric pianos of Chick Corea, Lonnie Liston Smith and Herbie Hancock-era fusion than to any house or techno record.","Among DJs it was quickly recognised as a turning point, proof that jungle tempos could carry melancholy and calm. The label intelligent drum and bass, attached to this sound, drew criticism for implying that the harder styles were stupid, and Bukem himself was wary of it. Today the track is regarded as a founding text of the atmospheric strand of drum and bass, influential far beyond its modest original sales.","Listen once only to the drums, then once only to the pads, and notice how differently the two layers move through time. Then hear it with a harder jungle record such as Goldie's Terminator to understand the fork in the road. Headphones help, since the reverb tails carry much of the atmosphere. Follow it with Horizons and the Logical Progression compilation."],"ltj-bukem-logical-progression":["Logical Progression is a long compilation that gathers LTJ Bukem's own tracks with records by the producers around his Good Looking label into a single statement of the atmospheric drum and bass sound. Across its long running time the tempo stays high, but the mood is spacious and cool: soft synthesiser strings, electric-piano chords, sampled voices and soft percussion float over busy, quietly shuffling breakbeats. It is meant to be heard as a continuous journey rather than a collection of club tools.","By the mid-1990s drum and bass had moved from pirate radio into the mainstream press, and major labels were signing its leading figures. Bukem's compilation, taking its name from his club night, arrived in 1996 as the showcase for his camp, which included producers such as PFM, and it sold well enough to carry the sound to listeners who had never been to a rave. It sat alongside Goldie's and Roni Size's albums as proof that the genre could work at album length.","The sequencing is the art here. Tracks by different producers are placed so that keys and pads flow into each other, and the collection was designed to sound like one DJ's long evening. Listen for how often the bass is muted or absent entirely for long stretches, which makes its eventual arrival feel like a change of weather rather than a drop.","Critics praised the compilation as a gateway into drum and bass, though some jungle purists dismissed the sound as coffee-table music, too polished and too polite. That argument has not fully gone away. Its reputation has grown with time, and it is now seen as the defining document of a strand of the genre that shaped later liquid drum and bass and a good deal of ambient electronic music.","Treat it like a long radio set: put it on in the evening and let it run. Listen for the recurring use of jazz-fusion keyboards and the way breakbeats sit low in the mix. Then compare it with Goldie's debut album and Roni Size's New Forms from the same period. Original CD pressings are fine; vinyl editions often split the sequence and lose its flow."],"pentangle-the-pentangle":["The first Pentangle album brings together two virtuoso acoustic guitarists, Bert Jansch and John Renbourn, with Jacqui McShee's clear, bell-like soprano and a jazz rhythm section of Danny Thompson on double bass and Terry Cox on drums and glockenspiel. The songs mix traditional ballads, blues and original pieces, and the instrumental numbers let the guitars weave around each other while the bass walks and swings beneath. The sound is light, airy and intricate, never loud, yet rhythmically alive.","Jansch and Renbourn were stars of the London folk club circuit, and the band formed around their residencies in 1967. The Transatlantic label hired Shel Talmy, known for producing The Kinks and The Who, and he gave the acoustic group an unusually bright, close-miked sound for 1968. The album appeared the same year Fairport Convention were electrifying folk music; Pentangle took the opposite route, keeping it acoustic but hiring jazz players.","Listen to Danny Thompson rather than the guitars. His double bass often plays a counter-melody rather than a root note, and on the instrumental Pentangling he and Cox push the ensemble into a jazz feel that no other British folk group was attempting. The traditional ballad Bruton Town, a murder tale, is set here with guitar parts that draw on the open tunings of the folk-club guitarists, a hint of the deeper traditional turn of later albums.","The record was well received and helped Pentangle fill concert halls rather than folk clubs. Critics have argued since about whether the band was really a folk group, a jazz group or neither, and that ambiguity is now counted as its strength. The debut is often considered less focused than Basket of Light but more adventurous, and guitarists still study its interplay.","Start with Let No Man Steal Your Thyme, which shows McShee's voice and the whole band in balance, then turn to Pentangling for the instrumental side. Try to follow one guitarist through a whole track, then the other. Compare it with Fairport Convention's Liege & Lief to hear two opposite answers to the same question. A remastered CD brings out the bass."],"deep-purple-in-rock":["In Rock is loud, fast and relentless. Ritchie Blackmore's guitar and Jon Lord's Hammond organ, run through distorted amplifiers, play riffs and solos as equal partners, while Ian Paice's drums drive everything at speed and Ian Gillan's voice climbs to high screams. The centrepiece is Child in Time, a ten-minute song that builds from quiet organ chords and near-whispered singing through a frantic guitar solo to a shattering climax, then begins again. Speed King opens the record with a burst of pure noise.","It was the first studio album by the classic line-up, with Gillan and bassist Roger Glover newly recruited. Lord had just staged his Concerto for Group and Orchestra at the Royal Albert Hall, and the band wanted to prove themselves as a hard rock group rather than a novelty. Recorded in London between late 1969 and spring 1970 and released by EMI's Harvest label, it reached the British top five and established them alongside Led Zeppelin and Black Sabbath.","The slow organ progression of Child in Time is adapted from Bombay Calling by the San Francisco band It's a Beautiful Day, slowed and darkened. Listen too for Lord's organ solos, which borrow Bach-like runs and classical sequences and then push the instrument into feedback; the famous cover, showing the band's faces carved into Mount Rushmore, announced the same mixture of grandeur and cheek.","Critics and fans took to it at once, and it is still often named the band's best studio album, rawer and more exploratory than the polished Machine Head. It is regularly cited in the founding trio of heavy metal records alongside Paranoid and Led Zeppelin II. Some modern listeners find Gillan's screaming overdone; others hear it as the template for every metal singer who followed.","Start with Child in Time and follow its long crescendo without skipping; then play Speed King for the opposite approach. Listen for the moments where organ and guitar trade solos, a duel at the heart of the band. Compare Lord's playing with a Bach organ toccata. The 25th anniversary remaster is a good choice and adds useful outtakes."],"yes-the-yes-album":["The Yes Album is where the band found its sound: long songs in several linked sections, Jon Anderson's high tenor floating over layered vocal harmonies, Chris Squire's trebly, melodic bass, Bill Bruford's crisp jazz-trained drumming and, new here, Steve Howe's guitar, which moves from country picking to jazz chords to long, sustained solos. Starship Trooper ends with a slowly repeating guitar coda, while Clap is a solo acoustic showpiece recorded live. I've Seen All Good People joins a gentle folk song to a boogie.","Yes had made two albums without much success and were close to losing their contract with Atlantic. Replacing guitarist Peter Banks with Howe changed everything; the band retreated to a farmhouse in Devon to write, then recorded in London with engineer Eddie Offord, who became their co-producer. Released in early 1971, it reached the British top five and was the last album with organist Tony Kaye before Rick Wakeman arrived.","Near the end of Starship Trooper, the section called Würm is simply three chords repeated with growing intensity while Howe's guitar solo gradually climbs over them: a rock version of the passacaglia, where a fixed pattern returns under changing melody. In I've Seen All Good People, listen for the brief quotation of Give Peace a Chance in the backing vocals, a small nod to John Lennon.","It was the band's commercial breakthrough and critics recognised it as a leap forward. Later it was overshadowed by Fragile and Close to the Edge, but many fans prefer its directness and the fact that it still feels like a band playing together rather than a studio construction. It is now seen as the moment the classic Yes style came together.","Begin with Starship Trooper and wait for the Würm section to build; it is the best introduction to how Yes use repetition and climax. Then listen to Clap for Howe alone. Follow Squire's bass as if it were a lead instrument. Afterwards play Close to the Edge to hear the same methods pushed to a whole-side suite. The Steven Wilson remix has good clarity."],"judas-priest-stained-class":["Stained Class is heavy metal stripped of blues. Glenn Tipton and K. K. Downing's twin guitars play tight, precise riffs and harmonised lead lines, Les Binks's drums are fast and exact, and Rob Halford's voice leaps from a menacing mid-range to operatic high screams. The songs, such as Exciter and Invader, are short and driven, but the album ends its first side with a slow, grand ballad, Beyond the Realms of Death, which alternates quiet acoustic verses with crushing electric choruses.","Judas Priest came from Birmingham, the same industrial city as Black Sabbath, and had spent years on small labels before signing to CBS. By 1978, with punk dominating the British press, they were refining a leaner, faster sound that owed nothing to American blues and much to the factories they had grown up among. The album was the first of two released that year, and it set out the style later bands of the New Wave of British Heavy Metal would take up.","Exciter, with its double-bass-drum gallop, is often cited as the first speed metal song, years before Metallica. The band's cover of Spooky Tooth's Better by You, Better than Me, added at the label's request, later became the subject of a 1990 civil trial in Reno, Nevada, in which the families of two young men claimed the song carried hidden subliminal messages; the judge found for the band.","The album sold modestly at first but has since become a favourite of critics and metal musicians, many of whom call it the band's most important record. It is praised for its precision and for the ambition of Beyond the Realms of Death, whose subject is suicide and escape into oneself, treated seriously rather than sensationally. The Reno trial made it notorious, but its musical influence on thrash and speed metal is what keeps it in view.","Play Exciter first and count the drum patterns; then listen to Beyond the Realms of Death for the way the band builds contrast between quiet and loud. Notice how the two guitars often play in harmony rather than one rhythm and one lead. Follow it with Metallica's Kill 'Em All to hear the lineage. The remastered edition is brighter than the original CD."],"pj-harvey-stories-from-the-city-stories-from-the-sea":["This is PJ Harvey's most open and radiant record: bright, ringing guitars, big choruses, and a voice that sounds confident rather than cornered. Many songs are love songs set in a hot, crowded New York, full of taxis, rooftops and nights out, while others, like Kamikaze, are fast and ferocious. Thom Yorke of Radiohead sings a duet with her on This Mess We're In, trading verses in a hushed, late-night mood. The album closes with We Float, a slow, cleansing song of release.","Harvey had spent time living in New York, and the record draws on that experience alongside her native Dorset coast, which gives the sea half of the title. She made it with long-time collaborators Rob Ellis and Mick Harvey after the darker, more experimental Is This Desire?. Released in 2000, it won the Mercury Prize in 2001; she was in Washington on the day of the September 11 attacks when the award was announced.","Behind the city postcards, several songs are about fear of losing the happiness they describe, so the brightness has a shadow in it. Listen for Yorke's voice beyond the duet: he also sings backing on other tracks, blending so closely with Harvey that it is easy to miss. The ringing, chiming guitar sound is a deliberate nod to the New York rock of Patti Smith and Television rather than to her own earlier blues roots.","Critics welcomed the change of tone, and it became her best-selling album, winning the Mercury Prize; she later won a second time with Let England Shake, the only artist to do so. Some longtime fans missed the rawness of Rid of Me, feeling the record was too polished. It is now generally seen as her most accessible work and a high point of 2000s rock songwriting.","Start with Good Fortune, which captures the album's mood of joyful city life, then This Mess We're In for the duet. Listen for how the guitars ring and sustain rather than scrape. Compare it with Rid of Me to hear how far her voice has travelled. The standard CD is fine; the demos released in later years show how the songs began."],"roni-size-brown-paper-bag":["The signature track of Roni Size and his Bristol collective Reprazent is built on a walking figure for acoustic double bass, the instrument of a jazz club rather than a rave, looped and sent tumbling over breakbeats chopped at jungle speed. Above it float muted horn-like stabs and fragments of voice. The bass line never hurries even as the drums race, and that double tempo, one foot in a smoky basement and one on a fast dancefloor, is what everyone remembers.","Reprazent grew out of the Full Cycle crew in Bristol, Roni Size working alongside Krust, DJ Die and Suv, with Dynamite MC and the singer Onallee. By 1997 drum and bass had moved from pirate-radio jungle into album culture, and Talkin' Loud, Gilles Peterson's label, released New Forms as a double album. Bristol already had Massive Attack and Portishead; Size wanted the city's jazz-soaked slowness to meet London's frantic breakbeat science. The track helped carry the album to the Mercury Prize that year.","Listen to how little the bass actually changes. The figure is short and repeats almost unaltered for minutes, and the sense of development comes entirely from what the drums do around it: breaks drop out, return half as dense, then double back. It is a jazz rhythm section turned inside out, with the drummer improvising and the bassist holding the chart, and the long single edit hides how patiently the full version builds.","Press at the time treated the track as proof that drum and bass could be musical in the old-fashioned sense, with real instruments and songs, and it was played far outside the scene. Some jungle purists heard that as a dilution, drum and bass dressed up for coffee tables and award juries. Time has been kinder to Size: the track now sounds like a hinge between the hardcore of 1993 and the liquid funk that followed.","Start with the full album version rather than the radio edit, and follow the double bass as your anchor while the drums scatter around it. Then try hearing it at the drums' speed instead, which is roughly twice as fast, and notice how your body chooses between them. Afterwards play Goldie's Timeless for the darker orchestral side of the same moment."],"roni-size-in-the-mode":["The second Reprazent album trades the airy jazz of New Forms for something harder and more physical. The beats are heavier and more compressed, the bass lines closer to rock riffs, and several tracks are built as vehicles for rappers and vocalists rather than as long instrumental journeys. It runs a little over an hour and moves in blocks: aggressive, stepping tracks, then a few that open out into the older, spacious Bristol sound, as if checking what the group had left behind.","After a Mercury Prize, Reprazent faced the problem every celebrated debut creates: what next. Released on Talkin' Loud in 2000, In the Mode arrived as drum and bass was splintering into tougher techstep and smoother liquid styles, and as nu-metal and rap-rock were selling in huge numbers. Size answered by inviting American voices, including Method Man, Rahzel and Zack de la Rocha of Rage Against the Machine, into a genre that had mostly relied on British MCs.","Notice how the drums are treated. On New Forms the breaks sounded like a live kit recorded in a room; here they are clipped, squashed and pushed forward in the mix until they behave almost like distorted guitars. That production choice, more than the guests, explains why the album sits closer to rock radio than its predecessor, and why the few sparse tracks near the end sound so exposed.","Reviewers were divided. Some welcomed the energy and the transatlantic reach; others felt the album chased a crossover that drum and bass did not need, and that the delicacy which won the Mercury had been sacrificed. It sold less than New Forms and has been written about far less. Heard now, it is a useful document of the moment when British dance music and American rap-rock briefly tried to meet.","Play it loud and treat it as a band record rather than a DJ tool. Pick the Zack de la Rocha collaboration first to hear how far Size pushed the genre toward rock, then return to New Forms and compare the spaces between the drums. The contrast is the lesson: the same producers, three years apart, answering two different audiences."],"queen-a-night-at-the-opera":["Queen's fourth album is a deliberate grab-bag of styles held together by layered studio sound. There is a savage opening song, music-hall pastiche, a skiffle-tinged space ballad sung by Brian May, an eight-minute choral epic called The Prophet's Song, a small piano love song by Freddie Mercury, and the six-minute Bohemian Rhapsody near the end. It closes with May's guitar-orchestra arrangement of the British national anthem. The trademark sound is the stacked choir of the band's own voices.","Queen were in debt and in dispute with their first management when they made the record in 1975, and they spent heavily across several studios, with Roy Thomas Baker producing. The title comes from the Marx Brothers film, and the follow-up would borrow another. It was released by EMI in Britain and Elektra in America in November 1975, as progressive rock was at its most elaborate and just before punk arrived to declare all such ambition suspect.","Listen to Good Company, where May plays ukulele-banjo and then builds an entire trad-jazz band, clarinet, trombone and trumpet, out of guitar overdubs shaped with his home-made instrument and a small amplifier. The album sleeve proudly noted that no synthesizers were used, and much of what sounds like keyboards or brass on the record is May's guitar multitracked into choirs.","Critics in 1975 were often sniffy, hearing pastiche and showing off; the audience disagreed, and the album and its single made Queen stars. Later punk writing held it up as the very thing to rebel against. Its reputation has risen steadily since, and it is now widely read as a high point of studio-built rock, closer in spirit to Pet Sounds and Sgt. Pepper than to heavy metal.","Hear it as a variety show in one sitting, and do not skip to Bohemian Rhapsody; its operatic middle makes more sense after The Prophet's Song, where the same stacked-voice canon is tried out at greater length. Use headphones for the guitar orchestrations. Then play the Sex Pistols' God Save the Queen to hear the other side's argument two years later."],"queen-we-will-rock-you":["A two-minute song with almost no instruments. Freddie Mercury sings three short verses over nothing but a pattern of two stamps and a clap, repeated without change from first second to last, while the band's voices gather for the chorus. Only at the very end does Brian May's guitar enter, with a solo that sounds as if it has been waiting outside the door. The stomp-stomp-clap is probably the most widely performed rhythm in rock, repeated in every sports stadium in the world.","Brian May wrote it in 1977 after a concert where the audience sang back to the band, which set him thinking about a song a crowd could perform with its own bodies. It opened the album News of the World and went out as a double A-side with Mercury's We Are the Champions. Punk was loud in London that year, and Queen, suddenly unfashionable in the music press, answered with the most stripped-down thing they ever recorded.","The stamping was built in the studio by overdubbing the band stamping on boards many times, with small delays between layers, so it sounds like a crowd far larger than four people. The verses sketch a whole life in three stages, from noisy boy to young man to old man, which gives the song a quiet sadness beneath the swagger that is easy to miss in a stadium.","Critics treated it as a novelty, then as a piece of arena bombast; audiences made it one of the most recognised recordings ever made. Musicologists and sports writers alike now point to it as a rare pop song designed around participation rather than listening. Whether that is a triumph of democratic music or the start of rock's slide into sports entertainment is still a fair argument.","Listen once on headphones to hear how the stamping layers are spread across the stereo field, then listen to the lyric's three ages of a life. Wait for May's solo and notice how it changes the song's key feeling from chant to release. Hear it next to Bohemian Rhapsody: Queen's most elaborate and most elemental records, two years apart."],"roxy-music-avalon":["Roxy Music's final studio album is a record of surfaces and mist. Slow and mid-tempo songs glide on soft drums and fretless-sounding bass, Phil Manzanera's guitar reduced to glints and chimes, Andy Mackay's saxophone drifting in the distance, and Bryan Ferry singing close to the microphone, almost murmuring. Two short instrumentals, India and Tara, frame the mood. Opening with More Than This and the title song, it is one of the smoothest, most carefully mixed records of its decade.","By 1982 Roxy Music was effectively Ferry, Manzanera and Mackay plus session players, a long way from the art-school glam of 1972 and the departed Brian Eno. Produced with Rhett Davies and mixed by Bob Clearmountain, it was made partly in the Bahamas and New York, as digital synthesizers and new studio polish were reshaping pop. Ferry, recently married, drew on the Arthurian island of Avalon for the title. It was the band's biggest-selling album and they disbanded soon after.","Listen to how much of the sound is space. Clearmountain's mix leaves the drums quiet and dry and sets everything else in long reverbs, so that instruments seem to appear and fade rather than start and stop. The cover, a helmeted figure with a hawk against a misty lake, is part of the same design: Roxy Music always treated the whole package, sleeve included, as the artwork.","Some critics who loved the early, jagged Roxy Music heard Avalon as a retreat into luxury, the avant-garde band become a soundtrack for wine bars. Others, then and since, have heard its restraint as a culmination, a record in which the ironic crooner finally sings without the irony. It was a favourite test disc for hi-fi enthusiasts, and its influence on the sophisticated pop of the 1980s is plain.","Play it quietly, late at night, and do not wait for anything to happen; the pleasure is texture and pace. Start with the title track and listen for the backing vocalist behind Ferry. Then play the first Roxy Music album straight afterwards to hear the full distance travelled by the same three musicians in ten years."],"kode9-sine-of-the-dub":["Kode9 and the poet the Spaceape slow Prince's Sign o' the Times to a crawl and strip it to bones. What remains is a deep sine-wave bass, a few dry clicks and hits placed far apart, a haze of reverb, and the Spaceape's low, grave voice half-speaking the song's catalogue of social disasters. There is almost no melody. The sensation is of the original song heard from inside a sealed room, or through a wall, years after the event.","Kode9 is Steve Goodman, a Glasgow-born producer and DJ who had studied philosophy and ran the Hyperdub webzine in London, where the dark offshoot of UK garage later called dubstep was taking shape in south London clubs and on pirate radio. This was the first record on his Hyperdub label, and it set its tone. The Spaceape, Stephen Gordon, brought a reggae and dub poetry lineage, and his voice became part of the label's identity.","The title is a double pun: a sine wave is the purest electronic tone, the kind that makes the sub-bass here, and dub is the Jamaican art of subtraction. Prince's 1987 song was already stark, one drum machine and one bass line; Kode9 subtracts further, the way King Tubby emptied reggae songs to their skeletons, so the record is a cover version that works as a dub.","Released in tiny numbers, it was known mainly to a small scene at first, but it is now widely cited as the founding document of Hyperdub, the label that went on to release Burial, and as an early sign that dubstep could be slow, political and literary rather than just heavy. Some Prince fans find it a bleak reduction; for others, it reveals the dread that was always inside the original.","Play Prince's Sign o' the Times first, then this, on speakers or headphones that can reproduce very low bass; much of the record lives below what laptop speakers can play. Listen to the silences between hits as carefully as the sounds. Afterwards try Burial's first album to hear where the Hyperdub mood led."],"kode9-black-sun":["The second album by Kode9 and the Spaceape is brighter, faster and more restless than their first. The slow, dubby spaces of Memories of the Future give way to syncopated drum patterns borrowed from UK funky and Chicago footwork, glassy synthesizer chords and cleaner, more insistent bass. The Spaceape's voice still anchors the record, now weaving through busier rhythms, and a few tracks drop him altogether for instrumental workouts. It plays like science fiction told at dancefloor tempo.","By 2011 Hyperdub had become one of the most admired labels in electronic music, with Burial, Darkstar and Ikonika on its roster, and dubstep itself had split into a stadium-sized American form and a restless London underground that was absorbing house, funky and grime. Steve Goodman had published Sonic Warfare, a study of sound used as a weapon and an atmosphere. Black Sun reflects all of this: less fixed on one genre, more interested in rhythm as contagion.","Goodman has long written about sound in terms of contagion, rhythms that pass from body to body like a virus, and the Spaceape's texts return to images of infection and a darkened sky. Listen for how that idea is carried in the music as well as the words: rhythmic cells mutate across the running order, patterns from one track reappearing shifted or sped up on another, so the record behaves like something spreading.","Reviews were respectful rather than rapturous. Some felt the move toward faster, cleaner rhythms lost the claustrophobia that made Memories of the Future special; others heard the pair refusing to stand still while dubstep hardened into formula. After the Spaceape's death from cancer in 2014 the album has been heard more tenderly, as the last full statement of a partnership that shaped Hyperdub's voice.","Listen straight after Memories of the Future to hear the tempo and colour change. Focus on the drums, which shift pattern more than on almost any dubstep album, and notice where the Spaceape's voice rides across the beat rather than with it. For context, follow it with Burial's Untrue, the label's other great statement of London after dark."],"the-bothy-band-after-hours":["The Bothy Band's live album catches the group in full flight before a French audience. Sets of reels and jigs are strung together at ferocious speed, the uilleann pipes, flute and fiddle playing the tune in tight unison while bouzouki, guitar and keyboard drive underneath like a rhythm section. Between them come songs in Irish and English sung by Tríona Ní Dhomhnaill and her brother Mícheál Ó Domhnaill. The sound you remember is the lift when a new tune cuts in mid-set without a breath.","Formed in Dublin in 1974, the band gathered Paddy Keenan on pipes, Matt Molloy on flute, Kevin Burke on fiddle, Dónal Lunny on bouzouki and the two Ó Domhnaill siblings. They were the most energetic of the groups that followed Planxty in modernising Irish traditional music, and they toured Europe widely, finding especially warm audiences in France and Brittany. This record, released in 1979, came as the band was breaking up and served as its farewell.","Listen to the accompaniment rather than the tunes. Lunny and Mícheál Ó Domhnaill do not simply strum chords; they play syncopated patterns that push against the dance rhythm, and Tríona's clavinet adds a clipped, almost funky pulse. That backing, borrowed partly from rock, is the band's real innovation: it lets a small acoustic group sound as driving as an electric one.","Many listeners and musicians consider it the finest document of the band, because the studio albums, good as they are, never quite captured the speed and heat of their concerts. Traditionalists at the time sometimes objected to the tempos and the rock-derived backing as unsuited to dance music. Its influence on later Irish groups, from De Dannan onward, has settled that argument largely in the band's favour.","Begin with one of the long instrumental sets and follow a single instrument, the pipes, say, all the way through, noticing how Keenan decorates the tune differently each time it returns. Then listen again to the bouzouki alone. Compare it with Planxty's first album to hear the same revolution in a gentler key."],"iron-maiden-iron-maiden":["Iron Maiden's debut is heavy metal played with punk's speed and grime. Paul Di'Anno's rough, streetwise voice sits over twin lead guitars that often play harmonised lines, while Steve Harris's bass gallops at the front of the mix rather than hiding under the guitars. Short, fast songs like Prowler and Running Free alternate with longer, multi-part pieces, above all the seven-minute Phantom of the Opera, and there is a full instrumental, Transylvania. The galloping bass rhythm is the band's fingerprint.","Steve Harris founded the band in east London in 1975 and built a following in pubs just as punk was sweeping the capital. By 1979 a wave of young hard-rock bands, soon called the New Wave of British Heavy Metal, was touring Britain, and Iron Maiden was its leader, signing to EMI. The album came out in April 1980 with Dennis Stratton and Dave Murray on guitars and Clive Burr on drums, and introduced Derek Riggs's cartoon mascot, Eddie.","Phantom of the Opera is the key to the band's future: its sudden changes of tempo, long instrumental sections and interlocking guitar harmonies point straight toward the epics of the following years. Harris has said he disliked the production, which is rawer than what came after; that rawness is exactly why the record sounds closer to the punk bands the group officially despised.","Received well by the rock weeklies and by a young audience hungry for something tougher than 1970s hard rock, it sold strongly for a debut. Later judgment sometimes treats the Di'Anno era as a prelude to the Bruce Dickinson records, but many metal fans, and thrash musicians in particular, prefer these two early albums for their speed and street energy.","Start with Phantom of the Opera, then play Prowler to hear the band's other face. Keep your ear on the bass, which carries as much melody as the guitars. Afterwards play Judas Priest's Sad Wings of Destiny to hear where the twin guitars came from, and Metallica's Kill 'Em All to hear where they went."],"motorhead-overkill":["Motörhead's second studio album opens with its title track, a relentless thunder of double bass-drum played by Phil Taylor, with Lemmy's distorted bass chords and hoarse bark riding on top and Eddie Clarke's guitar scything through. The rest of the record mixes speeding rock and roll, slower blues-based grinds such as Metropolis, and songs of sardonic defiance like No Class and Damage Case. The sound is a power trio played as loud and as fast as the machinery allowed.","Lemmy Kilmister, sacked from the space-rock band Hawkwind in 1975, formed Motörhead to play what he called rock and roll, only faster and louder. After a shaky start the classic trio signed to Bronze Records, and Overkill, released in 1979 and produced by Jimmy Miller, known for his work with the Rolling Stones, was their first real success. The band drew punk and metal audiences alike at a moment when the two camps usually despised each other.","The title track ends, then starts again, then ends and starts again: a series of false endings that made it a concert favourite and a small joke about excess. Listen too to Lemmy's bass, played with a pick and heavy distortion through guitar amplification so that it behaves more like a rhythm guitar; that is why a trio sounds so full. Metropolis takes its name from Fritz Lang's silent film.","The music press gave it more respect than Motörhead's first album, and it remains among the band's most admired records. The double-kick drumming of the title track is routinely cited as a direct ancestor of thrash and speed metal. Lemmy himself resisted the heavy metal label for decades, insisting his band was rock and roll, and critics still argue over where Motörhead belongs.","Start with the title track at high volume and count the false endings. Then play No Class to hear the band's blues-boogie roots. Afterwards try Metallica's Kill 'Em All and hear how directly the drums were adopted, and the Ramones' first album to hear the same idea of speed and simplicity arrived at from the punk side."],"motorhead-no-sleep-til-hammersmith":["A live album from the classic Motörhead trio at the height of its powers, playing songs from its first four records faster and harder than on the studio versions. Ace of Spades, Overkill, Bomber and the band's own theme song all appear, stitched together by crowd noise and Lemmy's gruff remarks between numbers. Nothing is added: no overdubbed choirs or smoothing, just three players, walls of amplifiers and an audience roaring back. The sustained, relentless velocity is the point.","Released by Bronze in June 1981, after the success of the Ace of Spades album and the band's hit collaboration with Girlschool, it entered the British album chart at number one, an extraordinary result for a noisy live record. It captured the band on its tours of northern England in early 1981. The early 1980s were a peak for British metal in the charts, as the New Wave of British Heavy Metal crowded into the mainstream.","Despite the title, the album was not really recorded at the Hammersmith Odeon in London; most of it comes from concerts in Leeds and Newcastle. The name is a road-crew joke about the endless tour that would end, eventually, at Hammersmith. Listen for how Phil Taylor pushes the tempo ahead of the studio versions; the band played on the edge of control.","It is often named among the greatest live albums in rock, praised for honesty in an era when many live records were heavily repaired in the studio. Its title was parodied a few years later by the Beastie Boys. Some critics find a whole album at one speed and volume exhausting; for admirers, that relentlessness is exactly what live Motörhead was.","Play it loud, from start to finish, as a concert rather than a playlist. Compare the live Overkill with the studio version to hear how much faster it has become. Listen between songs for Lemmy's dry humour with the crowd. Then try a Ramones live recording to hear punk's version of the same ferocious economy."],"buzzcocks-singles-going-steady":["A collection of Buzzcocks' first eight singles for United Artists, A-sides in order on the first side and their B-sides on the second. It moves from the scrappy nerve of Orgasm Addict through What Do I Get?, Love You More and Ever Fallen in Love to the richer Promises and Steve Diggle's Harmony in My Head. The sound is buzzing, bright guitars, fast drums from John Maher, and Pete Shelley's high, wry voice singing about love going wrong.","Buzzcocks came from Manchester and helped create its punk scene by bringing the Sex Pistols to the Lesser Free Trade Hall in 1976. After Howard Devoto left, Shelley became frontman and chief songwriter, and between 1977 and 1979 the band, with producer Martin Rushent, released a run of singles that made them the great pop group of British punk. This compilation was assembled in 1979 partly to introduce them to American listeners on the IRS label.","Listen to the B-side Lipstick and then to Magazine's Shot by Both Sides: they share a guitar figure, because Shelley and Devoto had worked on it together before going separate ways. Notice also how rarely Shelley's love songs specify the gender of the person addressed, a deliberate choice for a songwriter who was open about his bisexuality in an unforgiving time.","Many critics rank it as the best album of the punk era, even though it was never conceived as an album, and it is often preferred to the band's studio LPs. It proved that punk's speed and noise could carry perfect three-minute pop, and it became a template for pop-punk and indie guitar music in Britain and America. Some punk purists found the band too tuneful; that complaint has aged badly.","Play the A-sides in order and hear a band growing up in two years: the songs get longer, the guitars more layered, the lyrics more rueful. Then turn to the B-sides for the stranger, more experimental material. Hear it next to Never Mind the Bollocks to feel the difference between punk as fury and punk as heartbreak."],"siouxsie-and-the-banshees-hong-kong-garden":["The Banshees' first single opens with a bright, tinkling xylophone figure before slashing guitar, a thudding tom-tom beat and Siouxsie Sioux's commanding, slightly aloof voice take over. It is under three minutes, fast and angular, with John McKay's guitar playing sharp chords that ring rather than chug, and Kenny Morris favouring drums that boom rather than crash. The combination of pop hook and chilly, jagged tone announced a new sound somewhere past punk.","Siouxsie and Steven Severin had been part of the Sex Pistols' Bromley entourage and first played together at the 100 Club Punk Festival in 1976. The band built a following for two years without a record deal, becoming famous as the best unsigned group in Britain, before Polydor signed them in 1978. Released that August, the single reached the British top ten and led to the debut album The Scream.","The title comes from a Chinese takeaway in Chislehurst, and the song was prompted by Siouxsie seeing racist skinheads harass the people who worked there; it describes that bullying obliquely rather than directly. That context has not stopped later listeners from questioning the song's pseudo-oriental xylophone hook, which borrows a musical stereotype even while the words side with the victims.","Critics welcomed it as one of the finest debut singles of the punk years, and it is regularly cited as a founding record of post-punk and, more loosely, of gothic rock. The debate over its exoticism has grown in recent decades, and the band itself has spoken about the song's intent. Few dispute that it made the Banshees the most original of the bands to emerge from the Pistols' circle.","Listen first for how the xylophone and the guitar share the same bright, metallic register, then for the drums, which avoid cymbals almost entirely. Play it next to Anarchy in the U.K. to hear the break with punk's shouting, then jump to Juju to hear how far the band travelled in three years."],"the-fall-this-nations-saving-grace":["The Fall at their most tuneful without being any less strange. Over locked, repetitive grooves from Steve Hanley's bass and Karl Burns's drums, Mark E. Smith half-speaks, half-sneers dense, cryptic stories, while Brix Smith and Craig Scanlon's guitars supply hooks that almost sound like pop. The record swings from the brisk rockabilly thrash of Bombast to the drum-machine pulse of L.A., the long psychedelic churn of Spoilt Victorian Child and the eight-minute Krautrock homage I Am Damo Suzuki. Repetition is the engine throughout.","By 1985 the Manchester group had moved from Rough Trade to Beggars Banquet, and Smith's American wife Brix, who had joined on guitar in 1983, was pulling the band toward melody and a brighter sound. John Leckie, who had engineered for Pink Floyd and would later produce the Stone Roses, produced. Britain was in the long aftermath of the miners' strike, and the Fall, rooted in the Salford-Prestwich fringe of Manchester, remained both the most prolific and the most contrary band on the independent circuit.","I Am Damo Suzuki is a love letter to Can, named for their Japanese singer: it borrows the rhythmic feel of Can's Oh Yeah and Bel Air and reshapes them into a Fall song, so hearing Tago Mago first changes it completely. On Paintwork, the mix seems to cut suddenly into stray snatches of television and other recordings; the story usually told is that Smith accidentally taped over part of a cassette of the song, and he kept the damage.","It sold modestly but was praised warmly, and over the decades it has become the album most often named as the best entry point to a catalogue of some thirty studio records. Some long-time admirers prefer the harsher early Fall and see the Brix years as a softening; others hear this as the moment the band's hypnotic method and a real melodic gift sat in perfect balance. The expanded reissues added the singles of the period, including Cruiser's Creek.","Start with Spoilt Victorian Child for the full force of the guitars, then move to L.A., where a drum machine and a single riff carry the whole song. Do not try to decode every line on first listen; treat Smith's voice as another rhythm instrument. Then play Can's Tago Mago and come back to I Am Damo Suzuki to hear the debt."],"the-fall-grotesque-after-the-gramme":["The Fall's third album, and the one where their sound hardens into something no one else could make: scratchy, lo-fi guitars from Marc Riley and Craig Scanlon, the rumbling bass of Steve Hanley, a clattering drummer, and Mark E. Smith ranting over the top like a street-corner satirist. Songs such as Container Drivers and Pay Your Rates run on a sped-up rockabilly shuffle; English Scheme is a sour two-minute snapshot of class Britain; the closing The N.W.R.A. is a long, droning, half-told science-fiction tale about the North rising against the South.","Recorded in 1980 for Rough Trade, the London independent label that was becoming the home of British post-punk, it came out in the first full year of Margaret Thatcher's government, with Northern manufacturing towns heading into mass unemployment. The group, from Prestwich on the edge of Manchester, were deliberately distant from the London scene. Smith, a former shipping clerk, was already writing about bureaucracy, rent, drink, pulp horror and the petty snobberies of English life in a voice closer to a short-story writer than a rock singer.","Smith's name for the Fall's sound was a joke genre he called country and northern: the rockabilly rhythms on Container Drivers are sincere, rooted in 1950s rockabilly, but pushed through Manchester sludge. Listen to The N.W.R.A.: the narrative never quite resolves, and the band vamp on as the story trails off, making the song itself feel like an unfinished rebellion. The kazoo on New Face in Hell is not a gag; it is the lead instrument.","It was admired in parts of the music press on release but stayed far from the mainstream charts. Its reputation has only grown: many critics rank it with Hex Enduction Hour as the peak of the early Fall, and English Scheme and The N.W.R.A. are regularly cited as the sharpest writing about the North of England in British rock. Listeners still divide over the thin, rough production, which some hear as a flaw and others as the point.","Begin with Container Drivers, which is funny, fast and catchy in a lopsided way, then English Scheme for Smith's satire at its most compact. Save The N.W.R.A. for when you can listen properly; let it go on longer than feels comfortable. Compare the rockabilly beat with Elvis Presley's Mystery Train to hear how far the Fall bent the model."],"the-slits-return-of-the-giant-slits":["The Slits' second and last album of their first life moves away from punk-reggae toward something looser and more global: long, rolling grooves built on hand percussion, loping bass from Tessa Pollitt and the drumming of Bruce Smith, with Viv Albertine's guitar used for texture rather than riffs. Ari Up chants, yelps and sings in a voice that sounds like play. Songs such as Earthbeat and Or What It Is? drift on for minutes, with horn lines, children's-rhyme melodies and bursts of improvised noise.","After Cut, made for Island in 1979, the band drifted into the orbit of the jazz trumpeter Don Cherry, whose stepdaughter Neneh Cherry moved in the same circle, and of the Bristol group the Pop Group, whose drummer Bruce Smith joined. CBS released the record in 1981, a year when major labels were still signing post-punk bands in the hope of hits. Themes of the earth, the body and the cycle of life reflect the countercultural, ecological strand of their thinking, and the band split up soon after.","The album's world-music leanings came from real contact, not from record collecting: Don Cherry's so-called organic music, with its African and Asian percussion and chanting, shaped how the Slits thought about rhythm, and you can hear the same open-ended, communal sound. Listen for how rarely anything resolves; songs fade or dissolve rather than end, which was a deliberate turn away from the tight structure of their earlier punk singles.","Reviews were baffled and sales were poor, and the record spent years out of print, remembered mostly as the moment the Slits lost their way. Reissues from the 2000s onward prompted a reassessment: critics now hear it as ahead of its time, anticipating the loose, percussive, feminist art-pop of later decades. It remains less loved than Cut, and even admirers concede that some tracks wander.","Play Earthbeat first and let the percussion carry you; do not wait for a chorus. Then hear it against Cut to understand how far the band travelled in two years. Follow with Talking Heads' Remain in Light, made the year before by a band chasing a similar polyrhythmic idea from a different direction."],"the-slits-typical-girls":["The Slits' best-known song and the single from Cut. A skipping reggae-inflected bass line and dry, springy drums hold everything together while Viv Albertine's guitar scratches and chimes and a jaunty keyboard figure bounces through the gaps. Ari Up delivers the verses in a sing-song, almost nursery voice, ticking off the worries that magazines and advertising press on young women: looks, clothes, romance, being obedient and easy to sell to. The arrangement keeps shifting its footing, so a protest song turns into a strange little dance.","Released on Island in 1979, backed with a cover of I Heard It Through the Grapevine, it was produced by Dennis Bovell, a London reggae producer and bass player who taught the band to use space and dub-style dropouts. The Slits, formed in 1976 by teenagers in the thick of the London punk scene, were one of the first all-women bands of the era, and had faced open hostility on tour. Budgie, later of Siouxsie and the Banshees, played drums on the Cut sessions.","The lyric's final turn is the sharpest thing in it: after listing all the ways typical girls are supposed to behave, the song asks who manufactured this ideal in the first place, suggesting it is a product sold to women rather than a nature they are born with. Listen also to the arrangement: Bovell's spacious production and the tinkling keyboard help turn a punk rant into a playful piece of pop theatre.","It did not become a hit, but it was admired at once and its reputation has grown with every decade, above all as the riot grrrl bands of the 1990s cited the Slits as ancestors. Viv Albertine's 2014 memoir, Clothes, Clothes, Clothes. Music, Music, Music. Boys, Boys, Boys., brought the song and the band renewed attention. It is now a staple of lists of the great feminist records and of post-punk singles.","Listen first to the bass and drums, which are pure reggae in their patience, then to how Ari Up's voice plays against them. Notice how the arrangement keeps changing direction. Afterwards play Patti Smith's Horses and X-Ray Spex's punk singles, then Bikini Kill, to follow the line of women in punk speaking back."],"wire-154":["Wire's third album completes the journey away from punk that Pink Flag began. The songs are longer, slower and heavily textured: layered guitars and synthesizers, echoing production and sudden changes of mood. I Should Have Known Better is a slow, almost gothic unease; The 15th is a delicate, circling pop song; Map Ref. 41°N 93°W is a bright, compressed anthem about aerial views of the American Midwest; A Touching Display is a long, grinding drone. The feeling is cool, cinematic and uneasy.","Released on Harvest in 1979 and produced, like the first two albums, by Mike Thorne, it was made while tensions between the band members and with the label were growing. The title is the number of live performances Wire had given up to that point. The group, from London art schools, were increasingly interested in synthesizers, tape and conceptual performance, and after one more year of concerts they stepped away from the band until the middle of the 1980s.","The title is a wry joke about career and exhaustion: a bare tally of gigs standing in for a band identity. Listen to how the songwriting splits: Colin Newman's melodic pop pieces sit beside Bruce Gilbert and Graham Lewis's darker, more abstract ones, and you can hear the group pulling in two directions. On A Mutual Friend the sudden entry of a woodwind line, played by a guest on cor anglais, is a jolt of orchestral colour in a rock record.","It reached the lower half of the British top forty and was well reviewed, but Wire's relationship with EMI fell apart soon afterward. Critics are split between those who prefer the raw brevity of Pink Flag and those who hear 154 as their most ambitious and beautiful record. Its textures fed directly into the moodier post-punk and later shoegaze sound, and many musicians cite it as the album that showed punk could become atmosphere.","Start with The 15th and Map Ref. 41°N 93°W, the two most immediately lovely songs, then go back to the opening track and hear how dark the setting is around them. Play Pink Flag straight after to hear the scale of the change in two years, then My Bloody Valentine's Isn't Anything for where the textures went."],"four-tet-pause":["Kieran Hebden's second album as Four Tet builds small, warm pieces from chopped-up acoustic sounds: plucked guitar, harp, bells, fragments of voice and brushed drums, all edited on a computer into loops that stutter, overlap and breathe. Beats in the hip-hop manner sit under what sounds like folk music heard through a cracked radio. Tracks such as Glue of the World, Parks and No More Mosquitoes are short and intimate rather than dance-floor ready, with a homemade, crackling surface.","Hebden, a Londoner, was still playing guitar with the post-rock group Fridge when he began making solo records, and Pause came out on Domino in 2001, as cheap laptops and sample software let one person produce a whole album in a bedroom. The press, needing a name for this mix of acoustic sampling and electronic editing, began to call it folktronica, a label Hebden himself treated warily. His record collecting in jazz, folk and hip-hop fed directly into the sound.","Almost nothing on the album is played straight: the guitars and harps are samples, sliced and re-sequenced so that phrases repeat at slightly wrong moments, giving the sense of a real musician whose playing keeps looping back. Listen to the little clicks and edits at the edges of each loop; Hebden left them audible on purpose, so the digital cut becomes part of the rhythm, the way a scratch is part of a record.","It was widely praised on release and became a defining record of early-2000s electronica, often grouped with Boards of Canada and later Caribou. The folktronica label stuck, sometimes to its detriment as imitators multiplied. Some listeners prefer the fuller, jazzier Rounds that followed; others hold that Pause's modesty and warmth are exactly its achievement, and that its method underlies a great deal of later bedroom production.","Listen on headphones at moderate volume so the small sounds and edits come through. Start with Parks, then Everything Is Alright. Notice how the loops are almost, but not quite, regular. Follow with Rounds to hear the approach expand, and with Boards of Canada's Music Has the Right to Children for a colder cousin."],"gang-of-four-damaged-goods":["The song that announced Gang of Four's sound: a bass line from Dave Allen that is pure funk, tight, dry drums from Hugo Burnham, and Andy Gill's guitar not playing chords so much as slashing noise in short, violent bursts, then dropping out entirely. Over it Jon King sings about the end of an affair, but in the language of shopping, sales and damaged stock, so that a relationship becomes a transaction. The groove is danceable; the mood is anything but comfortable.","It was the lead track of the band's debut three-song EP, released in 1978 on Fast Product, a small Edinburgh label run by Bob Last that also launched the Human League and the Mekons. The band had formed at Leeds University, where fine art and cultural studies were steeped in Marxist theory and the Situationists, and Leeds itself was a city of National Front marches and rising unemployment. The song was re-recorded the following year for their debut album, Entertainment!","The b-side Love Like Anthrax is the key that unlocks this one: there, King sings a love song in one speaker while Gill, in the other, reads out a flat statement about why love songs are so common, so the listener has to choose which to follow. Damaged Goods makes the same argument in miniature: that even private feelings are shaped by the market. Listen for the silences in the guitar, which do as much work as the noise.","The EP was praised immediately by John Peel and the music press, and it is often named among the essential singles of British post-punk. The Fast Product version is rawer than the album remake, and many listeners prefer it. The song's influence surged in the early 2000s, when bands such as Franz Ferdinand and the Rapture built careers on its fusion of funk rhythm and angular guitar, and critics debated whether the imitators had kept anything of the politics.","Follow the bass first; it never lets up. Then listen to how often the guitar stops, and how the song lurches when it returns. Compare the Fast Product original with the Entertainment! re-recording. Then play James Brown's Funky Drummer to hear the rhythm Gang of Four were stripping down, and the Sex Pistols for the punk they were answering."],"gang-of-four-solid-gold":["Gang of Four's second album is slower, heavier and more spacious than Entertainment! The rhythm section plays deep, deliberate funk grooves, the bass louder in the mix, and Andy Gill's guitar is still a weapon, arriving in stabs and feedback rather than riffs. Jon King's songs continue to dissect everyday life as politics: Paralysed is a bleak monologue about losing work, Why Theory? asks why ideas matter to ordinary living, and Cheeseburger turns a trip to America into a satire of consumer abundance.","Recorded for EMI and released in 1981, as Britain's recession reached its worst and unemployment rose past two and a half million, the album was made with an American producer and a studio polish that the band hoped would take them further into dance clubs. They had toured America and been influenced by the black dance music they heard there. Bassist Dave Allen left soon after its release to form Shriekback, and the band's line-up never settled again.","Listen to the space: where Entertainment! crams in activity, Solid Gold often leaves just bass and drums playing for bars on end, with guitar and voice entering as interruptions. That slowing-down was deliberate and anticipates the heavier, funk-driven records of the following years. On Paralysed the spoken, almost numb delivery is a character study, not King's own voice, and the arrangement is built to sound as stuck as the man speaking.","Reviews were respectful but many found it colder and less urgent than the debut, and it sold less. Its standing has risen modestly since: some critics now prefer its weight and patience, and hip-hop and dance producers have admired the rhythm section. It is still usually treated as the lesser sibling of Entertainment!, and fans argue over whether it is a maturing or a loss of nerve.","Start with What We All Want, the most urgent track, then Paralysed. Play loud enough to feel the bass. Listen for how the guitar comments on the song rather than carrying it. Afterwards compare with Entertainment! and with Talking Heads' Remain in Light, another guitar band chasing funk that year."],"public-image-ltd-first-issue":["John Lydon's first record after the Sex Pistols, and a deliberate break with them. Theme opens the album with nine minutes of grinding guitar from Keith Levene, a huge, dub-heavy bass from Jah Wobble and Lydon wailing in despair. Religion appears twice, first as a spoken attack on the Church, then with the band. Public Image, the single, is fast and ringing, a statement of independence from his old manager. The closing Fodderstompf is nearly eight minutes of mock-disco chatter over a bass groove.","Lydon, once Johnny Rotten, left the Sex Pistols in January 1978 after their American tour collapsed, and within months formed PiL with his friend Wobble, an untrained bassist obsessed with reggae, and Levene, an original member of the Clash. Virgin released the album in December 1978. The name mocked the idea of a band as a brand. Lydon was also locked in a legal fight with the Pistols' manager Malcolm McLaren, which colours the lyrics throughout.","Fodderstompf is partly a joke at the record business's expense: the band reportedly stretched it to fill the album to the length the contract required, and you can hear them laughing about it. But the bass line underneath is serious, a sign of how much Wobble's love of Jamaican dub would matter. The single Public Image was packaged as a mock newspaper, making fun of the tabloids that had made Lydon notorious.","British reviews were mixed and some of the punk audience felt betrayed, while Warner Bros. in America declined to release it at first, finding it uncommercial. Public Image nevertheless reached the British top ten. Critics now hear the album as a transitional record: patchier than Metal Box, but the moment post-punk's alliance of rock, dub and noise first appeared on a major label, and Theme as one of the rawest openers of its era.","Start with the single Public Image, which is closest to the Pistols, then go back to Theme and let its length wear you down. Pay attention to the bass: it is the lead instrument. Then play Never Mind the Bollocks, and Metal Box, to hear where Lydon came from and where PiL went next."],"public-image-ltd-the-flowers-of-romance":["An album built almost entirely from drums. With Jah Wobble gone and no replacement bassist, PiL made a record of huge, booming, echoing tom-toms and cymbals, played by Martin Atkins and by the others in turn, with Keith Levene contributing tape effects, keyboards and scraps of guitar, and John Lydon adding squealing violin and saxophone as well as his keening, chant-like voice. The single The Flowers of Romance sounds like a Middle Eastern procession; Four Enclosed Walls and Banging the Door are claustrophobic, ritual and unsettling.","Made in London in late 1980 and released by Virgin in 1981, the album was recorded partly at the Townhouse studio's stone-walled live room, famous that year for a big, gated drum sound. PiL was by then a loose collective rather than a band. The title reused the name of an early punk group that had included Sid Vicious and the future Slits Viv Albertine and Palmolive, a sly glance back at the London scene of 1976.","Levene reportedly played almost no conventional guitar on the record, and many of the melodic sounds are not what they seem: Lydon's untutored violin and sax, treated tapes, and Levene's keyboard work stand in for chords. Listen to how the drums, not the voice, carry the song structure. Virgin's executives were said to be dismayed on hearing the finished tapes, which only strengthened the band's resolve to release them unchanged.","The title track surprisingly became a top-thirty single in Britain, but many critics dismissed the album as wilful and unfinished. It has since become one of the most admired records of its time among musicians, praised as a daring precursor of industrial music, world-influenced art-rock and the drum-centred production of the 1980s. Opinion still divides over whether it is a triumph of nerve or a fascinating dead end.","Play it loud, so the drums fill the room. Start with the title track, then Four Enclosed Walls. Stop listening for a song and listen for a ritual. Then compare Peter Gabriel's third album and Talking Heads' Remain in Light, contemporaries also rebuilding rock around drums."],"the-streets-a-grand-dont-come-for-free":["A concept album told as a single story across eleven songs. Mike Skinner's narrator loses a thousand pounds, fails to return a DVD, falls for a woman called Simone, suspects his best friend, bets and loses, and has a broken television. The music is modest and warm: mid-tempo beats, piano, strings and guitar under Skinner's conversational, half-rapped Birmingham voice. Fit But You Know It is a loud, comic guitar romp; Dry Your Eyes is a tender breakup ballad with strings; Empty Cans brings the story to a double ending.","Released in 2004 on 679 Recordings, two years after Original Pirate Material, it was recorded largely in Skinner's home studio. Where the first record had been rooted in UK garage and clubland, this one aimed at the story, and it arrived as British youth culture was absorbing grime and indie in equal measure. Dry Your Eyes went to number one in Britain and Fit But You Know It into the top five, and the album was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize.","The final track, Empty Cans, plays its ending twice. In the first version the narrator stays angry and bitter; then the song rewinds and he discovers that the lost money was behind the television all along, and forgives those he blamed. Listen back through the album and notice how often the broken television comes up, a clue hiding in plain sight. The album is less about money than about how a man chooses to tell his own life.","It was widely hailed as a rare British concept album that worked as pop, and it sold far more than its predecessor, making Skinner a mainstream figure. Some garage fans missed the rawness of the debut, and critics debated whether the concept was a triumph of storytelling or a gimmick. Its reputation has held: it is frequently named among the best British albums of its decade, and admired as a model of narrative songwriting.","Listen in order, from It Was Supposed to Be So Easy to Empty Cans, like a radio play; the songs lose much of their meaning shuffled. Pay attention to small details, times of day and objects. Then play Dry Your Eyes on its own to hear how well it stands. Compare the Kinks' Village Green for another English story-album."],"the-streets-has-it-come-to-this":["The Streets' debut single. A two-step garage beat, skipping and syncopated, with a slightly melancholy keyboard line, carries Mike Skinner's flat, unpolished voice as he describes ordinary life in a British city: blocks of flats, arcades, lads getting stoned, clubs, arguments and boredom. Instead of the aspirational, champagne world of garage records of the time, he sings about the everyday texture of being young and skint. The chorus is a wistful, almost dreamy refrain over the beat.","Released in 2001 on Locked On, a label associated with the garage scene, the track was made on cheap equipment by Skinner, a Birmingham-born producer who had moved to London. UK garage was then at its commercial height, with slick, glossy records full of references to luxury and nights out. His record went into the British top twenty. It later became the opening single of his first album, Original Pirate Material, released the following year.","Skinner's lyrics were not the clever wordplay of American rap or the chat of garage MCs; they were closer to the tradition of English kitchen-sink realism, closer to Alan Bennett or Ray Davies than to Jay-Z. Listen for the details of place and routine, and for the way he speaks rather than performs, as if telling a mate about his week. The keyboard figure, too, is modest and homemade beside the gloss of other garage records.","It divided listeners: some garage purists found it amateur, while the music press seized on it as a fresh voice in British music. Its success paved the way for Original Pirate Material, which made Skinner a critical favourite. Today the single is seen as a hinge between UK garage and the grime and indie-rap that followed, and as one of the first records to put British ordinariness at the centre of a club genre.","Listen to the beat first, the skipping two-step rhythm, then to how Skinner's voice fits awkwardly and charmingly across it. Hear it as a short story. Compare it with Artful Dodger's Re-Rewind to hear the garage mainstream he was answering, and with Dizzee Rascal's Boy in da Corner for what came next."],"wiley-wot-do-u-call-it":["A stark, cold, bouncing beat of synth stabs and clipped percussion, and over it Wiley asks, over and over, what name should be given to the music he makes. He runs through the labels that journalists and the industry had tried, rejects the idea that it is just a form of garage, and insists that it is something new, made by East London teenagers with their own sound. The track works as both a club record and a manifesto, playful and combative at once.","Released in 2004 on XL Recordings, it came as grime was emerging from the pirate radio stations and council estates of Bow and East London. Wiley, a producer and MC from the Roll Deep crew who had been making instrumentals in the new style for several years, had seen the sound called eski, sublow, garage and more. His former protégé Dizzee Rascal had just won the Mercury Prize, and the music industry was suddenly paying attention and hunting for a label.","The song's question is also a claim of ownership. By refusing the garage label, Wiley was separating grime from the older, more glamorous garage scene of champagne and dress codes, and asserting that he and his peers had authored something of their own. Listen for how spare the beat is: it leaves nearly all the space to the voice, which is itself a grime principle, the MC's words carrying the weight rather than the production.","It became a top-forty hit in Britain and is regarded as a founding statement of grime, even though the name grime was arguably settled by others. Critics praise its wit and its clarity about identity. Some grime fans prefer Wiley's rawer instrumentals and pirate-radio sets. It is now seen as a turning point in how British black music named and defended itself.","Listen for the call-and-response between Wiley and the beat, and how he keeps returning to the question. Then play Eskimo to hear the instrumental sound he had already built, and Dizzee Rascal's Boy in da Corner, made by his former protégé, to hear the scene's first full album."],"wiley-treddin-on-thin-ice":["Wiley's debut album, a set of grime tracks built on the icy, stark style he had pioneered: square, clipped synth stabs, deep sub-bass, sudden changes of rhythm, and his quick, conversational flow across the top. Some tracks are tough and competitive; others are reflective, about growing up in East London, family, trust and the pressures of the scene. The single Wot Do U Call It? sits at its centre as a manifesto, but the album also holds more personal and melodic songs.","Released by XL Recordings in 2004, it arrived the year after Dizzee Rascal's Boy in da Corner had shown that grime could work as an album and win prizes. Wiley, later nicknamed the Godfather of Grime, was already the leading producer of the Roll Deep crew, but he had built his reputation on pirate-radio sets and twelve-inch white labels rather than albums. The record was an attempt to turn a scene built on short, live, competitive formats into a longer form.","Listen to the sound palette: many of the synth tones are the cheap preset sounds of consumer music software, played with deliberate coldness. That was not a limitation Wiley tried to hide; it was a signature, the same chilly aesthetic he had named eski. The contrast with his warmer, more reflective lyrics gives the album its tension, as though a personal voice were trying to be heard through a frozen landscape.","It was well reviewed but sold modestly, and many critics felt it lacked the focus of Boy in da Corner. Wiley's later career, with chart hits such as Wearing My Rolex, overshadowed it. Grime historians now treat it as a key document of the scene's first wave and a record that captured its founding producer at close to his creative source, before commercial pressures changed the sound.","Start with Wot Do U Call It?, then move to the more reflective tracks and follow how his voice changes. Pay attention to the synths: bright, frozen, deliberately simple. Then play Eskimo and Boy in da Corner, the scene's other founding documents, and Skepta's later records to hear the lineage continue."],"new-order-power-corruption-and-lies":["New Order's second album is where they found their own voice after Joy Division. Peter Hook's melodic high bass lines, Bernard Sumner's chiming guitar and plaintive voice, Stephen Morris's mix of live and programmed drums, and Gillian Gilbert's synthesizers combine rock and electronic dance music. Age of Consent opens with a racing bass and a rush of guitar; Your Silent Face is a slow, glowing electronic hymn; Ecstasy and 5 8 6 are sequencer-driven, close to club music. The mood is bittersweet.","Released by Factory Records in 1983, the album followed the loss of Ian Curtis in 1980, and the three surviving members of Joy Division, now joined by Gilbert, were still discovering how to be a new band. Through the early 1980s they had heard New York dance music, Italian disco and electro, and bought synthesizers and sequencers at a time when the technology was still temperamental. Factory's designer Peter Saville created the sleeve, and the band produced the record themselves.","The sleeve reproduces a still-life painting of roses by Henri Fantin-Latour, with a coloured code block in the corner instead of text; the colours encode the band's name and title in a cipher that Saville invented. The band's working nickname for Your Silent Face was said to be KW1, a nod to Kraftwerk, and you can hear the debt in its slow sequencer pulse. 5 8 6 shares its rhythm programming with Blue Monday, the single released around the same time.","It reached the British top five and was widely praised, and it has since been named among the best British albums of the decade. Critics credit it with fusing post-punk with electronic dance music, pointing towards much of what followed. Some early fans regretted the move away from Joy Division's darkness, but the album is now seen as the moment New Order found a lasting identity and one of the foundation records of alternative dance.","Start with Age of Consent for the guitar and bass, then play Your Silent Face slowly and at night. Listen for how live instruments and machines interlock. Compare it with Joy Division's Closer to hear what was kept and what was left behind, then with Blue Monday and Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express."],"new-order-technique":["New Order at the point where rock band and dance act become the same thing. Half the album is club music: Fine Time opens with a squelching acid-house bass line and pounding beat, and Round & Round, Vanishing Point and Mr. Disco are built on sequencers and bright synthesizer lines. The other half is guitar-based and wistful, such as Run, Love Less and Dream Attack, with Peter Hook's bass lines singing over acoustic and electric guitars. Sumner's voice ties the two moods together.","Recorded partly in Ibiza in 1988, the summer when acid house and the rave scene exploded in Britain and Manchester's Hacienda club, which Factory and the band co-owned, was at the centre of it, the album was released in early 1989. The band had spent the sessions as much in clubs as in the studio. It became New Order's first number-one album in Britain, arriving as the Madchester scene took off.","Technique captures the tension between the studio and the club: much of what was sketched in Ibiza was reportedly finished back in England, where the holiday mood had to be turned into records. Listen to Run: its chord pattern was felt to resemble John Denver's Leaving on a Jet Plane, and the resulting dispute was settled out of court. The album's gentleness sits alongside its club energy.","It reached number one in Britain and was widely acclaimed as the most complete New Order album. Critics praise its balance of acid-house energy and melancholy guitar songs. Some purists felt the band had gone too far into dance music, while others found the guitar songs more convincing than the club tracks. It is now seen as one of the essential albums of the Madchester era.","Start with Fine Time for the acid bass, then play Run and Dream Attack for the guitars. Listen to how each side pulls against the other. Compare it with Power, Corruption & Lies to hear six years of growth, and with Orbital's Chime and Frankie Knuckles's Your Love for the dance music around it."],"sade-promise":["Sade's second album is slower, darker and roomier than the debut that made them famous. Sade Adu's low, unhurried voice sits over Paul Denman's round bass, Andrew Hale's soft keyboards and Stuart Matthewman's saxophone, with drums played so lightly they are felt more than heard. The record moves from the long torch ballad Is It a Crime, which builds to the one full-voiced climax Adu allows herself, through the gently swinging hit The Sweetest Taboo to story songs and the instrumental Punch Drunk.","Diamond Life had sold in the millions around the world in 1984, turning a London club band into an international act almost overnight, and Promise arrived in late 1985 under heavy expectation. It went to number one in Britain and then topped the American album chart in 1986, the year Sade won the Grammy for Best New Artist. It was the Britain of Thatcher and the new City money, and the band's elegance was quickly adopted by an aspirational audience it had not courted.","Listen to how little happens in the rhythm section. Denman often holds a single bass figure for an entire song, and the drums rarely fill between phrases; the tension comes from Adu placing her words slightly behind the beat. Jezebel is really a short story, told in the third person, about a woman who leaves poverty on nerve and looks, and Adu sings it with sympathy rather than judgment, which changes what might have been a cautionary tale.","Promise sold enormously but drew the charge, aimed at the band throughout the decade, that this was designer soul, background music for wine bars and dinner parties. That verdict has aged badly. Later soul singers, from the neo-soul generation to Frank Ocean, cite Sade's restraint as a model, and critics now hear Promise as the album where the band's minimalism became a deliberate aesthetic rather than a smooth accident of taste.","Start with Is It a Crime and wait for the moment, several minutes in, when Adu finally lets her voice open; everything before it is preparation. Then play The Sweetest Taboo for its brushed percussion and patient groove. Listen late at night, at moderate volume, and compare the stillness with Diamond Life before it and Love Deluxe after."],"my-bloody-valentine-m-b-v":["Nine songs in three rough groups of three. The opening third returns to the smeared, pitch-bending guitar haze of Loveless, with Kevin Shields and Bilinda Butcher murmuring half-buried vocals. The middle third is lighter and almost pop, built on brighter chords, and includes Is This and Yes, where Butcher sings over a drifting organ with no guitar at all. The final third turns aggressive: pounding repeated riffs, and on Wonder 2 a roaring, jet-engine swirl over fast, jungle-like drums.","Loveless in 1991 had become a legend partly because of its long, costly making, and the band spent the next two decades as a rumour. Shields began a follow-up in the mid-1990s, abandoned much of it, and the band reunited to tour in 2008. In early 2013 he announced from the stage that the album was finished, and a few days later it appeared without warning, sold directly from the band's own website, which promptly buckled under the traffic.","Some of the material dates back to sessions from the 1990s, so the album is partly an archaeological dig: Shields finished ideas he had begun as a much younger musician. The drum patterns on the closing tracks echo the jungle records Shields admired at that time, which is why m b v can sound both new and oddly period-specific. On Nothing Is, one short drum-and-guitar figure simply repeats, getting louder, as an exercise in endurance.","The surprise release turned into an event, and most reviewers greeted m b v with relief and warmth, amazed that it existed at all and that it did not tarnish Loveless. The main debate is whether it is a true third chapter or an elegant coda. Those who prize the last three tracks argue it pushes the band somewhere new; others hear the first six as a beautiful restatement of an old idea.","Play it in order, loud, on headphones or good speakers, since the three-part shape is the point. Notice how Is This and Yes suddenly strips everything away, then brace for the relentless final stretch. Afterwards go back to Loveless and listen for what has changed: less blur, more rhythm, a sterner sense of momentum."],"dizzee-rascal-maths-and-english":["Dizzee Rascal's third album is his widest-ranging: hard grime tracks built on bleeping synths and heavy sub-bass sit beside rock guitar, electro, a jazzy pastiche and an American hip-hop collaboration. Sirens drives on a thudding beat and a sense of pursuit. Where's da G's brings in Bun B and Pimp C of the Texas duo UGK to mock tough-guy posturing, and Wanna Be, with Lily Allen, is a comic swing-era parody. His quick, sharp East London voice binds it all together.","Four years on from Boy in da Corner, grime had not become the chart force many predicted, and Dizzee was caught between the scene that made him and a mainstream still learning to hear him. Maths + English, released on XL in 2007, was his answer: an album that addressed both the Bow estates and a national audience. It was shortlisted for the 2008 Mercury Prize, and it also brought in a guest spot from Arctic Monkeys' Alex Turner.","The title itself is the argument. Maths and English were the school subjects that Britain's education system measured him on, and the record sets out to prove fluency in both: precise rhythmic arithmetic and wordplay. The video for Sirens turns an English fox hunt on its head, casting Dizzee as the quarry pursued by red-coated riders, which makes the song's sense of being chased into a pointed comment on class.","Critics welcomed it as a return to form after the patchier Showtime, praising the energy of Sirens and the wit of Where's da G's. Some grime purists felt the genre-hopping diluted what made him singular, while others argued it was the first grime album to act like a full pop record. In hindsight it reads as the bridge between the underground debut and the chart-topping run that followed.","Begin with Sirens at volume and listen to how the drums and bass leave space for his voice to cut through. Then play Where's da G's and notice how comfortably he trades verses with Southern American rappers. Finish with Boy in da Corner to hear how far the palette has widened in four years."],"dizzee-rascal-bonkers":["A three-minute rave single built with the New York house producer Armand Van Helden. It opens almost quietly, with Dizzee half-speaking over a sparse beat, then drops into a colossal, wobbling synthesizer bass line that lurches rather than pulses. The structure is simple, tension then release, repeated twice, and the drop is the whole point. His rapping here is plainer and more chant-like than on his grime records, written to be shouted back by a festival crowd.","After the chart-topping Dance wiv Me with Calvin Harris in 2008, Dizzee leaned fully into electro-house and big-room dance music. Bonkers, released in 2009 ahead of the album Tongue n' Cheek, went to number one in Britain and became one of the defining festival anthems of its moment. Three years later he performed it at the opening ceremony of the London 2012 Olympics, an East London artist playing to the world from his own borough's doorstep.","The song is a small reflection on sanity: Dizzee wonders aloud whether he is the mad one or whether everyone around him is, and concludes he cannot be sure. That doubt is dramatised by the arrangement, which shifts from calm, reasonable verses into the frenzied drop, so the music acts out the swing between composure and chaos that the words describe.","Grime loyalists often cite Bonkers as the moment Dizzee left the scene for the pop charts, and the argument over whether that was a betrayal or a victory has never quite settled. The public voted with its feet, and the song's Olympic moment turned it into a national singalong. Critics now tend to treat it as a clever piece of pop engineering rather than a lapse.","Listen for the drop, then listen to how the producer spends the quiet verses building suspense for it. Compare it with a track from Boy in da Corner, made six years earlier, to hear how the same voice adapts from claustrophobic grime to open-air rave. It works best loud, and in company."],"underworld-second-toughest-in-the-infants":["Underworld's second album with Darren Emerson opens with a sixteen-minute, three-part suite, Juanita / Kiteless / To Dream of Love, that shifts from hurtling breakbeats to rolling techno to something close to a hymn. From there the record ranges across long, patient grooves, the acoustic-tinged Confusion the Waitress, the instrumental Rowla and the fragile piano-led closer Stagger. Karl Hyde's vocals are chopped phrases rather than verses, repeated until they become rhythm instead of story.","Released in March 1996 on Junior Boy's Own, the album came out weeks after the film Trainspotting, whose closing use of Born Slippy would soon make Underworld famous far beyond the clubs. The group were a trio at this point: Hyde and Rick Smith, veterans of an unsuccessful 1980s synth-pop band, and the younger DJ Emerson. Their design collective Tomato gave the band a visual identity as distinctive as the music.","Hyde's words come from notebooks of overheard talk, shop signs and fragments picked up walking through cities, and he treats them as found sound. Listen for how a phrase gets repeated with a different emphasis each time, so that meaning drifts. The opening suite also works as a miniature DJ set, with each section handing over to the next as Emerson might at a club.","Critics received it as the equal of Dubnobasswithmyheadman, and many consider it the more adventurous record, praising its range and length. Its reputation was partly overshadowed by the sudden fame of Born Slippy, which was not on the original running order but was added to some later editions. It is now regularly named among the best British electronic albums of the 1990s.","Give the opening suite its full sixteen minutes without skipping; the transitions are the pleasure. Then jump to Stagger to hear the same band at its quietest. Headphones reward the detail, but this was made for large speakers. Pair it with Orbital's In Sides from the same spring to hear two very different ideas of the long electronic track."],"adele-19":["Adele's debut is a small-scale record built around one very large voice. Many songs start with only her voice and a guitar or piano before strings and drums join. The gentle Daydreamer opens the album and Hometown Glory, a slow piano song about London, closes it; between them Chasing Pavements swells with strings as she wonders whether a failing relationship is worth pursuing, and she sings a cover of Bob Dylan's Make You Feel My Love that became one of her best-loved performances.","Adele Adkins wrote much of the album as a teenager, naming it after the age at which she wrote it. A graduate of the BRIT School in Croydon, she was signed by XL after demos circulated online, and in 2008 she received the first-ever Brit Awards Critics' Choice prize. The album entered the UK chart at number one, arriving in the wake of Amy Winehouse's success, when British female singers with vintage soul sounds were in demand.","Her American breakthrough came almost by accident: she sang on Saturday Night Live in October 2008 on an episode that drew an enormous audience because of a political guest, and sales jumped the next day. Listen also for how often the production holds back, leaving room for the grain in her voice, including the slight catches and breaths that later, larger productions would smooth away.","Reviews were warm but measured, with some critics wary of the retro-soul trend and comparing her unfavourably with Winehouse. The album was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, and at the 2009 Grammys she won Best New Artist and Best Female Pop Vocal Performance for Chasing Pavements. With the arrival of 21 the debut came to be heard as a sketch for greater things, though fans often prefer its intimacy.","Start with Hometown Glory and notice the piano's repeated figure beneath the voice. Then hear Make You Feel My Love and compare it with Dylan's own version to hear how she remakes a song through phrasing. Finally, play 21 afterwards to hear the same voice given larger rooms."],"adele-25":["Eleven songs about time rather than heartbreak. The opener Hello, co-written with Greg Kurstin, begins with low piano and a hushed voice before breaking into one of her most powerful choruses. When We Were Young is a slow-burning ballad about nostalgia; Send My Love, made with Max Martin and Shellback, borrows a lighter, guitar-plucked pop pulse; and All I Ask is a piano ballad built for a final, high note. The sound is broad and polished, aimed at arenas.","It arrived in November 2015, four years after 21 had made Adele the best-selling artist in the world, and after she had stepped away to raise her son. She described the album as a reconciliation with her past self. In a period when streaming was overtaking sales, her team initially kept it off streaming services, and in the United States it sold more than three million copies in its first week, a record.","The title matters: 19 and 21 were written from inside youth, while 25 looks back on it, so the songs repeatedly imagine old friends, old homes and earlier versions of herself. Listen to Hello as a call across time rather than across town. The quiet, almost spoken opening of that song is placed so that the chorus lands as a release of stored-up feeling.","It sold vastly and reviews were generally positive, though many critics found it more cautious than 21, relying on familiar ballad shapes and famous co-writers. At the 2017 Grammys it won Album of the Year, and Adele used her speech to say that Beyoncé's Lemonade deserved the prize, reopening a long argument about the awards and race. It remains a measure of how big a single pop album could still be.","Listen to Hello and then When We Were Young back to back; together they state the album's theme. Notice the restraint of the verses and how she saves full power for the last chorus. Then compare it with Beyoncé's Lemonade, released a few months later, to hear two very different kinds of personal record."],"4hero-parallel-universe":["4hero's second album takes the fast breakbeats of jungle and sets them inside something that sounds closer to 1970s jazz fusion and science fiction. Chopped drum patterns race at double speed while slow, drifting chords, electric-piano harmonies and floating strings move at half speed above them. The bass is deep and warm rather than menacing. The effect is of two tempos at once: the dance floor below and a kind of cosmic lounge music overhead.","Marc Mac and Dego ran Reinforced Records from north-west London, a label central to the early hardcore and jungle scenes that had released early records by Goldie. By the mid-1990s jungle was splitting into darker and more musical strands, and 4hero pushed hard toward the latter. The samplers of the time could stretch and pitch drum loops in new ways, and Parallel Universe uses that technology for texture and harmony rather than aggression.","The album's space theme is more than decoration. The duo loved the Afrofuturist tradition of Sun Ra and fusion-era jazz, and many sounds here, spacey synthesizers and sampled scientific-sounding voices, place jungle in that lineage. Listen for how often the chords come from jazz rather than rave: the harmonies are extended and unresolved, giving tracks a questioning mood even at great speed.","It was admired in the scene as one of the first jungle albums that worked as a listening record, alongside Goldie's Timeless and LTJ Bukem's productions. Its reputation grew as later writers traced the move from hardcore to intelligent drum and bass, and it is now cited as a hinge in that story, though it remains less well known than the records it helped make possible.","Try listening to the upper layer only, the chords and strings, and then only to the drums; hearing both tempos at once is the pleasure. Headphones help. Then compare it with Goldie's Timeless and with Miles Davis's Bitches Brew to hear the two traditions it braids together."],"4hero-two-pages":["A double album in two halves. The first disc sounds like lush 1970s soul-jazz made with a string section, harp, flute and guest singers and poets, with gentle broken rhythms underneath. The second disc is more electronic, with fast programmed breakbeats and denser textures. The single Star Chasers, a soft, strings-led song with a skittering drum pattern, is the record's calling card. Across both discs the recurring sound is live orchestral strings set over programmed drums.","Released in 1998 on Gilles Peterson's Talkin' Loud label, Two Pages marked 4hero's move from the jungle underground toward a broader London scene of jazz, soul and electronic crossbreeds. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize that year, a year after Roni Size and Reprazent had won with New Forms. The duo had begun hiring real string players and singers rather than relying only on samplers, a costly shift for a small dance act.","The influences are the soul and jazz records of the early 1970s, especially the spiritual and orchestral kinds associated with Roy Ayers, Minnie Riperton and Alice Coltrane; on their next album 4hero recorded a cover of Riperton's Les Fleurs. Listen for how the drums on the first disc imitate jungle's breaks at a slower, more relaxed pace, pointing toward the broken beat style Dego would help develop in west London.","Critics praised its ambition, and it became a favourite of the jazz-dance audience, though some jungle fans felt it had left the dance floor behind. Its length divided reviewers, who often preferred one disc to the other. It is now remembered as a key record in the late-1990s nu-jazz movement and a bridge to the broken beat scene that followed.","Start with Star Chasers and listen to how the strings and the drums seem to be in different moods. Then play the first disc as a whole, as a soul album, before trying the second, more electronic one. Follow it with Alice Coltrane's Journey in Satchidananda to hear its roots."],"orbital-in-sides":["Long tracks, several running past ten minutes, in which sequenced synthesizer lines build and evolve like slowly unfolding landscapes. The Girl with the Sun in Her Head opens with bright, rippling patterns; The Box is an eerie piece with a clockwork, film-soundtrack feel; P.E.T.R.O.L. is harder and more driving; and the two-part Out There Somewhere? closes the album in a long, spacious arc. The melodies move in overlapping cycles rather than verses and choruses.","Paul and Phil Hartnoll, brothers from Kent who named their duo after the M25 orbital motorway around London, had become celebrated for live shows, above all a headline set at Glastonbury in 1994. In Sides, released in 1996, was their most ambitious studio album. It came out in a Britain in the middle of protest over roads, pollution and new laws against outdoor parties, and those concerns shape several track titles.","Dwr Budr is Welsh for dirty water, and its title quietly frames the track as an environmental lament; the album's titles carry a green politics the music never states outright. The video for The Box starred Tilda Swinton moving through a city in stop-motion. Listen also for how many parts are repeating loops of different lengths, so that they slip against each other, a technique close to minimalist composition.","Reviewers treated it as Orbital's high point and one of the defining electronic albums of the decade, praising its seriousness and emotional reach. Some found its long durations indulgent, a complaint often made of 1990s album-length dance music. Its standing has held, and it is frequently cited as the album where British electronic music most clearly aimed at the scale of classical minimalism.","Start with The Girl with the Sun in Her Head, and let it run its full length; the changes are gradual. Then play The Box to hear their darker, cinematic side. Compare it with Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians, and with Underworld's Second Toughest in the Infants, released the same year."],"charli-xcx-vroom-vroom":["A four-track EP, about thirteen minutes long, made largely with the producer SOPHIE. The title track is built from rubbery, squeaking synthesizer bass, metallic clanks and revving-engine sounds, with Charli's voice chanting over it like a playground taunt. Paradise, featuring the PC Music singer Hannah Diamond, is glassy and sugary; Secret (Shh) is quieter and more teasing; Trophy is loud and brash. Everything sounds synthetic on purpose, as if made from plastic and chrome.","Charli XCX had written hits for others and scored her own with Boom Clap, and her 2014 album Sucker had leaned toward punk-pop. In 2016 she changed course, launching her own label imprint and aligning with SOPHIE and the London PC Music circle, who treated pop as material to exaggerate and distort. Vroom Vroom was the first result of that alliance, released at a moment when mainstream pop was polished and tasteful.","The car theme is a pun on speed, luxury and pop as consumer product, and the lyrics mostly celebrate driving fast with a lover, deliberately shallow on the surface. The trick lies in the sound design: SOPHIE's synthesizer patches imitate physical materials such as latex, metal and liquid, so the production itself is the subject. Listen to how the bass line bounces rather than hits.","Reviews were sharply split. Some critics found the title track grating and hollow, while others heard a bold new direction. Over the following years, as the sound fed into her mixtapes and into what came to be called hyperpop, the EP was reappraised as a turning point, and after SOPHIE's death in 2021 it was widely revisited as one of that producer's defining pop collaborations.","Play the title track loud and focus on the textures rather than the tune; it rewards listening as sound sculpture. Then hear Paradise for the sweeter side. Follow with Pop 2 and Brat to trace how this short release became the foundation of Charli's later work."],"little-simz-no-thank-you":["A ten-track album that sounds calmer than its subject. Inflo's production leans on soft soul chords, gospel choir, strings and rolling live drums, and over it Little Simz raps in long, level paragraphs rather than punchlines. Cleo Sol's warm voice carries several hooks, so the record often feels like a conversation between a speaker who is angry and a singer who is trying to keep the peace. The single Gorilla, all brassy swagger, is the loudest moment; most of the rest simmers.","It arrived with almost no warning in December 2022, barely a year after Sometimes I Might Be Introvert had won her the Mercury Prize and a wave of critical attention. Released through her own Forever Living Originals imprint, it is largely about what came after that success: contracts, managers, money owed, the feeling of being treated as a product. Simz has spoken publicly about cancelling a tour because she could not afford it, and the record turns that bruising year into a statement of independence.","Listen to how the choir functions. On the grandest tracks it does not simply decorate a chorus; it answers her, like a congregation responding to a preacher, so that the complaints about the industry take on the shape of a sermon. The title itself is the thesis: a polite refusal. Notice also how few features there are beyond Cleo Sol, a deliberate narrowing of the circle after the crowded, orchestral scale of the previous album.","Reviewers received it warmly, some calling it her most direct and personal writing, though a few found it a little uniform after the cinematic variety of Introvert and wondered whether a surprise release so soon had been rushed. It has since settled as the quieter companion piece to that album, and its frank account of how independent artists are paid became a talking point in British music journalism well beyond hip-hop circles.","Start with Gorilla to hear her at full confidence, then go back to the opening track and play the album straight through, because it is built as one arc from grievance to resolve. Listen for the moments where Cleo Sol and the choir soften a verse that is actually furious. Afterwards compare it with Lauryn Hill's Miseducation, another soul-drenched record about faith, self-possession and the business."],"digital-mystikz-pathways":["The first record by Mala and Coki as Digital Mystikz, a short twelve-inch EP of slow, heavy instrumentals. The tempo sits around 140 beats a minute but the drums are placed so sparsely that it feels half that speed: a kick, a snare on the third beat, a lot of empty space. Into that space goes sub-bass so low it is felt more than heard, plus echoing percussion and minor-key pads. It is the sound of dubstep before it had a settled name.","It came out on Big Apple, the label run from the Big Apple record shop in Croydon, south London, which in the early 2000s was a meeting point for young producers such as Skream, Benga and Hatcha who were stripping UK garage down into something darker. Mala and Coki had grown up on reggae sound systems and jungle, and this EP shows both. Within a year they would start their own label and club night, DMZ, in Brixton.","Listen to what the bass is doing rather than the drums. Instead of pumping on every beat as in garage, it moves in slow, wide glides, almost like a dub bassline played an octave lower, and the whole track is arranged around its entrances and silences. The reggae inheritance is not in any sample but in the mixing habit of dropping instruments out entirely and letting echo carry the gap.","Few people outside a small circle of pirate radio listeners and record-shop regulars heard it at the time, and it was pressed in modest numbers. Its reputation grew backwards: once dubstep became an international genre, collectors and historians went looking for the earliest records to state its principles clearly, and this EP is regularly named among them. It is valued now less as a finished statement than as a first draft of a whole scene.","Use proper speakers or good headphones with real low end; on a phone speaker most of this music is simply missing. Let one track play without skipping and count how few events actually happen. Then play King Tubby, whose dub mixes taught this generation to treat space as an instrument, and afterwards Anti-War Dub, to hear how far Mala and Coki's writing developed in two years."],"digital-mystikz-haunted":["A slow, shadowed instrumental from the first run of twelve-inch records on the DMZ label. Eerie synthesiser tones hover over a deep, rolling sub-bass while the drums keep the characteristic half-time step, a single heavy snare landing late in every bar. Little melodic figures appear, repeat and vanish into echo. The mood is exactly what the title promises: not aggressive but uneasy, a track built for a dark room and a very large speaker stack.","In 2005 Mala and Coki, with their friend Loefah, founded DMZ, a label and then a bi-monthly night held in a club in Brixton, south London. The night's rule was 'meditate on bass weight': the music was meant to be felt physically through a sound system in the reggae tradition, not played loud on the radio. Early DMZ sides like this were pressed as dubplates and vinyl for DJs, and circulated through a handful of shops and the online forum dubstepforum.","The track works almost entirely through subtraction. Much of what gives it its haunted character is not what is played but the long decay of each sound, the echo tails left to fade until the next hit. Listen for the moment the bass drops out altogether; in a club that sudden absence of pressure is the most dramatic event in the piece, a trick borrowed directly from Jamaican dub engineers.","Early DMZ releases were treated almost as sacred objects by the scene that formed around them, and the Brixton nights are often described as the moment dubstep found its community. When the genre later swung toward the loud, mid-range 'wobble' style that dominated festivals, older fans and critics pointed back to records like this as the original, more meditative ideal. That argument about what dubstep was supposed to be continues.","Play it late and on a system with real bass, at a volume where the low end is felt in the chest. Do not wait for a drop in the pop sense; follow instead the slow rotation of a few sounds. Then compare it with Burial's first album, which took this same half-time step and haunted atmosphere out of the club and into headphones."],"burial-kindred":["A three-track EP running about half an hour: Kindred, Loner and Ashtray Wasp. Two of the pieces stretch past eleven minutes, far longer than anything on his albums, and each passes through several distinct sections, dropping its beat, drifting through rain-hiss and fragments of pitched-up vocal, then reassembling. The title track is heavy and grinding, Loner borrows the pulse of trance and rave, and Ashtray Wasp ends in a slow, glowing coda that feels like dawn after a night out.","Burial had stayed almost invisible since Untrue in 2007, revealing his name only after a tabloid hunt and releasing no further album. Kindred came out on Hyperdub, Kode9's London label, in February 2012, and began a run of long-form EPs, including Truant and Rival Dealer, that became his main format. By then dubstep had become a festival genre in America, and his decision to go longer, slower and stranger read as a turn away from that loud mainstream.","Listen for the way the longer tracks are stitched together. Rather than developing one groove, he cuts between almost unrelated passages with crackle, silence or a burst of static, like changing channels on a radio, so a single track becomes a sequence of scenes. The vocals are cut from other records and bent until the words dissolve; what survives is a feeling of longing rather than a sentence.","Critics greeted it as a major return, and many ranked it among his finest work; some argued that the long form suited him better than the album, freeing him from the four-minute track. Others found the collage structure diffuse compared with the tight focus of Untrue. Over time the EP run it began has come to be seen as a distinct second phase of his career, with Kindred as its most commanding entry.","Give it a single uninterrupted half hour, ideally at night, walking or on a train, which is how Burial has described imagining his listeners. Start with Ashtray Wasp if you want the most rewarding arc, and wait for its final section. Then return to Untrue to hear how the same ingredients sounded in compressed form, and try Eno's On Land for another portrait of place built from atmosphere."],"lorde-pure-heroine":["Ten songs in under forty minutes, built from very little: a low, deliberate voice stacked into choirs of herself, finger clicks, booming programmed kicks and a few dark synthesiser chords. There is almost no guitar and very little high-frequency sparkle, so the record sounds dim and spacious next to the pop of its year. Songs such as Tennis Court, Team, Ribs and the hit Royals share one texture, which is why the album works as a single mood rather than a string of singles.","Ella Yelich-O'Connor recorded it in Auckland with the producer Joel Little when she was sixteen, after a small record-company development deal and a free online EP that included Royals. The album was released in September 2013, by which time Royals was spreading around the world and would go on to top the American chart and win Grammys. She wrote as a suburban New Zealand teenager looking sceptically at the wealth and glamour that dominated pop radio and hip-hop videos.","The trick of the production is restraint. Joel Little leaves the middle of the sound nearly empty, so the stacked harmonies of Lorde's own voice fill the space normally occupied by instruments. On Ribs, the one song with a racing pulse, listen for how the synth pattern repeats almost unchanged while the vocal grows more urgent; it is a song about fearing the end of youth, and the looping figure sounds like time running on.","Reviewers were struck by the maturity of the writing and the unusual sparseness, and it was named on many year-end lists. A few critics questioned whether its gentle digs at luxury were a fair reading of hip-hop, a debate that followed Royals in particular. Its influence has been easier to measure than its reception: the hushed, bass-heavy, vocally layered pop of Billie Eilish and many others in the following decade traces a clear line back to it.","Play Tennis Court first, then Ribs, then the whole album in order with headphones so the stacked vocals open up. Notice how rarely anything but voice and beat is present, and how that makes each small synth entry feel large. Then compare it with Melodrama, where Lorde and Jack Antonoff bring in piano and far more drama, to hear how she grew out of this minimalism."],"bts-love-yourself-tear":["An eleven-track album about love that goes wrong, sequenced like a story. It opens with Singularity, V's slow, smoky solo, and closes with Tear, a furious showcase for the group's four rappers. At its centre sits the single Fake Love, an emo-tinged mix of rock guitar, trap drums and pleading choruses. Between them the album moves through Latin pop, hip-hop and bright synth-pop, the seven members trading lines so quickly that the voices themselves become the main instrument.","Released by Big Hit Entertainment in May 2018, it was the middle panel of the Love Yourself series, which ran from Her in 2017 to Answer later in 2018 and traced a journey from infatuation through loss to self-acceptance. BTS were by then drawing enormous international audiences through social media, and Tear became the first Korean-language album to reach number one on the American Billboard 200, a moment widely reported as a turning point for K-pop in the West.","Listen to how the album's first and last tracks mirror each other: a lone, quiet voice at the start, four voices shouting at the end, as if the heartbreak has turned from private sorrow into anger. The series also has a visual and narrative layer, a loose storyline told across music videos and short films, and song titles and images across the three albums refer to one another, rewarding fans who follow the whole cycle.","Western critics, some hearing BTS properly for the first time, praised its range and the darker tone of Fake Love, while others argued that the chart success owed more to an extraordinarily organised fan base than to the music. That debate about fandom and charts has followed the group ever since. In Korea it was received as a confident, grown-up record, and it is often cited as the album that made BTS a global rather than an Asian phenomenon.","Start with Fake Love, then play the album from Singularity to Tear in order to feel the story. Try to pick out individual voices: the smooth tenor lines against the rapid rap verses. If you like it, go back to Spring Day for the group's gentler side, then forward to Dynamite to hear how they later reached for a sunny, English-language disco sound."],"bts-dynamite":["A three-minute disco-pop single, sung entirely in English. A bouncing slap bassline, bright brass stabs, handclaps and sweeping strings recall the dance records of the late 1970s, and over them the seven members pass short, cheerful lines between them before gathering for a big, sing-along chorus. There is no darkness or tension anywhere in it; the song is about feeling lit up and dancing through the night, and its sound is built to match that single idea.","It was released by Big Hit in August 2020, early in the Covid-19 pandemic, after the group's world tour had been cancelled. BTS described it as an attempt to give people some energy during lockdown. The song was written by the British songwriters David Stewart and Jessica Agombar, and the choice of English was a deliberate reach toward American radio. It became the first song by an all-South-Korean act to reach number one on the Billboard Hot 100.","Listen to the bass and brass together: the song is a loving pastiche of Chic, Earth, Wind and Fire and early Michael Jackson, down to the octave-jumping bassline and the string swoops before the chorus. The key change near the end, a lift of the whole song upward, is a classic old-fashioned pop gesture, used here without irony. The video likewise dresses the group in bright retro costumes and sets.","It was a huge commercial success and earned BTS their first Grammy nomination, for best pop duo or group performance, making them the first K-pop act nominated in that field. Some critics and longtime fans felt the English-language, songwriter-for-hire approach made it less distinctively theirs than the Korean albums. Others simply treated it as a well-made disco revival alongside Dua Lipa and The Weeknd in the same year.","Play it loud and follow the bassline from the first bar; it drives everything. Notice how each member gets only a line or two before handing on, which keeps the song moving. Then play Chic's Good Times and Michael Jackson's Off the Wall to hear the sources, and follow with Love Yourself: Tear to hear how different BTS sound in Korean on their own albums."],"boogie-down-productions-ghetto-music":["The third Boogie Down Productions album is KRS-One at his most openly didactic: a record that treats the rap song as a lecture hall. The beats are spare and hard, built from booming drum machines, thin sample loops and plenty of space, and several tracks borrow the rolling cadence of Jamaican dancehall chatting. Songs such as You Must Learn and Why Is That? are essays in rhyme, while Who Protects Us from You? turns the same schoolteacher's voice on police violence. The one sound you remember is that voice: loud, patient, relentless.","By 1989 Boogie Down Productions was no longer the duo of KRS-One and the DJ Scott La Rock, who had been shot dead in the Bronx in August 1987. KRS-One kept the name and turned it into a loose collective around himself, his brother Kenny Parker, D-Nice and Ms. Melodie. The same year he led the Stop the Violence Movement, which gathered New York rappers on the charity single Self Destruction. Ghetto Music, released by Jive, is the sound of that mission: rap as community education, aimed squarely at teenagers.","Why Is That? is built almost like a sermon: KRS-One walks through the genealogies of the Book of Genesis, citing chapters as he goes, to argue that the biblical patriarchs were African, and so that schoolbook images of them are wrong. You Must Learn works the same way with a reading list, naming Black scientists and inventors a school curriculum left out. The subtitle is a claim, not a boast: he meant this record as a manual for the art itself.","Ghetto Music sold respectably and was well reviewed, but it has always stood in the shadow of Criminal Minded and By All Means Necessary. Admirers hear the moment the 'Teacha' persona fully arrived and credit You Must Learn with making conscious rap a genre of its own. Sceptics find the preaching heavy and the production thin next to Public Enemy's dense collages from the same years. Most now treat it as essential for its best four or five tracks rather than as a whole.","Start with You Must Learn, then Why Is That?, and listen to how the beat drops out of the way so the argument can be heard. Then play Who Protects Us from You? next to Public Enemy's Fight the Power from the same year and compare two ways of shouting at power: one a lesson, one a riot. A plain remaster is all you need; the power is in the voice."],"drake-nothing-was-the-same":["Drake's third studio album is his most tightly focused: about an hour of muffled, underwater production, mostly by his long-time partner Noah '40' Shebib, in which drums seem heard through a wall and synthesizers hang like fog. It opens with Tuscan Leather, six minutes with no chorus in which the beat changes shape three times, and ends with Pound Cake, a long victory lap shared with Jay-Z. Between them sit the bragging anthem Started from the Bottom and the pastel, eighties-flavoured love song Hold On, We're Going Home.","By 2013 Drake, a Toronto former child actor, had become the most commercially dependable rapper in North America, and his OVO Sound label, co-founded with 40 and Oliver El-Khatib, was building a whole Toronto aesthetic of late-night melancholy. The album arrived in September 2013, the same year as Kanye West's abrasive Yeezus. It deliberately doubled down on intimacy: songs about family, his parents' split, old girlfriends and the loneliness of fame, rapped and sung in the same breath.","Tuscan Leather is built on a Whitney Houston sample, her ballad I Have Nothing, slowed, sped up and chopped differently in each of the song's three sections, so the same few seconds of her voice keep returning in new disguises. The two covers, painted by the artist Kadir Nelson, show Drake as a small boy and as an adult man: the album's argument about who he was before and after fame, set out before a note is heard.","Reviews were largely warm, and the record sold heavily from its first week, but critics split on the persona. Some heard the most honest self-portrait in mainstream rap, a man admitting doubt and pettiness in plain words; others found the self-pity and grievance wearing across an hour. Hold On, We're Going Home is widely treated as one of the best pop songs of its decade, and 40's muted sound became a template that radio R&B and rap borrowed for years.","Play Tuscan Leather in full on good headphones and count the moments the beat transforms; then Hold On, We're Going Home, which drops rap altogether for a soft-rock pulse. Compare Kanye West's Yeezus from the same summer for the opposite answer to fame. After that, Take Care shows where this sound began and The Weeknd's House of Balloons its darker Toronto cousin."],"gang-starr-step-in-the-arena":["Gang Starr's second album is where the duo found the sound that made them famous: Guru's calm, level, almost conversational voice laid over DJ Premier's short, chopped loops of jazz and soul records, with scratched vocal fragments standing in for sung choruses. Eighteen tracks move from the title song's boxing-ring bravado to Just to Get a Rep, a cautionary tale about a young stick-up kid, and Who's Gonna Take the Weight?, a call for responsibility. The signature is that grain: dusty, clipped, head-nodding, never loud.","Guru, a Boston-born son of a judge, and Premier, from Houston, met through New York's small independent label scene; their 1989 debut on Wild Pitch had been uneven. In 1990 they recorded Jazz Thing for Spike Lee's film Mo' Better Blues, which cemented the idea of Gang Starr as the bridge between hip-hop and jazz. Step in the Arena, released on Chrysalis in early 1991, arrived as New York rap was tilting toward sample-heavy, jazz-literate production, alongside A Tribe Called Quest.","Listen to how Premier builds his hooks: instead of a sung refrain he scratches in a word or phrase from someone else's record, often another rapper's, so the chorus becomes a collage of borrowed voices. That technique, heard in embryo here, became his lifelong trademark. Notice too that Guru almost never raises his voice; the drama is carried by the stories in songs like Just to Get a Rep, which end in consequence rather than triumph.","The album was admired in the rap press on release and built a devoted following rather than big sales. Its standing has grown steadily as Premier became one of the most influential producers in hip-hop history; many now hear this, not the more polished Daily Operation, as the true beginning of his style. The only real debate is length: eighteen tracks include some filler, and some listeners prefer the tighter records that followed.","Begin with Just to Get a Rep and follow the story from verse to verse, then play the title track for the beat alone. Listen for the scratched hooks and the way a single horn or piano stab is looped until it hypnotises. Then move on to Daily Operation for the refinement, and to Eric B. and Rakim's Paid in Full for the calm-voiced style Guru inherited."],"gang-starr-moment-of-truth":["Gang Starr's fifth album is long, dark and deliberate: twenty tracks, close to eighty minutes, in which DJ Premier's beats have grown heavier and more minimal, often little more than a thudding drum, a fragment of strings or piano, and a scratched hook. Guru sounds older and more reflective, rapping about loyalty, betrayal, legal trouble and survival. Highlights include You Know My Steez, the brooding title track, Above the Clouds with Inspectah Deck of Wu-Tang Clan, and Royalty with the singers K-Ci and JoJo.","Five years had passed since the duo's previous album, Hard to Earn. In the meantime Premier had become the most sought-after producer in New York, crafting beats for Nas, the Notorious B.I.G. and Jay-Z, while Guru had launched his Jazzmatazz projects with live jazz musicians. By 1998 shiny, big-budget rap dominated radio, and Moment of Truth, released that spring, was a statement that the plainer New York sound still had an audience. It became their best-selling album.","The title track and JFK 2 LAX are more autobiographical than they sound at first: Guru was facing legal problems at the time, and the album repeatedly turns on themes of judgment, testimony and truth, as if it were a courtroom. Listen also to how Premier's scratched choruses quote other rappers' lines, turning the album into a web of references to the wider New York scene he had helped build.","Moment of Truth was greeted as a triumphant return and is often ranked alongside Daily Operation as Gang Starr's best. Critics praised its seriousness and the unusual maturity of Guru's writing. The main complaint, then and now, is length: twenty tracks strain attention, and some guest spots feel dutiful. After Guru's death in 2010 the album gained extra weight as the fullest portrait of him at his peak.","Start with You Know My Steez for the purest Premier beat, then the title track for Guru at his most candid, and Above the Clouds for its eerie, spiritual mood. Listen to it in two halves rather than one sitting. Compare Mobb Deep's The Infamous for another bleak New York vision, and Step in the Arena to hear how far the duo had travelled."],"geto-boys-grip-it-on-that-other-level":["The first album by the classic Geto Boys lineup of Scarface, Willie D and Bushwick Bill is raw, lurid and made on a shoestring: booming drum machines, blunt funk and soul samples, and three voices trading grim stories of Houston street life. Scarface's cool, heavy delivery and Willie D's shouted aggression are balanced by Bushwick Bill's high, cackling voice. Some tracks are straightforward boasts; others push into shock-horror storytelling, graphic violence narrated in the first person, which gave the record its notoriety.","Rap-A-Lot Records was founded in Houston's Fifth Ward by the entrepreneur James Prince, who assembled the Geto Boys from rotating members. With this album, in 1989, the classic trio took shape. Southern rap was then ignored by the New York and Los Angeles industries, and this was among the first records to prove Houston could produce its own voice. Rick Rubin then remixed and repackaged much of it as The Geto Boys (1990), which his distributor Geffen refused to release over its content.","Much of what people know as the Geto Boys' 1990 major-label debut is actually this record, remixed and reshuffled by Rick Rubin; comparing the two shows how a New York producer tried to reshape a Texas sound. Listen for Scarface's solo showcase, named after his own adopted persona, which already reveals the brooding introspection that would make him one of the most admired storytellers in rap once he went solo.","On release the album circulated mostly in the South, but the Geffen refusal of Rubin's version turned the group into a national free-speech story during the censorship battles of 1990. Critics have long been divided: some hear crude shock value, others an early and influential form of horror-tinged storytelling that anticipated much later rap. Its historical importance for Houston and Southern hip-hop is no longer disputed, even by those who find it hard to listen to.","Approach it as a document rather than easy listening. Start with the Scarface showcase for the voice that mattered most, then sample the group tracks to hear the three personalities colliding. Then jump to We Can't Be Stopped two years later, and to Mind Playing Tricks on Me, to hear how quickly the shock tactics matured into psychological depth. Be aware the content is graphic."],"geto-boys-we-cant-be-stopped":["The Geto Boys' commercial breakthrough is a darker, more musical record than their first: slow, heavy grooves built from soul and funk samples, with space for the three voices to tell longer stories. At its centre is Mind Playing Tricks on Me, a haunted account of paranoia and guilt over a mournful guitar loop. Around it sit defiant title-track posturing, horror-flavoured tales from Bushwick Bill, and a pointed protest track against the 1991 Gulf War, sung from the view of young men sent to fight.","The album followed the national uproar over the group's 1990 major-label record, and it was distributed through Priority, the independent that also handled N.W.A. Shortly before release Bushwick Bill lost an eye in a shooting; the cover photograph shows him on a hospital gurney, with Scarface and Willie D beside him, a picture that became one of the most notorious images in rap. The Gulf War was under way, and the group, like Houston itself, felt far from the coastal centres of the music industry.","The cover was not staged: it was taken in the hospital days after the shooting, with Bill still bandaged, so the album's defiant title is literally being tested in the photograph. Listen past the bluster of the opening tracks and you find that the album's real subject is fear, of death, of the police, of one's own mind; Mind Playing Tricks on Me simply says openly what the other songs hint at.","It became the group's biggest seller, carried by Mind Playing Tricks on Me, which critics now rank among the finest rap singles ever made. The album as a whole was more contested: reviewers praised its seriousness in places and deplored its violence and misogyny in others. Today it is seen as a founding document of Southern rap's emotional depth, the bridge between Houston's early shock tactics and the introspection of UGK and OutKast.","Start with Mind Playing Tricks on Me, then return to the beginning and hear the album as a whole, noticing how often the bravado cracks. Listen for Scarface's measured delivery against Willie D's shouting. Next, play UGK's Ridin' Dirty and OutKast's Southernplayalisticadillacmuzik to hear how Southern rap grew out of this unvarnished, slow-rolling Houston sound. Expect graphic content throughout."],"lady-gaga-the-fame-monster":["Eight songs in barely over half an hour, The Fame Monster is Lady Gaga's darkest and most concentrated record. It opens with Bad Romance, whose grinding synthesizers and chanted hook set the tone, and moves through the Euro-disco melodrama of Alejandro, the paranoid club track Monster, the Beyoncé duet Telephone and the brooding Dance in the Dark. In the middle sits Speechless, a seventies-style piano ballad sung almost live. The recognisable sound is dance-pop pushed toward gothic horror.","Lady Gaga, born Stefani Germanotta in New York, had become a global star in 2008 with her debut The Fame. The Fame Monster was first released in November 2009 as an expanded reissue, then sold separately. She described its songs as explorations of fears she faced while touring the world, set against the glamour of her first album. It arrived amid the late-2000s boom in electronic dance-pop, with producers such as RedOne defining the sound of American radio.","Each song was meant to embody a different fear: of love, sex, death, loneliness, addiction, commitment, which is why the collection feels like a series of monster-movie episodes. Speechless, the one ballad, is personal: Gaga said she wrote it about her father's reluctance to have heart surgery, and she recorded it with a live band and analogue equipment to set it apart from the machine-made dance tracks around it.","The Fame Monster was widely received as a leap forward, and many critics still prefer it to everything else in her catalogue. It won the Grammy for Best Pop Vocal Album. Debate has centred on whether Gaga's theatrical borrowings from Madonna, Bowie and Queen amount to homage or imitation; the prevailing view is that the songwriting, particularly on Bad Romance and Dance in the Dark, justifies the costumes.","Play it straight through: it is short enough to hear as one piece. Notice how Speechless drops the drum machines for a piano and a live band, a deliberate break in the spell. Then compare Madonna's Like a Prayer for the model of the pop star as provocateur, and Bowie's Ziggy Stardust for the idea of a persona as the artwork itself."],"lady-gaga-born-this-way":["Lady Gaga's second full album is loud, maximal and sprawling: an hour of pounding European techno, eighties arena rock and heavy-metal guitar, with operatic backing choirs and cathedral-sized reverb. The title track is a disco anthem of self-acceptance; Judas and Bloody Mary rework biblical imagery as club drama; Marry the Night and The Edge of Glory aim for the euphoria of a stadium. The sound you remember is the Springsteen-style saxophone of The Edge of Glory soaring over a dance beat.","Released in May 2011, the album arrived with Gaga at the peak of her fame and as an outspoken advocate for gay rights, which the title song explicitly championed, naming gay, lesbian, bisexual and transgender listeners as equally worthy. It was produced chiefly with Fernando Garibay, RedOne and DJ White Shadow. The pop landscape of 2011 was dominated by electronic dance music, and Gaga pushed it toward a heavier, rock-influenced sound; the record sold more than a million copies in the United States in its first week.","The saxophone on The Edge of Glory and Hair is played by Clarence Clemons of Bruce Springsteen's E Street Band, among his last recordings before his death in June 2011. On Yoü and I, a stomping country-rock ballad produced by Robert John 'Mutt' Lange, the guitar solo is by Brian May of Queen, whose song Radio Ga Ga gave her the stage name: the album quietly acknowledges its ancestors.","Reviews were largely positive, though some critics found the album overlong and overproduced, and the title track was widely compared to Madonna's Express Yourself, a comparison Gaga resisted. Its reputation has grown: the title song became an enduring LGBTQ anthem, and the record's fusion of techno, metal and pop now looks bolder than it did in 2011. Whether its sheer volume is a strength or a weakness remains the central argument.","Begin with Marry the Night and The Edge of Glory for the album's euphoric core, and listen for Clemons's saxophone arriving like a guest from another decade. Then play the title track and Yoü and I. Compare Springsteen's Born to Run for the same appetite for romantic grandeur, and The Fame Monster for the tighter, darker record that came first."],"pixies-where-is-my-mind":["A four-minute song from Pixies' first full album, Surfer Rosa, built on the band's favourite trick: quiet, then loud. Kim Deal's wordless, high backing vocal floats over the opening, Black Francis sings a disoriented, half-dreamy verse, and Joey Santiago's lead guitar plays a simple, descending two-note figure that sounds like a warning siren heard underwater. The chorus crashes in with distorted guitars and David Lovering's dry, boxy drums, then recedes again. The song you recognise instantly is that eerie opening vocal.","Pixies formed in Boston in 1986 and signed with the British label 4AD. Surfer Rosa was recorded in 1987 by Steve Albini, then an abrasive underground engineer, who captured the band nearly live with a harsh, roomy drum sound. American college radio was the natural home for such music; the major labels ignored it. Where Is My Mind? was never a big hit on release, but it became the band's most famous song over the following decade.","Black Francis has said the song came from snorkelling in the Caribbean, when a small fish seemed to chase him: the disorientation of the lyric is literal, a head half underwater, not a metaphor for madness. The song's second life began in 1999, when David Fincher used it over the final scene of the film Fight Club, as buildings fall; for many listeners that image and this song are now inseparable.","On release it was one album track among many on a record British critics admired far more than American ones. Its reputation grew with Pixies' influence on Nirvana and the alternative rock boom, and then exploded after Fight Club. It has since been covered countless times, including in solo piano arrangements, and is now among the most recognisable alternative rock songs of its era. Some fans argue its fame overshadows stranger, better Pixies songs.","Listen first for Kim Deal's high, wordless line at the start and notice how it returns, half hidden, under the chorus. Feel the switch from near-silence to roar, the trick Nirvana later made famous with Smells Like Teen Spirit. Then play the whole of Surfer Rosa for context, and Doolittle for the band's more polished follow-up. The original album mix is the one to hear."],"solange-when-i-get-home":["Solange's fourth album is a nineteen-track, thirty-nine-minute collage: songs dissolve into interludes, phrases repeat like chants, and grooves drift rather than resolve. Warm electric pianos, analogue synthesizers and slow, rolling Houston-style drums carry fragments of melody; Solange sings softly, often repeating a single line until it becomes a mantra. Guests from Gucci Mane to Tyler, the Creator and Sampha appear in passing. The recognisable sound is a hazy, circling loop with a voice floating inside it.","After A Seat at the Table (2016), with its essays on Black identity, Solange turned to her hometown of Houston, where she and her sister Beyoncé grew up. When I Get Home, released in March 2019, is a portrait of that city: its streets, its churches, its rodeos and Black cowboys, its slowed-down 'screwed' hip-hop. She released it alongside a film shot in Houston and debuted it on BlackPlanet, the early Black social network, a pointed nod to digital history.","Many interludes carry the voices of Houstonians, among them the sisters Phylicia Rashad and Debbie Allen, and song titles such as Almeda, Binz and Exit Scott name real streets and neighbourhoods of the city's Third Ward. The slowed textures nod to DJ Screw, the Houston DJ whose 'chopped and screwed' remixes defined the city's sound; the album is as much a map as a record.","Reviews were largely admiring, praising its mood and daring, though some listeners expecting the focused songs of A Seat at the Table found it fragmentary and unfinished. Its influence has grown among musicians who value texture over structure. Critics have compared its looping, meditative approach to Alice Coltrane's spiritual jazz and to Stevie Wonder's synthesizer records, and debate continues about whether it is best heard as a suite or a sketchbook.","Listen straight through in one sitting, ideally on headphones, and let the tracks run together rather than hunting for singles. Start noticing the repetitions: a phrase sung ten times becomes a different thing by the tenth. Then play Almeda and Binz, the most song-like tracks. Compare UGK's Ridin' Dirty for the Houston roots and Alice Coltrane's Journey in Satchidananda for the meditative spirit."],"solange-cranes-in-the-sky":["The centrepiece of A Seat at the Table is a slow, hovering soul song: a gentle drum pattern, a round, melodic bass line, soft keyboards and a string arrangement that swells and falls like breathing. Solange sings in a light, high voice, listing all the ways she has tried to escape a sadness: drinking, dancing, travelling, shopping, working, sex, mentioned in passing. Nothing works, and the song's quiet refusal to resolve is its point. The recognisable moment is the high, sighing refrain over the strings.","Solange said she began writing the song years before its 2016 release, while living in Miami, and finished it with the producer and multi-instrumentalist Raphael Saadiq, whose bass and arrangements shape its sound. A Seat at the Table arrived during a period of intense public debate about police violence and racial justice in the United States, and it framed personal depression within a wider story of Black exhaustion and resilience.","The cranes of the title are construction cranes, not birds: Solange has explained that she saw them rising over Miami during the property boom before the 2008 crash, and they became her image of building over a problem instead of facing it. Listen for Saadiq's bass, which moves melodically in counterpoint with the voice, almost a second singer beneath her.","The song was widely hailed as the album's highlight, and it won the Grammy for Best R&B Performance. Critics praised its restraint and its frank treatment of depression, rare in mainstream R&B. Its video, filmed in stark landscapes with dancers moving in slow unison, was admired as an artwork in its own right. It is now widely considered one of the defining songs of its decade in soul music.","Listen on headphones, focusing first on the bass line under the verses, then on the strings as they rise in the refrain. Notice how the song never builds to a climax; it stays suspended. Hear it within A Seat at the Table and its spoken interludes, then compare D'Angelo's Voodoo for a similarly unhurried groove and Marvin Gaye's What's Going On for soul as personal and political confession."],"de-la-soul-stakes-is-high":["De La Soul's fourth album trades the cartoon collage of their early records for something sober and grown-up: long, loose-limbed tracks built from warm jazz and soul loops, crisp drums and plenty of space for the three members to trade verses. The title track, produced by a young Jay Dee, later known as J Dilla, rides a hypnotic piano figure while listing everything wrong with the state of rap. Guests include Common on The Bizness and an early Mos Def on Big Brother Beat.","By 1996 the trio from Amityville, Long Island, had split from the producer Prince Paul, who had shaped their first three records, and they produced this album largely themselves. It was released on Tommy Boy in the year that mafioso luxury rap and big-budget videos dominated the charts,, and as the feud between East and West Coast rap was turning deadly. Stakes Is High is an explicit argument against that culture, calling for rap to be taken seriously as art and community.","The opening track is a montage of voices, including the group members, recalling where they were when they first heard Boogie Down Productions' Criminal Minded, as if remembering a historic event: a statement of lineage before a note of music. The title song's piano loop comes from a record by the jazz pianist Ahmad Jamal, and its producer, then almost unknown, would soon reshape hip-hop's sense of rhythm.","It was well received but sold modestly, and at first seemed a minor record in a crowded year. Its reputation has risen steadily: many critics now rank it alongside 3 Feet High and Rising, and its title track is regarded as one of the great statements of hip-hop's conscience. Some find it too long and too earnest; others hear the moment underground New York rap found its voice for the next decade.","Begin with the opening montage and the title track, listening for J Dilla's slightly behind-the-beat drums. Then play Itzsoweezee and The Bizness. Compare 3 Feet High and Rising to hear how far the group travelled from its playful collages, and J Dilla's Donuts to hear where that production style eventually led."],"ugk-international-players-anthem":["A five-minute collaboration between Texas duo UGK and Atlanta duo OutKast, built on a lush, soaring soul sample. It opens with André 3000 rapping a long, breathless verse over the sample alone, with no drums, as if talking to himself about giving up bachelor life for marriage. Only when he finishes do the drums and bass crash in, and the song becomes a slow, rolling Southern anthem with verses from Pimp C, Bun B and Big Boi. That drumless opening is its signature.","The track appeared on UGK's 2007 album Underground Kingz, their first after Pimp C's release from prison at the end of 2005. It was produced by DJ Paul and Juicy J of Memphis's Three 6 Mafia and sampled Willie Hutch's 1973 soul song I Choose You. OutKast and UGK had long admired each other; the pairing was a summit of Southern rap. Pimp C died in December 2007, only months after its release, making it an unexpected farewell.","André 3000's opening verse is conceived as a wedding vow in reverse, a man talking himself into commitment; the video, directed by Bryan Barber, stages the whole song as a wedding. Pimp C's verse turns sharply the other way, into crude bravado, a deliberate contrast. The Willie Hutch sample, from the soundtrack of the blaxploitation film The Mack, links the song back to seventies soul cinema.","The song was a critical triumph, widely hailed as one of the best rap singles of the 2000s, and André 3000's verse in particular is often ranked among the finest ever recorded. It earned a Grammy nomination. After Pimp C's death, it took on the weight of a memorial. Some listeners have been troubled by the tonal whiplash between the verses, but most hear the contrast as the point.","Start at the beginning and notice how long André raps without drums, and the release when the beat finally lands. Listen to how each of the four voices has a different rhythm. Then play UGK's Ridin' Dirty and OutKast's Aquemini to hear the two groups at their peaks, and seek out Willie Hutch's original to hear the source."],"ugk-super-tight":["UGK's second album is where their sound came together: slow, heavy grooves led by live bass, organ and wah-wah guitar, closer to seventies Southern soul and funk than to the sample-based rap of New York. Pimp C, who produced most of the music, sings as much as he raps, with a drawling, high voice, while Bun B supplies precise, rapid verses in a deeper register. Songs such as Pinky Ring and Front, Back & Side to Side set the pace: patient, bass-heavy, made for driving.","Pimp C and Bun B came from Port Arthur, Texas, an oil refinery town near the Louisiana border. Signed to Jive, they received little promotion outside the South, and Super Tight, released in 1994, sold largely through regional word of mouth. At the time, rap's national attention was divided between New York and Los Angeles, and Southern rap was dismissed as regional. UGK, along with Houston's Rap-A-Lot artists and Atlanta's OutKast, would change that.","Pimp C had studied music in school and played several instruments, and it shows: listen for how the bass lines move melodically and how chord changes in the keyboard parts follow soul-music logic rather than simple looping. Many sounds that seem sampled were in fact played live, which gives the grooves their loose, human swing.","Super Tight received little national attention on release; its reputation grew slowly as Southern rap rose to dominance. Today it is recognised as the album where UGK's formula was established, overshadowed only by Ridin' Dirty, which refined it. Critics praise Pimp C's production as a crucial model for later Southern producers, though the album's content is often crude and some tracks are uneven.","Listen on a system with good bass, ideally in a car, as intended. Start with Pinky Ring and notice how slowly the groove unfolds. Pay attention to the contrast between Pimp C's drawl and Bun B's precision. Then move on to Ridin' Dirty to hear the refinement, and Isaac Hayes's Hot Buttered Soul for the slow Southern soul in its bloodstream."],"uncle-tupelo-march-16-20-1992":["A punk band puts down its amplifiers. Jay Farrar and Jeff Tweedy, with drummer Mike Heidorn, play almost everything here on acoustic guitars, mandolin, banjo and upright-sounding bass, and roughly half the songs are old: mining laments, a moonshiner's song, a gospel number about Satan's kingdom, the Louvin Brothers' Cold War sermon 'Atomic Power'. The originals, like Farrar's 'Grindstone' and Tweedy's 'Wait Up', sit beside them so naturally that you have to check the credits. The sound is dry, close and unhurried, two plain voices trading verses.","Uncle Tupelo came out of Belleville, Illinois, a declining river town across from St Louis, and had made two loud records mixing hardcore punk with country. Their friend Peter Buck of R.E.M. offered to produce them, and they recorded in Athens, Georgia, over the five days that give the album its title. It came out on the small Rockville label in 1992, the year grunge dominated the radio, as a step sideways: young men from a factory town reaching back to the folk songs of other factory and mining towns.","Listen to how the traditional songs are chosen. Nearly all of them are about work, debt, drink or judgment, the same subjects as Farrar's and Tweedy's own songs about their struggling hometown. The album quietly argues that the Anthology-era repertoire was never nostalgic; it was the protest music of its day. The Louvin Brothers song, a nuclear-age hymn, sounds far stranger and darker played by a young band in 1992 than it does in the original's close harmony.","Reviews were warm but small, and the album sold modestly. Its reputation grew with the band's legend after their 1994 split into Son Volt and Wilco. Many fans now rank it beside No Depression as the purest Uncle Tupelo statement, though some find the reverence a little stiff. It is often credited, along with the magazine that took its name from their first album, with making old-time folk material thinkable for a generation of indie musicians.","Start with 'Grindstone' and then play a traditional track straight after it; notice how hard it is to hear the join. Pay attention to the difference between Farrar's grave baritone and Tweedy's lighter, more wry voice. Then go back to the Harry Smith Anthology or the Carter Family and hear where these songs came from, and forward to Son Volt's Trace to hear where Farrar took the mood."],"uncle-tupelo-anodyne":["The fullest, most country Uncle Tupelo record, cut largely live in the studio by a five-piece band. Max Johnston's fiddle, lap steel and dobro weave through the guitars; John Stirratt's bass and Ken Coomer's drums give it a relaxed bar-band swing. The songwriting splits cleanly: Jay Farrar's songs, such as 'Chickamauga' and 'New Madrid', are grave and historical, while Jeff Tweedy's, like 'The Long Cut' and 'Acuff-Rose', are looser and more melodic. Doug Sahm drops in to sing his own 'Give Back the Key to My Heart'.","Anodyne was the band's major-label debut, made for Sire in Austin, Texas, in 1993 after years on an indie. The lineup had grown from a trio, and the choice of Texas and of Sahm, the Sir Douglas Quintet veteran who had mixed rock, country and Tex-Mex since the 1960s, placed the band in a longer lineage. But the partnership between the two singers, friends since school, was fraying. Within months of release Farrar quit, and Uncle Tupelo ended in 1994.","Hear the album as two records interleaved. Farrar's songs look outward, at Civil War battlefields and earthquakes along the Mississippi, and his voice stays flat and serious. Tweedy's look inward, at the music business, loneliness and his own doubts, and already have the pop instinct that would drive Wilco. 'Acuff-Rose', named after the Nashville publishing house, is a small love letter to the country song as a craft.","Critics received it as the band's most accomplished record, and it sold better than anything before it, but it arrived just as the group fell apart, which gives it an elegiac cast in retrospect. It is often described as the moment alt-country became a recognisable style rather than a stance. Arguments continue among fans about whose songs are better, an argument the split effectively turned into two careers.","Start with 'The Long Cut' and then 'New Madrid', one song from each writer, and notice how the fiddle and steel colour both. The Sahm duet is the best place to hear the band loosen up. Then follow the fork in the road: Son Volt's Trace for Farrar, Wilco's early records up to Yankee Hotel Foxtrot for Tweedy."],"pavement-wowee-zowee":["Eighteen songs in under an hour, and no two in the same mood. Stephen Malkmus drifts from the lazy, lovely 'We Dance' and the country ache of 'Father to a Sister of Thought', with its pedal steel, to the shouted punk of 'Serpentine Pad' and the long, collapsing jam of 'Fight This Generation'. 'Grounded' is one of the band's most beautiful guitar songs; 'Rattled by the Rush' has a lopsided swagger. The record feels like a sprawling notebook: sketches, jokes and finished songs left side by side.","After Crooked Rain, Crooked Rain made Pavement unlikely college-radio stars in 1994, the obvious next step was a cleaner, catchier record. Instead the band, now five with Bob Nastanovich and Steve West, delivered this sprawling set on Matador in 1995, partly recorded in Memphis. It came out as grunge was cresting commercially and alternative rock was becoming an industry; Wowee Zowee reads as Pavement refusing, with a shrug rather than a manifesto, to become a professional alternative band.","Under the chaos there is real craft. Listen to how Malkmus writes melodies that seem to wander and then land exactly; 'Grounded' and 'Half a Canyon' are built on slowly unwinding guitar figures that pay off minutes later. Scott Kannberg's songs, such as 'Kennel District', add a sunnier, plainer counterweight. The album's sequencing, which feels random, deliberately follows every tender song with a noisy one so you are never allowed to settle.","Many reviewers in 1995 heard it as self-indulgent and underwhelming after Crooked Rain, and sales disappointed. Over the next two decades opinion reversed, helped by a 2006 expanded reissue, and it is now often named by fans and musicians as Pavement's richest record. The argument has not ended: for some it is a slacker's scrapbook, for others the purest expression of what made the band special.","Do not start at the beginning. Play 'Grounded', then 'Father to a Sister of Thought', then 'Rattled by the Rush', and you will hear three bands in one. Once you know those, play the album straight through and enjoy being thrown around. Compare its mood swings with the Fall's Hex Enduction Hour, a record Malkmus loved."],"sza-sos":["Twenty-three tracks that roam across genres while staying in one voice. SZA moves from slow, hazy R&B like 'Snooze' and 'Blind' to hard rap cadences on the title track and 'Low', to a pop-punk guitar song in 'F2F' and a hushed acoustic ballad in 'Nobody Gets Me'. 'Kill Bill' sets a darkly comic revenge fantasy against a sweet, almost lullaby-like groove. Phoebe Bridgers, Travis Scott and Don Toliver guest. What holds it together is her conversational singing, which slides between rapping and melody in a single breath.","SOS arrived in December 2022, more than five years after Ctrl had made SZA a star, and after long public delays and label frustrations at Top Dawg Entertainment. Two singles, 'Good Days' and 'I Hate U', had come out years earlier and were folded in. The cover shows her sitting alone on a diving board over open sea, a deliberate echo of a famous 1997 photograph of Princess Diana on a yacht. Streaming had made long albums commercially rewarding, and SOS became one of the biggest releases of the decade.","Listen to the genre switches as a portrait of mood rather than a marketing plan. The songs that sound most confident, with rapped verses and bravado, often sit next to the ones in which she admits the most insecurity, and the order makes each undercut the other. 'Kill Bill' works because the music refuses to match the violence of its premise; the joke and the hurt are both in that gap.","Critics and listeners embraced it almost immediately; it topped the Billboard 200 for many weeks and won a Grammy for Best Progressive R&B Album. Some reviewers found it overlong and less cohesive than Ctrl, a debate the expanded 2024 reissue, Lana, only sharpened. Others argue that its sprawl is its point: a record built for an era of playlists that still reads as one person's diary.","Begin with 'Kill Bill', then 'Nobody Gets Me', then 'F2F', to hear the range in ten minutes. Then play the opening run from the title track through 'Snooze' in order. Compare her looseness with Ctrl and with Frank Ocean's Blonde; notice how she keeps the tone of a voice memo even on the most produced tracks."],"taylor-swift-red":["Sixteen songs caught halfway between Nashville and Stockholm. Banjo and acoustic guitar still drive 'State of Grace', 'Red' and 'Begin Again', but three tracks made with Max Martin and Shellback, 'We Are Never Ever Getting Back Together', 'I Knew You Were Trouble' and '22', are pure pop, one even borrowing a dubstep-style drop. At the centre is 'All Too Well', a slow-building country-rock ballad that recalls a failed relationship through small objects and remembered rooms. Ed Sheeran and Gary Lightbody of Snow Patrol join for duets.","By 2012 Taylor Swift was the biggest star in country music, but she was writing beyond it. Big Machine released Red that October with a roster of producers from both worlds, from her longtime collaborator Nathan Chapman to Martin, Jeff Bhasker and Dan Wilson. The mix of sounds was partly a conscious transition, completed by the full pop of 1989 two years later. Much later, after her early masters were sold in 2019, she re-recorded the album as Red (Taylor's Version), released in 2021.","Listen to 'All Too Well' for the way detail carries emotion: a scarf, a refrigerator light, a dance in a kitchen. The song keeps adding verses where most pop songs would repeat a chorus, and the band keeps building beneath her, so the climax arrives as release rather than hook. The 2021 re-recording included a ten-minute version with extra verses, which became the longest song ever to top the Billboard Hot 100.","Critics praised the songwriting but many found the album unfocused, a record pulled between two genres. It was nominated for Album of the Year and sold enormously. Over time the view has reversed: the variety now reads as ambition, and 'All Too Well' is widely considered her best song. The re-recording and its short film made the album a cultural event a second time, a rare case of an artist rewriting her own catalogue's history.","Play 'All Too Well' first and give it full attention; then the ten-minute version from Red (Taylor's Version) to hear the song's fuller shape. Then contrast 'I Knew You Were Trouble' with 'Begin Again' to hear the two directions the album pulls in. Joni Mitchell's Blue is the obvious ancestor for the confessional detail."]});